  <graph edgedefault="undirected">
    <node id="Basal Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Basal Cell Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Basal Cell Skin Cancer (Basal Cell Carcinoma) is the most common type of skin cancer, characterized by abnormal growth of basal cells in the epidermis. It rarely metastasizes and is often treatable when detected early.&lt;SEP&gt;Basal cell skin cancer is a type of skin cancer that can recur or metastasize, requiring specific treatment strategies based on risk factors and recurrence patterns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Skin Cancer is a broad category of cancers originating in the skin, with basal cell skin cancer being the most common subtype. It includes various types such as squamous cell carcinoma and melanoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Skin cancer is a broad category of cancers that originate in the skin, encompassing various types such as basal cell carcinoma, melanoma, and squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Skin cancer is a broad category of malignancies affecting the skin, including basal cell nevus syndrome and xeroderma pigmentosum-related conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app is a digital tool provided by NCCN.org to help patients understand and manage their breast cancer treatment, including test results and care coordination.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app is a digital tool provided by NCCN.org to help patients understand and manage their breast cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app is an application offering accessible cancer-related information and guidance for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app is a digital platform offering accessible medical information and treatment guidelines for patients with lymphomas.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app is a digital platform offering cancer care information and guidelines developed by NCCN.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app is a mobile application offering accessible information on cancer care, including supportive care strategies and treatment details.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app is a resource that provides guidelines and information about cancer, specifically focusing on liver cancer types like HCC and its treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app is a resource that provides guidelines and information about cancer, specifically focusing on liver cancer types like HCC and their treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app is an application offering accessible information on cancer-related topics, including lung cancer screening.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app is an organization offering mobile access to cancer-related patient guidelines, including resources for basal cell skin cancer diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app provides patient resources and guidelines on immunotherapy side effects and treatment options for various cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UV Radiation">
      <data key="d0">UV Radiation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">UV Radiation refers to ultraviolet light from the sun or tanning beds, which is a primary cause of basal cell skin cancer by damaging DNA in skin cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment encompasses all medical interventions for cancer, including chemotherapy, immunotherapy, and targeted therapies, tailored to the patient's specific cancer type and biomarker profile.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment encompasses medical interventions for basal cell skin cancer, including surgery, radiation therapy, and systemic therapy, aimed at removing or controlling the cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment encompasses therapeutic interventions for prostate cancer, including surgery, radiation therapy, and systemic therapies, tailored based on risk groups and molecular biomarker findings.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment encompasses various medical interventions, including surgery, chemotherapy, and immunotherapy, tailored to the stage and type of bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment involves medical interventions aimed at targeting the BCR::ABL1 gene and its protein activity to control CML, often conducted at specialized cancer centers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Risk Factors">
      <data key="d0">Risk Factors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk Factors are conditions or characteristics that increase the likelihood of developing prostate cancer, including age, race, family history, and genetic mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Risk Factors are conditions or exposures that increase the likelihood of developing basal cell skin cancer, such as UV light exposure, fair skin, and a history of skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Risk Factors are elements such as age, family history, race, genetic factors, diet, and lifestyle that increase the likelihood of developing prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Risk factors are conditions or exposures that increase the likelihood of developing bladder cancer, including smoking, chemical exposure, and genetic predispositions.&lt;SEP&gt;Risk factors for pancreatic cancer include lifestyle factors (e.g., smoking, alcohol use), exposure to carcinogens, health conditions (e.g., diabetes, chronic pancreatitis), and family history of hereditary cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Recurrence">
      <data key="d0">Recurrence</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Recurrence denotes the return of cancer after treatment, requiring ongoing surveillance and follow-up procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;Recurrence is the return of cancer after a cancer-free period, requiring tailored treatment strategies based on the location and extent of the recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;Recurrence refers to the return of anal cancer after treatment, requiring additional interventions such as surgery or systemic therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Recurrence refers to the return of basal cell skin cancer after treatment, which may occur in the original site or spread to lymph nodes or distant body parts.&lt;SEP&gt;Recurrence refers to the return of cancer after treatment, a critical factor in assessing treatment efficacy and planning follow-up strategies for prostate cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Recurrence refers to the return of cancer after treatment, necessitating further evaluation and potentially new treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Recurrence refers to the return of cancer after treatment, which may require additional testing, imaging, or treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Recurrence refers to the return of cervical cancer after treatment, necessitating imaging, biomarker testing, and tailored therapeutic interventions.&lt;SEP&gt;Recurrence refers to the return of cervical cancer after treatment, requiring imaging and biomarker testing to assess and manage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Epidermis">
      <data key="d0">Epidermis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Epidermis is the top layer of the skin, composed of basal cells and other cells, serving as a waterproof barrier and producing melanin for skin color.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dermis">
      <data key="d0">Dermis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Dermis is the middle layer of the skin, containing connective tissue, blood vessels, glands, nerves, and hair follicles, which supports the epidermis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hypodermis">
      <data key="d0">Hypodermis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Hypodermis is the deepest layer of the skin, composed of fat and connective tissue, providing insulation and cushioning for underlying structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cells">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous Cells are flat cells in the upper part of the epidermis, which are replaced by basal cells and contribute to the skin’s surface layer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Melanocytes">
      <data key="d0">Melanocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Melanocytes are cells in the lower part of the epidermis that produce melanin, which determines skin, hair, and eye color and protects against UV radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adrenal Glands">
      <data key="d0">Adrenal Glands</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adrenal glands are small, triangle-shaped glands located on top of the kidneys, responsible for producing hormones that regulate metabolism, stress response, and other critical bodily functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adrenal Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Adrenal Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adrenal tumors are abnormal growths in the adrenal glands, which can be benign or malignant and may produce excess hormones, requiring specialized diagnostic approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Adrenal tumors are abnormal growths in the adrenal glands, which can be benign or malignant and may produce excess hormones.&lt;SEP&gt;Adrenal tumors are abnormal growths in the adrenal glands, which can be benign or malignant, and may produce hormones or cause symptoms depending on their type and location.&lt;SEP&gt;Adrenal tumors are abnormal growths that develop in the adrenal glands, which are located on top of the kidneys and produce hormones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adrenocortical Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Adrenocortical Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adrenocortical Carcinoma is a rare and aggressive type of adrenal cancer that originates in the adrenal cortex and can affect children and adults.&lt;SEP&gt;Adrenocortical carcinoma (ACC) is a rare, malignant tumor of the adrenal cortex that can produce excess hormones and is associated with genetic conditions and metastatic potential.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neuroblastoma">
      <data key="d0">Neuroblastoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Neuroblastoma is a rare, cancerous tumor of the adrenal glands that primarily affects children under 5 years old and has the potential to spread to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Neuroblastoma is a rare, cancerous tumor that primarily affects children under the age of 5 and can spread to other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cushing Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Cushing Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cushing syndrome is a hormonal disorder caused by prolonged exposure to high levels of cortisol, often linked to adrenal tumors that produce excess hormones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pheochromocytoma">
      <data key="d0">Pheochromocytoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pheochromocytoma is a tumor of the adrenal medulla that can be benign or malignant and is characterized by symptoms like high blood pressure and sweating.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adenoma">
      <data key="d0">Adenoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adenoma is a non-cancerous growth on the inner lining of the colon and rectum that can progress to rectal cancer over time.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenoma is a noncancerous tumor of the adrenal glands, which can be functioning or nonfunctioning and may not require treatment if asymptomatic.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenoma is a specific type of polyp that has the potential to develop into invasive colon cancer, classified as an adenocarcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;An adenoma is a type of polyp that can develop into invasive colon cancer, specifically an adenocarcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beckwith-Wiedemann Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Beckwith-Wiedemann Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Beckwith-Wiedemann Syndrome is a hereditary condition linked to an increased risk of adrenal tumors, including neuroblastoma and adrenocortical carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carney Complex">
      <data key="d0">Carney Complex</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Carney Complex is a genetic disorder associated with an elevated risk of adrenal tumors and other endocrine-related conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Familial Adenomatous Polyposis (FAP)">
      <data key="d0">Familial Adenomatous Polyposis (FAP)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FAP is a genetic disorder characterized by the development of numerous polyps in the colon and rectum, which can progress to invasive colon cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;FAP is a hereditary condition causing hundreds to thousands of benign polyps in the colon and rectum, which can progress to invasive colon cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;FAP is a rare inherited syndrome causing hundreds to thousands of polyps in the colon and rectum, which can progress to invasive rectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Familial Adenomatous Polyposis (FAP) is a hereditary condition increasing the risk of thyroid cancer and colorectal cancer, caused by mutations in the APC gene.&lt;SEP&gt;Familial Adenomatous Polyposis is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of developing adrenal tumors and other polyps in the gastrointestinal tract.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hereditary Paraganglioma-Pheochromocytoma (PGL/PCC) Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Hereditary Paraganglioma-Pheochromocytoma (PGL/PCC) Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hereditary Paraganglioma-Pheochromocytoma Syndrome is a genetic disorder that predisposes individuals to adrenal tumors, particularly pheochromocytomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Li-Fraumeni Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Li-Fraumeni Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Li-Fraumeni Syndrome is a rare hereditary condition that significantly increases the risk of developing adrenal tumors and other cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lynch Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Lynch Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lynch Syndrome is a genetic disorder associated with an increased risk of adrenal tumors and other cancers, including colorectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch Syndrome is a hereditary condition caused by mutations in DNA mismatch repair genes, increasing risks for ovarian, colon, and uterine cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch Syndrome is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of various cancers, including ovarian cancer, and is linked to mutations in mismatch repair genes.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of several types of cancer, including bladder cancer, and is often associated with family history.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of various cancers, including bladder cancer, due to genetic mutations in DNA repair genes.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is a hereditary genetic disorder associated with mismatch repair deficiency (dMMR) and high microsatellite instability (MSI-H), increasing cancer risk and requiring genetic testing for diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is a hereditary genetic disorder linked to mismatch repair deficiency (dMMR) and high microsatellite instability (MSI-H), increasing cancer risk and requiring genetic testing for diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is an inherited condition that increases the risk of developing colorectal and other cancers due to mutations in mismatch repair genes.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is an inherited genetic condition that significantly increases the risk of colorectal and other cancers due to mutations in mismatch repair (MMR) genes.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is an inherited genetic disorder linked to mutations in mismatch repair (MMR) genes, significantly increasing the risk of colorectal and other cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is an inherited genetic disorder that significantly increases the risk of developing colon cancer, often detected through family health history and genetic testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 1 (MEN 1)">
      <data key="d0">Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 1 (MEN 1)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 1 is a genetic syndrome that predisposes individuals to adrenal tumors and other endocrine tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 2 (MEN 2)">
      <data key="d0">Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 2 (MEN 2)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 2 is a hereditary condition linked to an increased risk of adrenal tumors and other endocrine malignancies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neurofibromatosis Type 1 (NF1)">
      <data key="d0">Neurofibromatosis Type 1 (NF1)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Neurofibromatosis Type 1 is a genetic disorder that increases the risk of adrenal tumors and other neurofibromas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Von Hippel-Lindau Syndrome (VHL)">
      <data key="d0">Von Hippel-Lindau Syndrome (VHL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Von Hippel-Lindau Syndrome is a hereditary condition associated with an elevated risk of adrenal tumors and other tumors in the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients® Adrenal Tumors, 2022">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients® Adrenal Tumors, 2022</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients® Adrenal Tumors, 2022 is a medical guideline document providing information about adrenal tumors and their management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Binder">
      <data key="d0">Medical Binder</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical binder is a tool used to organize and store medical records, test results, and other health-related documents for easy access.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical binder is a tool used to organize patient health information, including skin cancer screening records and treatment plans.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical binder organizes patient health information, including skin cancer screening records and biopsy results for tracking management.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical binder organizes patient records, test results, and medical documents for easy access and coordination of care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physical Exam">
      <data key="d0">Physical Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A physical exam assesses lymph node swelling, spleen/liver enlargement, and other signs of CLL progression through palpation and observation.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical exam is a medical procedure where a healthcare provider assesses a patient's health by checking vital signs, body systems, and identifying abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical assessment performed by healthcare providers to evaluate signs and symptoms related to AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Physical Exam is a clinical assessment of the body to detect signs of cancer, including head and neck exams, lymph node checks, and organ palpation.&lt;SEP&gt;Physical Exam is a clinical assessment performed by healthcare providers to evaluate a patient's overall health, including vital signs and physical findings.&lt;SEP&gt;Physical Exam is a clinical assessment to detect cancer signs through physical inspection and palpation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI Scan">
      <data key="d0">MRI Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A magnetic resonance imaging scan uses radio waves and strong magnets to produce detailed images of the body, sometimes employing contrast material for enhanced visibility.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan employs magnetic fields and radio waves to produce high-resolution images of soft tissues, avoiding ionizing radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan is a non-invasive imaging technique using magnetic fields and radio waves to create detailed images of internal structures, particularly useful for assessing prostate cancer and detecting spread to nearby lymph nodes or bones.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan is an imaging test that uses magnetic fields and radio waves to generate detailed images of organs and tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan uses magnetic fields and radio waves to create detailed images of the body's internal structures, often used to detect breast cancer metastasis to the spine or brain.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI Scan (Magnetic Resonance Imaging) is an imaging category utilizing magnetic fields and radio waves to create detailed body images, used for cancer staging and diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI Scan is a medical imaging procedure that uses powerful magnets to create images of the body without radiation, available in closed and open designs.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI Scan is a non-invasive imaging technique using magnetic fields and radio waves to produce detailed images of soft tissues, including the prostate and surrounding lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI Scan uses magnetic fields and radio waves to produce detailed images of internal body structures, commonly used in conjunction with ultrasound for precise prostate cancer diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI scan is an imaging technique that uses magnetic fields to create detailed images of the body, used to confirm the presence of lymphoma in the central nervous system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET Scan">
      <data key="d0">PET Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A PET scan combines imaging with a radioactive tracer to show how cells are functioning, helping identify cancer spread and metabolic activity.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan combines imaging with a radioactive tracer to show metabolic activity in cells, aiding in detecting cancer spread and treatment response.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan is an imaging test that uses a radioactive tracer to detect cancer cells and assess metabolic activity in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan uses a radioactive tracer to detect cancer cells by showing areas of increased metabolic activity, often combined with CT for PET-CT scans.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan uses a radioactive tracer to identify cancer cells and their metabolic activity, often combined with CT for enhanced imaging.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan uses radioactive tracers to highlight metabolically active cells, aiding in detecting prostate cancer metastasis and monitoring treatment response by visualizing cancerous activity.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan utilizes a radioactive tracer to detect metabolic activity in tissues, helping identify cancerous cells and their spread.&lt;SEP&gt;A diagnostic imaging procedure that uses a radioactive tracer to visualize cancer cells, which appear as bright spots on scans, and is often combined with CT for enhanced imaging.&lt;SEP&gt;A positron emission tomography scan uses a radioactive tracer to detect cancer cells and metabolic activity, often combined with CT for PET/CT scans.&lt;SEP&gt;PET Scan is a diagnostic imaging procedure that uses a radioactive tracer to visualize cancer cells and metabolic activity in the body, often combined with CT scans.&lt;SEP&gt;PET Scan is a medical imaging procedure used to detect cancer and other abnormalities by tracking metabolic activity in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;PET Scan is a nuclear medicine imaging method that visualizes cellular metabolism by tracking sugar uptake, useful for identifying cancerous cells.&lt;SEP&gt;PET Scan is a nuclear medicine imaging technique that visualizes cellular activity by tracking sugar metabolism, useful for identifying cancerous cells.&lt;SEP&gt;PET Scan uses radioactive tracers to highlight metabolically active cancer cells, aiding in staging and monitoring treatment effectiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;PET scan is an imaging technique using radioactive drugs to detect cancer cells throughout the body, often used in lymphoma diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Test Results">
      <data key="d0">Test Results</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Test Results include findings from diagnostics to determine cancer stage and treatment plans.&lt;SEP&gt;Test Results include findings from imaging studies, biopsies, and other diagnostics, which are essential for determining cancer stage and treatment plans.&lt;SEP&gt;Test Results is a category representing the outcomes of various medical tests, crucial for diagnosing cancer, monitoring treatment efficacy, and adjusting care plans.&lt;SEP&gt;Test results are the outcomes of medical diagnostic tests, providing information about a patient's health status and potential conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Test results provide critical information about the patient's condition, guiding treatment decisions and follow-up care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Records">
      <data key="d0">Medical Records</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical records are comprehensive documents containing a patient's medical history, diagnoses, treatments, and other health-related information.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical records document the patient's health history, test results, and treatment plans for continuity of care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical History">
      <data key="d0">Medical History</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A comprehensive record of an individual's health issues, treatments, medications, and supplements, used to inform treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;A record of all health issues and treatments a person has had in their life, including illnesses, injuries, medications, and supplements.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical History is a record of a patient's health issues, treatments, and medications, used to inform cancer diagnosis and treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical History is a record of all health issues and treatments a patient has had, used to inform treatment decisions for nasopharyngeal carcinoma and other conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical History refers to the record of a patient's health issues, treatments, and family medical background, crucial for diagnosing and managing conditions like AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical history includes a record of past health issues, treatments, and medications to inform breast cancer diagnosis and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical history includes family and personal health data, critical for identifying CLL risk factors and genetic predispositions.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical history is a record of a patient's health issues, treatments, and medications, used to inform diagnosis and treatment plans for conditions like follicular lymphoma (FL).&lt;SEP&gt;Medical history is a record of a patient's past health issues, treatments, medications, and family health background.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical history refers to a record of the patient's health issues, treatments, and family health background, used to inform diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Record of a patient's health history, including family history, crucial for AML diagnosis and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Family History">
      <data key="d0">Family History</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Family History refers to the collection of health information from blood relatives, crucial for identifying genetic risks and hereditary conditions like cancer or diabetes.&lt;SEP&gt;Family History refers to the health history of blood relatives, which may provide insights into genetic risks for head and neck cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Family History refers to the medical history of blood relatives, which can influence an individual's risk of developing prostate cancer due to genetic or environmental factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Family History refers to the medical history of blood relatives, which influences the risk assessment for prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Family History refers to the medical history of close relatives, which is important in assessing the risk of developing certain cancers and other diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history involves the health background of the patient's relatives, which can indicate genetic risks for certain diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history of CLL increases genetic risk, with first- and second-degree relatives 7–8 times more likely to develop the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history of bladder cancer or related conditions, such as Lynch syndrome, elevates an individual's risk due to inherited genetic factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history refers to the health conditions and medical history of a patient's relatives, which can influence their own health risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history refers to the health history of blood relatives, which can indicate genetic risks for breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Information about the health conditions of blood relatives, which can indicate inherited risks for diseases like breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Information about the health history of family members who are blood relatives, which can indicate inherited risks for certain diseases like cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Issues">
      <data key="d0">Health Issues</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Health issues encompass a range of medical conditions, diseases, or symptoms that affect an individual's well-being.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Diseases">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Diseases</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic diseases are long-term health conditions that persist over time and require ongoing management and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A broad category of diseases characterized by the uncontrolled growth of abnormal cells, encompassing conditions like prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A group of diseases characterized by uncontrolled cell growth, with specific types like head and neck, lung, and other cancers linked to tobacco use and imaging diagnostics.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer is a broad category of diseases characterized by uncontrolled cell growth, which can affect various organs and systems in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer is a broad medical category encompassing malignant tumors, with subcategories like HPV-related cancers and non-HPV-related cancers, often addressed through imaging and biomarker testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer is a category of disease that affects the ovaries and sperm, requiring specific treatment plans and precautions to prevent pregnancy during therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer is a category of disease that requires specialized treatment protocols, including precautions to prevent pregnancy during therapy and the use of diagnostic tests to monitor disease progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer is a disease characterized by the uncontrolled growth of cells, including lymphoma, which is a type of cancer affecting lymphocytes and often associated with primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer is a group of diseases characterized by the uncontrolled growth and spread of abnormal cells in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer is a group of diseases characterized by uncontrolled cell growth, often detected through imaging techniques like PET scans, ultrasounds, and endoscopic procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Heart Disease">
      <data key="d0">Heart Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Heart disease refers to various conditions affecting the heart, including coronary artery disease, heart failure, and arrhythmias.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High Blood Pressure">
      <data key="d0">High Blood Pressure</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High blood pressure is a condition where the force of blood against artery walls is consistently too high, increasing cardiovascular risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High Cholesterol">
      <data key="d0">High Cholesterol</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High cholesterol is a condition marked by elevated levels of lipids in the blood, contributing to atherosclerosis and heart disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stroke">
      <data key="d0">Stroke</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A stroke is a medical emergency caused by interrupted blood flow to the brain, leading to brain cell damage and potential long-term disability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contrast">
      <data key="d0">Contrast</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Contrast is a substance used in imaging tests to enhance the visibility of internal structures, aiding in diagnosis and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Allergic Reactions">
      <data key="d0">Allergic Reactions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Allergic reactions are immune system responses to foreign substances, causing symptoms ranging from mild to severe anaphylaxis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medicines">
      <data key="d0">Medicines</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Medicines are substances used to treat, manage, or prevent diseases, with varying mechanisms of action and side effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diabetes">
      <data key="d0">Diabetes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diabetes is a metabolic disorder characterized by high blood sugar levels due to insulin resistance or deficiency.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tracer">
      <data key="d0">Tracer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A radioactive substance used in PET scans to detect cancer cell activity and metabolic processes in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;A tracer is a substance used in imaging tests, such as PET scans, to highlight metabolic activity and detect abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous cell skin cancer is a specific type of skin malignancy often treated with surgery, radiation therapy, or chemotherapy, particularly in high-risk areas like the scalp.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell skin cancer is a type of non-melanoma skin cancer arising from squamous cells in the epidermis, linked to environmental, genetic, and immunological risk factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell skin cancer is a type of non-melanoma skin cancer that arises from the squamous cells in the epidermis, often linked to environmental, genetic, and immunological risk factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell skin cancer is a type of non-melanoma skin cancer that can be classified based on its spread and risk levels, such as local, regional, or metastatic.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell skin cancer is a type of skin cancer that can spread to nearby lymph nodes (regional) or other areas of the body (metastatic). It is the focus of the text's treatment recommendations.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell skin cancer is the second most common type of skin cancer, characterized by its origin in squamous cells of the epidermis, and is often treatable with early detection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Basal Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Basal Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Basal cell carcinoma is a type of skin cancer that arises from basal cells in the epidermis, typically growing slowly and rarely spreading to other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Melanoma">
      <data key="d0">Melanoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of skin cancer, referenced in NCCN guidelines as a condition requiring specialized treatment approaches, often related to HPV-associated malignancies.&lt;SEP&gt;Melanoma is a type of skin cancer that develops from melanocytes, the cells responsible for producing melanin. It is less common than other skin cancers but can be more aggressive.&lt;SEP&gt;Melanoma is a type of skin cancer that originates from melanocytes, the pigment-producing cells in the skin, and is known for its potential to be more aggressive compared to other skin cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nonmelanoma Skin Cancers">
      <data key="d0">Nonmelanoma Skin Cancers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nonmelanoma skin cancers are a category of skin cancers that do not originate from melanocytes, including basal cell carcinoma and squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Nonmelanoma skin cancers are a category of skin cancers that do not originate from melanocytes, including squamous cell carcinoma and basal cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Keratinocyte Carcinomas">
      <data key="d0">Keratinocyte Carcinomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Keratinocyte carcinomas are a category of skin cancers arising from keratinocytes, which include squamous cell carcinoma and basal cell carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Keratinocyte carcinomas are a classification of skin cancers that arise from keratinocytes, the primary cells in the epidermis, encompassing squamous cell skin cancer and basal cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FDG-PET/CT Scan">
      <data key="d0">FDG-PET/CT Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FDG-PET/CT scan is a diagnostic imaging technique that combines positron emission tomography (PET) and computed tomography (CT), using a radioactive tracer (FDG) to detect metabolically active cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;FDG-PET/CT scans are diagnostic imaging techniques that use a radioactive tracer to detect metabolically active cancer cells, often used for adrenal tumors and neuroendocrine tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SSTR-PET/CT Scan">
      <data key="d0">SSTR-PET/CT Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">SSTR-PET/CT scan is a specialized imaging method that uses a tracer targeting somatostatin receptors to detect certain types of tumors, particularly adrenal tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;SSTR-PET/CT scans are specialized imaging techniques targeting somatostatin receptors, aiding in the detection of adrenal tumors and other hormone-producing tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biochemical Tests">
      <data key="d0">Biochemical Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biochemical tests analyze substances like hormones in bodily fluids to assess adrenal tumor activity and hormone secretion.&lt;SEP&gt;Biochemical tests are diagnostic procedures that measure hormones or other substances in bodily fluids to assess adrenal tumor activity or hormonal imbalances.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Tests">
      <data key="d0">Blood Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood Tests are diagnostic procedures that analyze blood samples to detect diseases, monitor organ function, and assess the effectiveness of treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood Tests are diagnostic procedures that analyze blood samples to detect diseases, monitor organ function, and assess treatment effectiveness in conditions like AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood Tests are used to assess general health, liver and kidney function, and other factors before cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood Tests are used to assess kidney function and other parameters, often ordered if physical exams suggest cancer recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood Tests assess general health, organ function (liver, kidneys), and other factors before cervical cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood Tests assess systemic indicators such as kidney function and tumor markers, often ordered if physical exams or imaging suggest recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood Tests is a category of diagnostic procedures used to assess organ function, disease markers, and overall health status in cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood Tests measure levels of thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH), thyroglobulin (Tg), and anti-thyroglobulin antibodies (Tg ab) to monitor for cancer recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood tests are conducted to assess general health, liver and kidney function, and other organ health before initiating cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood tests are diagnostic procedures to evaluate the patient's general health and detect biomarkers related to cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood tests are diagnostic procedures used to analyze blood samples for various health indicators, including cancer-related markers.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood tests are diagnostic procedures used to assess various health indicators, including disease presence and organ function.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood tests are used to detect biomarkers, hormone receptor status, and genetic cancer risk factors in breast cancer diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood tests are used to detect signs of hormone secretion or monitor responses to treatments in conditions like adrenal tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood tests are used to measure various health indicators, including blood cell counts, chemical levels, and tumor markers, to assess overall health and cancer-related conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood tests, including CBC, CMP, and LDH tests, are used to evaluate cancer progression and monitor treatment effectiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood tests, such as a complete blood count (CBC), are used to assess the extent of cancer and guide treatment planning for lymphoma patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagnostic tests to assess blood cell counts and other parameters relevant to AML diagnosis and monitoring.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagnostic tests used to analyze blood components, including white blood cell counts, to detect signs of AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Marjolin">
      <data key="d0">Marjolin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Marjolin refers to a condition where squamous cell carcinoma develops in scars or chronic wounds, such as ulcers and burns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Actinic Keratoses">
      <data key="d0">Actinic Keratoses</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Actinic keratoses are pre-cancerous skin lesions caused by long-term sun exposure, often treated with photodynamic therapy, cryotherapy, or topical therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Actinic keratoses are precancerous skin lesions caused by chronic sun exposure, characterized by scaly or rough patches that may progress to squamous cell carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Actinic keratoses are precancerous skin lesions caused by prolonged sun exposure, characterized by scaly or rough skin patches that may progress to squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Xeroderma Pigmentosum (XP)">
      <data key="d0">Xeroderma Pigmentosum (XP)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Xeroderma pigmentosum is a rare genetic disorder impairing DNA repair in response to sunlight, significantly elevating skin cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Xeroderma pigmentosum is a rare genetic disorder that impairs DNA repair mechanisms in response to sunlight, significantly increasing the risk of skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Recessive Dystrophic Epidermolysis Bullosa (RDEB)">
      <data key="d0">Recessive Dystrophic Epidermolysis Bullosa (RDEB)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Recessive dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa is a severe genetic skin disorder causing fragile skin that blisters easily, increasing susceptibility to squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphoma is a cancer of the lymphatic system that weakens the immune system, increasing vulnerability to squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphoma is a type of cancer affecting the lymphatic system, which can weaken the immune system and increase susceptibility to squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphoma is a type of cancer characterized by the presence of tumors, often involving the central nervous system, and is the primary condition being discussed in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphoma is an abnormal overgrowth of lymphocytes, a type of cancer that can affect the central nervous system and is often associated with primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic lymphocytic leukemia is a slow-progressing cancer of the white blood cells that compromises immune function, elevating the risk of squamous cell carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic lymphocytic leukemia is a slow-progressing cancer of white blood cells that compromises immune function, elevating squamous cell carcinoma risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)">
      <data key="d0">Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HIV is a virus that attacks the immune system, leading to AIDS and increased susceptibility to squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;HIV is a virus that attacks the immune system, leading to AIDS and increasing vulnerability to squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AIDS">
      <data key="d0">AIDS</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AIDS (Acquired Immunodeficiency Syndrome) is the advanced stage of HIV infection, characterized by severe immune system failure and heightened skin cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;AIDS (Acquired Immunodeficiency Syndrome) is the final stage of HIV infection, characterized by severe immune system failure and heightened risk of squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Marjolin’s Ulcer">
      <data key="d0">Marjolin’s Ulcer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A rare and difficult-to-treat form of squamous cell skin cancer that develops in old wounds, burns, or scars.&lt;SEP&gt;Marjolin’s ulcer is a rare, aggressive form of squamous cell carcinoma developing in chronic wounds, scars, or inflamed skin.&lt;SEP&gt;Marjolin’s ulcer is a rare, aggressive form of squamous cell carcinoma that develops in chronic wounds, scars, or inflamed skin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Skin Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A diagnostic procedure to remove a sample of inflamed skin on the breast to identify inflammatory breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Skin Biopsy takes a small sample of inflamed skin on the breast to analyze for potential abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;Skin biopsy is a diagnostic procedure involving the removal of skin tissue for examination, essential for confirming squamous cell carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Skin biopsy is a diagnostic procedure involving tissue removal for histopathological analysis to confirm squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Organ Transplantation">
      <data key="d0">Organ Transplantation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Organ transplantation is a medical procedure that requires immunosuppressants, increasing cancer risk due to immune system suppression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Punch Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Skin Punch Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical procedure where a small piece of skin and connective tissue is removed using a hand-held tool, often followed by stitches to close the incision.&lt;SEP&gt;Skin punch biopsy is a medical procedure used to obtain a small tissue sample from the skin, often performed to investigate suspected hereditary leukemia predisposition syndromes.&lt;SEP&gt;Skin punch biopsy is a procedure obtaining skin tissue samples to investigate hereditary leukemia risk factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Skin punch biopsy is a procedure to collect DNA from non-affected skin tissue, used to test for inherited leukemia predisposition syndromes when AML cells are in remission.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Excision Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Excision Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical procedure where all or part of a lesion is removed to diagnose or treat conditions like skin cancer, often performed in a doctor’s office or hospital.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma (CSCC)">
      <data key="d0">Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma (CSCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A common type of skin cancer that occurs more frequently in lighter-skinned individuals, often found in sun-exposed areas like the head and neck, and can be cured by complete removal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Actinic Keratosis">
      <data key="d0">Actinic Keratosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A precancerous skin condition characterized by scaly or rough skin, which has a higher risk of developing into squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anatomical structures where cancer may metastasize, often targeted for biopsy to assess cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph Nodes are components of the lymphatic system that filter lymph and house immune cells to combat infections.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph Nodes are part of the immune system, and their involvement in cancer metastasis is a critical factor in determining the stage and treatment approach for prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph nodes are part of the immune system and are assessed in the TNM system to determine if cancer has spread to regional nodes (N classification).&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph nodes are part of the lymphatic system where B-cell lymphoblasts proliferate in B-cell ALL, leading to conditions like B-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph nodes are small, bean-shaped glands that filter lymph fluid and play a role in the immune system, often sites of cancer spread in metastatic bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Small, bean-shaped structures that are part of the immune system, responsible for filtering lymph and containing lymphocytes to fight infections and diseases, which can be affected if cancer spreads.&lt;SEP&gt;Small, disease-fighting clusters within the lymphatic system that filter lymph fluid and are common sites for cancer metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Cells">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Abnormal cells that can grow uncontrollably, spread through blood or lymphatic fluid, and form tumors in distant areas of the body, often detected using imaging tests like PET, CT, or MRI.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Actinic Keratorem">
      <data key="d0">Actinic Keratorem</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A precancerous skin condition characterized by scaly or rough skin, which has a higher risk of developing into squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contrast Dye">
      <data key="d0">Contrast Dye</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A liquid used in imaging scans (CT and MRI) to enhance the visibility of tissues and organs by highlighting areas of interest.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous cell skin cancer">
      <data key="d0">Squamous cell skin cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous cell skin cancer is a type of skin cancer that can form in old wounds, burns, or scars, known as Marjolin’s ulcer, and requires various diagnostic and treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Marjolin’s ulcer">
      <data key="d0">Marjolin’s ulcer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Marjolin’s ulcer is a rare and aggressive form of squamous cell skin cancer that develops in chronic wounds, burns, or scars, making it more challenging to treat.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Photodynamic therapy">
      <data key="d0">Photodynamic therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Photodynamic therapy is a superficial treatment that uses a light-sensitive drug and light exposure to kill cancer cells, often used for early-stage squamous cell skin cancer and actinic keratoses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cryotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Cryotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cryotherapy is a treatment that freezes and destroys abnormal skin cells using extreme cold, often used for actinic keratoses and superficial skin cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Cryotherapy is a treatment that freezes and destroys precancerous and cancerous cells using liquid nitrogen, causing temporary skin changes and healing over 2-3 weeks.&lt;SEP&gt;Cryotherapy is a treatment that uses extreme cold to freeze and destroy abnormal tissue, commonly used for actinic keratoses and small skin cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Topical therapies">
      <data key="d0">Topical therapies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Topical therapies involve prescription creams like 5-fluorouracil and imiquimod, applied directly to the skin to treat early squamous cell skin cancer and actinic keratoses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Superficial treatments">
      <data key="d0">Superficial treatments</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Superficial treatments target only the top layer of the skin and are used for early-stage squamous cell skin cancer, such as Bowen’s disease and actinic keratoses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation therapy">
      <data key="d0">Radiation therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiation therapy is a treatment method that uses high-energy radiation to kill cancer cells, often divided into small doses.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiation therapy uses high-energy radiation to kill cancer cells and shrink tumors, often employed as part of local therapy after surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiation therapy uses high-energy radiation to kill cancer cells and shrink tumors, often used in combination with other therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiation therapy uses ionizing radiation to destroy cancer cells, often employed for deeper or more advanced squamous cell skin cancer cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Systemic therapy">
      <data key="d0">Systemic therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Systemic therapy involves medications that travel through the bloodstream to treat cancer, including chemotherapy drugs like 5-fluorouracil, used for more aggressive or widespread cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic therapy is a category of cancer treatment that targets cancer cells throughout the body, often involving chemotherapy or immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic therapy is a treatment approach that targets cancer cells throughout the body, including chemotherapy, HER2-targeted therapy, immunotherapy, and endocrine therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic therapy is a treatment approach that works throughout the body and includes various types of medications and therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical trials">
      <data key="d0">Clinical trials</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical trials are events where treatment options for AML are evaluated, offering potential new therapies and regimens.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials are research studies that test new therapies and treatments, providing patients access to experimental interventions and advancing medical knowledge.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials are research studies that test new treatments, therapies, or diagnostic methods for squamous cell skin cancer, offering potential advancements in care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dermatologists">
      <data key="d0">Dermatologists</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Dermatologists are medical professionals who diagnose and treat skin conditions, including squamous cell skin cancer, and educate patients on self-examinations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mohs surgeons">
      <data key="d0">Mohs surgeons</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mohs surgeons are specialized dermatologists trained in Mohs surgery, a precise technique to remove skin cancer while preserving healthy tissue and ensuring complete tumor removal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical oncologists">
      <data key="d0">Surgical oncologists</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical oncologists are specialists who diagnose and surgically remove cancerous tumors, including those that have spread beyond the skin, using reconstructive techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical oncologists">
      <data key="d0">Medical oncologists</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical oncologists specialize in prescribing chemotherapy and other systemic treatments for cancer, managing drug therapies and monitoring patient responses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pathologists">
      <data key="d0">Pathologists</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pathologists analyze tissue and cell samples from biopsies to determine the stage and characteristics of cancerous tumors, guiding treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Plastic surgeons">
      <data key="d0">Plastic surgeons</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Plastic surgeons remove cancerous tissue and perform reconstructive surgery to restore the appearance of affected areas, often collaborating with pathologists during procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Head and neck surgeons">
      <data key="d0">Head and neck surgeons</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Head and neck surgeons treat cancerous and noncancerous conditions in the head and neck region, including the ears, nose, and throat, with specialized surgical techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation oncologists">
      <data key="d0">Radiation oncologists</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiation oncologists use radiation therapy to treat cancer, targeting tumors while minimizing damage to surrounding healthy tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pharmacists">
      <data key="d0">Pharmacists</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pharmacists prepare and administer medications, including chemotherapy drugs, and provide guidance on their proper use and potential side effects for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Geneticists">
      <data key="d0">Geneticists</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Geneticists study genetic disorders and provide counseling for families at risk, assessing how genetic factors may influence cancer susceptibility and treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oncology nurses">
      <data key="d0">Oncology nurses</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Oncology nurses provide direct patient care, administering systemic therapies, managing treatment side effects, and offering emotional support to cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Skin biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Skin biopsy is a diagnostic procedure used to confirm the presence of squamous cell skin cancer by examining tissue samples under a microscope for abnormal cells."|&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph node biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Lymph node biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph node biopsy is performed to check for cancer spread to lymph nodes, helping determine the stage of squamous cell skin cancer and guide treatment decisions."|&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer metastasis">
      <data key="d0">Cancer metastasis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer metastasis refers to the spread of cancer cells from the original tumor to other parts of the body, often requiring systemic treatments like chemotherapy or radiation therapy."|&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer staging">
      <data key="d0">Cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer staging is a process used to determine the extent of cancer spread, guiding treatment decisions and reflecting prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging is the process of determining the extent of cancer spread, which influences treatment options and prognosis for squamous cell skin cancer."|&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer treatment protocols">
      <data key="d0">Cancer treatment protocols</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer treatment protocols are standardized approaches to managing squamous cell skin cancer, incorporating surgery, radiation, chemotherapy, and supportive care based on cancer stage and patient factors."|&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer recurrence">
      <data key="d0">Cancer recurrence</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer recurrence refers to the return of squamous cell skin cancer after treatment, requiring close monitoring and potential adjustments to the treatment plan."|&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer prevention">
      <data key="d0">Cancer prevention</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer prevention involves measures to reduce the risk of developing squamous cell skin cancer, such as regular skin examinations, sun protection, and early detection strategies."|&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer survivorship">
      <data key="d0">Cancer survivorship</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer survivorship encompasses the physical, emotional, and social aspects of living with and beyond squamous cell skin cancer, including long-term follow-up and quality of life considerations."|&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer-related side effects">
      <data key="d0">Cancer-related side effects</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer-related side effects include fatigue, skin changes, and systemic effects from treatments like chemotherapy, requiring management by healthcare providers and support from oncology nurses."|&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer support groups">
      <data key="d0">Cancer support groups</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer support groups provide emotional and informational support for patients and families dealing with squamous cell skin cancer, fostering a sense of community and shared experiences."|&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer survivorship support">
      <data key="d0">Cancer survivorship support</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer survivorship support involves a multidisciplinary approach to address the physical, emotional, and social needs of patients living with and beyond squamous cell skin cancer."|&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer-related fatigue">
      <data key="d0">Cancer-related fatigue</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer-related fatigue is a common side effect of treatment for squamous cell skin cancer, requiring management through lifestyle adjustments and medical interventions."|&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer-related skin changes">
      <data key="d0">Cancer-related skin changes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer-related skin changes, such as rashes or discoloration, are common side effects that may require dermatological care and monitoring."|&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer-related emotional distress">
      <data key="d0">Cancer-related emotional distress</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer-related emotional distress encompasses the psychological impact of squamous cell skin cancer, including anxiety and depression, which may require counseling and support."|&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer-related nutritional needs">
      <data key="d0">Cancer-related nutritional needs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer-related nutritional needs refer to the dietary requirements of patients undergoing treatment for squamous cell skin cancer, which may be affected by treatment side effects."|&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer-related financial burden">
      <data key="d0">Cancer-related financial burden</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer-related financial burden refers to the economic impact of squamous cell skin cancer treatment, which may require assistance programs and financial counseling."|&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer survivorship education">
      <data key="d0">Cancer survivorship education</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer survivorship education is a critical component of long-term care for patients with squamous cell skin cancer, focusing on managing side effects, promoting health, and preventing recurrence."|&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer survivorship resources">
      <data key="d0">Cancer survivorship resources</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer survivorship resources include information, support groups, and financial assistance programs that help patients navigate life after cancer treatment."|&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer survivorship follow-up">
      <data key="d0">Cancer survivorship follow-up</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer survivorship follow-up involves regular medical check-ups and monitoring to detect cancer recurrence or late effects of treatment, ensuring ongoing health and well-being."|&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer survivorship lifestyle adjustments">
      <data key="d0">Cancer survivorship lifestyle adjustments</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer survivorship lifestyle adjustments include changes in diet, exercise, and stress management to support long-term health and quality of life for cancer survivors."|&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer survivorship mental health support">
      <data key="d0">Cancer survivorship mental health support</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer survivorship mental health support addresses the psychological needs of cancer survivors, including counseling, support groups, and stress management techniques."|&gt;7</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="actinic keratoses">
      <data key="d0">actinic keratoses</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Actinic keratoses are precancerous skin growths caused by long-term sun damage, often treated with topical therapies and surgical procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="squamous cell skin cancer">
      <data key="d0">squamous cell skin cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous cell skin cancer is a type of non-melanoma skin cancer that can develop from actinic keratoses and is treated with various surgical and topical methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mohs surgery">
      <data key="d0">Mohs surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mohs surgery is a specialized surgical technique used to remove skin cancers, allowing for immediate microscopic examination of tissue margins to ensure complete removal.&lt;SEP&gt;Mohs surgery is a specialized surgical technique used to remove skin cancers, particularly high-risk CSCC, by precisely excising layers of tissue and examining them under a microscope to ensure complete removal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PDEMA">
      <data key="d0">PDEMA</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PDEMA (Papillary Dermatofibroma Excision and Mohs Surgery) is a treatment approach for CSCC, often used as an alternative to Mohs surgery, involving the removal of cancerous tissue with wide margins.&lt;SEP&gt;PDEMA (Peripheral and deep en face margin assessment) is a surgical approach used to evaluate and remove cancerous tissue while preserving healthy tissue, particularly in sensitive areas.&lt;SEP&gt;PDEMA (Punch Debridement and Excision Method) is a surgical approach used for removing skin cancer, often considered alongside Mohs surgery for high-risk cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Curettage and electrodesiccation (C&amp;E)">
      <data key="d0">Curettage and electrodesiccation (C&amp;E)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Curettage and electrodesiccation (C&amp;E) is a procedure involving scraping and burning to remove skin cancers, commonly used for early-stage cases.&lt;SEP&gt;Curettage and electrodesiccation is a procedure for removing superficial skin cancers like low-risk CSCC, involving scraping the lesion and using an electric current to destroy remaining cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Topical tazarotene">
      <data key="d0">Topical tazarotene</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Topical tazarotene is a retinoid medication used to treat actinic keratoses by promoting skin cell turnover and reducing abnormal growths.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Topical keratolytics">
      <data key="d0">Topical keratolytics</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Topical keratolytics, such as urea or salicylic acid, are used to soften and remove thickened skin layers before treating actinic keratoses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Topical diclofenac">
      <data key="d0">Topical diclofenac</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Topical diclofenac is a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug used to treat actinic keratoses by reducing inflammation and abnormal cell proliferation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemical peels">
      <data key="d0">Chemical peels</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemical peels, such as trichloroacetic acid, are used to resurface the skin and treat precancerous lesions like actinic keratoses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Laser or dermabrasion">
      <data key="d0">Laser or dermabrasion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Laser or dermabrasion techniques are used to remove damaged skin layers and treat skin conditions such as actinic keratoses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Standard surgical excision">
      <data key="d0">Standard surgical excision</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Standard surgical excision is a procedure to remove skin cancers, involving the excision of the tumor and surrounding tissue for pathological analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph node dissection">
      <data key="d0">Lymph node dissection</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph node dissection is a surgical procedure to remove enlarged lymph nodes that may contain cancer cells, typically performed when cancer has spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sentinel lymph node biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Sentinel lymph node biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sentinel lymph node biopsy is a procedure to determine if cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes by removing and examining the first few nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5-fluorouracil">
      <data key="d0">5-fluorouracil</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">5-fluorouracil (5-FU) is a chemotherapy drug applied topically to treat actinic keratoses by targeting abnormal skin cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Calcipotriene">
      <data key="d0">Calcipotriene</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Calcipotriene is a vitamin D derivative used in topical treatments for actinic keratoses to modulate the skin’s immune system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imiquimod">
      <data key="d0">Imiquimod</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Imiquimod is an immune response modifier used to treat actinic keratoses by stimulating the immune system to attack abnormal skin cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tirbanibulin">
      <data key="d0">Tirbanibulin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tirbanibulin is a microtubule inhibitor used topically to treat actinic keratoses by killing fast-growing abnormal cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hyperkeratosis">
      <data key="d0">Hyperkeratosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hyperkeratosis is a condition characterized by thickened skin layers that are often addressed before applying treatments for actinic keratoses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical procedure to remove sentinel lymph nodes during surgery to determine if cancer has spread, commonly performed during mastectomy or lumpectomy.&lt;SEP&gt;Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy is a category of procedure used to detect cancer spread to lymph nodes, aiding in staging and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy is a procedure to identify and remove the first lymph nodes draining a tumor, used to determine if cancer has spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Sentinel lymph node biopsy is a surgical procedure that removes tissue samples to examine sentinel lymph nodes for cancer cells, typically performed under general anesthesia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mohs Procedure">
      <data key="d0">Mohs Procedure</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Mohs procedure is a meticulous surgical technique used to remove skin cancer, involving the stepwise excision of tissue layers and microscopic examination to ensure complete removal of cancerous cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Standard Surgical Excision">
      <data key="d0">Standard Surgical Excision</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Standard surgical excision is a common method for removing skin cancer, involving the removal of the tumor and surrounding tissue to ensure complete excision.&lt;SEP&gt;Standard surgical excision is a procedure involving the removal of cancerous tissue along with a margin of healthy tissue, commonly used for high-risk or very high-risk CSCC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adjuvant Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Adjuvant Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adjuvant therapy is a treatment category used post-surgery to reduce cancer recurrence risk, including radiation therapy for high-risk tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Basal Cell Nevus Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Basal Cell Nevus Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Basal cell nevus syndrome is a genetic condition increasing the risk of skin cancer, particularly in areas previously exposed to radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Xeroderma Pigmentosum">
      <data key="d0">Xeroderma Pigmentosum</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Xeroderma pigmentosum is a genetic disorder that heightens susceptibility to skin cancer due to DNA repair defects, making radiation therapy contraindicated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lupus">
      <data key="d0">Lupus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lupus is a chronic autoimmune disease that causes inflammation and damage to various body tissues, potentially contributing to the development of primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Lupus is an autoimmune connective tissue disease that may contraindicate radiation therapy due to increased tissue vulnerability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Scleroderma">
      <data key="d0">Scleroderma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Scleroderma is a connective tissue disease that may limit the use of radiation therapy due to compromised tissue healing and increased scarring risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CSCC">
      <data key="d0">CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CSCC (Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma) is a specific type of skin cancer frequently managed with surgical excision, Mohs procedures, or radiation therapy, depending on tumor depth and location.&lt;SEP&gt;CSCC (Squamous Cell Carcinoma) is a type of skin cancer characterized by the uncontrolled growth of squamous cells, often linked to risk factors like UV exposure, smoking, and weakened immune systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FDA">
      <data key="d0">FDA</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The FDA (Food and Drug Administration) is a federal agency responsible for approving new drugs and medical treatments, including those developed through clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The FDA (U.S. Food and Drug Administration) is a federal agency responsible for ensuring the safety, efficacy, and security of drugs, biological products, and medical devices, including overseeing the approval of investigational treatments from clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The FDA (U.S. Food and Drug Administration) is a federal agency responsible for ensuring the safety, efficacy, and security of drugs, biological products, and medical devices.&lt;SEP&gt;The FDA (U.S. Food and Drug Administration) is responsible for approving new drugs, devices, and treatment approaches for cancer based on clinical trial results.&lt;SEP&gt;The Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is a regulatory body responsible for approving new cancer treatments and conducting clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is a regulatory body responsible for approving new cancer treatments, overseeing clinical trials, and ensuring their safety and efficacy for public use.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is the regulatory body responsible for approving new drugs and treatments, including those developed through clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Trial</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A clinical trial is a medical research study that tests new treatments, drugs, or approaches for cancer, often involving patients with specific conditions like primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A clinical trial is a research study involving human participants to evaluate a new treatment or intervention, allowing patients to receive treatment while contributing to medical research.&lt;SEP&gt;A clinical trial is a research study involving human participants to evaluate new treatments, drugs, or procedures, often funded by organizations like the NCI or NLM.&lt;SEP&gt;A clinical trial is a research study involving human participants to evaluate new treatments, such as chemotherapy, targeted therapy, or immunotherapy, for cancer management.&lt;SEP&gt;A clinical trial is a research study involving human participants to evaluate the safety and effectiveness of new medical treatments, drugs, or devices, often conducted under the oversight of regulatory bodies like the FDA.&lt;SEP&gt;A clinical trial is a research study involving human participants to evaluate the safety and effectiveness of new medical treatments, drugs, or devices.&lt;SEP&gt;A clinical trial is a research study involving patients to evaluate new treatments or interventions for cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A clinical trial is a research study testing new treatments, recommended for patients with primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A clinical trial is a research study testing new treatments, such as experimental therapies or combinations of existing treatments, for conditions like primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A clinical trial is a structured medical research study designed to evaluate the safety and efficacy of new treatments, including systemic therapies like chemotherapy, targeted therapy, and immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical Trial is a research category involving patients with metastatic cancers to test new treatments and improve care outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical Trial is an event where patients can enroll to test new treatments, such as targeted therapies and immunotherapies for recurrent ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical Trial is an option for patients when standard treatments are not available or appropriate, offering access to experimental therapies and treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials are research studies evaluating new treatments for metastatic anal cancer, often encouraged for patients with advanced disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials are research studies that test new treatments, therapies, or interventions for gliomas, offering potential advancements in care and outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phase 1">
      <data key="d0">Phase 1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Phase 1 is a stage of clinical trials focused on determining the safety, dosage, and initial side effects of an investigational drug or treatment approach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phase 2">
      <data key="d0">Phase 2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Phase 2 is a stage of clinical trials that assesses the effectiveness of a treatment against a specific type of cancer while monitoring for side effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phase 3">
      <data key="d0">Phase 3</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Phase 3 is a stage of clinical trials that compares the investigational treatment to a standard treatment to confirm its efficacy and monitor long-term side effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phase 4">
      <data key="d0">Phase 4</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Phase 4 is a stage of clinical trials that studies the long-term safety and benefits of an FDA-approved treatment after it has been marketed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Side Effects">
      <data key="d0">Side Effects</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Side Effects are adverse reactions or complications that may occur during cancer treatments, such as chemotherapy, radiation, or surgery, affecting patients' quality of life.&lt;SEP&gt;Side Effects are temporary or long-term consequences of cancer treatments, including surgery, radiation, and systemic therapies, which are managed to optimize patient outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Side effects are adverse effects associated with AML treatments, such as chemotherapy and radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Side effects are adverse effects of cancer treatments, such as nausea and vomiting, which require management by patients and their care teams.&lt;SEP&gt;Side effects are adverse physical or emotional conditions caused by cancer treatments, which vary depending on the type of treatment, dosage, and individual patient factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Side effects are adverse reactions or complications associated with cancer treatments like chemotherapy, immunotherapy, and surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;Side effects are adverse reactions or complications associated with systemic therapies, ranging from mild (e.g., fatigue) to severe (e.g., organ damage or secondary cancers).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immune-Related Adverse Events (irAEs)">
      <data key="d0">Immune-Related Adverse Events (irAEs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immune-related adverse events (irAEs) are side effects caused by immunotherapy, involving overactivation of the immune system and potential damage to healthy tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cisplatin">
      <data key="d0">Cisplatin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cisplatin is a chemotherapy drug used in ovarian cancer treatment, linked to hypersensitivity reactions and allergic responses.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapy drug used as a first-line treatment for metastatic cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapy drug used to treat various cancers, including squamous cell skin cancer, and is associated with significant side effects due to its systemic impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is an intravenous chemotherapy drug used in regimens for anal cancer, often combined with 5-FU to enhance radiation therapy efficacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carboplatin">
      <data key="d0">Carboplatin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Carboplatin is a chemotherapy drug used in ovarian cancer treatment, associated with hypersensitivity reactions during repeated use.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapy drug used in combination with paclitaxel for treating metastatic cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapy drug used to treat cancers like squamous cell skin cancer, with a similar mechanism to cisplatin but potentially fewer side effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cetuximab">
      <data key="d0">Cetuximab</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cetuximab is an EGFR inhibitor used in targeted therapy for cancers such as squamous cell skin cancer, targeting specific proteins on cancer cells to block growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cemiplimab">
      <data key="d0">Cemiplimab</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cemiplimab is a PD-1 inhibitor used in immunotherapy to enhance the immune system's ability to attack cancer cells, particularly in advanced squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pembrolizumab">
      <data key="d0">Pembrolizumab</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pembrolizumab (Keytruda) is a checkpoint inhibitor immunotherapy drug used for dMMR/MSI-H cancers to improve immune response against cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Pembrolizumab is a PD-1 inhibitor used in immunotherapy to improve the immune system's response to cancer, often in combination with other treatments for various malignancies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EGFR">
      <data key="d0">EGFR</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">EGFR (epidermal growth factor receptor) is a protein on the surface of cancer cells that promotes growth and survival, making it a target for specific therapies like cetuximab.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PD-1">
      <data key="d0">PD-1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PD-1 (programmed cell death protein 1) is a protein on immune cells that inhibits their activity, and its blockade by immunotherapies like pembrolizumab helps the immune system attack cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer (CSCC)">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Skin Cancer (CSCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous cell skin cancer (CSCC) is a type of non-melanoma skin cancer that arises from squamous cells in the outer layer of the skin.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell skin cancer (CSCC) is a type of skin cancer characterized by the uncontrolled growth of squamous cells, often treated with systemic therapies like chemotherapy, targeted therapy, and immunotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Cancer Institute (NCI)">
      <data key="d0">National Cancer Institute (NCI)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The National Cancer Institute (NCI) is a U.S. federal agency that conducts and supports cancer research, including clinical trials and treatment guidelines.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Cancer Institute (NCI) is a U.S. federal agency under the National Institutes of Health (NIH) that leads cancer research and provides information on cancer treatment and clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Cancer Institute (NCI) is a U.S. federal agency under the National Institutes of Health (NIH) that leads cancer research and provides information on cancer treatment, including anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Cancer Institute (NCI) is a U.S. federal agency under the National Institutes of Health (NIH) that leads cancer research, provides treatment guidelines, and offers resources on anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Cancer Institute (NCI) is a U.S. federal agency under the National Institutes of Health that conducts and supports cancer research, training, and education, including clinical trials and treatment guidelines.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Cancer Institute (NCI) is a U.S. federal agency under the National Institutes of Health that conducts and supports cancer research, training, and education.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Cancer Institute (NCI) is a U.S. government agency that funds and conducts cancer research, including clinical trials, to improve cancer treatment and prevention.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Cancer Institute (NCI) is a federal agency under the National Institutes of Health (NIH) that conducts and funds cancer research, provides treatment guidelines, and offers resources for clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM)">
      <data key="d0">U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM) is a federal agency that collects, provides access to, and advances biomedical information through databases and resources, including clinical trial information.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM) is a federal agency that collects, provides access to, and advances biomedical information through databases and resources.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM) is a federal agency that provides access to biomedical information and resources, including the ClinicalTrials.gov database.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM) is a federal agency that provides access to biomedical information, including clinical trial databases like ClinicalTrials.gov.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM) is a federal agency that provides access to biomedical information, including resources on anal cancer and related conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Cancer Centers">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Cancer Centers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN Cancer Centers are a network of cancer treatment centers recognized for their expertise in providing comprehensive cancer care, including for anal cancer, and are involved in clinical trial enrollment.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN Cancer Centers are a network of cancer treatment centers recognized for their expertise in providing comprehensive cancer care, including for anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN Cancer Centers are a network of cancer treatment centers that provide resources and information for patients seeking clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN Cancer Centers are a network of cancer treatment facilities that provide clinical care, research, and resources for cancer patients, including access to clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Cancer Centers are a network of cancer treatment facilities that provide care and research opportunities for patients, including information on clinical trials and treatment guidelines.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Cancer Centers are a network of cancer treatment facilities that provide care and research opportunities for patients, including information on clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Cancer Centers are a network of cancer treatment facilities that provide expert care and resources for cancer patients, including information on clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Local Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Local cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma refers to squamous cell skin cancer that has not spread to nearby lymph nodes and is typically treated with surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Risk Squamous Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">High-Risk Squamous Cell Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-risk squamous cell skin cancer has an increased risk of local recurrence and may require more aggressive treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Very High-Risk Squamous Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Very High-Risk Squamous Cell Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Very high-risk squamous cell skin cancer has an increased risk of local recurrence and metastasis (spread), often requiring specialized treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Photodynamic Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Photodynamic Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Photodynamic therapy is a treatment that uses light-sensitive drugs and laser light to destroy cancer cells, often used for actinic keratoses and superficial skin cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Topical Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Topical Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Topical therapy involves applying medications directly to the skin to treat conditions like actinic keratoses, often in the form of creams or gels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Perineural Invasion">
      <data key="d0">Perineural Invasion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Perineural invasion is a condition where cancer cells invade surrounding nerves, often associated with high-risk or very high-risk squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ClinicalTrials.gov">
      <data key="d0">ClinicalTrials.gov</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">ClinicalTrials.gov is a database maintained by the U.S. National Library of Medicine that lists ongoing and completed clinical trials for various diseases, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;ClinicalTrials.gov is a database of clinical trials registered and managed by the U.S. National Library of Medicine, providing information on ongoing and completed studies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="low-risk">
      <data key="d0">low-risk</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Low-risk CSCC refers to a classification of squamous cell skin cancer with minimal likelihood of spreading, typically treated with less aggressive interventions like curettage or standard excision.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="high-risk">
      <data key="d0">high-risk</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-risk CSCC denotes a more aggressive form of squamous cell skin cancer with a higher probability of recurrence or metastasis, requiring advanced treatments like Mohs surgery or systemic therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="very high-risk">
      <data key="d0">very high-risk</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Very high-risk CSCC is a severe classification of squamous cell skin cancer, often associated with deep tumor invasion, lymphovascular invasion, or compromised immune systems, necessitating intensive treatment protocols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="shave excision">
      <data key="d0">shave excision</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Shave excision is a method of removing skin lesions by slicing the tumor off the surface of the skin, typically used for early-stage, low-risk CSCC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="standard surgical excision">
      <data key="d0">standard surgical excision</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Standard surgical excision involves removing the tumor along with a margin of healthy tissue, commonly used for low-risk CSCC when deeper invasion is not suspected.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="radiation therapy">
      <data key="d0">radiation therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiation therapy uses high-energy beams to kill cancer cells and is recommended when surgery is not feasible, particularly for high-risk or very high-risk CSCC.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiation therapy uses high-energy rays to kill cancer cells, often employed for localized or advanced-stage prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="platinum-based chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">platinum-based chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Platinum-based chemotherapy is a systemic treatment involving platinum compounds to target cancer cells, often used in combination with radiation for advanced CSCC cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="immunotherapy with an immune checkpoint inhibitor">
      <data key="d0">immunotherapy with an immune checkpoint inhibitor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunotherapy with immune checkpoint inhibitors enhances the body's immune response to cancer, preferred for certain high-risk CSCC cases where other treatments are insufficient.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="lymphovascular invasion">
      <data key="d0">lymphovascular invasion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphovascular invasion refers to the presence of cancer cells in lymph or blood vessels outside the main tumor, indicating a higher risk of metastasis and influencing treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="smoking">
      <data key="d0">smoking</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Smoking and vaping are risk factors for various cancers and can negatively impact cancer treatment outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking is a significant risk factor for CSCC, increasing the likelihood of cancer development and complicating treatment outcomes due to its impact on healing and immune function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="World Health Organization">
      <data key="d0">World Health Organization</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The World Health Organization (WHO) is a global health organization that provides information and guidance on various health conditions, including kidney cancer and cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;The World Health Organization (WHO) is a global health organization that provides information and guidance on various health conditions, including kidney cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The World Health Organization (WHO) is a specialized agency of the United Nations responsible for international public health, including guidelines and initiatives for cancer treatment and prevention.&lt;SEP&gt;The World Health Organization (WHO) is a specialized agency of the United Nations responsible for international public health, including the classification of lymphomas like follicular lymphoma (FL).&lt;SEP&gt;The World Health Organization (WHO) is an international organization that classifies and standardizes medical conditions, including follicular lymphoma grades.&lt;SEP&gt;The World Health Organization is an international public health organization that provides guidelines and information on cancer treatment and management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="American Cancer Society">
      <data key="d0">American Cancer Society</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The American Cancer Society is a nonprofit organization that offers resources and information on cancer treatment options and patient support.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Cancer Society is a nonprofit organization that provides resources and support for cancer patients, including information on anal cancer treatment and research.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Cancer Society is a nonprofit organization that provides resources, support, and advocacy for cancer patients, including information on anal cancer treatment and research.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Cancer Society is a prominent organization providing resources and support for cancer patients, including information on treatment options and survivorship.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Cancer Society is a prominent organization providing resources and support for cancer patients, including information on treatment options, survivorship, and advocacy for anal cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local CSCC">
      <data key="d0">Local CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Local CSCC refers to squamous cell skin cancer that has not spread to nearby lymph nodes and is typically treated with surgical or radiation methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regional CSCC">
      <data key="d0">Regional CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Regional CSCC is squamous cell skin cancer that has spread to nearby lymph nodes, requiring more aggressive treatment options like surgery or systemic therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic CSCC">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic CSC, or squamous cell skin cancer that has spread to distant parts of the body, often necessitating systemic therapy or immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic CSCC is squamous cell skin cancer that has spread to distant parts of the body, often necessitating systemic therapy or immunotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low-risk CSCC">
      <data key="d0">Low-risk CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Low-risk CSCC is a classification of squamous cell skin cancer with minimal likelihood of recurrence, often treated with less invasive procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-risk CSCC">
      <data key="d0">High-risk CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-risk CSCC is a classification of squamous cell skin cancer with a higher chance of recurrence or progression, requiring more intensive treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Very High-risk CSCC">
      <data key="d0">Very High-risk CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Very High-risk CSCC is a severe form of squamous cell skin cancer that requires immediate and aggressive treatment to prevent further spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mohs Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Mohs Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mohs surgery is a specialized surgical technique used to remove skin cancer layer by layer, ensuring complete excision of malignant tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Platinum-based Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Platinum-based Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Platinum-based chemotherapy is a systemic treatment option used in combination with radiation therapy for advanced or high-risk squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fine-needle Aspiration">
      <data key="d0">Fine-needle Aspiration</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fine-needle aspiration is a biopsy technique used to extract fluid or tissue samples from suspicious lymph nodes to assess for cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Core Needle Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Core Needle Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Core Needle Biopsy is a procedure to remove tissue samples from a suspicious area, often guided by imaging techniques like ultrasound or MRI.&lt;SEP&gt;Core needle biopsy is a diagnostic procedure that removes a small core of tissue from a suspicious lymph node to confirm the presence of cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Squamous Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Squamous Cell Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic squamous cell skin cancer refers to cancer that has spread through blood or lymph to form tumors in other parts of the body. This condition is less commonly studied and requires systemic therapy as a primary treatment option.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regional Squamous Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Regional Squamous Cell Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Regional squamous cell skin cancer is cancer that has spread to nearby lymph nodes. Surgery to remove the tumor and affected lymph nodes is the recommended treatment, followed by radiation or systemic therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Comprehensive Cancer Network">
      <data key="d0">National Comprehensive Cancer Network</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is an organization that provides a feedback survey for patients to evaluate cancer information and resources.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is an organization that provides cancer information and offers a patient feedback survey to improve resources and understanding.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is an organization that provides clinical guidelines and resources for cancer care, including the NCCN Guidelines for Patients, which are trusted by healthcare providers and patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is an organization that provides guidelines and recommendations for cancer treatment, including follow-up care and monitoring protocols.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is an organization that provides guidelines and recommendations for cancer treatment, including the recommendation for clinical trials in primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is an organization that provides guidelines and resources for cancer care, including information on diagnosing and treating Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML).&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is an organization that provides guidelines for patients and healthcare providers regarding cancer care, including information on Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) and its subtypes.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network is an alliance of leading cancer centers that develops evidence-based cancer care recommendations used globally.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follow-Up Exams">
      <data key="d0">Follow-Up Exams</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Follow-up exams are regular medical assessments conducted by doctors to monitor for cancer recurrence or new skin cancers, especially for patients who have completed treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Platinum-Based Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Platinum-Based Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Platinum-based chemotherapy involves drugs like cisplatin and oxaliplatin, which are effective against certain cancers but can cause kidney damage and peripheral neuropathy as side effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Platinum-based chemotherapy involves drugs like cisplatin and oxaliplatin, which can cause kidney damage and nerve toxicity.&lt;SEP&gt;Platinum-based chemotherapy is a specific type of chemotherapy that includes drugs like cisplatin or carboplatin, preferred for use with radiation therapy in certain cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Platinum-based chemotherapy is a systemic treatment using platinum compounds to kill cancer cells, often used for metastatic cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor">
      <data key="d0">Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immune checkpoint inhibitors are a class of immunotherapy drugs that block proteins preventing the immune system from attacking cancer cells, recommended for eligible patients with metastatic squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stereotactic Body Radiotherapy (SBRT)">
      <data key="d0">Stereotactic Body Radiotherapy (SBRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stereotactic body radiotherapy (SBRT) is a specialized radiation technique that delivers high-dose radiation to small, precise areas over a short period, used for treating distant tumors causing symptoms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgery is a medical procedure used to remove as much of the glioma as possible, often combined with other treatments like radiation therapy and chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery is a primary treatment method for thyroid cancer, involving the removal of the thyroid gland, either partially (lobectomy) or entirely (total thyroidectomy), depending on the cancer's characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery is a primary treatment modality for bladder cancer, including procedures like TURBT and radical cystectomy, depending on the stage and grade of the tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery is a procedure to remove cancerous tissue, including secondary cytoreductive surgery for recurrent ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery is a treatment method for early or locally advanced NSCLC, involving the removal of cancerous tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery is a treatment method involving the removal of tumors and nearby lymph nodes, often followed by radiation therapy or systemic therapy to address residual cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery is a treatment modality for localized cervical cancer recurrence, often combined with radiation or systemic therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery is a treatment modality for prostate cancer, often considered for higher-risk cases, with potential complications and side effects that influence treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery is a treatment option for cancer that can be removed based on its size and location, particularly for localized thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery is a treatment option for localized cervical cancer recurrence, often followed by radiation or systemic therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery is the most effective treatment for thyroid cancer, involving the removal of the thyroid gland or affected lobes, often followed by hormone therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Carcinoma (CSCC)">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Carcinoma (CSCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous Cell Carcinoma (CSCC) is a type of skin cancer that requires regular monitoring and specific follow-up exam schedules, particularly for patients with high or very high risk factors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML)">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) is a type of blood cancer caused by a genetic abnormality involving the BCR::ABL1 gene and the Philadelphia (Ph) chromosome.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) is a type of blood cancer caused by a genetic mutation involving chromosomes 9 and 22, resulting in the BCR::ABL1 gene and the Philadelphia chromosome.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Philadelphia Chromosome">
      <data key="d0">Philadelphia Chromosome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Philadelphia Chromosome is a shortened chromosome 22 resulting from a reciprocal translocation between chromosomes 9 and 22, leading to the formation of the BCR::ABL1 gene fusion.&lt;SEP&gt;The Philadelphia chromosome is a shortened chromosome 22 formed by a reciprocal translocation between chromosomes 9 and 22, which is a defining genetic abnormality in Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML).&lt;SEP&gt;The Philadelphia chromosome is an abnormal chromosome (chromosome 22) formed by a translocation between chromosome 9 and 22, leading to the BCR::ABL1 gene fusion and hallmark of CML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BCR::ABL1 Gene">
      <data key="d0">BCR::ABL1 Gene</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The BCR::ABL1 gene is a fused gene resulting from the translocation between chromosomes 9 and 22, which is the primary genetic cause of Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML).&lt;SEP&gt;The BCR::ABL1 gene is a fusion gene resulting from the Philadelphia chromosome translocation, which produces a protein causing uncontrolled blood cell growth and is central to CML pathogenesis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Cells">
      <data key="d0">Blood Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood cells are the components of blood, including red blood cells (RBCs), white blood cells (WBCs), and platelets, which play critical roles in oxygen transport, immune function, and clotting.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Exams">
      <data key="d0">Skin Exams</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Skin exams are medical procedures recommended for patients with skin cancer or high-risk conditions to monitor for recurrence or progression of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CML">
      <data key="d0">CML</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CML (Chronic Myeloid Leukemia) is a type of blood cancer characterized by the overproduction of myeloid progenitor cells and the presence of the Philadelphia chromosome, which results from a genetic fusion of the BCR and ABL genes.&lt;SEP&gt;CML (Chronic Myeloid Leukemia) is a type of cancer caused by the BCR::ABL1 gene and the Philadelphia chromosome, leading to the overproduction of immature blood cells and uncontrolled growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NSCLC">
      <data key="d0">NSCLC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NSCLC (Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer) is a type of lung cancer characterized by slow growth and is often linked to smoking and exposure to carcinogens.&lt;SEP&gt;NSCLC (Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer) is the most common type of lung cancer, originating from cells lining the airways of the lungs and characterized by uncontrolled growth of lung carcinoma cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Myeloid Progenitor Cells">
      <data key="d0">Myeloid Progenitor Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Myeloid progenitor cells are precursor cells in the bone marrow that differentiate into non-lymphoid blood cells, and their overproduction is a key feature of CML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphoblasts">
      <data key="d0">Lymphoblasts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphoblasts are immature lymphoid cells that, when overproduced, can contribute to blast phase CML, a more aggressive form of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Myeloblasts">
      <data key="d0">Myeloblasts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Myeloblasts are immature myeloid cells that, when overproduced, are referred to as blasts and are associated with blast phase CML and the progression of the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloblasts are immature white blood cells that mature into granulocytes (such as neutrophils) and are central to the development of Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blast Phase CML">
      <data key="d0">Blast Phase CML</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blast phase CML (BP-CML) is an advanced stage of CML characterized by the proliferation of immature blasts (myeloid or lymphoid) and requires more aggressive treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lung Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Lung Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lung Cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the cells lining the airways of the lungs, characterized by uncontrolled cell growth leading to tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Lung cancer is a broad category of cancers affecting the lungs, encompassing subtypes like NSCLC and SCLC, with causes including smoking, secondhand smoke, and carcinogens.&lt;SEP&gt;Lung cancer is a malignant tumor originating from lung cells, with NSCLC being the most common type, and includes subtypes such as small cell lung cancer and neuroendocrine tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Lung cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the cells of the lungs, with subtypes including non-small cell and small cell lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neuroendocrine Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Neuroendocrine Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Neuroendocrine tumors are a type of lung carcinoma that arises from neuroendocrine cells in the lungs, often associated with specific genetic and molecular markers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alveoli">
      <data key="d0">Alveoli</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Alveoli are tiny air sacs in the lungs where gas exchange occurs, and their structure is critical for lung function and the progression of lung diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bronchi">
      <data key="d0">Bronchi</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bronchi are the large airways that branch off the trachea into the lungs, forming part of the respiratory system and serving as sites for lung cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bronchioli">
      <data key="d0">Bronchioli</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bronchioli are small airways that branch from the bronchi, leading to the alveoli, and are involved in the pathophysiology of lung diseases like NSCLC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trachea">
      <data key="d0">Trachea</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The trachea (windpipe) is the main airway that connects the throat to the lungs, and its function is critical for respiration and the spread of lung cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Airways">
      <data key="d0">Airways</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Airways refer to the entire network of passages in the respiratory system, including the trachea, bronchi, bronchioli, and alveoli, and are central to lung cancer progression and diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Carcinoma is a type of cancer that originates from epithelial cells, including lung carcinomas like NSCLC and neuroendocrine tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer (NSCLC)">
      <data key="d0">Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer (NSCLC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NSCLC is a type of lung cancer characterized by its origin in specific lung cells, with subcategories like adenocarcinoma, large cell carcinoma, and squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Early and Locally Advanced NSCLC">
      <data key="d0">Early and Locally Advanced NSCLC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Early and locally advanced NSCLC refers to cancer stages 1, 2, and 3, where the cancer has grown into lung tissue or spread to nearby lymph nodes but not distant organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic NSCLC">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic NSCLC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic NSCLC is stage 4 cancer, where the disease has spread to other organs or tissues beyond the lungs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Supportive Care">
      <data key="d0">Supportive Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Supportive care addresses symptoms and improves quality of life for pancreatic cancer patients, including pain management and psychological support.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care addresses symptoms and improves quality of life for patients with NSCLC, including managing side effects and enhancing daily living.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care focuses on relieving cancer symptoms and treatment side effects, improving quality of life without directly targeting cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care focuses on relieving symptoms and improving quality of life for cancer patients, addressing side effects of treatment and managing distress.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care involves managing symptoms and side effects of AML and its treatments to improve quality of life.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care refers to healthcare services designed to relieve symptoms caused by cancer or its treatments, aiming to improve the patient's quality of life through symptom management and emotional support.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care refers to the management of side effects and complications associated with cancer treatments, enhancing patient quality of life.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care refers to treatments aimed at relieving cancer symptoms and side effects, improving quality of life, and is often used alongside cancer therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lung Nodules">
      <data key="d0">Lung Nodules</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lung nodules are small masses of tissue in the lungs that may be benign or malignant, requiring expert evaluation to determine if they are cancerous.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pulmonologist">
      <data key="d0">Pulmonologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A pulmonologist is a medical doctor specializing in lung diseases and diagnosing lung-related conditions, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pulmonologist is a medical doctor specializing in lung diseases and respiratory conditions, often involved in lung cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thoracic Radiologist">
      <data key="d0">Thoracic Radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A thoracic radiologist is a medical doctor specializing in imaging of the chest, used to diagnose lung conditions and nodules.&lt;SEP&gt;A thoracic radiologist is a specialist in imaging the chest, including diagnostic evaluation of lung nodules and tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thoracic Surgeon">
      <data key="d0">Thoracic Surgeon</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A thoracic surgeon is a medical doctor specializing in operations within the chest, including procedures for lung cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A thoracic surgeon is a medical professional who performs operations on the chest, including procedures for lung cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN.org">
      <data key="d0">NCCN.org</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN.org is a medical organization providing patient guidelines and resources for lymphomas, including Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphomas and Cutaneous T-cell lymphomas.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is a medical organization that provides patient guidelines and resources for cancer treatment, including information on throat cancer and head and neck cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is a medical organization that provides patient guidelines and resources for cancer treatment, including information on throat cancer, head and neck cancers, and diagnostic procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is a resource website providing patient guidelines and information on cancer survivorship, including care for healthy living and cancer-related late effects.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is an organization providing resources and guidelines for cancer patients, including fertility preservation and patient education.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is an organization providing resources and guidelines for cancer patients, including fertility preservation, patient education, and access to patient guides and apps.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is an organization that provides guidelines and resources for breast cancer patients, including information on treatment, testing, and care coordination.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is an organization that provides guidelines, resources, and educational materials for breast cancer patients, including treatment options, testing procedures, and care coordination.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is an organization that provides patient guidelines and resources for cancer-related information, including details on gallbladder and bile duct tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is an organization that provides patient guidelines and resources for lung cancer screening and management.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is an organization that provides patient-focused information and treatment guidelines for bladder cancer, including details on staging, grading, and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is the National Comprehensive Cancer Network, providing patient guidelines and resources for breast cancer management and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is the National Comprehensive Cancer Network, which provides patient guidelines and resources for cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is the official website of the National Comprehensive Cancer Network, offering patient guidelines and educational materials on kidney cancer and related conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is the website associated with the NCCN Guidelines for Patients® and hosts patient guidelines and cancer-related resources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lung Nodule">
      <data key="d0">Lung Nodule</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A lung nodule is a small mass of tissue in the lung, often detected through imaging, which may require further evaluation for cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lung Cancer Screening">
      <data key="d0">Lung Cancer Screening</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Lung Cancer Screening is a medical procedure aimed at detecting lung cancer at an early stage, even before symptoms appear, through methods like low-dose CT scans.&lt;SEP&gt;Lung cancer screening is a medical procedure to detect lung cancer at an early stage, particularly for high-risk individuals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer-causing agents">
      <data key="d0">Cancer-causing agents</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer-causing agents are substances or factors that increase the risk of developing lung cancer, such as radon, asbestos, and tobacco smoke.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radon">
      <data key="d0">Radon</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radon is a radioactive gas that can accumulate in buildings and is a significant risk factor for lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Asbestos">
      <data key="d0">Asbestos</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Asbestos is a mineral fiber that, when inhaled, can lead to lung cancer and other respiratory diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Arsenic">
      <data key="d0">Arsenic</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Arsenic is a toxic metal that, when exposed, increases the risk of developing lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beryllium">
      <data key="d0">Beryllium</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Beryllium is a lightweight metal that, when inhaled, can contribute to the development of lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cadmium">
      <data key="d0">Cadmium</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cadmium is a heavy metal that, when inhaled or ingested, increases the risk of lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chromium">
      <data key="d0">Chromium</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chromium is a metal that, when exposed, can contribute to the development of lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nickel">
      <data key="d0">Nickel</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nickel is a metal that, when inhaled, increases the risk of developing lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Coal smoke">
      <data key="d0">Coal smoke</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Coal smoke is a byproduct of burning coal and contains harmful particles that increase lung cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soot">
      <data key="d0">Soot</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Soot is a fine particulate matter produced by combustion processes, which can contribute to lung cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Silica">
      <data key="d0">Silica</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Silica is a mineral that, when inhaled in fine particles, increases the risk of lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diesel fumes">
      <data key="d0">Diesel fumes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diesel fumes are emissions from diesel engines and contain harmful particulates that raise lung cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Air pollution">
      <data key="d0">Air pollution</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Air pollution refers to the presence of harmful substances in the air, including fine particulates, which can increase lung cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fine particulates">
      <data key="d0">Fine particulates</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fine particulates are microscopic particles in the air that can penetrate deep into the lungs, increasing cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ozone">
      <data key="d0">Ozone</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ozone is a gas found in the atmosphere that, when inhaled, can contribute to lung cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nitrogen oxides">
      <data key="d0">Nitrogen oxides</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nitrogen oxides are pollutants that, when inhaled, increase the risk of lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sulfur dioxide">
      <data key="d0">Sulfur dioxide</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sulfur dioxide is a gas emitted by industrial processes that can contribute to lung cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tobacco smoke">
      <data key="d0">Tobacco smoke</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tobacco smoke contains over 50 cancer-causing compounds and is the leading risk factor for lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Secondhand smoke">
      <data key="d0">Secondhand smoke</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Secondhand smoke is the smoke exhaled by others and the smoke from burning tobacco products, which increases lung cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC)">
      <data key="d0">Non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NSCLC is a broad category of lung cancer that accounts for the majority of cases, further divided into subtypes like adenocarcinoma, squamous cell carcinoma, and large cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small cell lung cancer (SCLC)">
      <data key="d0">Small cell lung cancer (SCLC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">SCLC is an aggressive type of lung cancer that originates in neuroendocrine cells, characterized by rapid growth and early metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adenocarcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Adenocarcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adenocarcinoma is a malignant form of colon cancer that originates in the adenoma, characterized by the invasion of surrounding tissues and potential metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a subtype of NSCLC that arises from gland-forming cells in the airways, often associated with smoking and environmental factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a type of bladder cancer that originates in glandular cells, which are not typically found in the urinary tract but can develop in rare cases.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a type of cancer originating in glandular tissue, commonly associated with rectal polyps.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a type of cervical cancer originating in glandular cells of the endocervix, accounting for about 20% of cases.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a type of rectal cancer that develops from adenomas, characterized by gland-forming cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is the most common type of colon cancer that originates from adenomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous cell carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Squamous cell carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of cancer arising from squamous cells in the anal canal, commonly associated with HPV infection and a primary focus of anal cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell carcinoma is a subtype of NSCLC that develops in the flat, scale-like cells lining the airways, commonly linked to tobacco use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Large cell carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Large cell carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Large cell carcinoma is a subtype of NS,CLC characterized by large, undifferentiated cells that can appear in any part of the lungs.&lt;SEP&gt;Large cell carcinoma is a subtype of NSCLC characterized by large, undifferentiated cells that can appear in any part of the lungs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adenosquamous carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Adenosquamous carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adenosquamous carcinoma is a rare subtype of NSCLC combining features of adenocarcinoma and squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sarcomatoid carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Sarcomatoid carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sarcomatoid carcinoma is a rare and aggressive subtype of NSCLC with spindle-shaped cells resembling sarcomas.&lt;SEP&gt;Sarcomatoid carcinoma is a rare and aggressive subtype of NSCLC with spindle-shaped cells, often associated with poor prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SCLC">
      <data key="d0">SCLC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">SCLC (Small Cell Lung Cancer) is a rapidly growing type of lung cancer strongly associated with smoking and exposure to carcinogens like radon and asbestos.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer App">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer App</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer App is an organization offering digital cancer-related information and guidance through an application.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer App is an organization offering digital resources and information for cancer survivors and patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app is a digital resource provided by NCCN to deliver patient-specific cancer information and guidelines.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app is an organization offering mobile resources for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carcinogens">
      <data key="d0">Carcinogens</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Carcinogens are substances or agents that can cause cancer, such as radon and asbestos, and are linked to the development of lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Secondhand Smoke">
      <data key="d0">Secondhand Smoke</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Secondhand smoke is the smoke exhaled by another person and the smoke from combustible tobacco products, which increases the risk of lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nicotine Addiction">
      <data key="d0">Nicotine Addiction</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nicotine addiction is a condition that makes quitting smoking difficult and is associated with an increased risk of lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Esophageal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Esophageal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Esophageal cancer can occur in the cardia of the stomach and is treated similarly to esophageal cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Esophageal cancer refers to cancer originating in the esophagus, categorized into squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma, with progression stages from early to metastatic.&lt;SEP&gt;Esophageal cancer refers to cancer that originates in the esophagus, with two main types: squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma. It is staged based on progression and metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Esophageal Squamous Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Esophageal Squamous Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma (ESCC) is a type of esophageal cancer arising from squamous cells in the upper and middle esophagus, often linked to chronic reflux.&lt;SEP&gt;Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma (ESCC) is a type of esophageal cancer that begins in the thin, flat squamous cells lining the upper and middle esophagus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Esophageal Adenocarcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Esophageal Adenocarcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Esophageal adenocarcinoma (EAC) is a type of esophageal cancer originating in mucus-producing cells of the lower esophagus, frequently associated with Barrett esophagus.&lt;SEP&gt;Esophageal adenocarcinoma (EAC) is a type of esophageal cancer that starts in the mucus-producing cells of the lower esophagus, often linked to Barrett esophagus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Barrett Esophagus">
      <data key="d0">Barrett Esophagus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Barrett esophagus (BE) is a condition where the normal squamous cells lining the lower esophagus are replaced by abnormal cells due to chronic acid reflux, increasing the risk of adenocarcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Barrett esophagus (BE) is a precancerous condition where the lower esophageal lining is replaced by abnormal cells due to chronic acid reflux, increasing adenocarcinoma risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Siewert Types">
      <data key="d0">Siewert Types</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Siewert types are a classification system for tumors near the esophagogastric junction, used to determine staging and treatment approaches for esophageal cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Siewert types are a classification system used in cancer staging to describe the location of tumors relative to the esophagogastric junction (EGJ) and stomach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stages of Esophageal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stages of Esophageal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stages of esophageal cancer categorize the disease based on tumor spread: early-stage (confined to the esophagus), locoregional (invaded wall layers or nearby lymph nodes), and metastatic (spread to distant organs like the liver or lungs).&lt;SEP&gt;Stages of esophageal cancer describe disease progression: early (confined to the esophagus), locoregional (spread to nearby tissues), and metastatic (distant spread to organs like the liver or lungs).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Esophagogastric Junction">
      <data key="d0">Esophagogastric Junction</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The esophagogastric junction (EGJ) is the anatomical boundary between the esophagus and stomach, located beneath the diaphragm, critical for separating esophageal and gastric contents.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Foundation">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Foundation</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Foundation supports the development and distribution of NCCN Guidelines for Patients, funding cancer research and patient resources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pfizer Inc.">
      <data key="d0">Pfizer Inc.</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Pfizer Inc. is a corporate supporter of the NCCN Guidelines for Patients, contributing to their availability and accessibility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hodgkin Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Hodgkin Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hodgkin Lymphoma is a type of cancer that originates in the lymphatic system, characterized by abnormal lymphocyte growth and Reed-Sternberg cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology are evidence-based recommendations for cancer treatment, updated regularly to reflect new medical data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Cancer Centers Index">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Cancer Centers Index</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Cancer Centers Index lists member cancer centers affiliated with the National Comprehensive Cancer Network, providing information on specialized care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hodgkin lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Hodgkin lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hodgkin lymphoma is a type of cancer characterized by the presence of Reed-Sternberg cells, affecting the lymphatic system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="lymphatic system">
      <data key="d0">lymphatic system</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The lymphatic system is part of the immune system, responsible for fighting infections and diseases, and includes organs like lymph nodes, spleen, and bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Classic Hodgkin lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Classic Hodgkin lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Classic Hodgkin lymphoma is a subtype of Hodgkin lymphoma characterized by the presence of Reed-Sternberg cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nodular lymphocyte-predominant Hodgkin lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Nodular lymphocyte-predominant Hodgkin lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nodular lymphocyte-predominant Hodgkin lymphoma is a rare form of Hodgkin lymphoma characterized by popcorn-shaped cells, which can transform into other cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reed-Sternberg cells">
      <data key="d0">Reed-Sternberg cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Reed-Sternberg cells are large lymphocytes associated with Classic Hodgkin lymphoma, serving as a diagnostic marker.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="popcorn-shaped cells">
      <data key="d0">popcorn-shaped cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Popcorn-shaped cells are a characteristic feature of Nodular lymphocyte-predominant Hodgkin lymphoma, distinguishing it from other types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bile Duct Cancer Stages">
      <data key="d0">Bile Duct Cancer Stages</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bile duct cancer stages refer to the progression of cancer within the bile duct, including measurements of tumor size and spread to nearby organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Guide T = Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Guide T = Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Guide T = Tumor is a document that outlines the staging criteria for bile duct cancer, including tumor size and spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphoma Basics">
      <data key="d0">Lymphoma Basics</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphoma Basics is a section explaining the fundamentals of lymphoma, including its classification and the role of the lymphatic system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follicular Lymphoma (FL)">
      <data key="d0">Follicular Lymphoma (FL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Follicular Lymphoma (FL) is a slow-growing type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL) that originates from B lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma (NHL)">
      <data key="d0">Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma (NHL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma (NHL) is a category of cancers that arise from lymphocytes, specifically B cells or T cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="International Consensus Classification (ICC)">
      <data key="d0">International Consensus Classification (ICC)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The International Consensus Classification (ICC) is an organization that groups lymphoma grades and provides treatment guidelines for follicular lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Germinal Centers">
      <data key="d0">Germinal Centers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Germinal centers are temporary structures in lymph nodes where B cells proliferate and undergo mutations, leading to the development of follicular lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Centrocytes">
      <data key="d0">Centrocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Centrocytes are smaller B cells in the light zone of germinal centers, involved in the maturation process of lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Centroblasts">
      <data key="d0">Centroblasts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Centroblasts are larger B cells in the dark zone of germinal centers that divide rapidly and contribute to the formation of follicular lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphatic System">
      <data key="d0">Lymphatic System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A network of organs and vessels that filters lymph fluid, plays a role in immune function, and facilitates cancer metastasis through lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;The Lymphatic System is a major part of the body’s immune system, a network of tissues and organs that includes bone marrow, spleen, thymus, lymph nodes, and lymphatic vessels.&lt;SEP&gt;The lymphatic system includes lymph vessels and nodes that drain fluid from breast tissue and play a role in cancer metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphocytes">
      <data key="d0">Lymphocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell critical to the immune system, including B cells (which produce antibodies) and T cells (which fight infections and tumors).&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell that plays a critical role in the immune system, including fighting infections and producing antibodies (B lymphocytes) or directly attacking pathogens (T lymphocytes).&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are white blood cells critical for immune function, including B cells (antibody producers), T cells (immune regulators), and natural killer cells (tumor/virus fighters).&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are white blood cells that help fight infection and disease, including B cells, T cells, and Natural Killer (NK) cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are white blood cells that play a critical role in the immune system by producing antibodies and fighting infections, and they are the origin of lymphomas when they grow uncontrollably.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone Marrow is a component of the lymphatic system where lymphocytes originate and mature.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is a sponge-like tissue in bones that produces blood cells, including red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, through hematopoietic stem cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is a spongy tissue in bones where hematopoietic stem cells mature into blood cells, including lymphoblasts affected in ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the sponge-like tissue in bones where hematopoietic stem cells reside, responsible for producing all types of blood cells through a process called differentiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the spongy tissue inside bones where blood cells are produced. It contains hematopoietic stem cells that differentiate into various blood cell types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spleen">
      <data key="d0">Spleen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Spleen is a component of the lymphatic system that filters blood and removes foreign material, including bacteria and cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;The spleen is an organ of the lymphatic system that filters blood, stores immune cells, and plays a role in immune responses and blood cell production.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thymus">
      <data key="d0">Thymus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The thymus is an organ located in front of the windpipe where T-cell lymphoblasts mature, and its enlargement can cause breathing complications in T-ALL cases.&lt;SEP&gt;The thymus is an organ of the lymphatic system where T cells mature and undergo selection to ensure immune tolerance and functionality.&lt;SEP&gt;Thymus is a component of the lymphatic system that plays a role in the maturation of T cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphatic Vessels">
      <data key="d0">Lymphatic Vessels</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphatic Vessels are components of the lymphatic system that transport lymph throughout the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B cells">
      <data key="d0">B cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B cells are a type of lymphocyte that produce antibodies to fight infections and are involved in the development of follicular lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;B cells are a type of lymphocyte that produce antibodies to neutralize pathogens like bacteria and viruses, and they are implicated in Cutaneous B-cell lymphoma (CBCL).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T cells">
      <data key="d0">T cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T cells are a type of lymphocyte that directly attack infected cells and play a role in immune responses.&lt;SEP&gt;T cells are a type of lymphocyte that directly kill infected or cancerous cells and regulate immune responses, and they are involved in Cutaneous T-cell lymphoma (CTCL).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Natural Killer (NK) cells">
      <data key="d0">Natural Killer (NK) cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Natural Killer (NK) cells are a type of lymphocyte that can recognize and destroy infected or cancerous cells without prior activation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="International Consensus Classification">
      <data key="d0">International Consensus Classification</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The International Consensus Classification (ICC) is a collaborative system used by medical professionals to categorize lymphomas, grouping grades 1 and 2 as classic FL (cFL).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follicular Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Follicular Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Follicular lymphoma (FL) is a type of indolent (slow-growing) non-Hodgkin lymphoma characterized by abnormal B lymphocytes forming follicles in lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL) is a broad category of cancers originating from lymphocytes, including follicular lymphoma and diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) is an aggressive subtype of non-Hodgkin lymphoma, which can be associated with transformed follicular lymphoma (FL) or FL grade 3B (FL3B).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pediatric-Type FL">
      <data key="d0">Pediatric-Type FL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pediatric-type FL (PTFL) is a variant of follicular lymphoma typically occurring in children, though it can also affect adults, with limited lymph node involvement and minimal symptoms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transformed FL">
      <data key="d0">Transformed FL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Transformed FL is a more aggressive form of follicular lymphoma that can progress into diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL), often requiring intensified treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B Symptoms">
      <data key="d0">B Symptoms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B symptoms (fevers, night sweats, weight loss) are systemic symptoms of CLL that indicate disease activity and poor prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;B symptoms refer to systemic symptoms like fever, night sweats, fatigue, and weight loss associated with lymphomas, including follicular lymphoma (FL).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mouth Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Mouth Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mouth Cancer is a type of cancer that affects the lips, tongue, gums, and other parts of the mouth, classified under Head and Neck Cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Mouth Cancer is a type of head and neck cancer that develops in the oral cavity, including the lips, cheeks, tongue, gums, and other mouth structures.&lt;SEP&gt;Mouth cancer is a type of cancer affecting the mouth area, with staging processes to determine prognosis and treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Head and Neck Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Head and Neck Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Head and Neck Cancer refers to a group of cancers originating in the head or neck area, including the mouth, throat, and surrounding structures.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancer is a type of cancer that affects the jaw, teeth, gums, and other areas in the head and neck region, impacting treatment and dental care.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancer refers to cancers affecting the head, neck, and throat areas, including the oral cavity, pharynx, larynx, and surrounding structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC)">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) is the most common type of mouth cancer, originating from the thin, flat cells of the mucous membranes in the mouth.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous Cell Carcinoma is the most common type of Mouth Cancer, arising from the squamous cells that line the mouth and throat.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nasopharyngeal Cancer (NPC)">
      <data key="d0">Nasopharyngeal Cancer (NPC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nasoph, a type of Head and Neck Cancer that originates in the nasal cavity and paranasal sinuses.&lt;SEP&gt;Nasopharyngeal Cancer is a type of Head and Neck Cancer that originates in the nasal cavity and paranasal sinuses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oropharyngeal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Oropharyngeal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Oropharyngeal Cancer is a type of Head and Neck Cancer that occurs in the upper part of the throat near the nasal cavity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma of the Vermilion Lip">
      <data key="d0">Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma of the Vermilion Lip</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma of the Vermilion Lip is a type of skin cancer affecting the outer part of the lip, not included in the scope of this book.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Head and Neck Cancers">
      <data key="d0">Head and Neck Cancers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Head and Neck Cancers is a broad category of cancers affecting the head, neck, and adjacent areas, including the mouth, throat, nasal cavity, and larynx.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and Neck Cancers refer to a group of cancers that arise in the head or neck region, encompassing various types including mouth cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and Neck Cancers refer to cancers affecting the head, neck, and surrounding structures, often linked to smoking/vaping and requiring specialized diagnostic and treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancers are a group of cancers arising in the head or neck area, including nasopharyngeal carcinoma as a subset. They are categorized by their anatomical location and treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancers are a group of cancers arising in the head or neck area. They are categorized by their location and include nasopharyngeal carcinoma as a subset.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancers are a specific type of cancer affecting the head and neck region, often linked to tobacco use and requiring specialized treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing for Mouth Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Testing for Mouth Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing for Mouth Cancer involves diagnostic procedures such as imaging studies, biopsies, and biomarker testing to determine cancer stage and treatment plans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Planning">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Planning</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The process of determining the most effective treatment options for a cancer patient based on diagnostic results and other factors.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of developing personalized treatment strategies based on diagnostic results, medical history, and genetic risk factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment Planning is a category of medical strategy that involves selecting therapies based on test results, cancer type, and patient-specific factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment Planning is the process of determining cancer management strategies based on test results, medical history, and patient-specific factors, including risks like fertility preservation and genetic testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment Planning is the process of developing a personalized cancer care strategy based on test results, medical history, and other factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment planning for CLL involves integrating diagnostic results, patient factors, and genetic profiling to select optimal therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d0">Biomarker Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A diagnostic process to identify genetic mutations or proteins (e.g., MSI-H/dMMR, PD-L1) that guide cancer treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;A diagnostic process to identify genetic mutations, protein levels, or molecular features in tumors to guide personalized treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker Testing analyzes biological markers in tissue or blood to identify cancer types, assess prognosis, or guide treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker Testing analyzes biological markers in tissues or blood to guide cancer treatment decisions and predict patient outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker Testing identifies genetic or molecular markers to guide targeted cancer therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker Testing identifies genetic or protein markers in tumors to guide targeted therapies, immunotherapies, and clinical trial eligibility.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker Testing involves analyzing genetic or protein markers in tumors to guide targeted therapies and clinical trial eligibility.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker Testing involves analyzing tumor tissue or blood samples to identify specific genetic mutations or features that can guide targeted therapy or immunotherapy for recurrent or advanced thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker Testing is a diagnostic category used to identify genetic markers or mutations that determine eligibility for targeted therapies or immunotherapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker Testing is a diagnostic procedure discussed in the text to identify cancer-related genetic mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing analyzes cancer tissue for specific genetic or molecular markers to determine eligibility for targeted therapies or immunotherapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing identifies genetic mutations or proteins in tumors to guide personalized cancer treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing identifies specific proteins or genetic mutations in breast cancer cells to guide targeted therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing involves analyzing tumor tissue or blood samples to identify genetic mutations or other features that guide treatment decisions, including clinical trials and maintenance therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Complete Head and Neck Exam">
      <data key="d0">Complete Head and Neck Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Complete Head and Neck Exam is a detailed examination of the mouth, throat, and surrounding areas to identify cancerous lesions or abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;Complete Head and Neck Exam systematically evaluates the oral cavity and surrounding structures for cancer abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chest CT">
      <data key="d0">Chest CT</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chest CT is a diagnostic imaging test used to evaluate cancer spread to the lungs or chest area.&lt;SEP&gt;Chest CT is an imaging test to assess cancer spread to the lungs or chest area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CT with Contrast">
      <data key="d0">CT with Contrast</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CT with Contrast is an imaging technique that uses contrast dye to enhance visualization of tumors and other abnormalities in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;CT with Contrast uses contrast dye to enhance visualization of tumors in the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FDG-PET/CT">
      <data key="d0">FDG-PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A hybrid imaging technique combining fluorodeoxyglucose (FD.M) PET and CT scans to identify cancer in lymph nodes and distant sites, requiring fasting before the procedure.&lt;SEP&gt;A hybrid imaging technique combining fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG) PET and CT scans to identify cancer in lymph nodes and distant sites, requiring fasting before the procedure.&lt;SEP&gt;FDG-PET/CT combines PET and CT scans to provide comprehensive metabolic and anatomical information for cancer evaluation.&lt;SEP&gt;FDG-PET/CT combines PET and CT scans to provide detailed images of cancerous tissues and metabolic activity.&lt;SEP&gt;FDG-PET/CT combines PET and CT to detect cancer metabolism and spread.&lt;SEP&gt;FDG-PET/CT combines positron emission tomography (PET) and computed tomography (CT) to detect cancer metabolism and spread.&lt;SEP&gt;FDG-PET/CT combines positron emission tomography (PET) and computed tomography (CT) to provide detailed images of metabolic and anatomical structures, aiding in cancer detection and staging.&lt;SEP&gt;FDG-PET/CT combines positron emission tomography and computed tomography to visualize cancer metabolism, using a radiotracer called fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG).&lt;SEP&gt;FDG-PET/CT is a combined imaging test that uses radioactive tracers to identify areas of increased metabolic activity, aiding in the detection of extramedullary leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;FDG-PET/CT is a combined imaging test using a radiotracer to detect cancer cells and assess their metabolic activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging test to detect extramedullary leukemia, indicating AML spread beyond bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Endoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Endoscopy is a diagnostic and therapeutic procedure using a flexible scope to examine internal organs, such as the throat and digestive tract, to assess tumor size and collect tissue samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Endoscopy is a procedure using a flexible scope to examine and biopsy internal organs, including the throat and digestive tract.&lt;SEP&gt;Endoscopy is a procedure using a flexible scope to examine and biopsy internal structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dental Exam">
      <data key="d0">Dental Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Dental Exam includes imaging to assess oral health and detect early signs of mouth cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Dental Exam includes x-rays or CT scans to assess oral health and detect early signs of mouth cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Dental Exam is a procedure to assess oral health, particularly relevant for patients with head and neck cancers, as treatment can impact jaw, teeth, and gums.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nutrition, Speech, and Swallowing Evaluation">
      <data key="d0">Nutrition, Speech, and Swallowing Evaluation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nutrition, Speech, and Swallowing Evaluation assesses functional impacts of cancer on daily activities.&lt;SEP&gt;Nutrition, Speech, and Swallowing Evaluation assesses how cancer affects a patient's ability to eat, speak, and swallow, guiding treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Nutrition, speech, and swallowing evaluation assesses functional impairments caused by head and neck cancers, guiding dietary and rehabilitative care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hearing Test (Audiogram)">
      <data key="d0">Hearing Test (Audiogram)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hearing Test (Audiogram) evaluates auditory function affected by head and neck cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Hearing Test (Audiogram) evaluates auditory function, which may be affected by head and neck cancers and their treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Hearing Test (Audiogram) evaluates auditory function, which may be impacted by head and neck cancers or their treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Help to Quit Smoking">
      <data key="d0">Help to Quit Smoking</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Help to Quit Smoking is a supportive intervention to reduce tobacco use, a known risk factor for head and neck cancers, including nasopharyngeal carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Help to Quit Smoking is a supportive intervention to reduce tobacco use, a major risk factor for head and neck cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Help to Quit Smoking is an intervention to reduce tobacco use, a major risk factor for head and neck cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility Counseling">
      <data key="d0">Fertility Counseling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility Counseling addresses reproductive health concerns during cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility Counseling addresses reproductive health concerns for patients undergoing cancer treatment, including options for preserving fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility Counseling provides guidance for patients concerned about preserving fertility during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Screening for Hepatitis B">
      <data key="d0">Screening for Hepatitis B</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Screening for Hepatitis B identifies infections that may influence cancer treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Screening for Hepatitis B is a diagnostic test to identify hepatitis B infection, which may influence cancer treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Testing">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A diagnostic process to identify abnormal genetic changes (mutations) that may increase the risk of prostate cancer, including germline and molecular biomarker testing.&lt;SEP&gt;A process to identify harmful changes in genes, used to assess cancer risk and guide treatment, including hereditary gene testing and tumor testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic Testing analyzes DNA to identify inherited genetic mutations that may increase cancer risk or affect treatment response.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic Testing analyzes DNA to identify inherited mutations affecting cancer risk or treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic Testing identifies inherited cancer risks and mutations, aiding in personalized treatment planning for patients with a family history of head and neck cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic Testing is a category of diagnostic process used to identify hereditary cancer risks, particularly in cases of familial or genetic predisposition.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic Testing is a diagnostic process analyzing DNA for mutations in genes like BRCA1, BRCA2, and Lynch Syndrome-related genes to assess hereditary cancer risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic Testing is a diagnostic process that analyzes DNA to identify mutations in genes like BRCA1 and BRCA2, which are associated with hereditary cancer risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic Testing is used to identify inherited genetic mutations that may increase the risk of developing bladder cancer, such as mutations in the TP53 gene.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing involves analyzing DNA to identify inherited mutations that increase cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing involves analyzing leukemia cells for mutations, chromosomal abnormalities, and genetic changes to determine AML subtypes, prognosis, and targeted treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing refers to the analysis of DNA to detect inherited mutations, such as BRCA mutations, which are critical for understanding hereditary cancer risks and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Tests to detect gene mutations or abnormal chromosomes that contribute to AML diagnosis and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tobacco and Alcohol Use History">
      <data key="d0">Tobacco and Alcohol Use History</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tobacco and Alcohol Use History is a critical factor in assessing cancer risk and determining treatment effectiveness for head and neck cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Tobacco and Alcohol Use History is a critical factor in assessing cancer risk and treatment effectiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Tobacco and alcohol use history is a critical risk factor for head and neck cancers, including nasopharyngeal carcinoma, and influences treatment outcomes and survival rates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Stage">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Stage classifies the extent of cancer spread, guiding treatment and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer Stage is a classification system that describes the extent of cancer spread, guiding treatment decisions and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Care Team">
      <data key="d0">Care Team</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A multidisciplinary team of healthcare professionals involved in the treatment and management of AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Care Team is a multidisciplinary group of healthcare professionals responsible for coordinating and personalizing treatment plans for prostate cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Care Team is a multidisciplinary group of healthcare providers involved in diagnosing, treating, and managing prostate cancer, including doctors, genetic counselors, and other specialists.&lt;SEP&gt;Care Team is a multidisciplinary group of healthcare providers managing cancer care.&lt;SEP&gt;Care Team refers to the multidisciplinary group of healthcare providers involved in a patient's cancer care, including oncologists, surgeons, and support staff.&lt;SEP&gt;The Care Team is a multidisciplinary group of healthcare professionals involved in diagnosing, staging, and treating breast cancer, including oncologists, surgeons, and support staff.&lt;SEP&gt;The care team consists of healthcare professionals who manage the diagnosis, treatment, and support for glioma patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The care team is an organization of healthcare professionals involved in diagnosing and treating brain tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Patient Portal">
      <data key="d0">Patient Portal</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Patient Portal is an online platform allowing patients to access medical records, test results, and communicate with their care team.&lt;SEP&gt;Patient Portal is an online platform for accessing medical records and communicating with care teams.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MyChart">
      <data key="d0">MyChart</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MyChart is a digital health record system enabling patients to manage appointments, track test results, and communicate with healthcare providers.&lt;SEP&gt;MyChart is a digital health record system for managing appointments and test results.&lt;SEP&gt;MyChart is a patient portal system enabling access to medical records, test results, and communication with healthcare providers, facilitating patient engagement in their care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Support Groups">
      <data key="d0">Support Groups</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Support Groups are community resources where cancer patients and survivors share experiences, provide emotional support, and discuss challenges related to their condition and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Support Groups provide emotional and social support for cancer patients and their families through in-person meetings or online communities.&lt;SEP&gt;Support Groups provide emotional and social support for cancer patients through community networks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Social Support">
      <data key="d0">Social Support</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Social Support encompasses the network of friends, family, and community resources aiding cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Social Support encompasses the network of friends, family, and community resources that help patients cope with cancer diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG)">
      <data key="d0">Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) is an organization that develops a scale to assess a patient's general health and fitness level, known as the ECOG score.&lt;SEP&gt;The Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) is an organization that develops and uses a performance status scale to assess cancer patients' general health and activity levels for treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;The Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) is an organization that develops the ECOG score, a scale used to assess a patient's general health and performance status.&lt;SEP&gt;The Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) is an organization that develops the ECOG score, a standardized scale to assess a patient's general health and ability to perform daily tasks.&lt;SEP&gt;The Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) is an organization that rates performance status for cancer patients to inform treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="head and neck cancer">
      <data key="d0">head and neck cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">head and neck cancer is a category of cancer affecting the head and neck region, requiring specialized exams, treatments, and support.&lt;SEP&gt;head and neck cancer is a category of cancer affecting the head and neck region, requiring specialized exams, treatments, and supportive care to address its physical, emotional, and functional impacts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="distress screening">
      <data key="d0">distress screening</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">distress screening is a process to assess the emotional and psychological state of cancer patients, addressing mental, physical, and social well-being.&lt;SEP&gt;distress screening is a process to assess the emotional and psychological state of cancer patients, addressing mental, physical, social, and spiritual well-being through structured evaluations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="endocrine tests">
      <data key="d0">endocrine tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">endocrine tests are medical procedures to evaluate hormone levels in the head and neck region, particularly for thyroid and pituitary glands affected by radiation therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;endocrine tests are medical procedures to evaluate hormone levels in the head and neck region, particularly for thyroid and pituitary glands.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="hearing test">
      <data key="d0">hearing test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">hearing test is a medical assessment to determine hearing loss caused by head and neck tumors or their treatments, evaluating the severity and type of auditory impairment.&lt;SEP&gt;hearing test is a medical assessment to determine hearing loss caused by head and neck tumors or their treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="nutrition assessment">
      <data key="d0">nutrition assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">nutrition assessment is a process to evaluate a patient's nutritional needs before cancer treatment, ensuring adequate sustenance to support overall health and treatment outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;nutrition assessment is a process to evaluate a patient's nutritional needs before cancer treatment, ensuring adequate sustenance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="speech and swallowing assessment">
      <data key="d0">speech and swallowing assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">speech and swallowing assessment is a medical evaluation to test a patient's ability to swallow and speak after treatment, addressing functional impairments caused by head and neck cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;speech and swallowing assessment is a medical evaluation to test a patient's ability to swallow and speak after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="performance status">
      <data key="d0">performance status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">performance status is a measure of a patient's general health and ability to perform daily tasks, rated using the Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) scale to inform treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;performance status is a measure of a patient's general health and ability to perform daily tasks, rated using the Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) scale.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="fertility (all genders)">
      <data key="d0">fertility (all genders)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">fertility (all genders) refers to the ability to have children, which may be affected by cancer treatments, requiring preservation strategies to maintain reproductive options.&lt;SEP&gt;fertility (all genders) refers to the ability to have children, which may be affected by cancer treatments, requiring preservation strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Changes in fertility">
      <data key="d0">Changes in fertility</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Changes in fertility are the potential temporary or permanent effects of cancer treatment on reproductive health, influenced by age and treatment type.&lt;SEP&gt;Changes in fertility are the potential temporary or permanent effects of cancer treatment on reproductive health, influenced by age, treatment type, and dosage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Preventing pregnancy during treatment">
      <data key="d0">Preventing pregnancy during treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Preventing pregnancy during treatment is a recommended practice to avoid risks to the developing baby, using contraception methods like I,UDs and barrier methods to ensure treatment safety.&lt;SEP&gt;Preventing pregnancy during treatment is a recommended practice to avoid risks to the developing baby, using contraception methods like IUDs and barrier methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="medical binder">
      <data key="d0">medical binder</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical binder is a tool used to organize medical records, including blood tests, imaging results, and cancer-related reports.&lt;SEP&gt;medical binder is a tool to organize patient records for better management and second opinions, containing blood tests, imaging results, and cancer-specific reports to support comprehensive care.&lt;SEP&gt;medical binder is a tool to organize patient records for better management and second opinions, containing blood tests, imaging results, and cancer-specific reports.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="testicular cancer">
      <data key="d0">testicular cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Testicular cancer is a type of cancer that affects the testicles and is discussed in the context of treatment and imaging diagnostics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="radiologist">
      <data key="d0">radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A radiologist is a medical professional who interprets imaging tests and provides reports to healthcare providers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="health care provider (HCP)">
      <data key="d0">health care provider (HCP)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A health care provider is an organization or individual responsible for managing patient care and interpreting medical results.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="imaging tests">
      <data key="d0">imaging tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging tests are diagnostic procedures that use technologies like MRI or CT scans to visualize internal organs and detect abnormalities such as tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging tests are diagnostic procedures used to visualize internal body structures and detect cancerous tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaring tests are diagnostic procedures that use technologies like MRI or CT scans to visualize internal organs and detect abnormalities such as tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="contrast material">
      <data key="d0">contrast material</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Contrast material is a substance used in imaging tests to enhance the visibility of internal organs and structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CT scan">
      <data key="d0">CT scan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A CT scan uses x-rays and computer technology to create detailed images of the body, often with the use of contrast material.&lt;SEP&gt;CT scans are a category of imaging tests that combine X-rays and computer technology to produce detailed cross-sectional images of the body, often enhanced with contrast material.&lt;SEP&gt;CT scans are a category of imaging tests that use X-rays and computer technology to produce detailed cross-sectional images of the body, often with contrast material.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI scan">
      <data key="d0">MRI scan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An MRI scan uses magnetic fields and radio waves to produce images of the body, avoiding ionizing radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI scans are a category of imaging tests that use radio waves and strong magnetic fields to generate detailed images of soft tissues, without exposing patients to ionizing radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI scans are a category of imaging tests that use radio waves and strong magnets to create detailed images of soft tissues, without radiation exposure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET scan">
      <data key="d0">PET scan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A PET scan is a medical imaging technique that uses a radioactive tracer to visualize metabolic activity in the body, particularly useful for detecting cancer cells and assessing their sugar metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan uses a radioactive tracer to detect cancer cells and assess their metabolic activity, providing insights into cancer growth and spread.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan uses a radioactive tracer to detect cancer cells and metabolic activity within the body.&lt;SEP&gt;PET scans are a category of imaging tests that use a radioactive tracer to detect cancer cells by tracking metabolic activity in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;PET scans are a category of imaging tests that use a radioactive tracer to detect cancer cells by tracking metabolic activity, providing insights into disease progression and treatment response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ultrasound uses sound waves to create images of internal body structures, commonly used for non-invasive diagnostics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="panoramic dental x-ray">
      <data key="d0">panoramic dental x-ray</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A panoramic dental x-ray captures images of the entire mouth, used for dental planning and diagnostics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging Results">
      <data key="d0">Imaging Results</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging results refer to the findings from diagnostic imaging tests, crucial for assessing cancer progression and treatment effectiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reports">
      <data key="d0">Reports</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Reports are detailed documents generated from medical tests and procedures, providing critical information for cancer diagnosis and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contrast Material">
      <data key="d0">Contrast Material</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Contrast Material is a substance used in imaging tests to enhance the visibility of internal body structures and improve diagnostic accuracy.&lt;SEP&gt;Contrast material enhances imaging clarity by improving visibility of internal body structures, administered orally or intravenously depending on the imaging modality (CT or MRI).&lt;SEP&gt;Contrast material is a substance used in imaging tests to enhance the visibility of internal structures, aiding in cancer detection and visualization.&lt;SEP&gt;Contrast material is a substance used in imaging tests to enhance the visibility of internal structures, commonly used in CT, MRI, and PET scans.&lt;SEP&gt;Contrast material is a substance used to enhance imaging of internal body structures, typically excreted via urine after a test.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">Ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A non-invasive imaging method used to visualize internal structures, such as the prostate, and guide needle insertion during biopsy procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;An imaging technique using high-energy sound waves to visualize internal structures, commonly used for diagnosing small cancers and guiding biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a non-invasive imaging technique used to detect thyroid nodules and assess their characteristics, aiding in thyroid cancer diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a non-invasive imaging technique used to guide prostate biopsies, providing real-time visualization of the prostate gland for accurate tissue sampling.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a non-invasive imaging technique used to visualize the liver and detect abnormalities such as tumors or nodules.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a non-invasive imaging technique used to visualize thyroid nodules, their size, shape, and location, and to detect suspicious lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a non-invasive imaging technique using sound waves to visualize internal organs, including the ovaries, for detecting masses or tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a non-invasive imaging technique using sound waves to visualize internal structures, commonly used in breast cancer diagnosis and biopsy guidance.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is an imaging test frequently used as the first step in diagnosing ovarian cancer, providing visual data of internal organs.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound uses high-frequency sound waves to create images of internal body structures, often employed to detect small cancers in lymph nodes and guide biopsies without radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound uses high-frequency sound waves to generate real-time images of internal organs, commonly used for non-invasive diagnostics.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound uses sound waves to visualize internal organs, detecting ovarian tumors and their characteristics like size and fluid content.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Panoramic Dental X-ray">
      <data key="d0">Panoramic Dental X-ray</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A panoramic dental x-ray captures a broad view of the entire mouth, used for dental diagnostics and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiographic imaging method capturing the entire mouth, used for dental planning and diagnosing oral structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Smoking">
      <data key="d0">Smoking</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Smoking is a major risk factor for head and neck cancers, impacting cancer development, treatment efficacy, and post-operative recovery.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking is a risk factor for anal cancer, contributing to DNA damage and immune suppression, which may exacerbate the effects of HPV infection.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking is a risk factor for cervical cancer and other health issues, with nicotine dependence complicating cancer treatment and recovery.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking is a significant risk factor for bladder cancer and can also affect the effectiveness of cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking is a significant risk factor for bladder cancer, as carcinogens in tobacco smoke can damage bladder cells and increase cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking is a significant risk factor for various cancers, including testicular cancer, and can impair treatment outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking is the leading risk factor for bladder cancer, as carcinogens from tobacco smoke can damage the urinary tract lining.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vaping">
      <data key="d0">Vaping</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vaping, like smoking, increases cancer risk and can interfere with cancer treatment outcomes and surgical healing.&lt;SEP&gt;Vaping, like smoking, increases cancer risk and may reduce the effectiveness of cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Care Provider (HCP)">
      <data key="d0">Health Care Provider (HCP)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A health care provider is an organization or individual responsible for managing patient care, interpreting medical results, and coordinating treatment plans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiologist">
      <data key="d0">Radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Radiologist is a medical expert who interprets imaging tests and provides diagnostic reports for patient care.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a healthcare professional (person) who interprets medical imaging scans, such as CT scans, to diagnose and assess conditions like CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical doctor who interprets medical imaging, such as X-rays, CT scans, and MRIs, to diagnose and monitor thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical professional specializing in interpreting imaging scans (e.g., MRI, CT) to detect abnormalities and assist in diagnosing conditions like CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical professional specializing in interpreting imaging scans (e.g., MRI, CT) to detect abnormalities and support CNS lymphoma diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical professional specializing in interpreting imaging tests and providing diagnostic reports for cancer assessment.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical professional who interprets imaging results to diagnose conditions like brain tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical professional who specializes in interpreting imaging tests such as MRI, CT, and PET scans to diagnose and monitor conditions like prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiologist is a medical doctor specializing in interpreting imaging tests to diagnose conditions like cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiologist is a medical professional specializing in imaging diagnostics, crucial for assessing thyroid cancer and guiding treatment decisions like radioactive iodine therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiologist is a medical professional specializing in interpreting imaging tests to diagnose prostate cancer and related conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiologist is a medical professional specializing in interpreting medical imaging tests, such as MRI, CT, and PET scans, to diagnose and assess cancer progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CDC">
      <data key="d0">CDC</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The CDC (Centers for Disease Control and Prevention) is a U.S. federal agency that provides public health information and resources, including tobacco-related health risks and cessation programs.&lt;SEP&gt;The CDC is a public health organization that provides information and resources related to cancer risk factors and smoking cessation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Mutational Burden">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Mutational Burden</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor Mutational Burden (TMB) measures the number of genetic mutations in cancer cells, influencing treatment decisions for immune checkpoint inhibitors.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutational burden measures the number of genetic mutations in tumor DNA, influencing treatment decisions for cancers that may respond to immune checkpoint inhibitors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MSI-H/dMMR Mutation">
      <data key="d0">MSI-H/dMMR Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A genetic mutation affecting microsatellite instability, linked to defective mismatch repair proteins and cancer progression.&lt;SEP&gt;MSI-H/dMMR (Microsatellite Instability-High/Deficient Mismatch Repair) is a genetic abnormality that affects DNA repair, often linked to specific cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PD-L1 Testing">
      <data key="d0">PD-L1 Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A biomarker test measuring PD-L1 protein expression on cancer cells to determine eligibility for immune checkpoint inhibitor therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;PD-L,1 Testing measures the expression of a protein that may suppress immune responses, guiding the use of immunotherapy in cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;PD-L1 Testing measures the expression of a protein that may suppress immune responses, guiding the use of immunotherapy in cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Mutation Testing">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Mutation Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor Mutation Testing analyzes genetic mutations in cancer cells to identify potential targets for targeted therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutation testing identifies specific DNA mutations in cancer cells, enabling targeted therapies and informing treatment decisions based on genetic profiles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anesthesia">
      <data key="d0">Anesthesia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anesthesia is used during surgical procedures, with cannabis use potentially affecting its required dosage and effectiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENT Doctor">
      <data key="d0">ENT Doctor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ENT (Ear, Nose, and Throat) Doctors specialize in diagnosing and treating head and neck cancers, including monitoring oral and throat health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cannabis Use">
      <data key="d0">Cannabis Use</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cannabis use can influence the dosage and effectiveness of anesthesia during surgical procedures for cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging Studies">
      <data key="d0">Imaging Studies</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging Studies are diagnostic tools used to visualize internal structures and detect cancerous abnormalities, often combined with biopsies for treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical imaging techniques used to evaluate the extent of AML and monitor treatment response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment encompasses therapies and interventions aimed at managing and curing cancer, including surgery, chemotherapy, immunotherapy, and targeted therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer Treatment encompasses various therapeutic approaches, including surgery, chemotherapy, radiation therapy, and immunotherapy, aimed at eliminating or controlling bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment involves multidisciplinary approaches, including surgery, chemotherapy, radiation therapy, and supportive care, tailored to the patient's cancer stage and overall health.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment involves procedures like imaging tests, biopsies, and targeted therapies, influenced by factors like smoking, alcohol use, and patient preferences.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment involves various procedures such as imaging tests, biopsies, and targeted therapies, and is influenced by factors like smoking, alcohol use, and patient preferences.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC)">
      <data key="d0">American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) created the TNM staging system used to determine cancer stages and guide treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) is an organization that developed the TNM staging system for classifying anal cancer severity.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) is an organization that developed the TNM staging system, which is used to classify the severity of anal cancer and inform treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) is an organization that develops a staging system for cancer, which helps determine the extent of cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mouth Cancer Staging">
      <data key="d0">Mouth Cancer Staging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mouth cancer staging refers to the process of determining the extent of cancer using the TNM system, which helps in predicting prognosis and guiding treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TNM Staging System">
      <data key="d0">TNM Staging System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The TNM staging system is a method used to classify cancer based on tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis, guiding treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML)">
      <data key="d0">Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AML is a rapidly progressing cancer of the myeloid cells, diagnosed through bone marrow tests and requiring specialized pathologic evaluation.&lt;SEP&gt;AML is a type of blood cancer originating in the bone marrow, characterized by the uncontrolled growth of immature white blood cells called myeloid blasts.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a rapidly progressing cancer of the myeloid cells, requiring specialized diagnostic and therapeutic approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer affecting immature white blood cells, characterized by the accumulation of blasts in bone marrow and blood, requiring systemic therapy and genetic testing for treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer affecting the blood and bone marrow, characterized by the overproduction of immature white blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) is a type of blood cancer that originates in the stem cells of bone marrow, with numerous subtypes found in adults.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL)">
      <data key="d0">Acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">APL is a subtype of AML marked by specific genetic mutations, often treatable with targeted therapies like all-trans retinoic acid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blastic plasmacytoid dendritic cell neoplasm (BPDCN)">
      <data key="d0">Blastic plasmacytoid dendritic cell neoplasm (BPDCN)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BPDC, a rare and aggressive subtype of AML involving abnormal plasmacytoid dendritic cells, typically found in the skin or bone marrow.&lt;SEP&gt;BPDCN is a rare and aggressive subtype of AML involving abnormal plasmacytoid dendritic cells, typically found in the skin or bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous cell carcinoma (SCC)">
      <data key="d0">Squamous cell carcinoma (SCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">SCC is a type of cancer arising from squamous cells, commonly found in the mouth and skin, with staging based on tumor size and depth of invasion.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) is the most common type of nasopharyngeal carcinoma, originating from squamous cells lining the nasopharynx.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TNM staging system">
      <data key="d0">TNM staging system</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The TNM system is a standardized method for classifying cancer stages based on tumor size (T), lymph node involvement (N), and metastasis (M) to guide treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Resectable">
      <data key="d0">Resectable</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Resectable describes tumors that can be completely removed surgically, indicating a favorable prognosis and treatment option.&lt;SEP&gt;Resectable tumors can be completely removed surgically, while unresectable tumors involve critical structures making complete removal unsafe.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Unresectable">
      <data key="d0">Unresectable</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Unresectable refers to tumors that cannot be entirely removed due to their location or spread, often requiring alternative treatments like chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Unresectable tumors cannot be fully removed due to involvement of nearby veins, arteries, or structures, requiring alternative treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic cancer has spread beyond its original site to other parts of the body, such as the lungs or lymph nodes, indicating advanced disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer has spread to distant parts of the body, including the lungs and lymph nodes, and is classified as very advanced disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carcinoma in situ (Tis)">
      <data key="d0">Carcinoma in situ (Tis)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Carcinoma in situ is an early stage of cancer where abnormal cells are confined to the original layer (epithelium) without invading deeper tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T1">
      <data key="d0">T1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T1 represents a small tumor (≤2 cm) with minimal depth of invasion, indicating a less aggressive form of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T2">
      <data key="d0">T2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T2 denotes a tumor between 2-4 cm with moderate depth of invasion, reflecting intermediate cancer progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T3">
      <data key="d0">T3</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T3 describes a larger tumor (&gt;4 cm) with significant depth of invasion, associated with higher risk of metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T4a">
      <data key="d0">T4a</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T4a is a moderately advanced local disease where the tumor invades nearby structures like bone or soft tissues but not distant organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T4b">
      <data key="d0">T4b</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T4b signifies very advanced local disease with tumor invasion into critical structures like the skull base or carotid artery, indicating severe progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regional lymph node metastasis">
      <data key="d0">Regional lymph node metastasis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Regional lymph node metastasis refers to cancer spread to lymph nodes near the primary tumor site, distinguishing it from distant metastases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Extranodal extension">
      <data key="d0">Extranodal extension</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Extranodal extension indicates cancer cells have spread beyond the lymph node into surrounding tissues, suggesting more aggressive disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood cancer">
      <data key="d0">Blood cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood cancer encompasses malignancies affecting blood-forming tissues, including leukemias, lymphomas, and myelomas, with AML being a common subtype.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone marrow">
      <data key="d0">Bone marrow</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone marrow is a primary site for lymphocyte production and a key component of the lymphatic system, housing stem cells that differentiate into immune cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the soft tissue in bones where blood cells are produced, serving as the origin for AML and other blood cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Plasma">
      <data key="d0">Plasma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Plasma is the liquid component of blood, primarily composed of water, and serves as a medium for transporting nutrients, hormones, and waste products throughout the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Plasma is the liquid component of blood, primarily water, that carries nutrients, hormones, and waste products, and serves as a medium for blood cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Red Blood Cells">
      <data key="d0">Red Blood Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Red Blood Cells (RBCs or erythrocytes) are responsible for carrying oxygen from the lungs to tissues and removing carbon dioxide from the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="White Blood Cells">
      <data key="d0">White Blood Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">White Blood Cells (WBCs or leukocytes) are critical for the immune system, fighting infections and defending the body against pathogens.&lt;SEP&gt;White blood cells (leukocytes) are a key component of the immune system, responsible for defending the body against infections, viruses, and cancerous cells, including subtypes like granulocytes, monocytes, and lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;White blood cells (leukocytes) are key components of the immune system, defending against infections, viruses, and cancer, with subtypes like granulocytes, monocytes, and lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Platelets">
      <data key="d0">Platelets</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Platelets (PLTs or thrombocytes) are essential for blood clotting and preventing excessive bleeding by forming clots at injury sites.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AML">
      <data key="d0">AML</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AML (Acute Myeloid Leukemia) is a type of blood cancer characterized by mutations in genes and chromosomes, requiring specialized testing for accurate diagnosis and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;AML (Acute Myeloid Leukemia) is a type of blood cancer characterized by the rapid growth of abnormal white blood cells, requiring a multidisciplinary treatment approach.&lt;SEP&gt;AML (Acute Myeloid Leukemia) is a type of cancer originating in the bone marrow, characterized by the rapid growth of abnormal myeloid cells that interfere with normal blood cell production.&lt;SEP&gt;AML (Acute Myeloid Leukemia) is a type of cancer that is primarily treated with chemotherapy and targeted therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;AML is a type of acute myeloid leukemia, a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the rapid growth of abnormal white blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia, a type of cancer affecting the bone marrow and blood, characterized by the presence of myeloid blasts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Stem Cells">
      <data key="d0">Blood Stem Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood stem cells (hematopoietic stem cells) are the precursor cells in bone marrow that differentiate into all types of blood cells, including red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood stem cells are immature cells in bone marrow that can differentiate into all types of blood cells, including red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, and are essential for blood cell regeneration.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood stem cells are immature cells in bone marrow that differentiate into all blood cell types, including red blood cells, white blood, and platelets, and are essential for blood regeneration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AML Specialist">
      <data key="d0">AML Specialist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">AML Specialist is a healthcare professional with expertise in diagnosing, treating, and managing Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML), including genetic and biomarker testing for personalized care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AML Medical History and Physical Exam">
      <data key="d0">AML Medical History and Physical Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AML Medical History and Physical Exam refers to the initial assessment process for patients, including gathering medical history and conducting a physical exam to evaluate health status.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Complete Blood Count (CBC)">
      <data key="d0">Complete Blood Count (CBC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A CBC measures blood cell counts, including white blood cells, red blood cells, and platelets, essential for diagnosing and monitoring CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;A complete blood count (CBC) measures various components of the blood, including red and white blood cells, hemoglobin, and platelets, used to assess overall health and detect abnormalities related to ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A key blood test measuring red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets to assess AML impact.&lt;SEP&gt;CBC is a blood test measuring red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, essential for assessing cancer-related blood abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;Complete Blood Count (CBC) is a blood test used to evaluate the impact of lymphoma on blood cells and monitor treatment effectiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Complete Blood Count (CBC) is a standard blood test that measures various components of blood, including red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, providing insights into overall health and potential abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP)">
      <data key="d0">Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood test panel evaluating organ function and metabolic status, relevant for AML management.&lt;SEP&gt;CMP is a group of blood tests evaluating blood sugar, electrolytes, and organ function, vital for monitoring cancer-related metabolic changes.&lt;SEP&gt;Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP) is a blood test that evaluates multiple substances in the blood, offering information about kidney and liver function, electrolyte balance, and other metabolic processes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Clotting Tests">
      <data key="d0">Blood Clotting Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood Clotting Tests assess the body's ability to form clots, which is crucial for diagnosing and managing conditions related to bleeding or coagulation disorders.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Aspirate and Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Aspirate and Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone Marrow Aspirate and Biopsy are procedures to examine the bone marrow for abnormalities, often used in diagnosing conditions like leukemia and assessing treatment responses.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow aspirate and biopsy are procedures to collect samples for genetic and biomarker analysis, essential for diagnosing AML and assessing its genetic profile.&lt;SEP&gt;Procedure to extract and analyze bone marrow cells for AML diagnosis and monitoring.&lt;SEP&gt;Procedures to collect samples from the bone marrow for diagnostic testing in AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human Leukocyte Antigen (HLA) Typing">
      <data key="d0">Human Leukocyte Antigen (HLA) Typing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Human Leukocyte Antigen (HLA) Typing is a test to determine genetic markers on white blood cells, essential for matching organ transplants and understanding immune responses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brain CT without Contrast">
      <data key="d0">Brain CT without Contrast</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Brain CT without Contrast is a diagnostic imaging test to visualize the brain structure, often used when there is suspicion of central nervous system bleeding.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging test to detect central nervous system involvement in AML without using contrast agents.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brain MRI with Contrast">
      <data key="d0">Brain MRI with Contrast</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Brain MRI with Contrast is an advanced imaging technique to detect inflammation or infection in the brain, particularly useful in diagnosing leukemic meningitis.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging test to detect central nervous system involvement in AML using contrast agents for better visualization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lumbar Puncture (LP)">
      <data key="d0">Lumbar Puncture (LP)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lumbar Puncture (LP) is a procedure to collect cerebrospinal fluid for analysis, often performed to check for leukemia cells in the central nervous system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Heart Tests">
      <data key="d0">Heart Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Heart Tests are medical procedures used to assess cardiac function, including electrocardiograms (ECG/EKG) and echocardiograms, which evaluate heart rhythm, structure, and blood flow.&lt;SEP&gt;Heart Tests evaluate cardiovascular function, including assessing heart rhythm, blood pressure, and potential abnormalities related to heart health.&lt;SEP&gt;Heart tests assess cardiac function and monitor treatment side effects, including electrocardiograms and echocardiograms.&lt;SEP&gt;Tests to evaluate cardiac health, which may be affected by AML and its treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="General Health Tests">
      <data key="d0">General Health Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">General Health Tests encompass a broad range of assessments to evaluate overall health, including physical exams and evaluations of medical history.&lt;SEP&gt;General health tests are part of the diagnostic process to assess overall health and identify potential risks or conditions related to breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Tests to evaluate overall health status, including medical history and physical exams, relevant for AML care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liver Function Tests (LFTs)">
      <data key="d0">Liver Function Tests (LFTs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Liver Function Tests (LFTs) evaluate liver health by measuring specific enzymes and proteins in the blood, essential for monitoring AML treatment impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Liver Function Tests (LFTs) measure enzymes and proteins in the blood to evaluate liver health and detect potential liver damage or disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vitamin B12 and Folic Acid">
      <data key="d0">Vitamin B12 and Folic Acid</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vitamin B12 and Folic Acid are essential nutrients monitored in blood tests to assess their role in red blood cell production and metabolic health in AML patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Vitamin B12 and Folic Acid are essential nutrients monitored in blood tests to assess their role in red blood cell production and overall metabolic health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN Guidelines are standardized clinical protocols for cancer diagnosis, staging, and treatment, including anal cancer management.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN Guidelines provide recommendations for treatment regimens, including immunotherapy side effects and preferred first-line therapies for metastatic cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Guidelines are a set of evidence-based recommendations developed by the National Comprehensive Cancer Network to guide cancer care and patient support.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Guidelines are a set of medical guidelines and patient resources for cancer care, including information on fertility preservation and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Guidelines for Patients provide detailed information on cancer treatment options, including radiation therapy, systemic therapy, and fertility preservation strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Guidelines for Patients provide detailed information on immunotherapy side effects and treatment protocols for metastatic anal cancer, including recommendations for immune checkpoint inhibitors like Nivolumab and Pembrolizumab.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Guidelines for Patients provide information on immunotherapy side effects and immune checkpoint inhibitors for metastatic anal cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Coagulation Panel">
      <data key="d0">Coagulation Panel</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A coagulation panel is a group of blood tests that assess the body's ability to form blood clots, often referred to as a disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) panel.&lt;SEP&gt;A coagulation panel is a group of blood tests that assess the body's ability to form blood clots, often referredव to as a disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) panel.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility">
      <data key="d0">Fertility</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility concerns are critical for cancer patients, as treatments like chemotherapy and radiation may impact reproductive health, necessitating discussions with care teams before treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility concerns include the impact of cancer treatments like chemotherapy on reproductive health and the need for fertility preservation strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility is a category of concern related to cancer treatment impacts, particularly in preserving reproductive health and future childbearing capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility is a category related to the ability to have children, which can be affected by cancer treatments and preserved through various medical interventions.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility is a long-term concern for patients undergoing anal cancer treatment, as radiation and chemotherapy may affect reproductive function.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility is a topic discussed in the text, focusing on how cancer treatments may impact reproductive health and preservation options.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility refers to the ability to have children, and cancer treatments like targeted therapy can impact fertility, necessitating preservation strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility refers to the biological capacity to conceive and carry a pregnancy, which can be impacted by medical conditions and treatments such as cancer therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Performance Status">
      <data key="d0">Performance Status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Performance Status is a category that evaluates a patient's general health and ability to perform daily tasks, measured using the ECOG PS scale.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance Status is a measure of a patient's functional ability and overall health, often evaluated using standardized scales like the Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) or Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS).&lt;SEP&gt;Performance status (PS) is a measure of a person's general health and ability to perform daily tasks, assessed using scales like the Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) or Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS).&lt;SEP&gt;Performance status (PS) is a scale used to assess a patient's general health and ability to perform daily activities, with the ECOG scale ranging from 0 (fully active) to 5 (not alive), influencing treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance status is a measure of a patient's ability to perform daily activities, used to assess treatment eligibility and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance status is a scoring system evaluating a patient's ability to perform daily activities, used to assess CLL prognosis and treatment tolerance.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance status measures a patient's ability to perform daily activities and assesses overall health during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Tests">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone Marrow Tests encompass diagnostic procedures to evaluate the health and function of bone marrow, including aspirate and biopsy techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow tests are diagnostic procedures used to evaluate the health of the bone marrow, including aspirate and biopsy techniques, often critical in diagnosing conditions like AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Tests that examine bone marrow to identify the presence of myeloid blasts, a key diagnostic indicator for AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flow Cytometry">
      <data key="d0">Flow Cytometry</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Flow Cytometry is a laboratory technique used to analyze and sort cells based on their physical and chemical properties, commonly employed in immunophenotyping.&lt;SEP&gt;Flow cytometry is a diagnostic technique used to analyze cells, particularly in detecting subtle differences between types of blood cancers by examining markers on immune system cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Flow cytometry is a laboratory technique analyzing cell properties to detect and classify blood cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Flow cytometry is a laboratory technique used to analyze and sort cells based on their characteristics, commonly employed in identifying blood cancers through immunophenotyping.&lt;SEP&gt;Flow cytometry is a laboratory technique used to detect protein patterns on cell surfaces, helping identify the immunophenotype of abnormal cells in CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;Flow cytometry is a laboratory technique used to detect, identify, and count specific cells by analyzing their physical and chemical properties under light.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunophenotyping">
      <data key="d0">Immunophenotyping</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunophenotyping is a process that identifies cell surface markers to classify cells, particularly in the immune system, and is a key application of flow cytometry.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunophenotyping is a process that identifies white blood cell antigens (biomarkers) using antibodies, crucial for diagnosing and classifying blood cancers like AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunophenotyping is a process that uses antibodies to identify surface markers on white blood cells, crucial for diagnosing and classifying leukemia subtypes.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunophenotyping is a process using antibodies to identify white blood cell surface markers for cancer classification.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunophenotyping is the process of identifying and characterizing cells based on their surface markers, critical for diagnosing and monitoring blood cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HLA Typing">
      <data key="d0">HLA Typing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic test to determine human leukocyte antigen types, relevant for AML treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;HLA Typing is a procedure to determine human leukocyte antigen (HLA) compatibility, essential for matching donors and recipients in transplantation.&lt;SEP&gt;HLA typing is a blood test that determines an individual's human leukocyte antigen (HLA) type, essential for matching donors in hematopoietic cell transplants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Allogeneic Hematopoietic Cell Transplant (HCT)">
      <data key="d0">Allogeneic Hematopoietic Cell Transplant (HCT)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Allogeneic Hematopoietic Cell Transplant (HCT) is a treatment involving the infusion of blood-forming cells from a donor to replace a patient's diseased bone marrow.&lt;SEP&gt;An allogeneic hematopoietic cell transplant involves transferring blood-forming cells from a donor to a recipient, requiring HLA typing for compatibility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Leukemia is a broad category of cancers affecting blood and bone marrow, marked by abnormal white blood cell proliferation.&lt;SEP&gt;Leukemia is a broad category of cancers affecting blood-forming tissues, including the bone marrow and lymphatic system, with subtypes like AML requiring diagnostic tests and targeted treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Leukemia is a broad category of cancers that affect the blood and bone marrow, characterized by the overproduction of abnormal white blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Leukemia is a category of cancers originating in the bone marrow, characterized by the overproduction of abnormal white blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Leukemia is a type of cancer affecting blood cells, which may spread to lymph nodes, liver, spleen, skin, and rarely the brain.&lt;SEP&gt;Leukemia is a type of cancer affecting the blood and bone marrow, characterized by the overproduction of abnormal white blood cells, with subtypes like AML and BPDCN.&lt;SEP&gt;Leukemia is a type of cancer affecting the blood and bone marrow, characterized by the overproduction of abnormal white blood cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blastic Plasmacytoid Dendritic Cell Neoplasm (BPDCN)">
      <data key="d0">Blastic Plasmacytoid Dendritic Cell Neoplasm (BPDCN)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BPDCN is a rare subtype of AML involving plasmacytoid dendritic cells, often requiring additional biopsies like lymph node or skin lesion tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Blastic Plasmacytoid Dendritic Cell Neoplasm (BPDCN) is a rare subtype of AML involving plasmacytoid dendritic cells, often requiring additional biopsies for diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunohistochemistry (IHC)">
      <data key="d0">Immunohistochemistry (IHC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A laboratory technique used to detect specific proteins (like hormone receptors or HER2) in tissue samples, aiding in cancer diagnosis and classification.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunohistochemistry (IHC) is a staining method detecting proteins in tissue samples to analyze cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunohistochemistry (IHC) is a staining method used to detect specific proteins in tissue samples, aiding in the analysis of cancer cells and their biomarkers.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunohistochemistry is a staining method used to study cell immunophenotypes from biopsy or tissue samples, aiding in the analysis of leukemia cell characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mutation Testing">
      <data key="d0">Mutation Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mutation Testing detects specific genetic mutations in tumors to determine eligibility for targeted therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Mutation testing identifies genetic alterations in AML cells, such as deletions, insertions, inversions, amplifications, translocations, and point mutations, guiding treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Mutation testing identifies genetic mutations in leukemia cells to guide treatment and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Mutation testing is a diagnostic method used to identify genetic mutations in leukemia cells, helping determine treatment options and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Karyotype">
      <data key="d0">Karyotype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Karyotype analysis creates a visual representation of chromosomes to detect structural abnormalities like extra, missing, or rearranged chromosomes, requiring cell growth and typically using bone marrow or blood samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Karyotype analysis creates a visual representation of chromosomes to identify extra, missing, or rearranged chromosomes, requiring cell growth and typically using bone marrow or blood samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Karyotype is a genetic analysis category that examines chromosome structure, detecting abnormalities such as complex karyotypes that may influence CLL treatment strategies and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Karyotype is a genetic testing category that examines chromosome structure and detects abnormalities, such as complex karyotypes, which may influence CLL treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Karyotype is a method to visualize chromosomes and detect structural abnormalities, critical for identifying chromosomal changes in AML cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Karyotype is a method used to analyze chromosomes, showing extra, missing, rearranged, or abnormal pieces of chromosomes in cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fluorescence in Situ Hybridization (FISH)">
      <data key="d0">Fluorescence in Situ Hybridization (FISH)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FISH is a technique using DNA probes to detect chromosomal abnormalities in AML cells, particularly for known genetic changes that are too small to see with other methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS)">
      <data key="d0">Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NGS is a high-throughput DNA sequencing method used to identify mutations and genetic profiles in AML cells, enabling detailed molecular analysis for treatment personalization.&lt;SEP&gt;Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS) is a high-throughput technology used to analyze tumor DNA and identify genetic alterations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR)">
      <data key="d0">Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PCR is a molecular biology technique used to amplify and detect specific genetic mutations in AML cells, supporting diagnosis and monitoring treatment responses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Leukemia Predisposition Syndromes">
      <data key="d0">Leukemia Predisposition Syndromes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Leukemia predisposition syndromes are hereditary conditions increasing cancer risk, requiring specialized diagnostic approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Leukemia predisposition syndromes are hereditary conditions that increase the risk of developing leukemia, often identified through genetic testing and skin biopsies to detect inherited mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Leukemia predisposition syndromes are hereditary conditions that increase the risk of developing leukemia, often requiring specialized diagnostic approaches like skin punch biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;Leukemia predisposition syndromes are hereditary conditions that increase the risk of developing leukemia, requiring genetic testing of unaffected tissues (like skin) to distinguish inherited risks from acquired mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Leukemia predisposition syndromes are hereditary conditions that increase the risk of leukemia, often detected through genetic testing and skin biopsies to identify inherited genetic mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Next-Generation Sequencing">
      <data key="d0">Next-Generation Sequencing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS) is a method used to determine a portion of a person’s DNA sequence, identifying mutations that may affect gene function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Polymerase Chain Reaction">
      <data key="d0">Polymerase Chain Reaction</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) is a technique that amplifies DNA or RNA, enabling the detection of 1 abnormal cell among over 100,000 normal cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brain CT Scan">
      <data key="d0">Brain CT Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Brain CT scan uses x-rays and computer technology to detect bleeding or structural abnormalities in the brain.&lt;SEP&gt;A brain CT scan uses X-rays and computer technology to detect intracranial bleeding or abnormalities, often performed with or without contrast material to enhance image quality.&lt;SEP&gt;A brain CT scan uses X-rays and computer technology to detect intracranial bleeding or other abnormalities, often performed with or without contrast material to enhance image quality.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brain MRI Scan">
      <data key="d0">Brain MRI Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Brain MRI scan uses radio waves and magnets to detect swelling or inflammation in the brain’s outer layer caused by leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;A brain MRI scan employs radio waves and strong magnets to visualize brain structures, detecting swelling from leukemia (leukemic meningitis) without radiation exposure, though it requires caution for individuals with metal implants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electrocardiogram">
      <data key="d0">Electrocardiogram</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An Electrocardiogram (ECG/EKG) records the heart’s electrical activity to detect arrhythmias or prolonged QT intervals.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrocardiogram (ECG/EKG) is a test that records the electrical activity of the heart, detecting abnormalities such as prolonged QT intervals that can lead to dangerous heart rhythms.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrocardyogram (ECG/EKG) is a test that records the electrical activity of the heart, detecting abnormalities such as prolonged QT intervals that can lead to dangerous heart rhythms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Echocardiogram">
      <data key="d0">Echocardiogram</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An Echocardiogram uses sound waves to create images of the heart, assessing its structure and function.&lt;SEP&gt;An echocardiogram is a medical test that uses sound waves to create images of the heart, assessing its structure and function.&lt;SEP&gt;Echocardiogram is an ultrasound-based test that creates images of the heart's structure and function, measuring ejection fraction to assess heart pumping efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cardiologist">
      <data key="d0">Cardiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Cardiologist is a heart specialist who evaluates cardiac function and manages heart-related conditions during treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lumbar Puncture">
      <data key="d0">Lumbar Puncture</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A lumbar puncture is a medical procedure to remove spinal fluid for testing, used to detect leukemia cells in the spinal fluid.&lt;SEP&gt;A lumbar puncture is a procedure to collect cerebrospinal fluid for testing, often used in CNS lymphoma diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;A procedure to examine cerebrospinal fluid for signs of AML in the central nervous system.&lt;SEP&gt;Lumbar Puncture (spinal tap) is a procedure to collect spinal fluid for testing, often used to detect leukemia cells in the central nervous system.&lt;SEP&gt;Lumbar puncture is a procedure to collect cerebrospinal fluid for testing, used to detect cancer cells in the spinal fluid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hematopoietic Cell Transplant">
      <data key="d0">Hematopoietic Cell Transplant</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A procedure to replace damaged bone marrow with healthy stem cells, used in AML treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic and Biomarker Tests">
      <data key="d0">Genetic and Biomarker Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnostic tests to identify genetic mutations and biomarkers, aiding in AML subtype determination and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic and Biomarker Tests are diagnostic procedures used to identify specific gene mutations or biomarkers in AML, aiding in determining treatment options and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hematologist">
      <data key="d0">Hematologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A hematologist is a medical specialist in blood diseases and blood cancers, responsible for diagnosing and managing conditions like AML.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical doctor specializing in blood disorders and blood cancers, including AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Medical Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A doctor specializing in cancer treatment using systemic therapies like chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist is a cancer specialist who uses chemotherapy, targeted therapy, and other systemic treatments to manage thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist specializes in cancer treatment using systemic therapies such as chemotherapy, targeted therapy, and immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist treats cancer with systemic therapies like chemotherapy and immunotherapy, often coordinating care with other specialists in a multidisciplinary team.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical Oncologist is a cancer specialist who manages systemic treatments, including chemotherapy, and collaborates with the treatment team for comprehensive thyroid cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pathologist">
      <data key="d0">Pathologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Pathologist analyzes tissue and bodily fluids to diagnose diseases, including cancer, through microscopic examination.&lt;SEP&gt;A Pathologist is a medical doctor who examines tissue samples under a microscope to diagnose diseases, including ovarian cancer, by identifying cancerous cells.&lt;SEP&gt;A Pathologist is a medical expert who analyzes tissue samples under a microscope to diagnose cancer and identify malignant cells.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical professional analyzing tissue and cell samples to diagnose cancer, including AML.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical professional specializing in examining cells under a microscope to confirm cancer presence and determine tumor histology.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical professional who examines tissue samples under a microscope to diagnose cancer and other diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical specialist who analyzes tissue and cell samples from biopsies to diagnose breast cancer and other conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist analyzes cells and tissues from biopsies to diagnose cancer, determine staging, and identify biomarkers for treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist examines cells and tissues under a microscope to detect abnormalities, playing a key role in diagnosing cancer and analyzing biopsy samples.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical specialist who examines tissue samples under a microscope to diagnose diseases, including identifying cancerous cells in biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical specialist who examines tissue samples under a microscope to identify cancerous cells, determine tumor type, and grade its severity in biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;Pathologist is a medical professional who analyzes biopsy samples and imaging results to determine the presence of cancer and its characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Pathologist is a medical professional who analyzes tissue samples to determine cancer staging and guide treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Pathologist is a medical professional who examines biopsy samples under a microscope to identify cancer cells, determine cancer extent, and provide critical diagnostic information.&lt;SEP&gt;Pathologists are medical professionals who analyze tissue samples to determine cancer types and grades, playing a critical role in diagnosis and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;The pathologist is a medical professional who examines cancer cells to determine their grade and characteristics, playing a key role in cancer diagnosis and classification.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hematopathologist">
      <data key="d0">Hematopathologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A hematopathologist is a medical professional specializing in diagnosing blood disorders, including leukemia, by analyzing tissue and blood samples.&lt;SEP&gt;A hematopathologist specializes in diagnosing blood cancers by examining cells and tissues, providing critical information for treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;A specialist in blood-related diseases, analyzing cells and tissues for cancer diagnosis, particularly in AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Center">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Center</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A specialized healthcare facility providing comprehensive care and research for cancer patients, including those with AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer Center is a healthcare organization specializing in cancer diagnosis, treatment, and research, often utilizing advanced imaging and biopsy techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Aspirate and Biyopsy">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Aspirate and Biyopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Procedures to collect samples from the bone marrow for diagnostic testing in AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Antimetabolites">
      <data key="d0">Antimetabolites</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Antimetabolites are a class of chemotherapy drugs that prevent DNA building blocks from being used, examples include Cladribine and Cytarabine.&lt;SEP&gt;Antimetabolites are chemotherapy drugs that inhibit DNA synthesis, examples include Cladribine, Cytarabine, and Fludarabine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anthracyclines">
      <data key="d0">Anthracyclines</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anthracyclines are chemotherapy agents that damage DNA, leading to cell death, but may cause heart issues with long-term use.&lt;SEP&gt;Anthracyclines are chemotherapy drugs that damage DNA, causing cell death, though they may cause heart issues and have dosage limits.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hypomethylating agents">
      <data key="d0">Hypomethylating agents</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hypomethylating agents (e.g., Azacitidine, Decitabine) block DNA methylation, reactivating genes to allow leukemic blasts to mature into normal cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Targeted therapy">
      <data key="d0">Targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Targeted therapy focuses on specific cancer cell features, using drugs like Gemtuzumab ozogamicin (GO) linked to chemotherapy agents.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is a category of cancer treatment that specifically targets cancer cells' genetic mutations or proteins.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy, including bevacizumab (Avastin), is used in combination with chemotherapy for certain types of metastatic cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Core binding factor (CBF)">
      <data key="d0">Core binding factor (CBF)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Core binding factor (CBF) is a genetic abnormality associated with AML, often treated with combinations of chemotherapy and targeted therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLT3">
      <data key="d0">FLT3</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FLT3 refers to genetic mutations (e.g., FLT3-ITD, FLT3-TKD) in AML, which are treated with drugs like Gilteritinib and Quizartinib.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Guide CD33">
      <data key="d0">Guide CD33</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Guide CD33 is a resource detailing targeted therapy examples, including Gemtuzumab ozogamicin (GO), for AML treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Female Reproductive System">
      <data key="d0">Female Reproductive System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Female Reproductive System is the group of organs including the uterus, cervix, vagina, ovaries, and fallopian tubes that work together for sexual reproduction and fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;The female reproductive system includes the ovaries, fallopian tubes, uterus, cervix, and vagina, responsible for sexual reproduction and hormone production.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian Cancer is a gynecological malignancy associated with germline mutations in genes such as BRCA1 and BRCA2, and is part of the spectrum of cancers linked to hereditary genetic risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian Cancer is a type of cancer originating in the ovaries, often diagnosed through imaging, biopsies, genetic testing, and surgical procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian Cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the ovaries and is a focus of treatment strategies discussed in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian Cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the ovaries and is discussed in terms of staging, treatment options, and associated risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian Cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the ovaries and is often diagnosed through various medical procedures and genetic testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer affecting the ovaries, characterized by symptoms like bloating, pelvic pain, and changes in bowel/bladder habits. It is diagnosed through various medical procedures and imaging tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that affects the ovaries and can cause symptoms such as bloating, pelvic pain, and changes in bowel or bladder habits. It is diagnosed through various medical tests and exams.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that can be treated with chemotherapy and maintenance therapy, including options like PARP inhibitors.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the ovaries and is discussed in detail, including its symptoms, diagnostic methods, and treatment considerations.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer refers to cancers originating in the ovaries, often affecting the epithelium, and includes various subtypes such as high-grade serous carcinoma and low-grade endometrioid carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Epithelium">
      <data key="d0">Epithelium</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The epithelium is the layer of tissue surrounding the ovaries, where most ovarian cancers originate, and plays a critical role in cellular abnormalities leading to malignancy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-grade Serous Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">High-grade Serous Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-grade serous carcinoma (HGSC) is the most common type of ovarian cancer, characterized by aggressive growth and rapid spread, often originating in the epithelium.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-grade Endometrioid Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">High-grade Endometrioid Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-grade endometrioid carcinoma is a less common but significant subtype of ovarian cancer, associated with endometrial-like features and hormonal influences.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low-grade Serous Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Low-grade Serous Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Low-grade serous carcinoma is a rare subtype of ovarian cancer, typically slower-growing compared to high-grade variants, and often linked to genetic predispositions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low-grade Endometrioid Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Low-grade Endometrioid Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Low-grade endometrioid carcinoma is a distinct subtype of ovarian cancer, characterized by endometrial-like histology and a generally better prognosis compared to high-grade counterparts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carcinosarcoma">
      <data key="d0">Carcinosarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Carcinosarcoma, also known as malignant mixed Mullerian tumors, is a rare and aggressive form of ovarian cancer combining features of both carcinoma and sarcoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clear Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Clear Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Clear cell carcinoma is a rare subtype of ovarian cancer, often associated with endometriosis and characterized by distinct histological features under the microscope.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mucinous Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Mucinous Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mucinous carcinoma is a rare ovarian cancer type characterized by the production of mucus and often associated with multiple cystic structures in the ovary.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Borderline Epithelial Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Borderline Epithelial Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Borderline epithelial tumors, also called low malignant potential tumors, are non-invasive or minimally invasive ovarian tumors with a lower risk of metastasis compared to malignant carcinomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Malignant Sex-cord Stromal Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Malignant Sex-cord Stromal Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Malignant sex-cord stromal tumors are rare ovarian cancers arising from supportive tissues, often linked to hormone-related symptoms and genetic factors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Malignant Germ Cell Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Malignant Germ Cell Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Malignant germ cell tumors are rare ovarian cancers originating from reproductive cells, often affecting younger women and associated with specific genetic markers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Peritoneal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Primary Peritoneal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary peritoneal cancer is a cancer that originates in the peritoneum, the tissue lining the abdominal cavity, and shares similar treatment approaches with ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fallopian Tube Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Fallopian Tube Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fallopian tube cancer is a rare type of gynecological cancer that develops in the fallopian tubes, often with overlapping features with ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gynecologic Oncologists">
      <data key="d0">Gynecologic Oncologists</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Gynecologic oncologists are medical specialists focused on diagnosing and treating cancers of the female reproductive system, including ovarian, cervical, and uterine cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Oncologists">
      <data key="d0">Medical Oncologists</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical oncologists are specialists who manage cancer treatment using systemic therapies like chemotherapy, often collaborating with gynecologic oncologists for comprehensive care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Abdominal Exam">
      <data key="d0">Abdominal Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An abdominal exam involves palpating the abdomen to check for abnormalities such as organ size, tenderness, or fluid buildup (ascites), aiding in ovarian cancer detection.&lt;SEP&gt;An abdominal exam is a medical procedure where a healthcare provider palpates the abdomen to check for abnormalities, such as organ size, tenderness, or fluid accumulation (ascites).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Exam">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A pelvic exam evaluates reproductive organs like the uterus, ovaries, and cervix for abnormalities, including masses or positional changes, to assess ovarian cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;A pelvic exam involves checking the reproductive organs, including the uterus, ovaries, and cervix, for abnormalities, pain, or changes in size/position.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Abdominal and Pelvic Exam">
      <data key="d0">Abdominal and Pelvic Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">This combined exam assesses both abdominal and pelvic regions for signs of ovarian cancer, including fluid accumulation, organ enlargement, or masses.&lt;SEP&gt;This combined exam assesses both the abdomen and pelvic region to evaluate for signs of ovarian cancer, including fluid buildup, organ enlargement, or masses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rectovaginal Exam">
      <data key="d0">Rectovaginal Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A rectovaginal exam checks the area between the rectum and vagina for tumors, which may indicate ovarian cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;A rectovaginal exam involves checking the rectum and vagina together to detect abnormalities, such as tumors, in the space between these areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pap Test">
      <data key="d0">Pap Test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Pap test examines cells from the cervix to detect cervical cancer or pre-cancerous changes, though it is not used for diagnosing ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A Pap test examines cervical cells to detect cervical cancer or pre-cancerous changes, though it is not used for ovarian cancer diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endometrial Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Endometrial Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An endometrial biopsy involves removing a sample of the uterine lining to rule out uterine cancer, often performed during pelvic exams.&lt;SEP&gt;An endometrial biopsy removes uterine lining cells to rule out uterine cancer, often performed during pelvic exams.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transvaginal Ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">Transvaginal Ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A specialized ultrasound where a probe is inserted into the vagina to clearly view the ovaries and detect abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;Transvaginal ultrasound is an imaging procedure using sound waves to visualize the cervix and surrounding areas, often used as an alternative to MRI when patients cannot undergo MRI scans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA1">
      <data key="d0">BRCA1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A gene associated with an increased risk of hereditary breast and ovarian cancer, also linked to prostate cancer susceptibility.&lt;SEP&gt;A gene associated with increased risk of various cancers, including breast, ovarian, pancreatic, and prostate cancer, and is often tested for hereditary mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA1 is a gene mutation associated with increased breast cancer risk. Patients with BRCA1/BRCA2 mutations may qualify for PARP inhibitor therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA1 is a gene that, when mutated, significantly increases the risk of developing ovarian, breast, and other cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA1 is a gene that, when mutated, significantly increases the risk of hereditary ovarian, breast, and other cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA2">
      <data key="d0">BRCA2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A gene associated with increased risk of various cancers, including breast, ovarian, pancreatic, and prostate cancer, and is often tested for hereditary mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;A gene that, when mutated, significantly increases the risk of various cancers, including prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA2 is a gene that, when mutated, elevates the risk of hereditary ovarian, breast, and other cancers, often linked to genetic syndromes.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA2 is a gene that, when mutated, increases the risk of developing ovarian, breast, and other cancers, often associated with hereditary cancer syndromes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Counselor">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Counselor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Genetic Counselor is a healthcare professional specializing in interpreting genetic test results and managing hereditary cancer risks.&lt;SEP&gt;A Genetic Counselor is a healthcare professional with specialized training in interpreting genetic test results and providing guidance on hereditary cancer risks.&lt;SEP&gt;An expert who provides guidance on genetic testing, helps interpret results, and advises patients on inherited cancer risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic Counselor is a category of professional providing guidance and support for patients undergoing genetic testing and hereditary cancer risk assessment.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic Counselor is an expert who provides guidance on genetic testing, interprets results, and helps patients understand hereditary cancer risks and testing options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gynecology Care Team">
      <data key="d0">Gynecology Care Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Gynecology Care Team is a medical team specializing in women's reproductive health, involved in diagnosing and treating ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The Gynecology Care Team is a specialized medical group focused on diagnosing and managing ovarian cancer in women.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oncology Care Team">
      <data key="d0">Oncology Care Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Oncology Care Team is a multidisciplinary group of specialists focused on cancer treatment, including ovarian cancer, and managing patient care.&lt;SEP&gt;The Oncology Care Team is a multidisciplinary team overseeing cancer treatment, including chemotherapy, radiation, and targeted therapies for ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic Laparoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic Laparoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnostic Laparoscopy is a minimally invasive procedure to assess ovarian cancer spread by examining abdominal tissues and organs.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagnostic Laparoscopy is a minimally invasive procedure used to assess the extent of ovarian cancer in the abdomen by examining tissue samples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chest X-ray">
      <data key="d0">Chest X-ray</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chest X-ray is a radiographic imaging method to evaluate lung involvement and detect cancer spread in ovarian cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Chest X-ray is a radiographic imaging technique used to evaluate the lungs and detect cancer metastasis to the respiratory system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fine-Needle Aspiration (FNA)">
      <data key="d0">Fine-Needle Aspiration (FNA)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Fine-Needle Aspiration (FNA) is a diagnostic procedure used to sample thyroid tissue, but it cannot definitively diagnose follicular or oncocytic cancers without additional testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Fine-Needle Aspiration (FNA) is a diagnostic procedure used to sample thyroid tissue, though it cannot definitively diagnose follicular or oncocytic cancers without further testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Fine-Needle Aspiration (FNA) is a procedure involving the removal of a small tissue sample using a thin needle to diagnose cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Fine-Needle Aspiration (FNA) is a procedure to extract small tissue samples using a thin needle for cancer diagnosis and staging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Core Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Core Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Core Biopsy is a procedure that extracts a cylindrical tissue sample using a hollow needle for cancer diagnosis and analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Core Biopsy is a procedure that removes cylindrical tissue samples via a hollow needle for detailed cancer analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Paracentesis">
      <data key="d0">Paracentesis</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Paracentesis is a medical procedure to drain abdominal fluid and assess cancer spread, often used in ovarian cancer management.&lt;SEP&gt;Paracentesis is a medical procedure to remove fluid from the abdominal cavity, often used to assess cancer spread and obtain samples for testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA Genes">
      <data key="d0">BRCA Genes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BRCA genes are a category of genes that, when mutated, can increase the risk of hereditary cancer, particularly ovarian cancer, and are a key focus in biomarker testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Markers">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Markers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Proteins or other molecules in blood or tissue that indicate the presence of cancer or its progression, used for diagnosis and monitoring.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor markers are substances found in body tissues or fluids that may indicate the presence of cancer, such as ovarian cancer, and are used alongside other data for diagnosis and treatment monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colonoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Colonoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A colonoscopy is a medical procedure used to examine the colon and rectum for polyps and other abnormalities, often performed to screen for colon cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonoscopy is a diagnostic procedure used to examine the colon and rectum, recommended for surveillance in Stage 1 cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonoscopy is a medical procedure to examine the large bowel using a scope, which is part of the diagnostic process for ovarian cancer and other gastrointestinal conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonoscopy is a medical procedure used to examine the colon for polyps and cancer, often involving the removal of polyps (polypectomy) to prevent cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Upper Endoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Upper Endoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Upper endoscopy is a procedure to examine the upper gastrointestinal tract, including the esophagus, stomach, and small bowel, using a scope for diagnostic purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CA-125 Test">
      <data key="d0">CA-125 Test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The CA-125 test measures a protein in the blood that may indicate ovarian cancer or other conditions, though it is not definitive on its own for diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liver Function Tests">
      <data key="d0">Liver Function Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Liver function tests assess the health of the liver by measuring specific chemicals, which can indicate liver damage or cancer spread, particularly in the context of ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Liver function tests evaluate the health of the liver by measuring specific chemicals, which can indicate cancer spread or liver damage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Homologous Recombination Deficiency (HRD)">
      <data key="d0">Homologous Recombination Deficiency (HRD)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HRD is a genetic feature of tumors that indicates impaired DNA repair mechanisms, often linked to BRCA mutations and used to guide treatment decisions for ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Mutational Burden (TMB)">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Mutational Burden (TMB)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">TMB refers to the number of genetic mutations in a tumor, which can influence cancer behavior and response to specific therapies, including immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor Mutational Burden (TMB) measures the number of genetic mutations in a tumor, influencing treatment decisions and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor Mutational Burden measures the number of mutations in tumor DNA, guiding immunotherapy and targeted treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Microsatellite Instability (MSI)">
      <data key="d0">Microsatellite Instability (MSI)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MSI is a condition where DNA repair mechanisms in tumors are faulty, leading to errors in DNA replication, and is associated with certain cancer types and treatment responses.&lt;SEP&gt;Microsatellite Instability (MSI) refers to errors in DNA repair processes, often linked to specific cancer behaviors and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Microsatellite Instability (MSI) refers to errors in DNA repair processes, often linked to treatment options and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Microsatellite Instability is a biomarker reflecting DNA repair system dysfunction, impacting treatment decisions for certain cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mismatch Repair (MMR)">
      <data key="d0">Mismatch Repair (MMR)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MMR is a DNA repair process that, when defective, can lead to genetic instability in tumors, often linked to conditions like Lynch syndrome and specific cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Mismatch Repair (MMR) is a DNA repair mechanism; defects in MMR are associated with certain cancer subtypes and treatment responses.&lt;SEP&gt;Mismatch Repair (MMR) is a DNA repair mechanism; defects in MMR are linked to specific cancer behaviors and treatment responses.&lt;SEP&gt;Mismatch Repair is a biomarker indicating defects in DNA repair processes, relevant to cancer diagnosis and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HER2 Expression">
      <data key="d0">HER2 Expression</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HER2 Expression is a biomarker showing the presence of HER2 protein in cancer cells, influencing targeted therapy approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;HER2 expression refers to the presence of the HER2 protein in cancer cells, which can influence treatment options and prognosis, particularly in certain types of ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Chemistry Profile">
      <data key="d0">Blood Chemistry Profile</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A blood chemistry profile assesses the health of organs and systems by measuring various chemicals in the blood, including electrolytes, enzymes, and metabolites, relevant for evaluating ovarian cancer impact.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HER2 Biomarker">
      <data key="d0">HER2 Biomarker</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The HER2 biomarker indicates the presence of the HER2 protein in cancer cells, influencing treatment options and prognosis for specific ovarian cancer subtypes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HE4 Biomarker">
      <data key="d0">HE4 Biomarker</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The HE4 biomarker measures a protein associated with ovarian cancer, used alongside other tests to improve diagnostic accuracy and monitor treatment response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ovarian cancer">
      <data key="d0">ovarian cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer originating in the ovaries, often diagnosed through tests like genetic testing and surgical staging, and treated with chemotherapy, debulking surgery, and targeted therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the ovaries and is often diagnosed through various tests and treatments, including genetic testing and surgical procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA genes">
      <data key="d0">BRCA genes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BRCA genes are a category of genes associated with hereditary ovarian cancer, where mutations in these genes significantly increase the risk of developing the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lynch syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Lynch syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lynch syndrome is a genetic disorder that increases the risk of hereditary ovarian cancer and other cancers, often linked to mutations in DNA repair genes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="gynecologic oncologist">
      <data key="d0">gynecologic oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A gynecologic oncologist is a specialized medical organization or professional group that provides expert care for cancers affecting the female reproductive system, including ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="biomarker testing">
      <data key="d0">biomarker testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biomarker testing is a diagnostic category that analyzes specific features of cancer, such as mutations, to guide treatment decisions and personalize care for ovarian cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="surgical staging">
      <data key="d0">surgical staging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical staging is a category of procedures used to determine the extent of ovarian cancer spread during surgery, involving tissue sampling and microscopic analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="laparotomy">
      <data key="d0">laparotomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Laparotomy is a surgical category involving a large abdominal incision to access and remove cancerous tissue, commonly used for staging or cytoreductive surgery in ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="laparoscopy">
      <data key="d0">laparoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Laparoscopy is a minimally invasive surgical category that uses small incisions and a camera to perform procedures like tumor removal or staging in early-stage ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemotherapy is a treatment category that uses drugs to kill cancer cells, often employed in combination with surgery for advanced ovarian cancer cases.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy uses drugs to destroy cancer cells, typically used for advanced-stage prostate cancer that has spread beyond the prostate gland.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="maintenance therapy">
      <data key="d0">maintenance therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Maintenance therapy is a treatment category designed to prolong remission after initial chemotherapy, particularly for advanced ovarian cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="debulking surgery">
      <data key="d0">debulking surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Debulking surgery is a surgical category aimed at removing as much cancerous tissue as possible, often used when ovarian cancer has spread beyond the ovaries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="cytoreductive surgery">
      <data key="d0">cytoreductive surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cytoreductive surgery is a specialized surgical category focused on reducing the tumor burden by removing cancerous tissue, typically in advanced ovarian cancer cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="fertility-sparing surgery">
      <data key="d0">fertility-sparing surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility-sparing surgery is a category of procedures that aim to preserve reproductive organs, such as the uterus, for patients with early-stage ovarian cancer who wish to maintain fertility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="unilateral salpingooophorectomy">
      <data key="d0">unilateral salpingooophorectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Unilateral salpingooophorectomy is a surgical category involving the removal of one ovary and fallopian tube, often used in fertility-sparing approaches for localized ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="in vitro fertilization">
      <data key="d0">in vitro fertilization</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">In vitro fertilization (IVF) is a reproductive technology category that enables pregnancy through laboratory fertilization of eggs, often used as an assisted reproductive option after ovarian cancer surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="omentum">
      <data key="d0">omentum</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The omentum is a fatty tissue category in the abdomen that is frequently sampled during surgical staging to detect microscopic cancer spread in ovarian cancer cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="lymph nodes">
      <data key="d0">lymph nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph nodes are a category of immune system structures that are examined during surgical staging to assess the spread of ovarian cancer and potential metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="tumor mutational burden">
      <data key="d0">tumor mutational burden</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor mutational burden is a biomarker category that quantifies the number of genetic mutations in a tumor, influencing treatment decisions for ovarian cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="microsatellite instability">
      <data key="d0">microsatellite instability</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Microsatellite instability is a biomarker category that indicates defects in DNA repair mechanisms, often linked to specific genetic mutations in ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="mismatch repair">
      <data key="d0">mismatch repair</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mismatch repair is a cellular process category that corrects DNA errors, and its dysfunction is associated with certain genetic mutations in ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HER2 expression">
      <data key="d0">HER2 expression</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HER2 expression is a biomarker category that indicates the presence of a protein involved in cell growth, influencing treatment strategies for ovarian cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="folate receptor alpha">
      <data key="d0">folate receptor alpha</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Folate receptor alpha is a biomarker category that may be expressed on cancer cells, potentially guiding targeted therapies for ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RET mutations">
      <data key="d0">RET mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">RET mutations are genetic alterations in the RET gene that may contribute to the development of certain types of ovarian cancer and influence treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NTRK gene fusion">
      <data key="d0">NTRK gene fusion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NTRK gene fusion is a genetic abnormality category that may be present in some ovarian cancer cases, potentially affecting treatment options and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="tumor marker tests">
      <data key="d0">tumor marker tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor marker tests are a category of blood tests that measure specific substances to aid in the diagnosis and monitoring of ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="genetic testing">
      <data key="d0">genetic testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic testing is a diagnostic category used to identify inherited mutations, such as BRCA gene alterations, which increase the risk of hereditary ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DNA repair">
      <data key="d0">DNA repair</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">DNA repair is a cellular process category that maintains genetic integrity, and its dysfunction is linked to the development of ovarian cancer and related genetic disorders.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="blood tests">
      <data key="d0">blood tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood tests are a diagnostic category that includes CBC, chemistry profiles, and tumor marker tests to assess ovarian cancer and monitor treatment responses.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood tests are diagnostic tools used to assess health conditions, including liver cancer, by analyzing blood samples for specific markers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO)">
      <data key="d0">International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIG.M) is an organization that develops a staging system for ovarian cancer, which is widely used to describe the cancer's progression.&lt;SEP&gt;The International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) is an organization that develops a staging system for ovarian cancer, which is widely used to describe the cancer's progression.&lt;SEP&gt;The International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) is an organization that establishes the staging system for cervical cancer, categorizing it into stages I to IV based on tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Menopause">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Menopause</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical Menopause is a condition caused by the removal of both ovaries during surgery, leading to a sudden drop in estrogen and symptoms such as hot flashes and vaginal atrophy.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgical Menopause is a condition caused by the sudden removal of ovaries during hysterectomy, leading to hormonal changes and associated symptoms like hot flashes and osteoporosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Interval Cytoreductive Surgery (ICS)">
      <data key="d0">Interval Cytoreductive Surgery (ICS)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Interval Cytoreductive Surgery (ICS) is a surgical procedure performed after chemotherapy to remove as much cancer as possible, often combined with hyperthermic intraperitoneal chemotherapy (HIPEC).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hyperthermic Intraperitoneal Chemotherapy (HIPEC)">
      <data key="d0">Hyperthermic Intraperitoneal Chemotherapy (HIPEC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hyperthermic Intraperitoneal Chemotherapy (HIPEC) is a specialized chemotherapy technique used during surgery to circulate heated chemotherapy drugs in the abdominal cavity to target cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Peritoneal Washing">
      <data key="d0">Peritoneal Washing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Peritoneal Washing is a diagnostic procedure where a special liquid is used to rinse the abdominal cavity, and samples are tested for cancer cells to detect microscopic metastases.&lt;SEP&gt;Peritoneal Washing is a procedure where a special liquid is used to rinse the abdominal cavity, and samples are tested for cancer cells to detect microscopic metastases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Menopausal Symptom Team">
      <data key="d0">Menopausal Symptom Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Menopausal Symptom Team is an organization that provides guidance and treatment options for patients experiencing severe menopausal symptoms following surgical menopause.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Thinners">
      <data key="d0">Blood Thinners</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood Thinners are medications or injections used to prevent blood clots (deep vein thrombosis) in patients recovering from ovarian cancer surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT)">
      <data key="d0">Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) is a condition involving blood clots in the deep veins, which is a risk factor for patients who have undergone recent abdominal surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FIGO">
      <data key="d0">FIGO</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">FIGO (Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics) is an international organization that establishes standardized staging systems for ovarian cancer, including the FIGO stages described in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1A">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1A</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1A is a classification of ovarian cancer where the tumor is confined to one ovary with intact capsule and no cancer cells in ascites or washings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1B">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1B</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1B is a classification of ovarian cancer where the tumor is present in both ovaries with intact capsules and no cancer cells in ascites or washings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1C1">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1C1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1C1 is a substage of ovarian cancer where the ovarian capsule broke open during surgery (surgical spill), indicating cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1C2">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1C2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1C2 is a substage of ovarian cancer where the ovarian capsule broke open before surgery or there is cancer on the outer surface of the ovary or fallopian tube.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1C3">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1C3</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1C3 is a substage of ovarian cancer where cancer cells are found in ascites or washings, indicating dissemination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 2A">
      <data key="d0">Stage 2A</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 2A is a classification of ovarian cancer where the tumor has spread to the uterus and/or fallopian tubes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 2B">
      <data key="d0">Stage 2B</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 2B is a classification of ovarian cancer where the tumor has spread to other pelvic organs like the bladder, colon, or rectum.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 3A1">
      <data key="d0">Stage 3A1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 3A1 is a classification of ovarian cancer where the tumor has spread to retroperitoneal lymph nodes, with subcategories indicating the size of the lymph node involvement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 3A1 (i)">
      <data key="d0">Stage 3A1 (i)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 3A1 (i) is a substage of ovarian cancer where cancer in the retroperitoneal lymph nodes is 2 mm or smaller.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 3A1 (ii)">
      <data key="d0">Stage 3A1 (ii)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 3A1 (ii) is a substage of ovarian cancer where cancer in the retroperitoneal lymph nodes is larger than 2 mm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 3A2">
      <data key="d0">Stage 3A2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 3A2 is a classification of ovarian cancer where the tumor has spread to the peritoneal lining with microscopic cancer cells and may involve lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 3B">
      <data key="d0">Stage 3B</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 3B is a classification of ovarian cancer where visible cancer is present on the peritoneal lining, smaller than 2 cm, with possible lymph node involvement.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 3B is bladder cancer that has spread to multiple lymph nodes in the pelvis but not distant organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 3C">
      <data key="d0">Stage 3C</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 3C is a classification of ovarian cancer where visible cancer is present on the peritoneal lining, larger than 2 cm, with possible lymph node involvement and distant spread to liver or spleen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 4A">
      <data key="d0">Stage 4A</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 4A is a classification of ovarian cancer where cancer has spread to the fluid around the lungs (malignant pleural effusion).&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 4A is bladder cancer that has invaded the pelvis or abdomen or spread to distant lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 4B">
      <data key="d0">Stage 4B</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 4B is a classification of ovarian cancer where the tumor has spread to the liver, spleen, distant lymph nodes, or other organs outside the abdomen.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 4B is metastatic bladder cancer that has spread to distant organs like bones, liver, or lungs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Platinum-based chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Platinum-based chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Platinum-based chemotherapy is a systemic therapy used for metastatic cervical cancer, particularly when the cancer has not previously been treated with cisplatin.&lt;SEP&gt;Platinum-based chemotherapy is a type of treatment for ovarian cancer involving drugs like carboplatin, cisplatin, and oxaliplatin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Taxane">
      <data key="d0">Taxane</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Taxane is a class of chemotherapy drugs used in combination with platinum-based therapies, including paclitaxel and docetaxel.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Paclitaxel">
      <data key="d0">Paclitaxel</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Paclitaxel is a chemotherapy drug used in combination with cisplatin or carboplatin for treating metastatic cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Paclitaxel is a taxane chemotherapy drug associated with side effects like peripheral neuropathy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Docetaxel">
      <data key="d0">Docetaxel</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Docetany is a taxane chemotherapy drug used in combination with platinum-based therapies for ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Docetaxel is a taxane chemotherapy drug used in combination with platinum-based therapies for ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bevacizumab">
      <data key="d0">Bevacizumab</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bevacizumab is a drug added to chemotherapy to inhibit tumor blood vessel growth, often used in advanced ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Maintenance therapy">
      <data key="d0">Maintenance therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Maintenance therapy is a treatment approach following chemotherapy to prolong remission in ovarian cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Maintenance Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Maintenance Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Maintenance Therapy is a treatment approach to delay cancer recurrence after initial treatment, involving drugs like PARP inhibitors and bevacizumab.&lt;SEP&gt;Maintenance therapy is a treatment approach used after initial treatment for ovarian cancer to reduce the risk of recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PARP Inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">PARP Inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PARP inhibitors are a class of targeted therapies used in maintenance therapy for ovarian cancer, particularly in HRD-positive cancers and BRCA-mutated cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Throat Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Throat Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Throat Cancer is a type of head and neck cancer that originates in the oropharynx, characterized by the growth of malignant cells in the throat area.&lt;SEP&gt;Throat Cancer refers to cancers affecting the throat and head and neck regions, including the pharynx and larynx, as mentioned in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;Throat cancer is a type of head and neck cancer that originates in the throat (oropharyngeal) region and is often associated with human papillomavirus (HPV) infection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PARP Inhibitor">
      <data key="d0">PARP Inhibitor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PARP Inhibitor is a class of drugs that inhibit PARP enzymes, used in cancer treatment to target DNA repair mechanisms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA1 Mutation">
      <data key="d0">BRCA1 Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BRCA1 Mutation is a genetic alteration linked to hereditary breast and ovarian cancer, influencing treatment decisions and biomarker testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA2 Mutation">
      <data key="d0">BRCA2 Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BRCA2 Mutation is a genetic mutation associated with increased risk of ovarian and breast cancer, affecting treatment strategies and biomarker analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Homologous Recombination Deficiency (HRD) Status">
      <data key="d0">Homologous Recombination Deficiency (HRD) Status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HRD Status is a biomarker indicating impaired DNA repair mechanisms, guiding treatment options for ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRAF V600E Mutation">
      <data key="d0">BRAF V600E Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BRAF V600E Mutation is a genetic alteration associated with specific cancer subtypes, influencing treatment options and biomarker testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Folate Receptor Alpha (FRα)">
      <data key="d0">Folate Receptor Alpha (FRα)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Folate Receptor Alpha is a biomarker indicating the presence of folate receptors on cancer cells, guiding targeted therapy selection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RET Mutations">
      <data key="d0">RET Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">RET Mutations are genetic alterations linked to certain cancers, affecting treatment approaches and biomarker analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NTRK Gene Fusions">
      <data key="d0">NTRK Gene Fusions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NTRK Gene Fusions are genetic abnormalities involving the NTRK gene, associated with specific cancer subtypes and targeted therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Platinum-Resistant Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Platinum-Resistant Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Platinum-Resistant Cancer refers to cancer that does not respond to platinum-based chemotherapy, necessitating alternative treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Platinum-Sensitive Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Platinum-Sensitive Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Platinum-Sensitive Cancer is cancer that responds well to platinum-based chemotherapy, guiding treatment decisions and recurrence management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bevacizumab (Avastin)">
      <data key="d0">Bevacizumab (Avastin)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bevacizum, also known as Avastin, is a drug used in treating ovarian cancer, particularly in platinum-resistant cases, and may be combined with chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Bevacizum,ab (Avastin) is a targeted therapy used in combination with chemotherapy to treat metastatic cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mirvetuximab Soravtansine-Gynx (Elahere)">
      <data key="d0">Mirvetuximab Soravtansine-Gynx (Elahere)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mirvetuximab Soravtansine-Gynx (Elahere) is a targeted therapy drug for ovarian cancer, preferred for tumors with folate receptor alpha (FRα) biomarkers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hypersensitivity Reaction">
      <data key="d0">Hypersensitivity Reaction</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hypersensitivity Reaction is an adverse immune response to chemotherapy drugs like carboplatin and cisplatin, potentially life-threatening.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endocrine Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Endocrine Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A treatment approach for hormone receptor-positive breast cancer that blocks hormone signaling to inhibit cancer growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Endocrine Therapy is a treatment modality for hormone receptor-positive breast cancers, using medications to block estrogen or progesterone effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Endocrine Therapy is a treatment strategy using hormones to manage cancer growth, particularly in hormone-sensitive cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Secondary Cytoreductive Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Secondary Cytoreductive Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Secondary Cytoreductive Surgery is a surgical procedure to remove visible cancer after initial treatment, often considered before recurrence treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hypersensitivity Reactions">
      <data key="d0">Hypersensitivity Reactions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hypersensitivity Reactions are adverse immune responses to chemotherapy drugs, requiring management strategies during treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Allergic Reaction">
      <data key="d0">Allergic Reaction</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Allergic Reaction is a type of hypersensitivity response to medications, particularly chemotherapy agents like carboplatin and cisplatin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biomarker-Based Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Biomarker-Based Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biomarker-Based Treatment is a treatment strategy guided by specific biomarker profiles, such as HRD status or BRCA mutations, to tailor therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunotherapy Side Effects">
      <data key="d0">Immunotherapy Side Effects</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunotherapy Side Effects are adverse effects associated with immunotherapy treatments, detailed in guidelines for patient management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients Immunotherapy Side Effects">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients Immunotherapy Side Effects</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN Guidelines for Patients Immunotherapy Side Effects is a document detailing side effects of immunotherapy treatments for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN.org/patientguidelines">
      <data key="d0">NCCN.org/patientguidelines</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN.org/patientguidelines is a website providing cancer patient guidelines and resources, including immunotherapy side effects.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org/patientguidelines is an organization providing patient guidelines for cancer treatment and information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous Cell Carcinoma is a type of cancer that originates in the squamous cells, which are flat, scale-like cells found in the outer layer of the skin and mucous membranes.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous Cell Carcinoma is the most common histological type of anal cancer, arising from squamous cells in the anal canal or perianal region.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous Cell Carcinoma is the most common type of anal cancer, arising from squamous cells in the anal epithelium and linked to chronic HPV infection.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous Cell Carcinoma is the most common type of anal cancer, originating from squamous cells in the anal canal or perianal region.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous Cell Carcinoma is the most common type of cervical cancer, arising from squamous cells in the ectocervix.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous Cell Carcinoma is the most common type of head and neck cancer, arising from the squamous cells lining the mucous membranes of the mouth, nose, and throat.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell carcinoma is a type of bladder cancer that arises from the squamous cells in the urinary tract, often linked to chronic irritation or infection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="p16 (HPV) Testing">
      <data key="d0">p16 (HPV) Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">p16 (HPV) Testing is a diagnostic procedure used to detect human papillomavirus (HPV) as a potential cause of head and neck cancers, particularly in squamous cell carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;p16 (HPV) Testing is a specific diagnostic category used to identify HPV-related cancers by detecting p16 protein overexpression, serving as a surrogate marker for HPV infection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Scoping Procedures">
      <data key="d0">Scoping Procedures</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Scoping Procedures are medical techniques used to examine the throat and surrounding areas for signs of cancer, often involving endoscopic visualization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility (All Genders)">
      <data key="d0">Fertility (All Genders)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility (All Genders) refers to reproductive health considerations for individuals undergoing cancer treatment, including strategies to preserve fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility considerations for all genders are part of breast cancer testing and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing for Throat Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Testing for Throat Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing for Throat Cancer is a diagnostic process that includes various procedures such as imaging tests, biopsies, and biomarker testing to confirm the presence and extent of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Support and Counseling">
      <data key="d0">Support and Counseling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Support and counseling services are available to help patients cope with emotional distress, anxiety, and other psychological challenges associated with cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hearing Test">
      <data key="d0">Hearing Test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A hearing test is a medical procedure to assess hearing loss, its type, severity, and potential causes, particularly relevant for patients with head and neck tumors or those undergoing treatments affecting hearing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lung Function Tests">
      <data key="d0">Lung Function Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lung function tests measure respiratory capacity and efficiency, including air volume and gas exchange, to evaluate how well the lungs are functioning during and after cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nutrition Assessment">
      <data key="d0">Nutrition Assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nutrition assessment involves evaluating dietary needs and monitoring nutritional status to ensure adequate intake before, during, and after cancer treatment, often involving specialists like nutritionists or dietitians.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pre-Anesthesia Exam">
      <data key="d0">Pre-Anesthesia Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A pre-anesthesia exam is a medical evaluation conducted by an anesthesiologist to assess a patient's health, allergies, and surgical readiness before procedures like head and neck cancer surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility Preservation">
      <data key="d0">Fertility Preservation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility Preservation is a medical category focusing on protecting reproductive health during cancer treatment, including considerations for age, treatment type, and birth control methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility Preservation refers to medical interventions to mitigate infertility risks caused by cancer treatments like radiation and chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility Preservation refers to medical strategies to maintain reproductive potential in patients undergoing cancer treatments like radiation or chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation involves medical strategies to protect or restore reproductive capabilities for cancer patients, often discussed in the context of treatment planning and clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation refers to medical interventions to protect or restore reproductive capabilities in cancer patients, often involving specialists and options like cryopreservation or hormone therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation refers to methods to maintain fertility options for cancer patients, especially those undergoing treatments that may affect reproductive health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN)">
      <data key="d0">National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is an organization that develops guidelines for cancer care, including distress management and treatment planning for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is an organization that provides guidelines and resources for cancer care, including fertility preservation and patient education materials.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is an organization that provides patient guidelines, resources, and treatment protocols for cancer care, including specific guidance for adolescent and young adult (AYA) patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC)">
      <data key="d0">Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) is a U.S. federal agency that provides public health information, including resources for quitting smoking and cancer staging guidance.&lt;SEP&gt;The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) is an organization that offers public health resources, including tobacco cessation information and cancer-related prevention strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatitis B and Hepatitis C Screening">
      <data key="d0">Hepatitis B and Hepatitis C Screening</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatitis B and C screening is a medical procedure to detect past or present viral infections, which can be reactivated by cancer treatments and pose risks to liver health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Smoking Cessation">
      <data key="d0">Smoking Cessation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Smoking cessation programs and resources are critical for reducing cancer risk and improving treatment outcomes, particularly for patients with head and neck or lung cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="p16 Testing">
      <data key="d0">p16 Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">p16 Testing is a diagnostic category used to detect human papillomavirus (HPV) infection in cancer cells, serving as a surrogate marker for HPV-related cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HPV">
      <data key="d0">HPV</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HPV (Human Papillomavirus) is a category of viral infection that is the primary cause of both anal and cervical cancers, prompting screening methods like HPV/Pap co-tests.&lt;SEP&gt;HPV (Human Papillomavirus) is a common sexually transmitted infection linked to nearly all cervical cancers, with high-risk strains like HPV-16 contributing to malignant transformation.&lt;SEP&gt;HPV (Human Papillomavirus) is a group of viruses that can cause various cancers, including anal cancer, and is classified into high-risk and low-risk strains.&lt;SEP&gt;HPV (Human Papillomavirus) is a sexually transmitted virus strongly associated with anal cancer risk, particularly long-term infections linked to high-risk HPV strains.&lt;SEP&gt;HPV (Human Papillomavirus) is a sexually transmitted virus strongly linked to anal cancer risk, particularly long-term infections.&lt;SEP&gt;HPV (Human Papillomavirus) is a sexually transmitted virus strongly linked to anal cancer, particularly in individuals with compromised immune systems or chronic infections.&lt;SEP&gt;HPV (Human Papillomavirus) is a viral category that causes various cancers, including throat, cervical, vulvar, and esophageal cancers, and is linked to specific tumor features.&lt;SEP&gt;HPV (Human Papillomavirus) is a viral infection that causes both anal and cervical cancers, prompting screening methods like HPV/Pap co-tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Human papillomavirus (HPV) is a group of viruses that can cause abnormal tissue growth and certain cancers, including HPV-related head and neck cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HPV Testing">
      <data key="d0">HPV Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HPV Testing is a diagnostic category used to identify HPV infections, which are linked to specific cancer subtypes and influence treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;HPV testing is a diagnostic procedure used to detect human papillomavirus (HPV) in tumor tissue, which is critical for staging and treatment planning in oropharyngeal cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HPV-Related Cancer">
      <data key="d0">HPV-Related Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HPV-Related Cancer is a specific cancer category linked to human papillomavirus (HPV) infection, characterized by distinct tumor features and improved treatment responses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Panoramic dental x-ray">
      <data key="d0">Panoramic dental x-ray</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A panoramic dental x-ray captures a wide view of the entire mouth, including teeth, jaw structures, and surrounding tissues, commonly used for dental treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Scoping procedures">
      <data key="d0">Scoping procedures</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Scoping procedures involve inserting a flexible tube with a camera into the body to visualize internal structures, often used for imaging the oropharynx and guiding biopsies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fiberoptic nasopharyngolaryngoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Fiberoptic nasopharyngolaryngoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">This procedure uses a fiberoptic scope to examine the larynx and nearby structures, helping diagnose conditions like vocal cord abnormalities or cancerous growths.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Laryngoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Laryngoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Laryngoscopy involves using a specialized scope to visualize the larynx and hypopharynx, enabling detection of tumors and assessment of vocal cord function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contrast material">
      <data key="d0">Contrast material</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Contrast material enhances imaging test accuracy by improving visibility of internal structures, though it may cause allergic reactions in some patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Contrast material is a substance used in imaging to enhance the visibility of internal organs and structures, aiding in the diagnosis of various conditions by improving image clarity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging">
      <data key="d0">Imaging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging encompasses various diagnostic techniques such as MRI, CT, ultrasound, and PET scans, used to visualize internal body structures and detect abnormalities like tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging is a diagnostic procedure used to determine the extent of cervical cancer before treatment, involving techniques like CT, MRI, PET, and transvaginal ultrasound.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging is a diagnostic test used to identify and monitor gliomas, providing visual information about tumor location and size.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging refers to diagnostic procedures like CT, MRI, or PET/CT scans used to detect cancer recurrence or metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging refers to diagnostic procedures like CT, MRI, or PET/CT scans used to detect cancer recurrence or spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging refers to diagnostic techniques like CT, MRI, and ultrasound used to visualize internal body structures and detect cancerous growths.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging refers to diagnostic tests like CT scans and MRIs used to monitor cancer progression, detect metastasis, and evaluate treatment effectiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging refers to medical techniques like X-rays, CT scans, and MRIs used to visualize internal structures and detect abnormalities such as cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging refers to techniques like CT, MRI, and ultrasound used to visualize internal body structures and detect cancerous growths.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging refers to the use of medical scans like MRI or CT to create images of the brain and detect tumors or abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging techniques are used to determine the extent of cervical cancer and guide treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging techniques like CT scans and MRIs are used for surveillance and staging metastatic anal cancer, recommended annually for 3 years post-treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging techniques such as X-rays, CT scans, or MRI are used to visualize the bladder and surrounding areas to assess the extent and spread of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic test">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnostic tests are medical procedures used to identify diseases or conditions, including imaging studies and endoscopic exams, to assess the presence and extent of conditions like cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fiberoptic Laryngoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Fiberoptic Laryngoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical procedure using a flexible scope to examine the throat, typically performed with numbing medicine and taking less than 1 minute.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Direct Laryngoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Direct Laryngoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A detailed medical exam performed under general anesthesia to examine the throat and take biopsies, allowing deeper visualization of the larynx.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Esophagoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Esophagoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A procedure to examine the esophagus for abnormal growths, performed under anesthesia to assess swallowing tube health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bronchoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Bronchoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A diagnostic procedure to examine the airway, trachea, and bronchi, often used to detect cancer or perform treatments under anesthesia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Panendoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Panendoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A comprehensive procedure combining laryngoscopy, pharyng, esophagoscopy, and bronchoscopy to thoroughly examine the head and neck region for tumors or abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;A comprehensive procedure combining laryngoscopy, pharyngoscopy, esophagoscopy, and bronchoscopy to thoroughly examine the head and neck region for tumors or abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor p16 (HPV) Testing">
      <data key="d0">Tumor p16 (HPV) Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A diagnostic test to detect HPV-associated tumors in the throat, using p16 as a marker for human papillomavirus infection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AJCC">
      <data key="d0">AJCC</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">AJCC (American Joint Committee on Cancer) is an organization that developed the staging system for cancer, including TNM scores and prognostic staging frameworks.&lt;SEP&gt;AJCC (American Joint Committee on Cancer) is an organization that developed the staging system for cancer, including TNM scores and prognostic staging.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) is an organization that developed a standardized staging system for cancer, which helps determine treatment options and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cell-free DNA Testing">
      <data key="d0">Cell-free DNA Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cell-free DNA testing analyzes circulating tumor DNA in blood samples to monitor cancer progression, treatment response, and recurrence, though it is not yet a standard diagnostic tool.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immune checkpoint inhibitor therapy is an advanced immunotherapy targeting proteins like PD-1 and PD-L1 to enhance the immune system's ability to fight cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Immune checkpoint inhibitor therapy is an advanced immunotherapy targeting proteins like PD-1 and PD-L3 to enhance the immune system's ability to fight cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PDL1">
      <data key="d0">PDL1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PDL1 (Programmed Death-Ligand 1) is a protein expressed on cancer cells that interacts with immune checkpoints, making it a key biomarker for selecting patients for immunotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prognosis">
      <data key="d0">Prognosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ProNone&lt;SEP&gt;Prognosis is a category that refers to the predicted course and outcome of a patient's cancer, influenced by staging, biomarker testing, and treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Prognosis is the predicted course and outcome of a disease, distinguishing it from diagnosis, and is influenced by factors like cancer stage, biomarkers, and treatment response.&lt;SEP&gt;Prognosis predicts the likely course and outcome of prostate cancer, guiding treatment decisions based on risk assessments and molecular biomarker results.&lt;SEP&gt;Prognosis refers to the likely course and outcome of a cancer, determined by factors like staging, biomarker testing, and the patient's overall health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Staging">
      <data key="d0">Staging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A process to determine the extent of cancer spread, crucial for tailoring treatment options for bladder and anal cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Staging is a category used to describe the extent of cancer spread, combining factors like tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;Staging is a classification system used to describe the extent of cancer spread within the bladder wall.&lt;SEP&gt;Staging is the process of determining cancer severity using the TNM system, guiding treatment plans and predicting outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Staging is the process of determining the extent of cancer (T, N, M, G) to guide treatment plans and predict patient outcomes, often using systems like the AJCC staging framework.&lt;SEP&gt;Staging is the process of determining the extent of cancer (tumor, node, metastasis) to guide treatment decisions and predict outcomes for anal cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Staging is the process of determining the extent of cervical cancer based on imaging results, biopsies, and other diagnostic tests to guide treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dental Cleanings and Checkups">
      <data key="d0">Dental Cleanings and Checkups</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Dental cleanings and checkups are preventive care measures recommended for patients with head and neck cancer to maintain oral health during treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Online Portals">
      <data key="d0">Online Portals</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Online Portals is a category of digital tools enabling patients to access medical information, test results, and healthcare resources remotely.&lt;SEP&gt;Online portals provide patients with secure access to medical records, test results, and treatment information, enhancing communication between patients and healthcare providers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Survey">
      <data key="d0">Survey</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surveys are tools used to gather patient feedback on medical guidelines and care experiences, helping improve the quality and relevance of patient-centered resources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Stage">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical stage refers to the rating given before any treatment, based on physical exams, biopsies, and imaging tests to determine the extent of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pathologic Stage">
      <data key="d0">Pathologic Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pathologic stage is determined after surgery by examining tissue removed during the procedure, providing more accurate details about cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TNM System">
      <data key="d0">TNM System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A classification system used in cancer staging to describe tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis, guiding treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;The TN, N, and M scores are combined to assign the cancer one of the following stages: 0, I (1), II (2), III (3), or IV (4).&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM (Tumor, Node, Metastasis) system is a classification framework used to stage breast cancer based on tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM (Tumor, Node, Metastasis) system is a standardized framework for describing cancer progression, categorizing tumors based on size (T), lymph node involvement (N), and distant metastasis (M).&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM (Tumor, Node, Metastasis) system is a standardized framework used to describe the extent of cancer spread, based on tumor size, lymph node involvement, and distant metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM System is a standardized categorization framework used in cancer staging, where T (tumor), N (nodes), and M (metastasis) scores define the stage and progression of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM System is a standardized method used to stage cancer, assessing the extent of the tumor (T), involvement of lymph nodes (N), and presence of metastasis (M) to determine the cancer's stage.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM system (tumor, node, metastasis) is a standardized framework used to stage throat cancer based on tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM system is a classification method used to stage bone cancers by evaluating the tumor (T), regional lymph nodes (N), and distant metastasis (M).&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM system is a standardized cancer staging framework that uses T (tumor size), N (lymph node involvement), and M (metastasis) to classify cancer stages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="p16-Positive">
      <data key="d0">p16-Positive</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">p16-positive cancer is HPV-related (HPV-mediated) and starts in the oropharynx, with distinct staging criteria compared to p16-negative cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="p16-Negative">
      <data key="d0">p16-Negative</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">p16-negative cancer is not caused by human papillomavirus (HPV) and uses the TNM staging system for squamous cell carcinoma without p16 testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Numbered Stages">
      <data key="d0">Numbered Stages</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Numbered stages (0-4) are based on TNM scores, indicating the progression of cancer from early (stage 0) to advanced (stage 4).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Locoregional">
      <data key="d0">Locoregional</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Locoregional disease refers to cancer that has spread to nearby tissues, organs, or lymph nodes in the head and neck region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Head and Neck">
      <data key="d0">Head and Neck</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Head and neck refers to the anatomical region containing lymph nodes, tumors, and structures like the carotid artery, where cancer often spreads.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL)">
      <data key="d0">Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing blood cancer that originates in lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell, and affects bone marrow function.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing blood cancer that originates in lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell, and disrupts normal blood cell production by overproducing immature lymphoblasts.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) is a type of blood cancer that affects lymphocytes, leading to the overproduction of immature lymphoblasts.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) is a type of blood cancer that originates in lymphocytes, leading to the overproduction of immature lymphoblasts.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing blood cancer characterized by the overproduction of immature lymphocytes (lymphoblasts) in the bone marrow, leading to impaired normal blood cell function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B-cell ALL">
      <data key="d0">B-cell ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B-cell ALL (Burkitt leukemia) is a rare subtype of ALL that originates from B-cell lymphoblasts, primarily affecting lymph nodes rather than bone marrow.&lt;SEP&gt;B-cell ALL is a subtype of ALL that arises from B-cell lymphocytes, characterized by the proliferation of B-cell lymphoblasts in the bone marrow and lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;B-cell ALL is a subtype of ALL that originates from B-cell lymphocytes and is more common than T-cell ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;B-cell ALL is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia that originates in B lymphocytes, characterized by the overproduction of immature B-cell lymphoblasts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T-cell ALL">
      <data key="d0">T-cell ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T-cell ALL (T-ALL) is a subtype of ALL that arises from T-cell lymphoblasts, often involving the thymus and leading to enlarged thymus complications.&lt;SEP&gt;T-cell ALL is a subtype of ALL that originates from T-cell lymphocytes and can cause an enlarged thymus, leading to breathing difficulties.&lt;SEP&gt;T-cell ALL is a subtype of ALL that originates from T-cell lymphocytes, often causing an enlarged thymus and potential respiratory complications.&lt;SEP&gt;T-cell ALL is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia that originates in T lymphocytes, marked by the proliferation of immature T-cell lymphoblasts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pediatric">
      <data key="d0">Pediatric</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pediatric refers to ALL cases in infants, children, and young adults under 18 years of age, as well as certain adolescents and young adults (AYAs).&lt;SEP&gt;Pediatric refers to ALL cases in infants, children, and young adults under 18 years of age, including certain adolescents and young adults (AYAs) in treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Pediatric refers to ALL cases in infants, children, and young adults under 18 years old, with specialized treatment approaches compared to adult cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AYA">
      <data key="d0">AYA</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AYA (Adolescents and Young Adults) refers to individuals aged 15 to 39 years at the time of initial cancer diagnosis, who may be treated in either pediatric or adult centers.&lt;SEP&gt;AYA (Adolescents and Young Adults) refers to individuals aged 15 to 39 years diagnosed with ALL, who may receive treatment in either pediatric or adult care settings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Comprehensive Cancer Network® (NCCN®)">
      <data key="d0">National Comprehensive Cancer Network® (NCCN®)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is an organization that provides guidelines and resources for cancer treatment, including information on acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL).&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is an organization that provides guidelines, educational resources, and support for cancer treatment, including information on acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL).&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is an organization that provides guidelines, resources, and expertise for cancer treatment and patient care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALL">
      <data key="d0">ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ALL (Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia) is a fast-growing cancer that originates in lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell, characterized by the overproduction of immature lymphoblasts that crowd out normal blood cell production.&lt;SEP&gt;ALL (Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia) is a type of cancer affecting lymphoid progenitor cells, leading to the overproduction of lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rectal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Rectal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Rectal Cancer is a type of cancer originating in the rectum, often treated with transabdominal surgery, chemotherapy, or chemoradiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Rectal cancer is a malignant tumor that originates in the rectum, often starting as a polyp like an adenoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Rectal cancer is a type of cancer originating in the rectum, often requiring staging procedures like MRI and CT scans to assess tumor depth, lymph node involvement, and metastasis to distant organs.&lt;SEP&gt;Rectal cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the rectum, part of the large intestine, and can be cured if detected early.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B-ALL">
      <data key="d0">B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B-ALL (B-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia) is a subtype of ALL that originates in B-cell lymphocytes and is the most common type of ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;B-ALL (B-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia) is a subtype of ALL that originates in B-cell lymphocytes, more common than T-ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T-ALL">
      <data key="d0">T-ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T-ALL (T-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia) is a less common ALL subtype with treatment options including chemotherapy and clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;T-ALL (T-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia) is a subtype of ALL that originates in T-cell lymphocytes and is less common than B-ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;T-ALL (T-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia) is a subtype of ALL that originates in T-cell lymphocytes, less common than B-ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;T-None (T-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia) is a subtype of ALL that originates in T-cell lymphocytes and is less common than B-ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing for ALL">
      <data key="d0">Testing for ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing for ALL involves diagnostic procedures to confirm the presence of the disease and determine treatment plans.&lt;SEP&gt;Testing for ALL provides an overview of diagnostic tests, fertility impacts, and genetic mutation testing for Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Infant ALL Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Infant ALL Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Infant ALL treatment is a specialized approach for children under 12 months of age, differing from other age groups.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL Treatment">
      <data key="d0">BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL treatment targets the BCR::ABL protein caused by the BCR::ABL1 gene, using systemic therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL Treatment">
      <data key="d0">BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL treatment focuses on managing these specific subtypes of B-ALL through clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hyperplastic Polyps">
      <data key="d0">Hyperplastic Polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hyperplastic polyps are non-cancerous growths in the colon and rectum that rarely develop into cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inflammatory Polyps">
      <data key="d0">Inflammatory Polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Inflammatory polyps are non-cancerous growths in the colon and rectum that rarely turn into cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mismatch Repair (MMR) Genes">
      <data key="d0">Mismatch Repair (MMR) Genes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mismatch repair genes are responsible for correcting DNA damage; mutations in these genes are associated with Lynch syndrome and certain types of rectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Microsatellite Instability-High (MSI-H)">
      <data key="d0">Microsatellite Instability-High (MSI-H)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MSI-H is a biomarker indicating defects in mismatch repair genes, which can influence treatment strategies for rectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Microsatellite Instability-High (MSI-H) is a genetic feature that can be targeted with specific therapies for thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Microsatellite Stable (MSS)">
      <data key="d0">Microsatellite Stable (MSS)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MSS refers to tumors with normal mismatch repair gene function, distinguishing them from MSI-H tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mismatch Repair Proficient (pMMR)">
      <data key="d0">Mismatch Repair Proficient (pMMR)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">pMMR describes tumors with intact mismatch repair gene function, contrasting with MSI-H and dMMR tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carcinoembryonic Antigen (CEA)">
      <data key="d0">Carcinoembryonic Antigen (CEA)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CEA is a protein biomarker often elevated in colorectal cancer, used to monitor treatment response and disease progression.&lt;SEP&gt;CEA is a protein found in blood that may be elevated in colorectal cancer, used for monitoring treatment response and recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Circulating Tumor DNA (ctDNA)">
      <data key="d0">Circulating Tumor DNA (ctDNA)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ctDNA is DNA released by tumor cells into the bloodstream, used in liquid biopsy tests to detect residual cancer cells after treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;ctDNA is a biomarker derived from cancer cells in the bloodstream, used to detect and monitor cancer progression and treatment efficacy.&lt;SEP&gt;ctDNA is a biomarker derived from cancer cells in the bloodstream, used to detect and monitor cancer progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liquid Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Liquid Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Liquid Biopsy is a non-invasive diagnostic method that analyzes blood samples to detect cancer cells, often used for monitoring metastasis in prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Liquid biopsy is a non-invasive diagnostic method analyzing circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) in blood to assess cancer presence and treatment response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CT">
      <data key="d0">CT</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CT (Computed Tomography) is a diagnostic imaging method that uses radiation to detect cancer spread to organs like the liver, lungs, or other areas, typically involving contrast agents for clearer images.&lt;SEP&gt;CT (Computed Tomography) is a diagnostic imaging method using radiation to detect cancer metastasis to organs like the liver, lungs, or other areas, typically involving contrast agents for clearer images.&lt;SEP&gt;CT (Computed Tomography) is an imaging technique that uses x-rays to create detailed cross-sectional images of the body, often used as an alternative to MRI when necessary.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endorectal Ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">Endorectal Ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Endorectal ultrasound is a procedure using sound waves to assess rectal cancer extent in the pelvis, guiding biopsies of lymph nodes or surrounding tissues and aiding in local staging.&lt;SEP&gt;Endorectal ultrasound is a procedure using sound waves to evaluate rectal cancer extent, involving a probe inserted into the rectum to create images of pelvic structures and guide biopsies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="dMMR">
      <data key="d0">dMMR</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">dMMR (DNA Mismatch Repair Deficiency) is a biomarker indicating defective DNA repair mechanisms in cancer cells, associated with Lynch syndrome and specific rectal cancer subtypes responsive to targeted therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;dMMR (DNA Mismatch Repair Deficiency) is a biomarker indicating defective DNA repair mechanisms, linked to Lynch syndrome and specific rectal cancer subtypes requiring targeted therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MSI-H">
      <data key="d0">MSI-H</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MSI-H (Microsatellite Instability-High) is a biomarker reflecting DNA repair defects in cancer cells, linked to Lynch syndrome and potential responsiveness to immunotherapy in rectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;MSI-H (Microsatellite Instability-High) is a biomarker reflecting DNA repair defects, associated with Lynch syndrome and potential responsiveness to immunotherapy in rectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Premature Ovarian Insufficiency (POI)">
      <data key="d0">Premature Ovarian Insufficiency (POI)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Premature Ovarian Insufficiency is a condition where ovaries lose function before age 40, often caused by radiation therapy, leading to infertility and hormonal imbalances in female cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Premature Ovarian Insufficiency is a condition where ovaries stop functioning normally before age 40, often caused by radiation therapy, leading to infertility and hormonal imbalances.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation-Induced Premature Menopause">
      <data key="d0">Radiation-Induced Premature Menopause</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiation-Induced Premature Menopause is a side effect of rectal cancer radiation therapy, causing ovarian hormone production to cease and impacting fertility and reproductive health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sperm Banking">
      <data key="d0">Sperm Banking</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sperm Banking is a fertility preservation method involving freezing and storing sperm for future use after cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Sperm Banking is a fertility preservation technique involving cryopreservation of semen for future reproductive use.&lt;SEP&gt;Sperm banking is a fertility preservation method involving semen cryopreservation (freezing) for later use, enabling future conception after cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Sperm banking is a fertility preservation method where semen is frozen using cryopreservation to enable future reproduction for cancer patients undergoing treatments that may affect fertility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Egg Freezing">
      <data key="d0">Egg Freezing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Egg freezing (oocyte cryopreservation) involves retrieving and freezing unfertilized eggs to preserve fertility for potential future use in cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Egg freezing (oocyte cryopreservation) is a procedure to preserve unfertilized eggs by freezing them, allowing for future use in assisted reproductive technologies.&lt;SEP&gt;Egg freezing (oocyte preservation) is a fertility preservation method involving the removal, freezing, and storage of unfertilized eggs for future use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Transposition">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Transposition</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian Transposition is a surgical procedure to reposition ovaries out of the radiation field, preserving fertility and hormonal function in premenopausal patients with cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian Transposition is a surgical procedure to reposition ovaries outside the radiation field, preserving fertility and hormonal function in premenopausal patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian transposition (oophoropexy) is a surgical procedure to relocate ovaries out of the radiation beam's path, preserving fertility in premenopausal patients with cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian transposition (oophoropexy) is a surgical procedure to reposition ovaries outside the radiation beam's path, preserving fertility and hormonal function during rectal cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-metastatic cancer">
      <data key="d0">Non-metastatic cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-metastatic cancer refers to cancer that has not spread beyond the rectum, requiring localized treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Early rectal cancer">
      <data key="d0">Early rectal cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Early rectal cancer is cancer detected at an early stage, often treatable with minimally invasive procedures like endoscopic submucosal dissection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Locally advanced cancer">
      <data key="d0">Locally advanced cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Locally advanced cancer refers to cancer that has progressed within the rectum but not to distant organs, necessitating surgical or combined treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Polyps with cancer">
      <data key="d0">Polyps with cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Polyps with cancer are growths on the rectal lining that may develop into invasive cancer, often detected via colonoscopy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pedunculated polyps">
      <data key="d0">Pedunculated polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pedunculated polyps are mushroom-shaped growths on the rectal wall, often removable via polypectomy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sessile polyps">
      <data key="d0">Sessile polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sessile polyps are flat, stalkless growths on the rectal wall, more likely to recur and requiring advanced imaging for evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Malignant (cancerous) polyp">
      <data key="d0">Malignant (cancerous) polyp</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A malignant polyp is a precancerous growth that has developed into cancer, requiring surgical removal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endoscopic submucosal dissection (ESD)">
      <data key="d0">Endoscopic submucosal dissection (ESD)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ESD is a minimally invasive procedure to remove small rectal cancers confined to the mucosal layer, often curing the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endoscopic mucosal resection (EMR)">
      <data key="d0">Endoscopic mucosal resection (EMR)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">EMR is a procedure used to remove early-stage rectal cancers, similar to ESD but with different technical approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transanal local excision">
      <data key="d0">Transanal local excision</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Transanal local excision involves removing rectal tumors through the anus, suitable for low-risk T1 tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transanal endoscopic microsurgery (TEM)">
      <data key="d0">Transanal endoscopic microsurgery (TEM)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">TEM is an advanced technique for removing rectal tumors higher in the rectum, using endoscopic tools for precision.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transanal minimally invasive surgery (TAMIS)">
      <data key="d0">Transanal minimally invasive surgery (TAMIS)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">TAMIS is a minimally invasive approach for excising rectal tumors, offering reduced recovery times compared to traditional surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transabdominal surgery">
      <data key="d0">Transabdominal surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Transabdominal surgery is a more extensive procedure for removing rectal tumors that have progressed beyond localized stages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage I">
      <data key="d0">Stage I</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage I is a classification of cancer staging indicating the tumor has grown into the second or third (muscle) layer of the rectal wall, requiring no further treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage II">
      <data key="d0">Stage II</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage II is a classification of cancer staging indicating the tumor has grown through the rectal wall and possibly into nearby tissues or organs, requiring more treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage III">
      <data key="d0">Stage III</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage III is a classification of cancer staging indicating cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes, requiring almost always more treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kidney Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Kidney Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Kidney Cancer is a broad category of cancers affecting the kidneys, with Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC) being the most common type.&lt;SEP&gt;Kidney Cancer is a broad category of cancers that affect the kidneys, with Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC) being the most common type.&lt;SEP&gt;Kidney Cancer is a type of cancer originating in the kidneys, with RCC (Renal Cell Carcinoma) as a common subtype, and information provided by the World Health Organization.&lt;SEP&gt;Kidney Cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the kidneys, involving the tissues of these organs and classified under renal cell carcinoma (RCC) and other subtypes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transabdominal Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Transabdominal Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Transabdominal Surgery is a surgical category involving abdominal incisions to remove rectal tumors, lymph nodes, and surrounding tissue, with variations like Low Anterior Resection (LAR) and Abdominoperineal Resection (APR).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colostomy">
      <data key="d0">Colostomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Colostomy is a procedure creating an artificial opening for stool elimination after APR, resulting in a permanent stoma on the abdomen.&lt;SEP&gt;Colostomy is a surgical procedure creating an opening (stoma) on the abdomen for stool to exit, often temporary or permanent after rectal surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;Colostomy is a surgical procedure that creates an opening in the abdomen to divert stool, often required after the removal of the anus in anal cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Colostomy is a surgical procedure that creates an opening in the abdomen to divert stool, often used when reconnecting the colon is not feasible after cancer removal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colorectal Anastomosis">
      <data key="d0">Colorectal Anastomosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Colorectal Anastomosis is a surgical procedure reconnecting the colon to the remaining rectum, allowing near-normal bowel movements after transabdominal surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colonoanal Anastomosis">
      <data key="d0">Colonoanal Anastomosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Colonoanal Anastomosis is a surgical procedure connecting the colon directly to the anus, used when colorectal anastomosis is not feasible.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Abdominoperineal Resection (APR)">
      <data key="d0">Abdominoperineal Resection (APR)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">APR is a surgical procedure involving the removal of the anus, rectum, and surrounding tissues, often used for metastatic anal cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Abdominoperineal Resection (APR) is a surgical category involving removal of the rectum, anus, and surrounding tissue, requiring a permanent colostomy for stool diversion.&lt;SEP&gt;Abdominoperineal Resection (APR) is a surgical procedure for anal cancer that involves removing the anus, rectum, and surrounding tissues, often followed by a permanent colostomy.&lt;SEP&gt;Abdominoperineal resection (APR) is a radical surgical procedure to remove the anus and surrounding tissues in cases of locally progressive or recurrent anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oxaliplatin">
      <data key="d0">Oxaliplatin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Oxaliplatin is a chemotherapy drug used in rectal cancer treatment, known to cause nerve damage (neuropathy) in fingers and toes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Irinotecan">
      <data key="d0">Irinotecan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Irinotecan is a chemotherapy drug used in rectal cancer treatment, associated with side effects like abdominal cramping, nausea, diarrhea, and hair loss.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RCC (Renal Cell Carcinoma)">
      <data key="d0">RCC (Renal Cell Carcinoma)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">RCC (Renal Cell Carcinoma) is a subtype of kidney cancer originating in the kidneys, with the World Health Organization providing information on its spread and management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RCC">
      <data key="d0">RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">RCC (Renal Cell Carcinoma) is the most common type of kidney cancer, characterized by tumors originating in the renal tubules and often referred to as kidney cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Renal Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC) is a specific subtype of kidney cancer, with clear cell RCC being the most prevalent form, and non-clear cell RCC encompassing other variations.&lt;SEP&gt;Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC) is the standard name for RCC, reflecting the same condition, with clear cell RCC being the most prevalent form.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urothelial Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Urothelial Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urothelial Carcinoma is a distinct type of kidney cancer that originates in the cells lining the renal pelvis and ureter, sharing features with bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wilms Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Wilms Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Wilms Tumor (nephroblastoma) is a rare kidney cancer typically occurring in young children, involving abnormal growth of kidney cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Sarcoma">
      <data key="d0">Renal Sarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Renal Sarcoma is a rare kidney cancer that arises in the blood vessels or connective tissue of the kidney, requiring specialized treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)">
      <data key="d0">Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC) is the most common type of kidney cancer, characterized by tumors in the renal tubules, and includes various subtypes such as Clear Cell RCC and Non-Clear Cell RCC.&lt;SEP&gt;Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC) is the most common type of kidney cancer, originating in renal tubules and classified into subtypes like Clear Cell RCC and Non-Clear Cell RCC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clear Cell RCC">
      <data key="d0">Clear Cell RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Clear Cell RCC is the most common RCC subtype, characterized by pale cells under a microscope and accounting for ~70% of RCC cases.&lt;SEP&gt;Clear Cell RCC is the most common subtype of RCC, occurring in about 7 out of 10 people with RCC, and is identified by its pale, clear appearance under a microscope.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Clear Cell RCC">
      <data key="d0">Non-Clear Cell RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-Clear Cell RCC is a broad category of RCC subtypes that includes types like Papillary RCC, Chromophobe RCC, and Translocation RCC, which are less common than Clear Cell RCC.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-Clear Cell RCC is a heterogeneous group of RCC subtypes, including Papillary RCC, Chromophobe RCC, and Translocation RCC, collectively comprising ~30% of RCC cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Papillary RCC">
      <data key="d0">Papillary RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Papillary RCC is the most common subtype of Non-Clear Cell RCC, characterized by long, thin finger-like growths under a microscope and accounting for about 1 out of 10 RCC cases.&lt;SEP&gt;Papillary RCC is the most common subtype of Non-Clear Cell RCC, featuring finger-like growths under a microscope and representing ~10% of RCC cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chromophobe RCC">
      <data key="d0">Chromophobe RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chromophobe RCC is a distinct Non-Clear Cell RCC subtype with larger pale cells differing from Clear Cell RCC under microscopic examination.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromophobe RCC is a subtype of Non-Clear Cell RCC, distinguished by pale, larger cells that differ from Clear Cell RCC under microscopic examination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Translocation RCC">
      <data key="d0">Translocation RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Translocation RCC is a rare Non-Clear Cell RCC subtype, often diagnosed in children or young adults, characterized by unique gene fusions promoting cancer growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Translocation RCC is a rare subtype of Non-Clear Cell RCC, often diagnosed in children or younger adults, characterized by unique gene fusions that promote cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Unclassified RCC">
      <data key="d0">Unclassified RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Unclassified RCC describes cancers that do not fit into any specific subtype or contain multiple subtypes, making classification challenging.&lt;SEP&gt;Unclassified RCC refers to tumors that do not fit into specific subtypes or contain multiple subtypes, complicating classification.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Collecting Duct RCC">
      <data key="d0">Collecting Duct RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Collecting Duct RCC is a rare Non-Clear Cell RCC subtype forming in kidney collecting ducts, often linked to genetic factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Collecting Duct RCC is a rare subtype of Non-Clear Cell RCC that forms in the collecting ducts or tubules of the kidney.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Medullary Carcinoma (RMC)">
      <data key="d0">Renal Medullary Carcinoma (RMC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Renal Medullary Carcinoma (RMC) is a rare Non-Clear Cell RCC subtype associated with sickle cell traits and found in young African descent individuals.&lt;SEP&gt;Renal Medullary Carcinoma (RMC) is a rare subtype of Non-Clear Cell RCC often found in young people of African descent, associated with genetic factors and sickle cell traits.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clear Cell Papillary Renal Cell Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Clear Cell Papillary Renal Cell Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Clear Cell Papillary Renal Cell Tumor is a rare hybrid subtype combining features of Clear Cell and Papillary RCC.&lt;SEP&gt;Clear Cell Papillary Renal Cell Tumor is a rare subtype of Non-Clear Cell RCC, combining features of Clear Cell and Papillary RCC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fumarate Hydratase-Deficient RCC">
      <data key="d0">Fumarate Hydratase-Deficient RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fumarate Hydratase-Deficient RCC is a rare Non-Clear Cell RCC subtype linked to mutations in the Fumarate Hydratase gene.&lt;SEP&gt;Fumarate Hydratase-Deficient RCC is a rare subtype of Non-Clear Cell RCC characterized by mutations in the Fumarate Hydratase gene, leading to specific metabolic defects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ELOC (formerly TCEB1)-Mutated RCC">
      <data key="d0">ELOC (formerly TCEB1)-Mutated RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ELOC (formerly TCEB1)-Mutated RCC is a rare Non-Clear Cell RCC subtype associated with TCEB1 gene mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;ELOC (formerly TCEB1)-Mutated RCC is a rare subtype of Non-Clear Cell RCC associated with mutations in the TCEB1 gene, contributing to tumor development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sarcomatoid Features">
      <data key="d0">Sarcomatoid Features</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sarcomatoid Features refer to sarcoma-like cells in RCC, indicating aggressive behavior and potential for rapid growth and metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;Sarcomatoid Features refer to the presence of sarcomatoid (sarcoma-like) cells in RCC, which can occur in any RCC subtype and indicate more aggressive tumor behavior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Histology">
      <data key="d0">Histology</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Histology is a classification method for gliomas based on the microscopic appearance of tumor cells, such as astrocytoma or oligodendroglioma.&lt;SEP&gt;Histology is the microscopic analysis of tissue samples, critical for diagnosing RCC subtypes and guiding treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastasis">
      <data key="d0">Metastasis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastasis is a category describing the spread of cancer from its primary site to distant organs such as the lung or bone.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis is a category describing the spread of cancer to distant organs or tissues, a critical factor in determining cancer stages and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis is the process by which cancer cells spread from the primary tumor to distant organs or tissues, often leading to more severe disease progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis is the process by which cancer spreads from the kidney to other parts of the body via blood or lymphatic systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis is the spread of cancer cells from the primary tumor to distant organs, a critical factor in determining cancer stages and treatment plans.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis refers to the spread of cancer from the prostate to other parts of the body, such as bones or lymph nodes, and is a critical factor in staging and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis refers to the spread of cancer to distant organs or bones, which is evaluated in the TNM system's M classification to determine cancer stage.&lt;SEP&gt;The spread of cancer from its primary site to other parts of the body, such as lymph nodes, bones, or internal organs, often requiring biopsy and genetic analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;The spread of cancer to distant organs or tissues, often requiring biopsy to confirm and guide treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Tubules">
      <data key="d0">Renal Tubules</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Renal Tubules are tiny kidney structures where Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC) originates, responsible for filtering blood and producing urine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood">
      <data key="d0">Blood</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood is a tissue composed of plasma, red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, playing a critical role in immune function and oxygen transport.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood is a tissue that functions to transport oxygen and nutrients throughout the body, carry away waste, and support the immune system. It consists of plasma, red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood is a vital tissue composed of plasma, red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, responsible for transporting oxygen, nutrients, and waste, as well as supporting the immune system and clotting.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood is a vital tissue composed of plasma, red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, responsible for transporting oxygen, nutrients, and waste, as well as supporting the immune system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Red Blood Cells (RBCs)">
      <data key="d0">Red Blood Cells (RBCs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Red blood cells (RBCs) are blood cells responsible for carrying oxygen from the lungs to tissues and removing carbon dioxide from the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Red blood cells (RBCs) are responsible for carrying oxygen throughout the body and are produced in bone marrow from hematopoietic stem cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Platelets (PLTs)">
      <data key="d0">Platelets (PLTs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Platelets (PLTs) are a category of blood cells that form clots to prevent or stop bleeding.&lt;SEP&gt;Platelets (PLTs) are cell fragments in the blood that play a critical role in clotting and controlling bleeding.&lt;SEP&gt;Platelets (PLTs) are cell fragments that help control bleeding by forming clots and are produced in bone marrow from megakaryocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Burkitt Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Burkitt Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Burkitt leukemia is a rare subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) that shares similarities with Burkitt lymphoma but is treated differently due to its distinct clinical presentation.&lt;SEP&gt;Burkitt leukemia is a rare, rapidly growing subtype of B-cell ALL, associated with genetic abnormalities and aggressive tumor growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Burkitt Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Burkitt Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Burkitt lymphoma is a type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma characterized by rapid growth of B-cell lymphomas, often associated with genetic mutations and Epstein-Barr virus infections.&lt;SEP&gt;Burkitt lymphoma is a type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma that shares similarities with B-cell ALL but is treated differently due to its distinct biological characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adolescent and Young Adult (AYA)">
      <data key="d0">Adolescent and Young Adult (AYA)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adolescent and young adult (AYA) refers to individuals aged 15 to 39 at the time of cancer diagnosis, a unique group requiring specialized care and treatment protocols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acute Myeloid Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Acute Myeloid Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of blood cancer that originates in the stem cells of bone marrow, leading to the accumulation of immature white blood cells called blasts and impairing normal blood cell production.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer originating in the bone marrow, characterized by uncontrolled white blood cell growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer that originates in the bone marrow and involves the uncontrolled growth of abnormal white blood cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia (APL) is a rare subtype of AML characterized by a specific genetic fusion gene (PML::RARA) and requires targeted treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blastic Plasmacytoid Dendritic Cell Neoplasm">
      <data key="d0">Blastic Plasmacytoid Dendritic Cell Neoplasm</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blastic Plasmacytoid Dendritic Cell Neoplasm (BPDCN) is an aggressive subtype of AML that originates in blood, bone marrow, lymph nodes, or skin and involves abnormal plasmacytoid dendritic cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blasts">
      <data key="d0">Blasts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blasts are a category of immature blood cells that may indicate leukemia and are monitored in blood tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Blasts are immature white blood cells that are central to AML pathology. Abnormal myeloblasts accumulate in the bone marrow and blood, disrupting normal blood cell production.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS)">
      <data key="d0">Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS) is a category representing a scale used to evaluate a patient's general health and ability to perform daily tasks.&lt;SEP&gt;The Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS) is a category representing a scale used to measure a patient's overall health and functional status, often used alongside the ECOG scale in treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;The Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS) is a category that measures a patient's general health and ability to perform daily tasks, used alongside the ECOG scale in treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Comprehensive metabolic panel (CMP)">
      <data key="d0">Comprehensive metabolic panel (CMP)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Comprehensive metabolic panel (CMP) is a category of blood tests that measure substances in the blood to assess kidney and liver function.&lt;SEP&gt;The comprehensive metabolic panel (CMP) is a category of blood test that evaluates kidney and liver function, as well as calcium levels, to detect hypercalcemia.&lt;SEP&gt;The comprehensive metabolic panel (CMP) is a category of blood test that evaluates kidney and liver function, electrolyte levels, and other metabolic indicators, including calcium and alkaline phosphatase.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood clotting tests">
      <data key="d0">Blood clotting tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood clotting tests are a category of medical tests that evaluate the body's ability to form blood clots, essential for preventing excessive bleeding.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liver function tests (LFTs)">
      <data key="d0">Liver function tests (LFTs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Liver function tests (LFTs) are a category of blood tests that assess the health and function of the liver.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HLA typing">
      <data key="d0">HLA typing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HLA typing is a category of blood tests that determine a person's HLA type, crucial for matching donors in hematopoietic cell transplants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Performance status (PS)">
      <data key="d0">Performance status (PS)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Performance status (PS) is a category that measures a patient's general fitness and ability to perform daily tasks, influencing treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone marrow tests">
      <data key="d0">Bone marrow tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone marrow tests are a category of medical tests used to diagnose leukemia and assess bone marrow function.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow tests, including aspirate and biopsy, are used to evaluate bone marrow health and detect abnormalities in cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Coagulopathy">
      <data key="d0">Coagulopathy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Coagulopathy is a category of disorders characterized by impaired blood clotting, commonly associated with leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Differential">
      <data key="d0">Differential</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Differential is a category of blood tests that analyze white blood cell counts and their balance, aiding in diagnosing conditions like leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Differential refers to the analysis of white blood cell types in a CBC, helping identify abnormal cell populations in CLL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CBC measurements">
      <data key="d0">CBC measurements</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CBC measurements are a category of blood tests that assess hemoglobin, platelets, absolute neutrophil count (ANC), and blasts to evaluate blood health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hemoglobin (Hgb)">
      <data key="d0">Hemoglobin (Hgb)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hemoglobin (Hgb) is a category of blood measurement that indicates oxygen-carrying capacity and the need for red blood cell transfusions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Absolute Neutrophil Count (ANC)">
      <data key="d0">Absolute Neutrophil Count (ANC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Absolute Neutrophil Count (ANC) is a category of blood measurement that assesses the body's ability to fight bacterial infections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lactate Dehydrogenase (LDH)">
      <data key="d0">Lactate Dehydrogenase (LDH)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lactate Dehydrogenase (LDH) is a category of enzyme that is released by dying cells, including tumor cells, and is measured in blood tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN)">
      <data key="d0">Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN) is a category of waste product filtered by the kidneys, with elevated levels indicating impaired kidney function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Creatinine">
      <data key="d0">Creatinine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Creatinine is a category of waste product produced in muscles, filtered by the kidneys, and used to assess kidney function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electrolytes">
      <data key="d0">Electrolytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Electrolytes are a category of ions or particles with electrical charges that regulate fluid balance and muscle function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Iron">
      <data key="d0">Iron</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Iron is a category of mineral essential for hemoglobin production, with deficiencies or excesses impacting blood health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Iron deficiency">
      <data key="d0">Iron deficiency</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Iron deficiency is a category of condition characterized by insufficient iron levels, leading to anemia and fatigue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Iron overload">
      <data key="d0">Iron overload</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Iron overload is a category of condition marked by excessive iron accumulation, potentially damaging organs like the liver.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Coagulation panel">
      <data key="d0">Coagulation panel</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Coagulation panel is a category of blood tests that evaluate clotting factors and diagnose coagulation disorders.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC)">
      <data key="d0">Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC) is a category of severe coagulation disorder involving widespread clot formation and bleeding.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Allogeneic hematopoietic cell transplant (HCT)">
      <data key="d0">Allogeneic hematopoietic cell transplant (HCT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Allogeneic hematopoietic cell transplant (HCT) is a category of treatment involving stem cell transplantation from a donor to treat blood disorders.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility preservation">
      <data key="d0">Fertility preservation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility preservation is a category of medical strategy to maintain reproductive options during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group">
      <data key="d0">Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) is an organization that defines the ECOG Performance Status (PS) scale, which assesses a patient's general health and activity level.&lt;SEP&gt;The Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) is an organization that develops a PS scale used to assess the general health of patients with cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) is an organization that develops the PS scale used to assess patient health in cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Karnofsky Performance Status">
      <data key="d0">Karnofsky Performance Status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS) is a scale used to evaluate a patient's functional status and overall health in cancer contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;The Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS) is a scale used to evaluate a patient's functional status and overall health in the context of cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Aspirate">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Aspirate</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone marrow aspirate is a medical procedure where liquid bone marrow is extracted using a hollow needle to analyze cells for diagnostic purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow aspirate is a procedure extracting liquid marrow for diagnostic analysis of blood cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A bone marrow biopsy involves extracting a core sample of marrow to analyze for abnormal cell proliferation, commonly used in diagnosing CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;A bone marrow biopsy involves extracting marrow samples to check for cancer cell presence, particularly in suspected lymphoma cases.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow biopsy is a medical procedure involving the removal of a small piece of bone marrow tissue to assess cellular abnormalities, often used in diagnosing leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow biopsy is a procedure removing bone tissue samples to diagnose and analyze leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Changes">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Changes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic changes refer to DNA mutations or chromosomal abnormalities critical in leukemia development and classification.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic changes refer to mutations or abnormalities in DNA that contribute to the development and classification of leukemia, often detected through molecular testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Translocation">
      <data key="d0">Translocation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Translocation is a chromosomal abnormality where segments swap between chromosomes, commonly observed in leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Translocation is a type of genetic abnormality where parts of chromosomes break and exchange, commonly observed in leukemia and affecting cell function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Point Mutation">
      <data key="d0">Point Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Point mutation is a genetic change involving a single nucleotide alteration, potentially contributing to leukemia progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Point mutation is a genetic change where a single nucleotide in DNA is altered, potentially leading to the development of leukemia or affecting its progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deletion">
      <data key="d0">Deletion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Deletion is a genetic change where DNA or chromosomal segments are missing, associated with leukemia subtypes.&lt;SEP&gt;Deletion is a genetic change where a segment of DNA or a chromosome is missing, contributing to the pathogenesis of certain leukemia subtypes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Insertion">
      <data key="d0">Insertion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Insertion is a genetic change where an extra segment of DNA is added, potentially disrupting normal cellular function and contributing to leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Insertion is a genetic change where extra DNA segments are added, disrupting normal cellular function in leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inversion">
      <data key="d0">Inversion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Inversion is a chromosomal abnormality where segments are reversed, linked to specific leukemia subtypes.&lt;SEP&gt;Inversion is a genetic abnormality where a segment of a chromosome is reversed, often associated with specific leukemia subtypes and their molecular profiles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Amplification">
      <data key="d0">Amplification</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Amplification is a genetic change where DNA segments are duplicated, increasing protein production in leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Amplification is a genetic change where a portion of a chromosome or gene is duplicated, leading to increased production of specific proteins and contributing to leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gene Rearrangement">
      <data key="d0">Gene Rearrangement</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Gene rearrangement is a chromosomal abnormality involving DNA segment reordering, critical for leukemia classification.&lt;SEP&gt;Gene rearrangement is a genetic abnormality involving the reordering of DNA segments, often observed in leukemia and influencing treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AML Mutation Testing">
      <data key="d0">AML Mutation Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AML mutation testing involves analyzing genetic and chromosomal abnormalities in acute myeloid leukemia (AML) cells using methods like karyotype, FISH, NGS, and PCR to guide treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;AML mutation testing involves methods like karyotype, FISH, NGS, and PCR to identify genetic abnormalities in acute myeloid leukemia (AML) cells, guiding treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Testing Methods">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Testing Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic testing methods encompass techniques such as fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH), karyotype analysis, next-generation sequencing (NGS), and polymerase chain reaction (PCR) to detect chromosomal and genetic abnormalities in leukemia cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FISH">
      <data key="d0">FISH</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FISH (Fluorescence In Situ Hybridization) is a technique using fluorescent probes to identify specific chromosomal abnormalities in leukemia cells, particularly useful for known genetic changes in blood or bone marrow samples.&lt;SEP&gt;FISH (Fluorescence In Situ Hybridization) is a technique using special dyes to detect chromosomal abnormalities in leukemia cells, particularly useful for known genetic changes and applicable to blood or bone marrow samples.&lt;SEP&gt;FISH (Fluorescence in situ hybridization) is a DNA testing method using probes to detect genetic abnormalities in cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH) is a genetic testing category used to detect chromosomal abnormalities, such as the 17p deletion, in CLL cells to guide treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH) is a molecular diagnostic category used to detect genetic abnormalities, such as chromosome deletions, in CLL cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NGS">
      <data key="d0">NGS</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Next-generation sequencing (NGS) is a high-resolution DNA analysis method that identifies mutations affecting gene function, surpassing traditional methods in detecting subtle genetic changes in leukemia-related genes.&lt;SEP&gt;Next-generation sequencing (NGS) is a high-resolution method to analyze DNA sequences, identifying mutations that affect gene function and may be missed by other testing methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PCR">
      <data key="d0">PCR</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PCR (Polymerase Chain Reaction) amplifies DNA or RNA to detect minute genetic abnormalities, enabling sensitive detection of mutations and aiding in treatment response monitoring.&lt;SEP&gt;PCR (Polymerase Chain Reaction) amplifies DNA or RNA to detect minute genetic abnormalities, enabling sensitive identification of mutations and supporting treatment response monitoring in leukemia management.&lt;SEP&gt;PCR (Polymerase Chain Reaction) amplifies DNA to detect mutations, often used to generate samples for next-generation sequencing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hypomethylating Agents">
      <data key="d0">Hypomethylating Agents</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hypomethylating agents (e.g., Azacitidine) reverse DNA methylation, reactivating genes to promote normal cell maturation in leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cytarabine">
      <data key="d0">Cytarabine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cytarab, a key chemotherapy drug, is used in various regimens to treat AML, with dosing tailored to individual patient needs.&lt;SEP&gt;Cytarabine is a chemotherapy drug that may be used as an alternative to high-dose methotrexate in the treatment of primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Cytarabine is a key chemotherapy drug used in various regimens to treat AML, with dosing tailored to individual patient needs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Methotrexate">
      <data key="d0">Methotrexate</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Methotrexate is a chemotherapy drug commonly used in high doses to treat primary CNS lymphoma, though it carries risks of organ damage and requires careful monitoring.&lt;SEP&gt;Methotrexate is a chemotherapy drug commonly used to treat primary CNS lymphoma, inhibiting cancer cell growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Methotrexate is an antimetabolite used in AML treatment, particularly for intrathecal chemotherapy to target the central nervous system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Azacitidine">
      <data key="d0">Azacitidine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Azacitidine is a hypomethylating agent that inhibits DNA methylation, helping to reactivate genes in leukemic cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multidisciplinary Team (MDT)">
      <data key="d0">Multidisciplinary Team (MDT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A multidisciplinary team (MDT) is a group of healthcare professionals from diverse backgrounds collaborating to plan and implement AML treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BCR::ABL1">
      <data key="d0">BCR::ABL1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BCR::ABL1 is a fused gene created by the translocation of chromosome 9 and chromosome 22, which is the genetic abnormality responsible for Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hypomethylating Agents (HMAs)">
      <data key="d0">Hypomethylating Agents (HMAs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hypomethylating agents are a class of drugs that block methyl groups from binding to DNA, reactivating silenced genes to allow leukemic blasts to mature into normal cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Azacitidine (Vidaza)">
      <data key="d0">Azacitidine (Vidaza)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Azacitidine is a hypomethylating agent used in the treatment of leukemia, which works by inhibiting DNA methylation and reactivating tumor suppressor genes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Decitabine (Dacogen)">
      <data key="d0">Decitabine (Dacogen)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Decitabine is a hypomethylating agent that inhibits DNA methylation, helping to reactivate genes that may be silenced in leukemic cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gemtuzumab Ozogamicin (GO)">
      <data key="d0">Gemtuzumab Ozogamicin (GO)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Gemtuzumab ozogamicin is a targeted therapy drug linked to a chemotherapy agent, which binds to CD33 proteins on leukemic blasts and delivers chemotherapy inside the cell.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLT3 Mutations">
      <data key="d0">FLT3 Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FLT3 mutations are genetic abnormalities in the FMS-like tyrosine kinase 3 gene, which are associated with certain subtypes of Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) and influence treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLT3-ITD">
      <data key="d0">FLT3-ITD</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FLT3-ITD (FLT3 Internal Tandem Duplication) is a specific type of FLT3 mutation characterized by a duplicated segment of DNA in the FLT3 gene, commonly found in AML cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Core Binding Factor (CBF)">
      <data key="d0">Core Binding Factor (CBF)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Core Binding Factor is a genetic abnormality involving mutations in the CBFβ-MYH11 gene, which leads to a shortage of mature blood cells and is associated with certain subtypes of AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hematopoietic Stem Cells">
      <data key="d0">Hematopoietic Stem Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hematopoietic stem cells are early blood-forming cells in bone marrow that give rise to all types of blood cells, including red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="White Blood Cells (WBCs)">
      <data key="d0">White Blood Cells (WBCs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">White blood cells (WBCs) are part of the immune system, fighting infections by including granulocytes, monocytes, and lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neutrophils">
      <data key="d0">Neutrophils</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Neutrophils are a type of granulocyte white blood cell that defends the body against bacterial infections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eosinophils">
      <data key="d0">Eosinophils</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Eosinophils are granulocytes involved in immune responses, particularly against parasitic infections and in allergic reactions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Basophils">
      <data key="d0">Basophils</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Basophils are granulocytes that release histamine and other mediators during allergic reactions and immune responses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLT3-TKD">
      <data key="d0">FLT3-TKD</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FLT3-TKD (FLT3 Tyrosine Kinase Domain) is another type of FLT3 mutation involving a substitution in the tyrosine kinase domain, contributing to the development of Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BCR::ABL1 gene">
      <data key="d0">BCR::ABL1 gene</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The BCR::ABL1 gene is an abnormal gene formed when parts of chromosome 9 and chromosome 22 break and trade places, leading to the Philadelphia chromosome and causing uncontrolled blood cell growth in Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Philadelphia chromosome">
      <data key="d0">Philadelphia chromosome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Philadelphia chromosome is a shortened chromosome 22 resulting from the fusion of parts of chromosome 9 and 22, which is a hallmark of CML and directly linked to the BCR::ABL1 gene's activity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing">
      <data key="d0">Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing refers to diagnostic procedures used to identify CML, determine treatment plans, and monitor disease progression through blood tests, bone marrow tests, and genetic analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blast phase CML">
      <data key="d0">Blast phase CML</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blast phase CML (BP-CML) is an advanced form of CML characterized by increased lymphoid or myeloid progenitor cells, affecting treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) is a type of blood cancer that affects lymphoid progenitor cells, leading to the overproduction of abnormal lymphoblasts in the bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adolescents and Young Adults (AYAs)">
      <data key="d0">Adolescents and Young Adults (AYAs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AYAs are patients aged 15 to 39 years diagnosed with ALL, who may receive treatment in pediatric or adult centers depending on their condition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Types of treatments">
      <data key="d0">Types of treatments</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Types of treatments discusses standard therapies like steroids, chemotherapy, and systemic therapies for ALL, along with clinical trial options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Supportive care">
      <data key="d0">Supportive care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Supportive care covers side effects management and care strategies for patients undergoing ALL treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care includes hospice care, pain management, and emotional support for patients with metastatic cancer, aiming to improve comfort and quality of life.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care is a key component of cancer care, focusing on managing symptoms and improving quality of life for patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL">
      <data key="d0">BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL refers to a specific subtype of B-cell ALL requiring clinical trial-based treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL">
      <data key="d0">BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL is a subtype of ALL treated with intensive systemic therapies targeting the BCR::ABL1 gene.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Infant ALL">
      <data key="d0">Infant ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Infant ALL is a distinct ALL type requiring specialized treatment approaches tailored for children under 12 months.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pancreatic cancer">
      <data key="d0">Pancreatic cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pancreatic cancer is a separate malignancy discussed in the text, focusing on its causes, stages, and treatment advancements.&lt;SEP&gt;Pancreatic cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the pancreas, often requiring multidisciplinary approaches for diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stages of Pancreatic Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stages of Pancreatic Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The stages of pancreatic cancer range from stage 0 to stage 4, describing the progression of the disease based on tumor size and spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hereditary Conditions">
      <data key="d0">Hereditary Conditions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hereditary conditions, such as Hereditary Breast-Ovarian Cancer Syndrome (HBOC), Peutz-Jeghers syndrome, Lynch syndrome, and Li-Fraumeni syndrome, increase the risk of pancreatic cancer through genetic mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Options">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Options</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment options for pancreatic cancer include surgery, drug therapy, clinical trials, and supportive care, tailored to the patient's stage and overall health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Tests">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic Tests analyze DNA to identify inherited mutations that may increase cancer risk, particularly for conditions like hereditary prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic Tests are diagnostic procedures used to identify inherited genetic mutations that may increase the risk of developing prostate cancer or influence treatment responses.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic tests identify inherited mutations linked to hereditary conditions that elevate pancreatic cancer risk, such as HBOC and familial melanoma syndrome.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multidisciplinary Team">
      <data key="d0">Multidisciplinary Team</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A multidisciplinary team of specialists collaborates to develop personalized treatment plans for pancreatic cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pancreatic Adenocarcinomas">
      <data key="d0">Pancreatic Adenocarcinomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pancreatic adenocarcinomas originate from exocrine cells in the pancreas and are the primary focus of treatment and research.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hereditary Pancreatitis">
      <data key="d0">Hereditary Pancreatitis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hereditary pancreatitis is a genetic condition that increases the risk of developing pancreatic cancer due to chronic inflammation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate cancer">
      <data key="d0">Prostate cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Prostate cancer is a common malignancy in males, characterized by uncontrolled cell growth in the prostate gland, with varying symptoms and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate cancer is a type of cancer originating in the prostate gland, discussed in detail with its symptoms, causes, risk factors, and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the prostate gland and is discussed in detail, including its symptoms, causes, and risk factors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multidisciplinary consult">
      <data key="d0">Multidisciplinary consult</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A multidisciplinary consult is a collaborative meeting involving specialists to evaluate and plan treatment for complex conditions like pancreatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Consult meeting">
      <data key="d0">Consult meeting</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A consult meeting is a structured discussion among healthcare providers to review a patient's case, tests, and treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic radiologist">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Diagnostic radiologist is a medical professional specializing in interpreting imaging tests to diagnose and monitor diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Interventional radiologist">
      <data key="d0">Interventional radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An Interventional radiologist performs minimally invasive procedures using imaging guidance to treat various medical conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;An interventional radiologist is a medical professional who performs minimally invasive procedures, such as core needle biopsies, using imaging guidance to diagnose and treat conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gastroenterologist">
      <data key="d0">Gastroenterologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Gastroenterologist specializes in diagnosing and treating disorders of the digestive system, including the pancreas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Medical oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Medical oncologist specializes in cancer treatment using chemotherapy, immunotherapy, and other systemic therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Surgical oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Surgical oncologist focuses on surgically removing cancerous tumors and managing cancer-related surgical interventions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Radiation oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Radiation oncologist uses radiation therapy to treat cancer, targeting malignant cells while minimizing damage to healthy tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Geriatrician">
      <data key="d0">Geriatrician</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Geriatrician specializes in the care of elderly patients, addressing age-related health conditions and treatment complexities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Registered dietitian">
      <data key="d0">Registered dietitian</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Registered dietitian provides nutritional guidance and support to patients, particularly those with cancer-related dietary needs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic counselor">
      <data key="d0">Genetic counselor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Genetic counselor helps patients understand genetic risks, inheritance patterns, and the implications of genetic mutations for cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Palliative care provider">
      <data key="d0">Palliative care provider</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Palliative care provider focuses on relieving symptoms and improving quality of life for patients with serious illnesses like cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mental health provider">
      <data key="d0">Mental health provider</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Mental health provider offers psychological and emotional support to patients and families dealing with cancer diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jaundice">
      <data key="d0">Jaundice</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Jaundice is a condition characterized by yellowing of the skin and eyes, often caused by liver or pancreatic dysfunction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH)">
      <data key="d0">Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) is a non-cancerous enlargement of the prostate gland, often confused with prostate cancer due to similar symptoms.&lt;SEP&gt;Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) is a non-cancerous enlargement of the prostate gland, which can cause urinary symptoms similar to prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood tests">
      <data key="d0">Blood tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood tests are a category of diagnostic procedures that assess organ function, disease markers, and blood composition, including complete blood counts (CBC) and comprehensive metabolic panels (CMP).&lt;SEP&gt;Blood tests are a category of diagnostic procedures that check for signs of disease and organ function, including complete blood counts (CBC) and comprehensive metabolic panels (CMP).&lt;SEP&gt;Blood tests are diagnostic procedures used to analyze blood samples for indicators of disease, including cancer markers and organ function.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood tests are diagnostic procedures used to analyze blood samples for signs of cancer or related conditions, such as H. pylori infection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic tests for inherited mutations">
      <data key="d0">Genetic tests for inherited mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic tests for inherited mutations identify genetic predispositions to cancer, aiding in risk assessment and personalized treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biomarker tests for profiling">
      <data key="d0">Biomarker tests for profiling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biomarker tests for profiling analyze biological markers in tissues or blood to characterize cancer characteristics and guide targeted therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endoscopic tests">
      <data key="d0">Endoscopic tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Endoscopic tests involve using flexible tubes with cameras to examine internal organs, often used to detect and biopsy pancreatic abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multidisciplinary team">
      <data key="d0">Multidisciplinary team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A multidisciplinary team is a collaborative group of healthcare professionals from various specialties working together to provide comprehensive cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of cancer originating in the prostate gland, characterized by its association with genetic, racial, and lifestyle factors, and its progression from early-stage to advanced-stage forms.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of cancer originating in the prostate gland, characterized by uncontrolled cell growth, and detectable through biopsies, imaging, and genetic testing.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of cancer originating in the prostate gland, which may metastasize to lymph nodes, bones, or internal organs and is often managed with biopsies and genetic testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate Cancer is a malignancy of the prostate gland, often detected through PSA testing, digital rectal exams, and imaging.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate Cancer is a type of cancer that affects the prostate gland and is the central focus of the text, discussed in terms of diagnosis, treatment, and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate Cancer is a type of cancer that develops in the prostate gland. Risk assessment involves factors like PSA level, Gleason score, Grade Group, and biopsy results to determine treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate Cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the prostate gland and is discussed in terms of its causes, risk factors, stages, and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate Cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the prostate gland and is often associated with germline mutations and biomarker testing for risk assessment and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate Cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the prostate gland, characterized by uncontrolled cell growth and the potential to spread to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate cancer is a type of cancer affecting the prostate gland, often detected and monitored using various imaging and biopsy techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate cancer is a type of cancer originating in the prostate gland, characterized by uncontrolled cell growth and the potential for metastasis to other body parts.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate cancer is a type of cancer originating in the prostate gland, discussed in the context of diagnostic imaging, treatment planning, and monitoring for recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the prostate gland and is often managed with hormone therapies such as androgen deprivation therapy (ADT).&lt;SEP&gt;Prostate cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the prostate gland, characterized by uncontrolled cell growth and the potential for metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Early-Stage Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Early-Stage Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer confined to the prostate gland, growing slowly and remaining localized, often termed 'localized prostate cancer' or 'early-stage cancer'.&lt;SEP&gt;Early-Stage Prostate Cancer refers to cancer that has not spread beyond the prostate gland and is often slow-growing and localized.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advanced-Stage Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Advanced-Stage Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Advanced-Stage Prostate Cancer describes cancer that has spread outside the prostate to other areas of the body, such as lymph nodes, bones, or organs.&lt;SEP&gt;Advanced-Stage Prostate Cancer refers to cancer that has metastasized beyond the prostate gland to other areas of the body, requiring specialized treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer that has metastasized beyond the prostate to distant organs or lymph nodes, characterized by rapid progression and aggressive behavior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Aggressive Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Aggressive Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Aggressive Prostate Cancer is a term used to describe cancer that grows or spreads more rapidly than average, often requiring more intense treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate Gland">
      <data key="d0">Prostate Gland</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Prostate Gland is an organ in the male reproductive system that produces semen and contains muscles that aid in ejaculation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urethra">
      <data key="d0">Urethra</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Urethra is a tube that carries urine from the bladder and is wrapped around by the prostate gland.&lt;SEP&gt;The urethra is the tube that carries urine from the bladder to the outside of the body. It is part of the urinary tract and can be affected by bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bladder">
      <data key="d0">Bladder</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An organ that stores urine, anatomically positioned beneath the prostate and involved in urinary function.&lt;SEP&gt;The Bladder is an organ that stores urine and is connected to the urethra.&lt;SEP&gt;The bladder is the organ where urothelial cancer originates and where cancer staging and grading are determined based on the depth of invasion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seminal Vesicles">
      <data key="d0">Seminal Vesicles</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Glands that produce a component of semen, located above the prostate and behind the bladder, contributing to reproductive physiology.&lt;SEP&gt;Seminal Vesicles are glands that contribute to the production of semen along with the prostate gland.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Semen">
      <data key="d0">Semen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Semen is a fluid composed of secretions from the prostate, seminal vesicles, and sperm from the testicles, released during ejaculation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSA">
      <data key="d0">PSA</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PSA (Prostate-Specific Antigen) is a protein produced by the prostate gland that is used as a biomarker for prostate health and cancer detection.&lt;SEP&gt;PSA (prostate-specific antigen) is a blood test measuring levels of a protein produced by the prostate, with elevated levels indicating potential cancer risk or disease progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diet and Lifestyle">
      <data key="d0">Diet and Lifestyle</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diet and Lifestyle factors, such as high-fat diets and obesity, are linked to increased risks of prostate cancer and its aggressiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Factors such as high-fat diets, smoking, and obesity associated with increased prostate cancer risk, while fruits/vegetables may reduce advanced disease risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exercise and Weight">
      <data key="d0">Exercise and Weight</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Exercise and Weight management are associated with reduced aggressiveness of prostate cancer and lower risks of mortality.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Smoking and Obesity">
      <data key="d0">Smoking and Obesity</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Smoking and Obesity are risk factors that increase the likelihood of developing and dying from prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Care Providers">
      <data key="d0">Health Care Providers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Health Care Providers are professionals involved in breast cancer care, including breast specialists, radiologists, gynecologists, and lactation consultants.&lt;SEP&gt;Health Care Providers are professionals involved in diagnosing, screening, and treating prostate cancer, particularly for high-risk populations like Black males.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="prostate cancer">
      <data key="d0">prostate cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Prostate cancer is a type of cancer that develops in the prostate gland, characterized by uncontrolled cell growth. It can be classified into localized and metastatic stages, with localized remaining within the prostate and metastatic spreading to other areas of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSA test">
      <data key="d0">PSA test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PSA test is a blood test that measures the amount of prostate-specific antigen (PSA) in the bloodstream, which can indicate the presence of prostate cancer or its progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="digital rectal exam">
      <data key="d0">digital rectal exam</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Digital rectal exam is a physical examination where a healthcare provider checks the prostate gland for abnormalities by inserting a gloved, lubricated finger into the rectum.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="active surveillance">
      <data key="d0">active surveillance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Active surveillance is a management approach for early-stage prostate cancer where patients undergo regular monitoring without immediate treatment, avoiding potential side effects of therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="hormone therapy">
      <data key="d0">hormone therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormone therapy is a treatment for prostate cancer that reduces testosterone levels to slow cancer growth, commonly used for advanced-stage disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="surgery">
      <data key="d0">surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgery is a primary treatment for early-stage cervical cancer, including hysterectomy or trachelectomy.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgery, such as radical prostatectomy, is a treatment for early-stage prostate cancer to remove the prostate gland and surrounding tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="localized prostate cancer">
      <data key="d0">localized prostate cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Localized prostate cancer is an early-stage form where the cancer remains confined within the prostate gland, often treatable with surgery or radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="metastatic prostate cancer">
      <data key="d0">metastatic prostate cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic prostate cancer is an advanced-stage form where the cancer has spread to other parts of the body, requiring systemic treatments like hormone therapy or chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="early-stage prostate cancer">
      <data key="d0">early-stage prostate cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Early-stage prostate cancer refers to cancer that has not spread beyond the prostate gland, typically managed through active surveillance, surgery, or radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="advanced-stage prostate cancer">
      <data key="d0">advanced-stage prostate cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Advanced-stage prostate cancer has spread beyond the prostate to other areas of the body, necessitating aggressive treatments like hormone therapy, chemotherapy, or radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6232dd9ed168a1113955e7300645338b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSA Testing">
      <data key="d0">PSA Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PSA Testing refers to the measurement of prostate-specific antigen levels in the blood to assess potential prostate cancer risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Digital Rectal Exam">
      <data key="d0">Digital Rectal Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A digital rectal exam is a physical examination of the prostate gland, used alongside PSA testing to assess prostate health and cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical examination technique used to detect abnormalities in the prostate, often combined with PSA tests to screen for potential cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Digital Rectal Exam (DRE) is a diagnostic procedure used to assess the anal region for abnormalities, often part of routine screening for anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Digital Rectal Exam is a diagnostic procedure used to assess the rectum and surrounding lymph nodes, repeated after an anal cancer diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Digital Rectal Exam is a medical procedure used to assess the rectum and surrounding areas, often repeated after an anal cancer diagnosis to evaluate lymph node involvement.&lt;SEP&gt;Digital Rectal Exam is a medical procedure used to detect abnormalities in the anal canal and rectum, often performed as part of the diagnostic process for anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Digital Rectal Exam is a medical procedure where a doctor inserts a finger into the rectum to examine the prostate for abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;Digital Rectal Exam is a physical diagnostic method used to assess the size and texture of the prostate gland, often employed in the initial evaluation of prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Digital Rectal Exam is a physical diagnostic procedure where a healthcare provider examines the prostate gland for abnormalities by inserting a gloved finger into the rectum.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood and Urine Tests">
      <data key="d0">Blood and Urine Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood and Urine Tests are diagnostic procedures used to detect bladder cancer by analyzing samples for abnormalities, such as blood in the urine or changes in blood composition.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood and Urine Tests include biomarker tests like 4Kscore, PHI, IsoPSA, ExoDx, miR Sentinel, MPS, PCA3, and SelectMDx to evaluate prostate health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic Tests">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnostic Tests are procedures used to confirm the presence of cancer, determine its stage, and guide treatment decisions, including imaging scans, biopsies, and genetic tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagnostic Tests encompass procedures such as biopsy and imaging to confirm or rule out prostate cancer after initial screenings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Symptoms and Quality of Life">
      <data key="d0">Symptoms and Quality of Life</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptoms and Quality of Life involve assessing issues like urinary problems, sexual dysfunction, and overall well-being to guide treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Symptoms and Quality of Life refer to the physical and emotional impacts of cancer on a patient's daily functioning and overall well-being, evaluated as part of comprehensive care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health History">
      <data key="d0">Health History</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Health History includes personal medical records, treatments, and medications to inform prostate cancer risk evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="4Kscore, PHI, IsoPSA">
      <data key="d0">4Kscore, PHI, IsoPSA</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">4Kscore, PHI, and IsoPSA are blood-based biomarker tests used alongside PSA to refine prostate cancer risk assessment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ExoDx, miR Sentinel, MPS, PCA3, SelectMDx">
      <data key="d0">ExoDx, miR Sentinel, MPS, PCA3, SelectMDx</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ExoDx, miR Sentinel, MPS, PCA3, and SelectMDx are urine-based tests that complement PSA testing to evaluate prostate cancer risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI, CT, PET, ultrasound, bone scan">
      <data key="d0">MRI, CT, PET, ultrasound, bone scan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MRI, CT, PET, ultrasound, and bone scan are imaging modalities used to detect prostate cancer, assess tumor size, and check for metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSMA/PET">
      <data key="d0">PSMA/PET</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PSMA/PET imaging is a specialized PET scan targeting the prostate-specific membrane antigen (PSMA) on prostate cancer cells, enhancing detection of localized and metastatic disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Scan">
      <data key="d0">Bone Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A bone scan detects cancer spread to bones using a radioactive tracer, identifying areas of bone damage caused by cancer, treatment, or other conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;A bone scan is a medical imaging test that uses a radiotracer to detect bone damage, such as cancer, previous injuries, or other health issues.&lt;SEP&gt;A bone scan is an imaging test that uses a radiotracer to detect cancer spread to the bones.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone Scan detects cancer metastasis to bones by identifying areas of increased metabolic activity, often used in advanced prostate cancer cases.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone Scan is a diagnostic procedure that detects cancer spread to bones using a radioactive tracer, often used in patients with suspected bone metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone Scan is an imaging procedure used to detect cancer metastases in bones, often replaced by Sodium Fluoride PET-CT.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET/CT Scan">
      <data key="d0">PET/CT Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A PET/CT scan combines PET and CT imaging to provide detailed metabolic and anatomical information about prostate cancer, showing both cancer spread and structural details.&lt;SEP&gt;PET/CT Scan combines positron emission tomography (PET) and computed tomography (CT) to create detailed images of the pelvis, identifying cancerous lymph nodes and metastases.&lt;SEP&gt;PET/CT scan combines positron emission tomography (PET) and computed tomography (CT) to detect cancer spread and provide detailed anatomical and functional imaging.&lt;SEP&gt;PET/CT scan combines positron emission tomography (PET) and computed tomography (CT) to detect cancer spread and provide detailed images of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSMA">
      <data key="d0">PSMA</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PSMA (prostate-specific membrane antigen) is a protein abundantly expressed on prostate cancer cells, targeted by specialized imaging agents for detection and monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biopsy Procedure">
      <data key="d0">Biopsy Procedure</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical procedure to remove tissue samples for cancer diagnosis, involving the use of imaging techniques like MRI and ultrasound to guide the needle insertion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transperineal Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Transperineal Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific type of prostate biopsy where a needle is inserted through the perineum into the prostate gland, guided by an ultrasound probe and MRI scans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI Scans">
      <data key="d0">MRI Scans</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A diagnostic imaging technique used to create detailed images of the prostate, often combined with ultrasound to guide biopsy procedures and assess cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Test">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A diagnostic category involving analysis of DNA to identify mutations that may contribute to cancer development, including germline and somatic mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic Test identifies abnormal changes (mutations) in genes associated with hereditary cancer risks, such as BRCA1, BRCA2, and others, to assess individual cancer susceptibility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mutations">
      <data key="d0">Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic alterations in DNA that can lead to abnormal cell function, classified into germline (inherited) or somatic (acquired) mutations, contributing to cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Germline Mutations">
      <data key="d0">Germline Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Inherited genetic changes present in all cells of the body, which may increase the risk of multiple cancers, including prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acquired Mutations">
      <data key="d0">Acquired Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic changes that occur spontaneously in specific cells during a person's lifetime, potentially leading to cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inherited Mutations">
      <data key="d0">Inherited Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic alterations passed down through families, present in all cells, which may predispose individuals to certain cancers like prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSA Test">
      <data key="d0">PSA Test</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A blood test that measures the level of prostate-specific antigen (PSA) in the blood, often used as a screening tool for prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;PSA Test is a blood test that measures prostate-specific antigen levels, which can indicate potential prostate issues including cancer, though it is not definitive on its own.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urologist">
      <data key="d0">Urologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A doctor specializing in diagnosing and treating diseases of the urinary system and male reproductive organs, including prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A urologist specializes in the urinary system and male reproductive organs, diagnosing and treating urinary tract cancers, including bladder cancer, with surgery and intravesical therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Urologist is a medical specialist who performs cystoscopy and other procedures to diagnose and treat urinary tract issues.&lt;SEP&gt;Urologist is a medical specialist who performs procedures like TURBT, radical cystectomy, and manages urinary diversion techniques for bladder cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Invasive Tests">
      <data key="d0">Invasive Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical procedures that involve entering the body to obtain tissue samples or perform diagnostic evaluations, carrying inherent risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Germline Testing">
      <data key="d0">Germline Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of genetic testing that identifies inherited mutations present in all cells of the body, which may increase cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Germline Testing is a diagnostic procedure to identify inherited genetic mutations that increase the risk of cancers such as prostate, breast, ovarian, and colorectal cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Molecular Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d0">Molecular Biomarker Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A genetic testing method that analyzes specific biomarkers in DNA or proteins to assess cancer risk and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular Biomarker Testing analyzes a tumor's molecular components to assess cancer risk and guide treatment decisions, particularly for prostate cancer and other malignancies.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular Biomarker Testing helps determine the likelihood of cancer spread and guides treatment decisions for prostate cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ATM">
      <data key="d0">ATM</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A gene involved in DNA repair and cell cycle regulation, with mutations linked to cancer susceptibility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEK2">
      <data key="d0">CHEK2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A gene that plays a role in DNA damage response and repair, with mutations associated with increased cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MLH1">
      <data key="d0">MLH1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A gene involved in DNA mismatch repair, with mutations leading to hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (HNPCC) and increased cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MSH2">
      <data key="d0">MSH2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A gene critical for DNA mismatch repair, with mutations contributing to hereditary cancer syndromes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MSH6">
      <data key="d0">MSH6</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A gene involved in DNA mismatch repair, associated with hereditary cancer predisposition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PALB2">
      <data key="d0">PALB2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A gene that interacts with BRCA1 and BRCA2 in DNA repair, with mutations linked to increased cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PMS2">
      <data key="d0">PMS2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A gene involved in DNA mismatch repair, with mutations contributing to hereditary cancer syndromes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A malignant tumor originating in breast tissue, often starting in ducts or lobules and potentially spreading to lymph nodes or other organs.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of cancer originating in the breast tissue, characterized by the presence of malignant cells, and a central category in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of cancer that originates in the breast tissue, often starting in the ducts or lobules and potentially spreading to surrounding tissue or lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of cancer that originates in the breast tissue, often starting in the ducts or lobules and spreading to surrounding areas.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast Cancer is a category of cancer discussed in the text, involving staging systems, hormone receptor testing, and treatment considerations.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast Cancer is a category of cancer that originates in the breast tissue, specifically in the epithelial cells of the ducts or lobules, and can affect individuals of any gender.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast Cancer is a category of malignancies originating in the breast tissue, classified into subtypes such as ductal carcinoma and lobular carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast Cancer is a malignant tumor that can be linked to germline mutations in genes like BRCA1 and BRCA2, and is often evaluated alongside prostate cancer in genetic risk assessments.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast Cancer is a type of cancer classified into noninvasive, invasive, and metastatic stages, with staging and grading systems used to determine treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast Cancer is a type of cancer that can be classified into noninvasive, invasive, and metastatic stages, with staging and grading systems used to determine treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast Cancer is a type of cancer that can occur in anyone, including those assigned male at birth, and is characterized by the uncontrolled growth of epithelial cells in the breast's ducts or lobules.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a malignant tumor that develops in the breast tissue, characterized by the uncontrolled growth of cells that can spread to other parts of the body through lymphatic or blood systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a type of cancer discussed in the text, with specific treatments and considerations for fertility and pregnancy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pancreatic Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Pancreatic Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pancreatic Cancer is a highly aggressive malignancy that can be influenced by germline mutations, particularly in genes like BRCA1 and BRCA2, and is evaluated alongside other cancers in genetic testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Cancer (Melanoma)">
      <data key="d0">Skin Cancer (Melanoma)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Skin Cancer (Melanoma) is a type of cancer that can be hereditary, associated with germline mutations in genes like BRCA1 and BRCA2, and is part of the broader context of cancer risk assessment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colorectal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Colorectal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Colorectal Cancer is a malignancy linked to germline mutations in genes such as MSH2, MSH6, MLH1, and PMS2, and is evaluated in conjunction with prostate cancer in genetic testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Colorectal cancer is a type of cancer originating in the colon or rectum, often detected through screening methods like colonoscopy and characterized by polyp formation.&lt;SEP&gt;Colorectal cancer is a type of cancer originating in the colon or rectum, often linked to polyps, genetic factors, and diagnosed through various tests like ctDNA analysis and imaging.&lt;SEP&gt;Colorectal cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the colon or rectum, often detected through screening methods like colonoscopy.&lt;SEP&gt;Colorectal cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the colon or rectum, often linked to polyps and genetic factors, and is a central focus of the text's discussion on diagnosis, treatment, and patient care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Uterine Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Uterine Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Uterine Cancer is a broad term for cancers affecting the uterus, including endometrial cancer and uterine sarcomas, which are treated differently from cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Uterine Cancer is a gynecological cancer associated with germline mutations in genes like MSH2, MSH6, MLH1, and PMS2, and is part of the spectrum of cancers evaluated in genetic risk assessments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Risk Groups">
      <data key="d0">Risk Groups</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk Groups classify prostate cancer patients based on clinical factors like PSA levels, tumor stage, and biopsy results to determine treatment approaches and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Risk Groups classify prostate cancer patients based on clinical factors to determine appropriate treatment strategies and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Risk groups classify prostate cancer patients based on their likelihood of disease progression, guiding treatment decisions and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nomograms">
      <data key="d0">Nomograms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nomograms are statistical tools used in risk assessment for prostate cancer to predict disease progression and guide treatment decisions based on clinical data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSA Level">
      <data key="d0">PSA Level</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PSA Level is a measure of prostate-specific antigen in the bloodstream, used to assess prostate cancer risk. It can vary by age, race, and other factors, with higher levels indicating potential risk but not definitive cancer diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;PSA Level refers to the concentration of prostate-specific antigen in the blood, which is a key biomarker for diagnosing and monitoring prostate cancer progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Profiling">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Profiling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor Profiling involves comprehensive genetic and molecular analysis of tumors to identify actionable therapeutic targets.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor Profiling involves comprehensive genetic and molecular analysis of tumors to identify potential therapeutic targets.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor Profiling is a molecular analysis technique that evaluates a tumor's genetic and molecular characteristics to inform personalized treatment strategies for cancers like prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Molecular Tumor Testing">
      <data key="d0">Molecular Tumor Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Molecular Tumor Testing is a comprehensive analysis of a tumor's molecular components to determine cancer risk, guide treatment decisions, and assess potential for recurrence or metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Life Expectancy">
      <data key="d0">Life Expectancy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Life Expectancy is a prognostic factor that estimates the likely survival duration for prostate cancer patients, influenced by risk group classifications and treatment responses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Complications">
      <data key="d0">Complications</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Complications are potential adverse effects of cancer treatments, such as surgery or radiation therapy, which are factored into treatment decisions to balance risk and benefit.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Plan">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Plan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment Plan outlines the specific interventions, including surgery, radiation, or watchful waiting, tailored to a patient's risk group, molecular biomarker results, and overall health status.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Risk Assessment">
      <data key="d0">Risk Assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk Assessment evaluates factors like PSA levels, tumor stage, and biopsy results to classify prostate cancer patients into risk groups and guide treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lower Risk Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Lower Risk Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lower Risk Prostate Cancer refers to cases where the cancer is unlikely to spread or recur, often managed with active surveillance or delayed treatment to avoid unnecessary interventions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Higher Risk Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Higher Risk Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Higher Risk Prostate Cancer indicates a greater likelihood of cancer progression or recurrence, typically requiring more aggressive treatment strategies to improve outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Providers">
      <data key="d0">Medical Providers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical Providers are healthcare professionals who assess cancer risk, perform diagnostic tests, and manage treatment plans for patients with prostate cancer and related conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSA Density">
      <data key="d0">PSA Density</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PSA (Prostate-Specific Antigen) density is a measurement used to assess prostate cancer risk, calculated by dividing PSA levels by the volume of the prostate gland.&lt;SEP&gt;PSA (Prostate-Specific Antigen) density is a metric calculated by dividing PSA levels by prostate gland volume, used to assess prostate cancer risk and guide diagnostic decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;PSA Density is a calculated metric that adjusts PSA level by prostate size, helping to better assess cancer risk. It is determined by dividing PSA level by prostate volume measured via ultrasound or MRI.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gleason Score">
      <data key="d0">Gleason Score</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Gleason Score is a grading system for prostate cancer aggressiveness, derived from biopsy samples. It ranges from 6 (least aggressive) to 10 (most aggressive) and is used to determine treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;The Gleason score is a histopathological grading system that evaluates the aggressiveness of prostate cancer based on the appearance of cancerous tissue under a microscope, ranging from 2 to 10.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade Group">
      <data key="d0">Grade Group</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade Group is a classification system derived from the Gleason score, categorizing prostate cancer into five groups (1-5) to reflect disease severity and progression risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Grade Group is a classification system that categorizes prostate cancer into five groups (1-5) based on the Gleason score, reflecting the likelihood of cancer progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Grade Group is a classification system used to categorize the aggressiveness of prostate cancer based on Gleason scores.&lt;SEP&gt;Grade Group is a simplified classification system that organizes Gleason scores into five groups (1-5), with higher groups indicating more aggressive cancer and guiding treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate Size">
      <data key="d0">Prostate Size</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Prostate Size refers to the physical dimensions of the prostate gland, measured via imaging techniques like transrectal ultrasound or MRI. It is used to calculate PSA density and assess cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gleason score">
      <data key="d0">Gleason score</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Gleason score is a grading system that assesses the aggressiveness of prostate cancer, with higher scores indicating more severe cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Gleason score is a grading system that evaluates the aggressiveness of prostate cancer, with higher scores indicating more severe disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Male reproductive system">
      <data key="d0">Male reproductive system</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The male reproductive system includes organs like the prostate, bladder, seminal vesicles, and testicles, playing a key role in sexual reproduction and urine storage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Black Males">
      <data key="d0">Black Males</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A demographic group more likely to develop prostate cancer at an earlier age, with more aggressive disease progression and higher mortality rates compared to white males.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hispanic and Asian Males">
      <data key="d0">Hispanic and Asian Males</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A demographic group less likely to be diagnosed with prostate cancer compared to white and Black males, influenced by biological, genetic, and healthcare access factors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate">
      <data key="d0">Prostate</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A glandular organ in the male reproductive system, surrounding the urethra, and a primary site for prostate cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Factors">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Factors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Inherited or non-inherited genetic mutations linked to increased prostate cancer risk, including BRCA2 gene alterations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Race">
      <data key="d0">Race</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A demographic category influencing prostate cancer incidence and outcomes, with Black males showing higher risk and mortality compared to other racial groups.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A form of advanced-stage prostate cancer that has spread to other parts of the body, such as bones, lungs, or liver, via the bloodstream or lymphatic system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="United States">
      <data key="d0">United States</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A country where racial disparities in prostate cancer incidence and mortality are observed, particularly among Black males compared to other ethnic groups.&lt;SEP&gt;The United States is the country where the National Cancer Institute and NCCN Cancer Centers are based, and where many clinical trials are conducted.&lt;SEP&gt;The United States is the country where the National Cancer Institute and other cancer-related organizations operate, and where clinical trials are often conducted.&lt;SEP&gt;United States is a geographic location where a vaccine protecting against high-risk HPV strains is available.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advanced-None-Stage Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Advanced-None-Stage Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer that has metastasized beyond the prostate to distant organs or lymph nodes, characterized by rapid progression and aggressive behavior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Best Practices">
      <data key="d0">Best Practices</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Best Practices represent evidence-based guidelines and protocols developed by leading cancer centers to optimize treatment outcomes and patient care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Center">
      <data key="d0">Medical Center</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical center is a healthcare facility where diagnostic imaging, biopsies, and surgical interventions like stereotactic biopsies are conducted.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical center is a healthcare facility where diagnostic procedures, imaging tests, and surgical interventions like stereotactic biopsies are conducted.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical Center is a healthcare facility specializing in cancer treatment, offering advanced diagnostic tools, therapies, and multidisciplinary care teams for complex conditions like prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Team">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment Team is a multidisciplinary group of specialists, including endocrinologists, radiologists, and surgeons, collaborating to manage thyroid cancer care.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment Team is a specialized group of oncologists, surgeons, and other healthcare providers who collaborate to determine and implement the most effective treatment strategies for prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Complementary Medicines">
      <data key="d0">Complementary Medicines</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Complementary Medicines include herbal supplements, vitamins, and alternative therapies that may be used alongside conventional cancer treatments, though their efficacy and safety require careful evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Second Opinion">
      <data key="d0">Second Opinion</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Second Opinion is a medical process where a patient seeks an additional doctor's evaluation to confirm or adjust a treatment plan, ensuring informed decision-making.&lt;SEP&gt;Second Opinion is a medical process where patients seek additional expert evaluation for their diagnosis and treatment plan, often to confirm or refine their care approach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Mutations">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic Mutations are alterations in DNA sequences that can predispose individuals to certain cancers, including prostate cancer, and may affect treatment responses.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic mutations in cells are the underlying cause of bladder cancer, leading to uncontrolled cell growth and tumor formation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Supplements">
      <data key="d0">Supplements</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Supplements are dietary products containing nutrients, herbs, or other ingredients that may be used to support health, though their impact on cancer detection or treatment requires scientific validation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Centers">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Centers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Centers are specialized healthcare institutions focused on research, diagnosis, and treatment of cancer, often leading the development of best practices and innovative therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate-Specific Antigen (PSA)">
      <data key="d0">Prostate-Specific Antigen (PSA)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Prostate-Specific Antigen (PSA) is a protein produced by prostate cells, with elevated levels in blood potentially indicating prostate issues, including cancer, though it is not a definitive diagnostic marker.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urinary Tract Infection">
      <data key="d0">Urinary Tract Infection</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urinary Tract Infection is a bacterial infection affecting the urinary system, which can temporarily elevate PSA levels and complicate prostate cancer diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Herbs">
      <data key="d0">Herbs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Herbs are plant-based substances used in traditional or complementary medicine, which may have potential health benefits but require scientific evaluation for their role in cancer management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Saw Palmetto">
      <data key="d0">Saw Palmetto</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Saw Palmetto is a herbal supplement often used for prostate health, though its efficacy in treating or preventing prostate cancer requires further research and clinical validation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="St. John’s Wort">
      <data key="d0">St. John’s Wort</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">St. John’s Wort is an herbal supplement with potential antidepressant properties, but its use may interfere with cancer treatments and requires careful monitoring by healthcare providers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biopsies">
      <data key="d0">Biopsies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biopsies involve removing a small tissue sample for laboratory analysis to confirm cancer diagnosis and determine its characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSMA-PET Scan">
      <data key="d0">PSMA-PET Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PSMA-PET Scan is a diagnostic imaging technique that uses a radioactive tracer to detect prostate cancer cells, particularly for monitoring recurrence and assessing targeted therapy effectiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;PSMA-PET Scan is a specialized PET scan targeting prostate-specific membrane antigen (PSMA), a protein overexpressed in prostate cancer cells, for precise imaging and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate-Specific Membrane Antigen (PSMA)">
      <data key="d0">Prostate-Specific Membrane Antigen (PSMA)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PSMA is a protein found on the surface of prostate cancer cells, serving as a biomarker for targeted imaging and therapy in prostate cancer management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="University of Chicago Medicine">
      <data key="d0">University of Chicago Medicine</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">University of Chicago Medicine is an expert in treating diseases of the urinary system and the male reproductive organs, providing specialized care for prostate cancer diagnostics and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chicago">
      <data key="d0">Chicago</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Chicago is the location associated with the University of Chicago Medicine, where certain diagnostic procedures and imaging techniques are performed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI-TRUS Fusion">
      <data key="d0">MRI-TRUS Fusion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MRI-TRUS Fusion integrates magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) and transrectal ultrasound (TRUS) to provide a highly detailed view of prostate cancer for precise biopsy guidance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transrectal Ultrasound (TRUS)">
      <data key="d0">Transrectal Ultrasound (TRUS)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Transrectal Ultrasound (TRUS) is a specific type of ultrasound imaging used to visualize the prostate gland and guide biopsy procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A biological structure in the body that is part of the lymphatic system, often involved in the spread of cancer cells from a primary tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph Node is a category of bodily structure where cancer may spread, critical for staging and determining the extent of disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph Node is a part of the lymphatic system where cancer can spread, often detected through imaging techniques like PET/CT scans and bone scans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hereditary Gene Testing">
      <data key="d0">Hereditary Gene Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of genetic testing that looks for inherited mutations in genes, which can increase the risk of cancers like prostate, breast, ovarian, and pancreatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Testing">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of genetic testing that analyzes mutations in tumor cells to determine treatment options, including targeted therapies like PARP inhibitors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Care Provider">
      <data key="d0">Health Care Provider</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical professional who recommends genetic testing, refers patients to genetic counselors, and manages cancer care decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bones">
      <data key="d0">Bones</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Anatomical structures where cancer may metastasize, often targeted for biopsy to assess cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Bones are a common site for metastatic bladder cancer, where cancer cells can spread and form secondary tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Internal Organs">
      <data key="d0">Internal Organs</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Anatomical structures where cancer may metastasize, often targeted for biopsy to assess cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormone Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Hormone Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormone Therapy refers to treatments like Menopausal Hormone Therapy (MHT) or Hormone Replacement Therapy (HRT) used to manage menopause symptoms from cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone Therapy refers to treatments like Menopausal Hormone Therapy (MHT) or Hormone Replacement Therapy (HRT) used to manage symptoms of menopause caused by cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone therapy is a treatment approach for advanced prostate cancer that involves managing hormone levels to slow cancer growth, including the use of LHRH agonists and antagonists.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone therapy is a treatment approach that modifies hormone levels to inhibit cancer growth, particularly targeting testosterone in prostate cancer to slow tumor progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone therapy is a treatment approach that modifies hormone levels to slow or stop cancer growth, particularly in prostate cancer where testosterone fuels tumor progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Androgen Deprivation Therapy (ADT)">
      <data key="d0">Androgen Deprivation Therapy (ADT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Androgen deprivation therapy (ADT) is a hormone therapy that reduces testosterone levels to starve cancer cells of their primary fuel, often through medication or surgical castration.&lt;SEP&gt;Androgen deprivation therapy (ADT) is a hormone therapy that reduces testosterone levels to starve cancer cells of their primary fuel, using medications or surgical castration.&lt;SEP&gt;Androgen deprivation therapy (ADT) is a specific form of hormone therapy that reduces testosterone levels to inhibit cancer progression, often involving medications like LHRH agonists and antagonists.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LHRH Agonists">
      <data key="d0">LHRH Agonists</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">LHRH (Luteinizing Hormone-Releasing Hormone) agonists are medications that suppress testosterone production by targeting the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis, commonly used in hormone therapy for prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Castration">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Castration</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical castration, such as orchiectomy, is a permanent procedure that removes the testicles to eliminate testosterone production, serving as a form of hormone therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgical castration, such as orchiectomy, is a procedure that removes the testicles to eliminate testosterone production, serving as a permanent form of hormone therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LHRH Antagonists">
      <data key="d0">LHRH Antagonists</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">LHRH antagonists are medications that directly block the action of luteinizing hormone-releasing hormone, reducing testosterone production and used in hormone therapy for prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LHRH Agonist">
      <data key="d0">LHRH Agonist</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">LHRH agonists are medications that stimulate the release of luteinizing hormone-releasing hormone (LHRH), leading to reduced testosterone production and are used in hormone therapy for prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LHRH Antagonist">
      <data key="d0">LHRH Antagonist</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">LHRH antagonists are medications that block the release of LHRH, reducing testosterone levels and are used in hormone therapy for prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anti-androgens">
      <data key="d0">Anti-androgens</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anti-androgens are medications that block the action of androgens (male hormones) and are used as part of hormone therapy for prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Corticosteroids">
      <data key="d0">Corticosteroids</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Corticosteroids are medications used in hormone therapy to manage inflammation and certain cancer-related symptoms, often alongside other treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Androgen Synthesis Inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">Androgen Synthesis Inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Androgen synthesis inhibitors are medications that prevent the body from producing testosterone, used in hormone therapy for prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Invasive Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Invasive Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Invasive Breast Cancer is a stage of breast cancer where cancer cells have spread from the milk ducts or lobules into surrounding breast tissue and nearby lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Invasive Breast Cancer is a subcategory of breast cancer where cancer cells have spread beyond the original ducts or lobules into surrounding breast tissue and nearby lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Invasive Breast Cancer is a type of cancer that has grown beyond the ducts or lobules of the breast, potentially spreading to lymph nodes and other tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Invasive breast cancer is cancer that has grown outside the ducts or lobules into surrounding tissue and can spread to other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Breast Cancer (MBC)">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Breast Cancer (MBC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic Breast Cancer (MBC) is a stage of breast cancer where cancer cells have spread to distant parts of the body, such as bones, lungs, liver, brain, or distant lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic Breast Cancer (MBC) is a stage where cancer has spread beyond the breast and lymph nodes to other parts of the body, such as the bone, lungs, liver, brain, or distant lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic breast cancer (MBC) is cancer that has spread outside the breast and lymph nodes under the arm (axilla) to other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing for Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Testing for Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing for breast cancer involves various diagnostic procedures to determine the type and stage of cancer, including blood tests, imaging tests, and biopsies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormone Receptor Status">
      <data key="d0">Hormone Receptor Status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A classification of breast cancer based on the presence of estrogen and/or progesterone receptors, influencing treatment decisions like end,ocrine therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;A classification of breast cancer based on the presence of estrogen and/or progesterone receptors, influencing treatment decisions like endocrine therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone receptor status indicates whether breast cancer cells have receptors for hormones like estrogen or progesterone, influencing treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone receptor status indicates whether the cancer cells have estrogen or progesterone receptors, influencing treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HER2 Status">
      <data key="d0">HER2 Status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A classification of breast cancer based on the presence of HER2 receptors, determining eligibility for targeted therapies and requiring specific testing methods.&lt;SEP&gt;HER,2 status identifies whether the cancer cells have the HER2 protein, which affects treatment options and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;HER2 Status is a category indicating the presence of human epidermal growth factor receptor 2 in cancer cells, influencing treatment options and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;HER2 status identifies whether the cancer cells have the HER2 protein, which affects treatment options and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;HER2 status refers to the presence of the HER2 protein on breast cancer cells, which affects treatment decisions and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Cancer Risk Testing">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Cancer Risk Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A diagnostic procedure to assess inherited genetic mutations that increase susceptibility to cancers like breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A test to determine an individual's risk of developing certain cancers based on genetic mutations, such as BRCA1 or BRCA2.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic Cancer Risk Testing assesses inherited genetic mutations that increase the likelihood of developing breast cancer, such as BRCA1/BRCA2 mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic Cancer Risk Testing is a diagnostic method mentioned in the text to assess hereditary cancer risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic cancer risk testing assesses inherited genetic mutations that may increase the risk of developing breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Distress Screening">
      <data key="d0">Distress Screening</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Distress screening evaluates emotional and psychological well-being during breast cancer diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Distress screening evaluates the patient's emotional and psychological well-being during diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Cancer Basics">
      <data key="d0">Breast Cancer Basics</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Breast cancer basics provide foundational information about the structure of the breast, how cancer spreads, and types of breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Anatomy">
      <data key="d0">Breast Anatomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Breast anatomy describes the structure of the breast, including ducts, lobules, fat, nerves, and lymphatic system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Primary Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A primary tumor is the initial mass of cancer cells that forms in the breast and can become invasive or metastatic.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Invasive Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Invasive Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Invasive cancer is a stage of cancer where malignant cells have spread beyond the original site into surrounding tissues, requiring more aggressive treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Invasive cancer refers to cancer cells that have grown into surrounding tissues and may spread to other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Axillary Lymph Nodes (ALNs)">
      <data key="d0">Axillary Lymph Nodes (ALNs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Axillary lymph nodes (ALNs) are located near the armpit and are sites where breast cancer can spread via the lymphatic system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone">
      <data key="d0">Bone</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone is a location in the human body where breast cancer commonly metastasizes, particularly the spine and other skeletal structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lungs">
      <data key="d0">Lungs</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Lungs are a common destination for metastatic bladder cancer, as cancer cells can travel through the bloodstream to form new tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Lungs are a location in the human body where breast cancer can metastasize, often due to the presence of blood vessels and lymphatic pathways.&lt;SEP&gt;The lungs are a major organ in the human body where colon cancer may metastasize, commonly affected in Stage 4 colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liver">
      <data key="d0">Liver</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Liver is a location in the human body where breast cancer can spread, often due to its role in processing blood and metabolic functions.&lt;SEP&gt;The liver is a major organ in the human body where colon cancer may metastasize, commonly affected in Stage 4 colon cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The liver is a potential site for metastatic bladder cancer, where cancer cells can spread and affect liver function.&lt;SEP&gt;The liver is a vital organ responsible for filtering blood, producing bile, and metabolizing nutrients, and it is the site of primary liver cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The liver produces bile, which aids in the digestion of fats in the small intestine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brain">
      <data key="d0">Brain</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Brain is a location in the human body where breast cancer can metastasize, often due to the presence of blood vessels and the central nervous system's connection to the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Axillary Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Axillary Lymph Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Axillary Lymph Nodes are a location in the human body where breast cancer can spread, serving as part of the lymphatic system that transports cancer cells to distant sites.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph nodes located near the armpit that receive drainage from breast tissue, often affected by breast cancer metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ductal Carcinoma In Situ (DCIS)">
      <data key="d0">Ductal Carcinoma In Situ (DCIS)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A non-invasive form of breast cancer confined to milk ducts, considered a pre-invasive stage that can progress to invasive cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A non-invasive form of breast cancer found in the cells lining the milk ducts, which has not spread beyond the ducts and is considered a preinvasive disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Ductal Carcinoma In Situ (DCIS) is a noninvasive breast cancer confined to the milk ducts, classified as stage 0 and requiring specific treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Screening Mammogram">
      <data key="d0">Screening Mammogram</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A routine imaging test used to detect breast cancer in asymptomatic individuals through low-dose x-rays.&lt;SEP&gt;A routine imaging test used to detect breast cancer in asymptomatic individuals, typically performed on a regular basis.&lt;SEP&gt;A screening mammogram is a routine breast imaging test performed without symptoms to detect breast cancer early, typically using 2D or 3D digital mammography.&lt;SEP&gt;Screening Mammogram is a category of routine breast imaging test used to detect early signs of breast cancer in asymptomatic individuals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic Mammogram">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic Mammogram</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A diagnostic mammogram is a specialized breast imaging test used to examine specific areas of the breast for tumors, often in cases of symptoms like lumps or abnormal findings on screening mammograms.&lt;SEP&gt;A specialized imaging test used to evaluate suspicious findings, symptoms, or abnormalities detected in screening mammograms.&lt;SEP&gt;A specialized imaging test used to evaluate symptoms such as lumps, pain, or abnormal findings from a screening mammogram, allowing for immediate evaluation by a radiologist.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagnostic Mammogram is a category of imaging test used to examine breast tissue in detail, differing from screening mammograms and aiding in cancer detection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Stages">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Stages</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A classification system that describes the extent of cancer spread, guiding treatment strategies and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;A classification system that describes the extent of cancer spread, helping to determine the appropriate treatment plan.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer Stages classify the extent of breast cancer progression, guiding treatment planning and prognosis assessment.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer stages describe the progression of cancer based on tumor size, spread, and involvement of nearby tissues, guiding treatment decisions and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Cancer Screening">
      <data key="d0">Breast Cancer Screening</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical process involving tests like mammograms and MRIs to detect breast cancer early, before symptoms appear.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast Cancer Screening is a medical event focused on detecting breast cancer at early stages through various diagnostic tests and procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Early Detection">
      <data key="d0">Early Detection</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Early Detection refers to the process of identifying breast cancer in its initial stages to improve treatment outcomes and survival rates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Key Points">
      <data key="d0">Key Points</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Key Points summarize critical information about anal cancer, including risk factors, treatment options, and long-term management strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Key Points summarize essential information about breast cancer screening, testing, and treatment planning for patients and healthcare providers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DCIS">
      <data key="d0">DCIS</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">DCIS (Ductal Carcinoma In Situ) is a category of non-invasive breast cancer that develops in the milk ducts and may progress to invasive cancer if untreated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mammogram">
      <data key="d0">Mammogram</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mammogram is a category of imaging tests that use low-dose x-rays to examine breast tissue for abnormalities, including 2D and 3D tomosynthesis variants.&lt;SEP&gt;Mammogram is an imaging technique used to detect breast abnormalities, often combined with ultrasound for biopsy guidance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Estrogen Receptor Status">
      <data key="d0">Estrogen Receptor Status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Estrogen Receptor Status is a category of biomarker testing that determines whether breast cancer cells have receptors for estrogen, influencing treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lactation Consultant">
      <data key="d0">Lactation Consultant</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A healthcare provider who specializes in supporting and educating individuals on breastfeeding techniques and management.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactation Consultant is a category of healthcare professionals specializing in breastfeeding support and management for patients with breast-related conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Specialist">
      <data key="d0">Breast Specialist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A healthcare professional specializing in breast health and diseases, including diagnosis and treatment of breast conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast Specialist is a category of healthcare providers with expertise in diagnosing and managing breast diseases, including cancer and benign conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast Specialist is a healthcare provider with expertise in diagnosing and treating breast-related conditions, including cancers and benign lesions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gynecologist">
      <data key="d0">Gynecologist</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A physician specializing in female reproductive health, diagnosing and treating conditions of the female reproductive organs.&lt;SEP&gt;Gynecologist is a category of healthcare providers specializing in female reproductive health, including breast cancer diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Care Physician">
      <data key="d0">Primary Care Physician</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary Care Physician is a category of healthcare providers who coordinate overall patient care, including breast cancer screening and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Diagnostic Radiologist">
      <data key="d0">Breast Diagnostic Radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical professional who interprets imaging tests like mammograms and MRIs and performs needle biopsies for breast cancer detection.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast Diagnostic Radiologist is a category of healthcare professionals who interpret imaging tests like mammograms and MRIs to detect breast abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mammogram, MRI, and Ultrasound Technologist">
      <data key="d0">Mammogram, MRI, and Ultrasound Technologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A healthcare professional who operates imaging equipment such as mammograms, MRIs, and ultrasounds to assess breast tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advanced Practice Provider">
      <data key="d0">Advanced Practice Provider</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A healthcare professional including nurse practitioners, physician assistants, and certified nurse midwives who provide comprehensive care for breast health and disease prevention.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Obstetrician">
      <data key="d0">Obstetrician</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A physician specializing in pregnancy, childbirth, and postpartum care, often involved in breast health assessments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Care Provider (PCP)">
      <data key="d0">Primary Care Provider (PCP)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A general healthcare provider who offers preventive care, diagnosis, and treatment for a wide range of health issues, including breast cancer screening.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inflammatory Breast Cancer (IBC)">
      <data key="d0">Inflammatory Breast Cancer (IBC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A rare, aggressive form of breast cancer where cancer cells block lymph vessels, causing redness, swelling, and warmth in the breast.&lt;SEP&gt;Inflammatory Breast Cancer (IBC) is a category of breast cancer characterized by rapid progression and specific staging, often classified as stage 3 or 4 disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Inflammatory Breast Cancer (IBC) is a type of breast cancer characterized by cancer cells blocking lymph vessels, causing redness, swelling, and warmth in the breast.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Invasive Ductal Carcinoma (IDC)">
      <data key="d0">Invasive Ductal Carcinoma (IDC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The most common type of breast cancer, originating in the milk ducts and spreading into surrounding breast tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lobules">
      <data key="d0">Lobules</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Structures within the breast that produce milk, often the site of breast cancer origin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ducts">
      <data key="d0">Ducts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tubes in the breast that transport milk from lobules to the nipple, commonly where breast cancer begins.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="I">
      <data key="d0">I</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The author of the text, who experienced breast cancer screening and reflects on its impact on their life.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pectoral Muscle">
      <data key="d0">Pectoral Muscle</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Muscle located beneath the breast that supports its structure and is part of the anatomical framework of the chest.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ribs">
      <data key="d0">Ribs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bony structures that form part of the chest cavity and support the breast tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nurse">
      <data key="d0">Nurse</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A nurse is a healthcare professional involved in the patient's care and diagnosis process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgeon">
      <data key="d0">Surgeon</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgeon is a medical doctor who performs operations, such as thyroidectomy, to treat thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgeon is a medical professional who performed the diagnostic and treatment procedures for the patient.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgeon is a medical professional who performs surgical procedures such as colectomy and colostomy to treat colon cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgeon is a medical professional who performs thyroidectomy, a critical step in treating thyroid cancer, often followed by radioactive iodine therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Care Physician (PCP)">
      <data key="d0">Primary Care Physician (PCP)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A primary care physician is a healthcare provider who manages the patient's overall medical care and coordinates treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Counseling and Testing">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Counseling and Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic counseling and testing assess the patient's risk for hereditary breast cancer and guide treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Risk Testing">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Risk Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic risk testing analyzes the patient's DNA for mutations linked to hereditary breast cancer risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Insurance Forms">
      <data key="d0">Insurance Forms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Insurance forms are required to manage financial aspects of healthcare and treatment coverage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Online Patient Portals">
      <data key="d0">Online Patient Portals</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Online patient portals allow patients to access medical records, test results, and communicate with healthcare providers remotely.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Patient">
      <data key="d0">Patient</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The patient is an individual diagnosed with colorectal cancer who shares personal experiences of challenges in receiving care and emphasizes self-care and screening.&lt;SEP&gt;The patient is an individual diagnosed with colorectal cancer who shares personal experiences of systemic biases, treatment challenges, and emphasizes self-care and screening.&lt;SEP&gt;The patient is the individual undergoing medical evaluation and treatment for breast cancer, experiencing symptoms like a tight bra and skin changes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Comprehensive Metabolic Panel">
      <data key="d0">Comprehensive Metabolic Panel</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Comprehensive Metabolic Panel is a blood test that measures multiple substances to assess organ function and overall health.&lt;SEP&gt;A Comprehensive Metabolic Panel is a blood test that measures various substances in the blood to evaluate kidney function, electrolyte balance, and other metabolic processes relevant to cancer diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;A comprehensive metabolic panel (CMP) measures important chemicals, proteins, and other substances in your blood to assess kidney and liver function, and is used in the diagnosis and management of bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A comprehensive metabolic panel screens for biochemical markers in blood, assessing organ function and aiding in CLL-related complications monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pregnancy Test">
      <data key="d0">Pregnancy Test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A pregnancy test is performed to ensure safe treatment options for female CLL patients, as some therapies may harm fetal development.&lt;SEP&gt;A pregnancy test is used to determine if a patient is pregnant, which is important for cancer treatment planning and avoiding harmful treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Complete Blood Count">
      <data key="d0">Complete Blood Count</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Complete Blood Count (CBC) is a blood test that provides information about the types and numbers of cells in the blood, including red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, which can indicate cancer-related abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;A Complete Blood Count is a blood test that measures various blood components, including red and white blood cells, and platelets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alkaline Phosphatase">
      <data key="d0">Alkaline Phosphatase</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Alkaline phosphatase is an enzyme measured in blood tests, which can indicate cancer spread to the bone or liver.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiotracer">
      <data key="d0">Radiotracer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A radiotracer is a substance used in imaging tests to detect cancer spread by emitting radiation and being visualized by special cameras.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X-Ray">
      <data key="d0">X-Ray</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An X-ray is a medical imaging technique that uses low-dose radiation to create images of bones and detect tumors or abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sodium Fluoride PET-CT">
      <data key="d0">Sodium Fluoride PET-CT</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A sodium fluoride PET-CT scan is an alternative to bone scans, using a radioactive tracer to detect cancerous bone activity and is often combined with CT for detailed imaging.&lt;SEP&gt;Sodium Fluoride PET-CT is an alternative to bone scans, using a radiotracer made of sodium fluoride to evaluate bone metastases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast MRI">
      <data key="d0">Breast MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A breast MRI is a specialized MRI scan used to evaluate breast abnormalities, often in conjunction with mammograms, and may use contrast to highlight suspicious areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spine and Brain MRI">
      <data key="d0">Spine and Brain MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A spine and brain MRI is a type of MRI scan used to detect breast cancer metastasis to the spine or brain, with specific imaging protocols for each area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FDG PET-CT">
      <data key="d0">FDG PET-CT</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">FDG PET-CT is a combined imaging procedure using a radiotracer called F-18 fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG) to detect cancer and assess its spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FES PET-CT">
      <data key="d0">FES PET-CT</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">FES PET-CT uses a radioactive form of estrogen (FES) to detect estrogen receptor-positive cancers, particularly inflammatory breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Axillary Lymph Node">
      <data key="d0">Axillary Lymph Node</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Axillary Lymph Node refers to the lymph nodes in the armpit area, often assessed for cancer metastasis during imaging and biopsy procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Standard of Care">
      <data key="d0">Standard of Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Standard of Care represents the established medical protocols and treatments for specific diseases, based on clinical trials and evidence-based practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inflammatory Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Inflammatory Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Inflammatory Breast Cancer is a rare and aggressive type of breast cancer that can be difficult to diagnose and is often detected using specialized imaging techniques like FES PET-CT.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Estrogen Receptor-Positive">
      <data key="d0">Estrogen Receptor-Positive</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Estrogen Receptor-Positive refers to cancers that have receptors for estrogen, making them susceptible to hormone therapies and detectable via FES PET-CT.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fine-Needle Aspiration">
      <data key="d0">Fine-Needle Aspiration</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Fine-Needle Aspiration is a minimally invasive biopsy technique using a thin needle to extract fluid or tissue samples for analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vacuum-Assisted Core Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Vacuum-Assisted Core Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Vacuum-Assisted Core Biopsy uses suction to remove tissue samples through a special device, often used for larger tissue collection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Incisional Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Incisional Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Incisional Biopsy removes a small amount of tissue through a surgical incision, typically used when other biopsy methods are not feasible.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Excisional Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Excisional Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Excisional Biopsy removes the entire abnormal area, often used when other methods fail or when the biopsy results are inconclusive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ultrasound-Guided Needle Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Ultrasound-Guided Needle Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Ultrasound-Guided Needle Biopsy uses real-time ultrasound imaging to guide the needle into the tumor for precise tissue sampling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stereotactic Needle Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Stereotactic Needle Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Stereotactic Needle Biopsy is a specialized biopsy technique using mammography to guide the needle into a precise location for tissue sampling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mastectomy">
      <data key="d0">Mastectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical procedure to remove the breast, often used in the treatment of breast cancer, and a context for sentinel lymph node biopsy.&lt;SEP&gt;Mastectomy is a surgical procedure to remove all or part of the breast, commonly used as a local therapy following systemic treatment for inflammatory breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Mastectomy is a surgical procedure to remove all or part of the breast, often used as a treatment for inflammatory breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lumpectomy">
      <data key="d0">Lumpectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical procedure to remove a tumor from the breast while preserving as much healthy tissue as possible, and a context for sentinel lymph node biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="In Situ Hybridization (ISH)">
      <data key="d0">In Situ Hybridization (ISH)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A molecular biology technique used to detect and locate specific DNA or RNA sequences in tissue samples, often used to confirm HER2 status when IHC results are inconclusive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biomarker testing">
      <data key="d0">Biomarker testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biomarker testing analyzes DNA mutations, proteins, or other molecular features in tumors to guide personalized cancer treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing analyzes biological markers in blood or tissue to identify specific characteristics of cancer cells and guide treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing refers to the analysis of genes and proteins to identify mutations and proteins that may guide cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor markers">
      <data key="d0">Tumor markers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor markers are proteins detected in blood or tissue samples to assess cancer progression, though they are not reliable alone for diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor mutational burden">
      <data key="d0">Tumor mutational burden</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor mutational burden (TMB) measures DNA mutations in cancer cells, helping predict response to immune checkpoint inhibitors.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutational burden (TMB) refers to the number of mutations in tumor DNA, with high TMB (TMB-H) often associated with immunotherapy response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor mutation testing">
      <data key="d0">Tumor mutation testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor mutation testing examines specific DNA mutations in cancer cells to identify targetable genetic alterations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MSI-H/dMMR mutation">
      <data key="d0">MSI-H/dMMR mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MSI-H/dMMR (microsatellite instability-high/deficient mismatch repair) is a DNA repair defect linked to certain cancers, affecting their response to immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;MSI-H/dMMR mutation refers to microsatellite instability or deficient mismatch repair, indicating potential for immune checkpoint inhibitor response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PD-1 and PD-L1 testing">
      <data key="d0">PD-1 and PD-L1 testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PD-1 and PD-L1 testing identifies immune proteins on cancer cells, guiding treatment with checkpoint inhibitor therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunohistochemistry">
      <data key="d0">Immunohistochemistry</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunohistochemistry (IHC) is a staining technique to detect proteins in cancer cells, aiding in diagnosis and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Next-generation sequencing">
      <data key="d0">Next-generation sequencing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Next-generation sequencing (NGS) is a high-throughput DNA sequencing method used to analyze cancer cell mutations after other biomarker tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic risk testing">
      <data key="d0">Genetic risk testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic risk testing evaluates an individual's genetic profile to assess inherited risks for cancer and other hereditary conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic risk testing identifies inherited mutations (germline mutations) to assess cancer risk and guide treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA tests">
      <data key="d0">BRCA tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BRCA tests detect mutations in the BRCA1 and BRCA2 genes, increasing risk for breast, ovarian, and other cancers, and affecting treatment efficacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Family health history">
      <data key="d0">Family health history</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Family health history involves documenting hereditary cancer risks to inform genetic testing and treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Distress screening">
      <data key="d0">Distress screening</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Distress screening assesses emotional well-being in cancer patients, addressing psychological impacts of diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Distress">
      <data key="d0">Distress</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Distress is a category related to the emotional and mental state of patients undergoing cancer diagnosis and treatment, often addressed through screening and support services.&lt;SEP&gt;Distress refers to psychological and emotional challenges faced by cancer patients and caregivers, requiring specialized support.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TNM Scores">
      <data key="d0">TNM Scores</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">TNM Scores are a category used in cancer staging to describe tumor size (T), lymph node involvement (N), and distant metastasis (M), guiding treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Staging System">
      <data key="d0">Staging System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Staging System is a category that organizes cancer progression based on tumor extent, lymph node status, and metastasis, including anatomic and prognostic staging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Estrogen Receptor (ER) Status">
      <data key="d0">Estrogen Receptor (ER) Status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Estrogen Receptor (ER) Status is a category assessing the presence of estrogen receptors in breast cancer cells, affecting hormone-based treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Progesterone Receptor (PR) Status">
      <data key="d0">Progesterone Receptor (PR) Status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Progesterone Receptor (PR) Status is a category evaluating the presence of progesterone receptors in breast cancer cells, impacting hormone receptor testing and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormone Receptor">
      <data key="d0">Hormone Receptor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormone Receptor is a category encompassing estrogen and progesterone receptor statuses, critical for determining cancer behavior and treatment responses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor is a category representing a mass of abnormal cells, central to cancer diagnosis, staging, and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Exam">
      <data key="d0">Breast Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Breast Exam is a category of physical examination used to detect abnormalities in breast tissue, often part of routine screening and diagnostic processes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Test">
      <data key="d0">Test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Test is a category encompassing all diagnostic and monitoring procedures used in cancer care, including imaging, blood tests, and biopsies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TNM system">
      <data key="d0">TNM system</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The TNM system is a standardized framework for staging breast cancer, categorizing tumor size (T), lymph node involvement (N), and distant metastasis (M) to determine cancer progression.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM system is used to stage breast cancer by evaluating tumor size (T), lymph node status (N), and distant metastasis (M).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prognostic stage">
      <data key="d0">Prognostic stage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Prognostic stage includes anatomic TNM plus tumor grade and biomarker status such as estrogen receptor (ER), progesterone receptor (PR), and human epidermal growth factor receptor 2 (HER2).&lt;SEP&gt;Prognostic stage integrates the TNM system with tumor grade and biomarker status (e.g., ER, PR, HER2) to predict outcomes and guide treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pathologic stage">
      <data key="d0">Pathologic stage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pathologic stage applies to patients who have undergone surgery as the initial treatment for breast cancer, providing more specific details about cancer extent and lymph node involvement.&lt;SEP&gt;Pathologic stage is determined post-surgery, using tissue analysis to assess tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis, providing more precise staging than clinical staging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical staging">
      <data key="d0">Clinical staging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical staging is conducted before treatment, relying on physical exams, biopsies, and imaging to estimate cancer extent without surgical intervention.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical staging is the rating given before any treatment, based on physical exams, biopsies, and imaging tests to assess cancer extent without surgical intervention.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast cancer">
      <data key="d0">Breast cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Breast cancer is a malignancy staged using the TNM system, biomarkers, and grading to evaluate treatment options and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a type of cancer that is staged using the TNM system and other factors like biomarkers and grading to determine treatment approaches and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Estrogen receptor (ER)">
      <data key="d0">Estrogen receptor (ER)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Estrogen receptor (ER) is a biomarker indicating whether cancer cells respond to hormone therapy, influencing treatment strategies and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Estrogen receptor (ER) is a protein that indicates whether the cancer responds to hormone therapy, with ER-positive tumors being more responsive to such treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Progesterone receptor (PR)">
      <data key="d0">Progesterone receptor (PR)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Progesterone receptor (PR) is a biomarker used alongside ER to assess hormone sensitivity and guide endocrine therapy decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Progesterone receptor (PR) is a protein that indicates the cancer's response to hormone therapy, often used alongside estrogen receptor status to guide treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human epidermal growth factor receptor 2 (HER2)">
      <data key="d0">Human epidermal growth factor receptor 2 (HER2)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HER2 is a protein marker indicating aggressive tumor behavior, with HER2-positive cancers often requiring targeted therapies like trastuzumab.&lt;SEP&gt;Human epidermal growth factor receptor 2 (HER2) is a protein that may indicate the cancer's aggressiveness and treatment options, with HER2-positive tumors often requiring targeted therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade">
      <data key="d0">Grade</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade describes how abnormal the tumor cells look under a microscope, with higher grades indicating more aggressive cancer and a higher risk of recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;Grade describes how abnormal tumor cells appear under a microscope (histology), with higher grades indicating faster-growing and more aggressive cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Grade describes the abnormality of cancer cells under a microscope, with higher grades correlating to faster growth and increased recurrence risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Grade describes the abnormality of tumor cells under a microscope (histology), with higher grades indicating more aggressive cancer behavior and higher recurrence risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Grade is a classification system for gliomas indicating how abnormal the tumor cells are, with grades 1-4 reflecting growth rates and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Grade measures the aggressiveness of cancer cells, with low grade (slow-growing) and high grade (fast-growing) classifications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic Breast Cancer refers to cancer that has spread to distant sites from the breast, often originating from earlier stages and requiring specialized treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Noninvasive Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Noninvasive Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Noninvasive Breast Cancer, such as Ductal Carcinoma In Situ (DCIS), is confined to the breast ducts and has not spread to surrounding tissues or lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HER2-Targeted Therapy">
      <data key="d0">HER2-Targeted Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HER2-Targeted Therapy is a treatment approach for breast cancer that targets the HER2 protein, often used in cases where cancer cells overexpress this protein.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone-Strengthening Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Bone-Strengthening Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone-Strengthening Therapy is a treatment aimed at preventing or managing bone loss in breast cancer patients, particularly those undergoing hormonal therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Myeloid Growth Factors">
      <data key="d0">Myeloid Growth Factors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Myeloid Growth Factors are medications used to stimulate blood cell production, often administered to manage side effects of chemotherapy in breast cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pathology Report">
      <data key="d0">Pathology Report</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pathology Report is a document containing detailed findings from tissue or cell analysis, used to determine cancer staging, grading, and biomarker status.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inflammatory breast cancer">
      <data key="d0">Inflammatory breast cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Inflammatory breast cancer is a type of breast cancer characterized by inflammation of the breast tissue, treatable with a combination of local and systemic therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Inflammatory breast cancer is a type of breast cancer that is treatable with a combination of local and systemic therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local therapy">
      <data key="d0">Local therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Local therapy focuses on localized treatment of the breast, chest wall, and lymph nodes, involving procedures like mastectomy and radiation therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Local therapy focuses on the breast, chest wall, and lymph node area and includes procedures like mastectomy and radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HER2-targeted therapy">
      <data key="d0">HER2-targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HER2-targeted therapy is a treatment that specifically targets the HER2 protein in cancer cells, often used in combination with other systemic therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;HER2-targeted therapy is a treatment that targets the HER2 protein in cancer cells, often used in combination with other therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;HER2-targeted therapy is drug therapy that treats HER2-positive breast cancer by targeting the HER2 protein, often used in combination with chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;HER2-targeted therapy is drug therapy that treats HER2-positive breast cancer by targeting the HER2 protein.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endocrine therapy">
      <data key="d0">Endocrine therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Endocrine therapy blocks estrogen/progesterone to treat hormone receptor-positive breast cancer. It includes drugs like aromatase inhibitors, SERMs, and ovarian suppression methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Endocrine therapy is a treatment that targets hormones that may contribute to cancer growth, often used in combination with other therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Endocrine therapy is systemic drug therapy that targets hormone receptors to slow cancer growth, often used in conjunction with other treatments like CDK4/6 inhibitors and mTOR inhibitors.&lt;SEP&gt;Endocrine therapy targets hormone-sensitive cancer cells, often used in combination with other systemic therapies for hormone receptor-positive cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Social workers">
      <data key="d0">Social workers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Social workers are professionals who help patients and their families navigate the complexities of cancer care, including financial and insurance issues.&lt;SEP&gt;Social workers assist patients and families in navigating financial, insurance, and emotional challenges related to cancer care and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="External beam radiation therapy">
      <data key="d0">External beam radiation therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">External beam radiation therapy (EBRT) is a treatment method using a machine outside the body to aim radiation at the breast and lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;External beam radiation therapy (EBRT) uses a machine outside the body to deliver radiation to the breast and lymph nodes, commonly used in breast cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CDK4/6 inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">CDK4/6 inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CDK4/6 inhibitors are a class of drugs used in hormone receptor-positive, HER2-negative breast cancer to control cancer growth when combined with endocrine therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;CDK4/6 inhibitors are a class of drugs used in hormone receptor-positive, HER2-negative breast cancer to control cancer growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="mTOR inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">mTOR inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">mTOR inhibitors are a class of drugs that target the mTOR protein, which helps cells grow and divide. They are used to restore the effectiveness of endocrine therapy in breast cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;mTOR inhibitors are used to restore the effectiveness of endocrine therapy in some breast cancer cases by targeting the mTOR protein pathway.&lt;SEP&gt;mTOR inhibitors are used to restore the effectiveness of endocrine therapy in some breast cancer cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Myeloid growth factors">
      <data key="d0">Myeloid growth factors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Myeloid growth factors are treatments that help increase white blood cell counts and prevent infections caused by chemotherapy, serving as supportive care.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloid growth factors are treatments that help increase white blood cell counts and prevent infections caused by chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Modified radical mastectomy">
      <data key="d0">Modified radical mastectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Modified radical mastectomy is a surgical procedure involving the removal of the entire breast and some lymph nodes, often used in breast cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph node radiation therapy">
      <data key="d0">Lymph node radiation therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph node radiation therapy is a treatment method targeting lymph nodes to reduce cancer spread, often part of a comprehensive treatment plan.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regional nodal irradiation (RNI)">
      <data key="d0">Regional nodal irradiation (RNI)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Regional nodal irradiation (RNI) is a specific form of lymph node radiation therapy used to target cancer in the lymph nodes of the armpit and collarbone regions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PARP inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">PARP inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PARP inhibitors are drugs that block the PARP protein, which repairs DNA damage in cancer cells. They are used in breast cancer treatment for patients with BRCA1/BRCA2 mutations and HER2-negative tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PIK3CA inhibitor">
      <data key="d0">PIK3CA inhibitor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PIK3CA inhibitors target the PIK3CA gene mutation, which promotes cancer cell growth and resistance to treatments. Alpelisib (Piqray) is an example of this category.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Aromatase inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">Aromatase inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Aromatase inhibitors (AIs) block the enzyme aromatase, preventing androgen conversion to estrogen. Examples include anastrozole (Arimidex) and exemestane (Aromasin).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Selective Estrogen Receptor Modulators (SERMs)">
      <data key="d0">Selective Estrogen Receptor Modulators (SERMs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">SERMs block estrogen from binding to receptors. Tamoxifen and toremifene (Fareston) are examples, used in endocrine therapy for breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Selective Estrogen Receptor Degraders (SERDs)">
      <data key="d0">Selective Estrogen Receptor Degraders (SERDs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">SERDs block and destroy estrogen receptors. Fulvestrant (Faslodex) and elacestrant (Orserdu) are examples, used in advanced hormone receptor-positive breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) agonists">
      <data key="d0">Gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) agonists</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">GnRH agonists suppress ovarian hormone production, used in ovarian suppression for endocrine therapy. Examples include goserelin (Zoladex) and leuprolide (Lupron Depot).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Primary CNS Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary CNS Lymphoma is a rare and aggressive cancer that most often affects the brain, spinal cord, cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), and/or the eyes. It is characterized by the overgrowth of lymphocytes and can cause neurological symptoms such as weakness, coordination issues, and cognitive impairments.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS Lymphoma is a specific category of lymphoma that originates in the central nervous system, including the brain, spinal cord, and eyes.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS Lymphoma is a type of cancer affecting the central nervous system, characterized by the presence of lymphoma cells in the brain, spinal cord, or surrounding tissues. It is treated with various therapeutic approaches including radiation, chemotherapy, immunotherapy, and stem cell rescue.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS Lymphoma is a type of cancer affecting the central nervous system, including the brain, spinal cord, and eyes, often treated with chemotherapy or radiation therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS Lymphoma is a type of cancer affecting the central nervous system, often treated with various therapeutic approaches such as radiation, chemotherapy, and immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS Lymphoma is a type of cancer that originates in the central nervous system, specifically the brain or spinal cord, and is discussed in the context of treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS lymphoma is a rare and aggressive cancer affecting the central nervous system (CNS), originating from lymphocytes in the immune system and often linked to immunodeficiency or viral infections like HIV and Epstein-Barr virus.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS lymphoma is a type of cancer affecting the central nervous system, requiring specialized diagnostic and treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS lymphoma is a type of cancer affecting the central nervous system, with questions about causes, diagnosis, and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS lymphoma is a type of cancer originating in the central nervous system (CNS), characterized by the presence of lymphocytes in the brain or spinal cord, requiring diagnostic confirmation through tissue sampling and imaging techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS lymphoma is a type of cancer originating in the central nervous system, often treated with chemotherapy, radiation, and stem cell rescue.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS lymphoma is a type of cancer that originates in the central nervous system (CNS), characterized by the presence of lymphocytes in the brain or spinal cord.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS lymphoma is a type of cancer that originates in the central nervous system, specifically in the brain, and is often treated with chemotherapy, steroids, or clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HER2-negative breast cancer">
      <data key="d0">HER2-negative breast cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HER2-negative breast cancer refers to tumors that lack the HER2 protein, making them eligible for certain targeted therapies like PARP inhibitors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Premenopause">
      <data key="d0">Premenopause</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Premenopause refers to the transitional phase before menopause, characterized by hormonal fluctuations and potential changes in menstrual cycles, often discussed in the context of endocrine therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIV">
      <data key="d0">HIV</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HIV (Human Immunodeficiency Virus) is a category of viral infection that increases the risk of developing anal cancer, necessitating HIV testing for at-risk individuals.&lt;SEP&gt;HIV (Human Immunodeficiency Virus) is a risk factor for anal cancer due to its association with immunosuppression, which increases susceptibility to HPV-related malignancies.&lt;SEP&gt;HIV (Human Immunodeficiency Virus) is a viral infection that increases the risk of developing anal cancer and necessitates HIV testing for at-risk individuals.&lt;SEP&gt;HIV (Human Immunodeficiency Virus) is a virus that attacks the immune system, increasing the risk of primary CNS lymphoma and other infections.&lt;SEP&gt;HIV is a virus that can affect cancer treatment, and patients with HIV may be referred to an HIV specialist.&lt;SEP&gt;HIV is a virus that may influence cervical cancer treatment, requiring specialist care and not affecting eligibility for standard therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Epstein-Barr Virus">
      <data key="d0">Epstein-Barr Virus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV) is a category of biomarker tested during cancer staging to assess potential treatment options and prognosis in nasopharyngeal carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Epstein-Barr Virus is a virus associated with certain stages of cervical cancer, such as T0, where it is detected in neck lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) is a biomarker tested during cancer staging to assess potential treatment options and prognosis in nasopharyngeal carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) is a common herpesvirus that can remain dormant in the body and is associated with an increased risk of primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Autoimmune Disease">
      <data key="d0">Autoimmune Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Autoimmune disease is a condition where the immune system mistakenly attacks healthy tissues, with examples like lupus, which may elevate the risk of primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Organ Transplant">
      <data key="d0">Organ Transplant</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Organ transplant is a medical procedure requiring immunosuppressive drugs, which increases the risk of infections and conditions like primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Older Adult">
      <data key="d0">Older Adult</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Older adults, particularly those in their 60s or 70s, are at higher risk for primary CNS lymphoma, though it can occur at any age.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Central Nervous System">
      <data key="d0">Central Nervous System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The central nervous system (CNS) consists of the brain and spinal cord, which are primary sites for primary CNS lymphoma to develop.&lt;SEP&gt;The central nervous system consists of the brain and spinal cord, where gliomas develop and can disrupt normal neurological function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Meninges">
      <data key="d0">Meninges</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Meninges are protective membranes covering the brain and spinal cord, which can be affected by the spread of primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cerebrospinal Fluid">
      <data key="d0">Cerebrospinal Fluid</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) is a liquid surrounding the brain and spinal cord, potentially harboring lymphoma cells and contributing to symptom manifestation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immune System">
      <data key="d0">Immune System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The immune system defends the body against infections and diseases, but its dysfunction, as in immunodeficiency disorders, increases the risk of primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunodeficiency Disorder">
      <data key="d0">Immunodeficiency Disorder</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunodeficiency disorders weaken the immune system, making individuals more susceptible to infections and cancers like primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Symptoms">
      <data key="d0">Symptoms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptoms are manifestations of a brain tumor, including nausea, weakness, speech difficulties, and changes in personality.&lt;SEP&gt;Symptoms of primary CNS lymphoma include neurological issues like headaches, seizures, and cognitive changes, often related to tumor location in the CNS.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Amenorrhea">
      <data key="d0">Amenorrhea</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Amenorrhea refers to the absence of menstrual periods, which can occur during or after endocrine therapy for conditions like premenopause.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neurological Exam">
      <data key="d0">Neurological Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A neurological exam is a diagnostic procedure assessing alertness, balance, coordination, reflexes, and sensory functions to evaluate CNS health and guide further testing.&lt;SEP&gt;A neurological exam is a diagnostic procedure involving tests to assess a patient's alertness, balance, coordination, reflexes, and sensory functions to evaluate CNS health.&lt;SEP&gt;A neurological exam is a medical procedure involving tests to assess alertness, thinking, balance, coordination, reflexes, and sensory functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neurosurgeon">
      <data key="d0">Neurosurgeon</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A neurosurgeon is a medical specialist who performs procedures like stereotactic and open biopsies to diagnose and treat brain tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;A neurosurgeon is a medical specialist who performs surgical procedures like biopsies and resections for brain tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;A neurosurgeon is a surgeon who performs stereotactic biopsies to obtain CNS tissue samples for diagnosing lymphoma, using precision-guided techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;A neurosurgeon is a surgeon who specializes in treating disorders of the nervous system, including performing stereotactic biopsies for CNS lymphoma diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Laboratory">
      <data key="d0">Laboratory</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A laboratory is a facility where tissue samples from biopsies are analyzed by pathologists to confirm cancer diagnoses and determine tumor characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;A laboratory is a facility where tissue samples from biopsies are analyzed by pathologists to confirm cancer diagnoses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stereotactic Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Stereotactic Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A stereotactic biopsy is a minimally invasive procedure using imaging guidance to extract tissue samples from the CNS for definitive cancer diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;A stereotactic biopsy is a minimally invasive procedure where a small tissue sample is removed from the CNS using precise imaging guidance to diagnose lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A stereotactic biopsy is a precise medical procedure used to diagnose primary CNS lymphoma by extracting tissue samples from hard-to-reach brain areas.&lt;SEP&gt;Stereotactic biopsy is a medical procedure used to diagnose primary CNS lymphoma by extracting a tissue sample from the brain or spinal cord.&lt;SEP&gt;Stereotactic biopsy is a precise procedure to remove a small tissue sample from a hard-to-reach brain tumor for diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FASTRO">
      <data key="d0">FASTRO</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">FASTRO is an organization associated with the image provided in the text, likely related to radiology or medical imaging, as mentioned in the context of Stephanie E. Weiss, MD.&lt;SEP&gt;FASTRO is an organization associated with the image provided in the text, likely related to radiology or medical imaging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stephanie E. Weiss">
      <data key="d0">Stephanie E. Weiss</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Stephanie E. Weiss, MD, is a pathologist who provided the image referenced in the text, specializing in analyzing tissue samples for cancer diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vitreoretinal Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Vitreoretinal Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vitreoret,inal Lymphoma is a subtype of primary CNS lymphoma that affects the retina and vitreous of the eye.&lt;SEP&gt;Vitreoretinal Lymphoma is a subtype of primary CNS lymphoma that affects the retina and vitreous of the eye.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vitrectomy">
      <data key="d0">Vitrectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Vitrectomy is a surgical procedure to remove a portion of the vitreous gel from the eye, used to test for lymphoma cells in the eye.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eye Exam">
      <data key="d0">Eye Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Eye exam is a diagnostic procedure to check for signs of lymphoma in the eyes, including the retina and vitreous, using a slit lamp microscope.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app is a digital tool offering patient-friendly information on cancer care and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app is a digital tool offering patient-friendly information on cancer care, treatment options, and distress management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stem Cell Rescue">
      <data key="d0">Stem Cell Rescue</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stem Cell Rescue is a procedure involving high-dose chemotherapy followed by the infusion of stored stem cells to restore bone marrow function after treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Stem Cell Rescue is a procedure involving high-dose chemotherapy followed by the infusion of stored stem cells to restore bone marrow function after treatment. It is used to regenerate blood cells and protect the body from infections.&lt;SEP&gt;Stem cell rescue is a medical procedure involving the removal, preservation, and reinfusion of a patient's stem cells to restore blood-forming cells after high-dose chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Stem cell rescue is a procedure to restore blood cell production after high-dose chemotherapy, commonly used in lymphoma treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Depression">
      <data key="d0">Depression</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Depression is a common psychological condition in cancer patients, often linked to distress and requiring integrated care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anxiety">
      <data key="d0">Anxiety</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anxiety involves persistent worry and stress in cancer patients, necessitating mental health interventions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIV Blood Test">
      <data key="d0">HIV Blood Test</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An HIV blood test is critical for patients with primary CNS lymphoma, as HIV-related cases require specialized treatment protocols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatitis B Testing">
      <data key="d0">Hepatitis B Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatitis B testing is required for patients on immunotherapy to monitor for reactivation of the virus, which can interfere with treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lactate Dehydrogenase (LDH) Test">
      <data key="d0">Lactate Dehydrogenase (LDH) Test</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">LDH test measures enzyme levels linked to cell damage, often indicating cancer progression or treatment response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Cancer Institute">
      <data key="d0">National Cancer Institute</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The National Cancer Institute (NCI) is a U.S. federal agency that conducts and supports cancer research, including clinical trials and treatment guidelines.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U.S. Food and Drug Administration">
      <data key="d0">U.S. Food and Drug Administration</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is a regulatory agency that approves new drugs and treatments, including those tested in clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Library of Medicine">
      <data key="d0">National Library of Medicine</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The National Library of Medicine (NLM) is a U.S. federal agency that provides access to medical information and resources, including clinical trial databases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Steroids">
      <data key="d0">Steroids</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Steroids are a class of medications used to reduce inflammation and shrink tumors, often temporarily in the treatment of primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary CNS lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Primary CNS lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary CNS lymphoma is a type of cancer that affects the central nervous system, including the brain, spinal cord, and eyes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intrathecal chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Intrathecal chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Intrathecal chemotherapy is a treatment method where chemotherapy is infused directly into the cerebrospinal fluid to target cancer cells in the central nervous system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intraocular chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Intraocular chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Intraocular chemotherapy is a treatment method where chemotherapy is administered directly into the eye to treat cancer cells present there.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Whole-brain radiation therapy (WBRT)">
      <data key="d0">Whole-brain radiation therapy (WBRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Whole-brain radiation therapy (WBRT) is a treatment method that delivers radiation to the entire brain to target cancer cells, including microscopic tumors not visible on MRI scans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intrathecal Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Intrathecal Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Intrathecal Chemotherapy is a procedure where chemotherapy drugs are infused directly into the cerebrospinal fluid to target cancer cells in the central nervous system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intraocular Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Intraocular Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Intraocular Chemotherapy is a procedure where chemotherapy drugs are administered directly into the eye to treat cancerous growths in ocular tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Whole-Brain Radiation Therapy (WBRT)">
      <data key="d0">Whole-Brain Radiation Therapy (WBRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">WBRT is a radiation technique used to treat CNS lymphoma by targeting the entire brain, often combined with chemotherapy or immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Whole-Brain Radiation Therapy (WBRT) is a treatment that delivers radiation to the entire brain to target cancer cells, including microscopic tumors not visible on MRI scans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-dose Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">High-dose Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-dose Chemotherapy is an intensive treatment that kills cancer cells but also damages healthy bone marrow, requiring stem cell rescue to recover blood cell production.&lt;SEP&gt;High-dose Chemotherapy is an intensive treatment that kills cancer cells but also damages healthy bone marrow, requiring stem cell rescue to recover blood cell production. It is a critical step in the stem cell rescue process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rituximab (Rituxan)">
      <data key="d0">Rituximab (Rituxan)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Rituximab is a monoclonal antibody drug used in immunotherapy to target CD20 proteins on cancer cells. It enhances the immune system's ability to destroy malignant cells and is a key component of immunotherapy for Primary CNS Lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Rituximab is a monoclonal antibody used in immunotherapy to target CD20 proteins on cancer cells, enhancing the immune system's ability to destroy malignant cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multidisciplinary Care">
      <data key="d0">Multidisciplinary Care</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Multidisciplinary care refers to a team of healthcare professionals (e.g., neuro-oncologists, radiation oncologists, palliative care specialists) collaborating to manage complex conditions like CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Quality of Life">
      <data key="d0">Quality of Life</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Quality of life encompasses a patient's overall well-being, ability to perform daily activities, and emotional health, prioritized in treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Dose Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">High-Dose Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-dose chemotherapy destroys cancer cells but also harms healthy bone marrow stem cells, necessitating stem cell rescue for recovery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rituximab">
      <data key="d0">Rituximab</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Rituximab is an immunotherapy drug often used in combination with chemotherapy to treat primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Rituximab is an immunotherapy drug that targets specific proteins on cancer cells, used to treat primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Palliative Care">
      <data key="d0">Palliative Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Palliative care focuses on symptom management and comfort for patients with advanced cancer, provided at any stage of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Integrative Medicine">
      <data key="d0">Integrative Medicine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Integrative medicine combines conventional treatments with complementary therapies (e.g., meditation, nutritional advice) to enhance patient well-being.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Counseling">
      <data key="d0">Counseling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Counseling provides psychological support to patients and families dealing with cancer diagnosis, treatment, and quality of life challenges.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nutritional Advice">
      <data key="d0">Nutritional Advice</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nutritional advice addresses dietary needs to support patients undergoing cancer treatment, improving strength and recovery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Meditation">
      <data key="d0">Meditation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Meditation is a stress-reduction technique used to improve mental well-being in cancer patients, often part of integrative medicine approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physical Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Physical Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Physical therapy helps patients regain mobility and strength after cancer treatment, supporting quality of life and daily functioning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Methot,rexate">
      <data key="d0">Methot,rexate</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Methotrexate is a chemotherapy drug commonly used to treat primary CNS lymphoma, inhibiting cancer cell growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Induction">
      <data key="d0">Induction</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Induction is a phase of treatment involving initial aggressive therapy to reduce tumor size or control cancer progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Consolidation">
      <data key="d0">Consolidation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Consolidation is a treatment phase following induction, aiming to eliminate residual cancer cells and prevent recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment for the Spine">
      <data key="d0">Treatment for the Spine</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment for the spine involves targeted therapies to address cancerous growths in the spinal region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment for the Eyes">
      <data key="d0">Treatment for the Eyes</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment for the eyes focuses on managing cancerous conditions affecting the ocular region, often using radiation or chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Colon Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Colon Cancer is a type of cancer that develops in the colon, part of the large intestine, and is covered in the text along with information on screening and prevention.&lt;SEP&gt;Colon Cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the colon and is staged using the TNM system to determine treatment options and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Colon Cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the colon, characterized by the uncontrolled growth of abnormal cells in the colon lining.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Induction Phase">
      <data key="d0">Induction Phase</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Induction Phase is the first stage of treatment for primary CNS lymphoma, aiming to eliminate as many lymphoma cells as possible through chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Consolidation Phase">
      <data key="d0">Consolidation Phase</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Consolidation Phase is the second stage of treatment for primary CNS lymphoma, designed to destroy any remaining cancer cells and prevent recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-dose Methotrexate">
      <data key="d0">High-dose Methotrexate</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-dose Methotrexate is a chemotherapy drug used in the induction phase to treat primary CNS lymphoma, overcoming the blood-brain barrier.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Temozolomide">
      <data key="d0">Temozolomide</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Temozolomide is a chemotherapy drug that may be used as an alternative to high-dose methotrexate in the treatment of primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ibrutinib">
      <data key="d0">Ibrutinib</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ibrutinib is a medication used as an alternative to high-dose methotrexate for the induction treatment of primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lenalidomide">
      <data key="d0">Lenalidomide</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lenalidomide is a drug that may be used in combination with rituximab for the induction treatment of primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pemetrexed">
      <data key="d0">Pemetrexed</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pemetrexed is a chemotherapy drug that may be used as an alternative to high-dose methotrexate in the treatment of primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pomalidomide">
      <data key="d0">Pomalidomide</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pomalidomide is a chemotherapy drug that may be used as an alternative to high-dose methotrexate in the treatment of primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Polyp">
      <data key="d0">Polyp</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A polyp is a non-cancerous growth that can develop in the colon or rectum, and can potentially become cancerous over time.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyps are abnormal growths in the colon or rectum that can become cancerous; they are often removed during colonoscopy to prevent cancer progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyps are abnormal growths on the inner lining of the colon wall, which can be benign or malignant, and are classified into pedunculated or sessile types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hyperplastic Polyp">
      <data key="d0">Hyperplastic Polyp</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A hyperplastic polyp is a type of polyp that rarely becomes cancerous and is considered a benign growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inflammatory Polyp">
      <data key="d0">Inflammatory Polyp</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An inflammatory polyp is a type of polyp that rarely becomes cancerous and is associated with inflammation in the colon or rectum.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Polypectomy">
      <data key="d0">Polypectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A polypectomy is a minor surgical procedure used to remove polyps from the colon or rectum, helping to prevent the development of colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mismatch Repair Deficiency (dMMR)">
      <data key="d0">Mismatch Repair Deficiency (dMMR)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">dMMR is a biomarker indicating defective DNA repair mechanisms, associated with Lynch syndrome and certain colore,ctal cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;dMMR is a biomarker indicating defects in DNA repair mechanisms, associated with Lynch syndrome and certain colorectal cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ctDNA Testing">
      <data key="d0">ctDNA Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ctDNA testing (circulating tumor DNA testing) is a non-invasive diagnostic method used to detect early-stage colon cancer by analyzing DNA fragments released by tumor cells into the bloodstream.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility and Family Planning">
      <data key="d0">Fertility and Family Planning</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility and family planning are critical considerations for young adults with colon cancer, involving strategies like sperm banking, egg freezing, and ovarian tissue banking to preserve reproductive options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mismatch Repair Testing">
      <data key="d0">Mismatch Repair Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mismatch repair testing identifies biomarkers like dMMR or MSI-H in colon cancer, helping classify tumors and guide treatment decisions, such as targeted therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Family Health History">
      <data key="d0">Family Health History</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Family health history is a key factor in assessing inherited risks for colon cancer, including conditions like Lynch syndrome and familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Tissue Banking">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Tissue Banking</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian tissue banking is a fertility preservation technique where part of the ovary is frozen to retain eggs for later reimplantation, particularly for patients facing treatments that may compromise fertility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Familial Adenomatous Polyposis">
      <data key="d0">Familial Adenomatous Polyposis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) is an inherited condition characterized by the development of numerous polyps in the colon, which can progress to cancer, and is evaluated through family health history and genetic testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Polyps with Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Polyps with Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Polyps with cancer refer to benign growths in the colon that may develop into malignant tumors, often monitored for progression to invasive cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemotherapy After Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Chemotherapy After Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemotherapy after surgery is a treatment strategy used to eliminate residual cancer cells following surgical removal of tumors, reducing the risk of recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surveillance">
      <data key="d0">Surveillance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surveillance involves monitoring patients for cancer recurrence or progression after treatment, using imaging, blood tests, and clinical assessments.&lt;SEP&gt;Surveillance involves regular medical check-ups and tests to monitor for cancer recurrence or progression, particularly in patients with early-stage or treated colorectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Surveillance refers to follow-up testing after treatment to detect new cancer growth early, recommended for patients with no signs of cancer recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;Surveillance refers to the monitoring process conducted after treatment to detect early signs of cancer recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;Surveillance refers to the ongoing monitoring process after treatment to detect early signs of cervical cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="American Joint Committee">
      <data key="d0">American Joint Committee</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The American Joint Committee is a medical organization that provides guidelines and staging information for cancer treatment and management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colectomy">
      <data key="d0">Colectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A colectomy is a surgical procedure involving the removal of all or part of the colon, often performed as a treatment for colon cancer, and is considered a key intervention in managing the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Colectomy is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of a portion of the colon, typically performed to treat colon cancer or severe blockages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stent">
      <data key="d0">Stent</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A stent is a mesh metal tube used to keep the colon open, allowing stool and gas to pass, often temporarily before further surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Doctor">
      <data key="d0">Doctor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Doctor is a medical professional who oversees patient care, surgical procedures, and post-operative follow-up for colon cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="American Joint Committee on Cancer">
      <data key="d0">American Joint Committee on Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) created a staging system for cancer, including the TNM system, to determine the extent of cancer for prognosis and treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) is a medical organization that develops the TNM staging system for cancer, which helps determine the stage of colon cancer based on tumor, node, and metastasis factors.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) is an organization that developed the TNM staging system for cancer, which is used to determine the extent of cancer and guide treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 0">
      <data key="d0">Stage 0</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 0 bladder cancer is non-invasive, with abnormal cells confined to the inner lining of the bladder, requiring less aggressive treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 0 colon cancer refers to abnormal cells confined to the innermost layer of the colon wall, also known as carcinoma in situ, indicating the earliest stage of cancer development.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 0 is the earliest stage of bladder cancer, affecting only the inner lining (urothelial layer) of the bladder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1 bladder cancer involves a tumor confined to the connective tissue layer of the bladder wall, indicating limited invasion and a need for localized treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 1 colon cancer has grown into the second or third layer of the colon wall without spreading to nearby lymph nodes or distant areas.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 1 is non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer that has grown through the inner lining into the connective tissue layer.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 1 refers to an early stage of cancer where treatment recommendations include colonoscopy and surveillance follow-up testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 2">
      <data key="d0">Stage 2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 2 bladder cancer is muscle-invasive, with the tumor extending into the bladder muscle, necessitating more aggressive treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 2 colon cancer has grown into or beyond the fourth layer of the colon wall without spreading to nearby lymph nodes or distant areas.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 2 is a cancer stage requiring additional surveillance testing beyond colonoscopy, including physical exams and blood tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 2 is muscle-invasive bladder cancer that has grown into the muscle layer but not the fatty layer or distant sites.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 3">
      <data key="d0">Stage 3</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 3 bladder cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes and fat tissue around the bladder, requiring systemic therapies and multimodal approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 3 colon cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes or contains tumor deposits in the fat around the colon.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 3 is an advanced cancer stage requiring comprehensive surveillance testing, such as CT scans and carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) blood tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 4">
      <data key="d0">Stage 4</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 4 bladder cancer is metastatic, having spread to distant organs, and typically requires systemic therapies and palliative care.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 4 colon cancer has spread to areas outside the colon and nearby lymph nodes, commonly metastasizing to the liver and/or lungs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Checkpoint Inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">Checkpoint Inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Checkpoint inhibitors are a class of immunotherapy drugs that block cancer cells' ability to evade the immune system, recommended for dMMR/MSI-H cancers before colectomy.&lt;SEP&gt;Checkpoint inhibitors are a class of immunotherapy drugs that block natural braking mechanisms to enhance the immune system's ability to attack cancer cells, including Keytruda and Opdivo.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nivolumab">
      <data key="d0">Nivolumab</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nivolumab (Opdivo) is a checkpoint inhibitor immunotherapy drug used for dMMR/MSI-H cancers, often combined with ipilimumab (Yervoy) for enhanced treatment efficacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ipilimumab">
      <data key="d0">Ipilimumab</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ipilimumab (Yervoy) is a checkpoint inhibitor drug used in combination with nivolumab to enhance immunotherapy effectiveness for dMMR/MSI-H cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anastomotic Leak">
      <data key="d0">Anastomotic Leak</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An anastomotic leak is a complication following colectomy where the surgical connection between the colon's ends fails, leading to leakage and potential infection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hernia">
      <data key="d0">Hernia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hernia is a condition where organs or tissues push through weakened muscles or tissues, often a complication of surgical procedures like colectomy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOLFOX">
      <data key="d0">FOLFOX</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FOLFOX is a chemotherapy regimen combining fluorouracil, leucovorin, and oxaliplatin, used for treating advanced colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CAPEOX">
      <data key="d0">CAPEOX</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CAPEOX is a chemotherapy regimen combining capecitabine and oxaliplatin, used for treating advanced colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5-Fluorouracil (5-FU)/Leucovorin">
      <data key="d0">5-Fluorouracil (5-FU)/Leucovorin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">5-Fluorouracil (5-FU) combined with leucovorin is a chemotherapy drug used in regimens like FOLFOX to treat colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Capecitabine">
      <data key="d0">Capecitabine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Capecitabine is an oral chemotherapy drug used in regimens for anal cancer, associated with hand-foot syndrome as a common side effect.&lt;SEP&gt;Capecitabine is an oral chemotherapy drug used in regimens like CAPEOX to treat colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Protocols">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Protocols</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment protocols are standardized guidelines for managing cancer, including surgical, chemotherapy, and immunotherapy approaches tailored to specific stages and patient profiles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Guidelines">
      <data key="d0">Medical Guidelines</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical guidelines are evidence-based recommendations provided by organizations like AJCC and NCCN to standardize cancer diagnosis, staging, and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Head and neck cancers">
      <data key="d0">Head and neck cancers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Head and neck cancers are a group of cancers arising in the head or neck area, including nasopharyngeal carcinoma, oral cavity cancers, and laryngeal cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nasopharyngeal carcinoma (NPC)">
      <data key="d0">Nasopharyngeal carcinoma (NPC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nasopharyngeal carcinoma (NPC) is a rare cancer affecting the nasopharynx, most commonly occurring in people from Asia, Native America, and North Africa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surveillance Follow-up testing">
      <data key="d0">Surveillance Follow-up testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Surveillance Follow-up testing is a medical procedure initiated after cancer treatment to monitor for recurrence, often involving imaging tests and blood work.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Asia">
      <data key="d0">Asia</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Asia is a geographic region where nasopharyngeal carcinoma (NPC) is most commonly diagnosed, particularly in populations from parts of Asia, Native America, and North Africa.&lt;SEP&gt;Asia is a geographic region where nasopharyngeal carcinoma (NPC) is most commonly observed, particularly in parts of Southeast Asia and China.&lt;SEP&gt;Asia is a geographic region with a high prevalence of nasopharyngeal carcinoma (NPC), particularly in populations from parts of Asia, Native America, and North Africa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Native America">
      <data key="d0">Native America</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Native America is a geographic region where nasopharyngeal carcinoma (NPC) is frequently diagnosed, alongside Asia and North Africa.&lt;SEP&gt;Native America refers to the geographic regions historically inhabited by Indigenous peoples, where NPC is frequently observed alongside populations in Asia and North Africa.&lt;SEP&gt;Native America refers to the geographic regions historically inhabited by Indigenous peoples, where nasopharyngeal carcinoma (NPC) is frequently observed alongside populations in Asia and North Africa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="North Africa">
      <data key="d0">North Africa</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">North Africa is a geographic region associated with a higher incidence of nasopharyngeal carcinoma (NPC), alongside Asia and Native America, due to shared genetic and environmental factors.&lt;SEP&gt;North Africa is a geographic region associated with a higher prevalence of nasopharyngeal carcinoma (NPC), alongside Asia and Native America.&lt;SEP&gt;North Africa is a geographic region where nasopharyngeal carcinoma (NPC) is commonly found, along with Asia and Native America.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma (NPC) is a category of cancer originating in the nasopharynx, a region of the upper respiratory tract, and is classified using staging systems like TNM.&lt;SEP&gt;Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma (NPC) is a type of cancer affecting the nasopharynx, a region connecting the nasal cavity to the pharynx. It is the central focus of this text, with emphasis on testing, treatment planning, and associated risk factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma (NPC) is a type of cancer that affects the nasopharynx, a region connecting the nasal cavity to the pharynx. It is the focus of this text, with emphasis on testing, treatment planning, and associated risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma (NPC) is a type of cancer that originates in the nasopharynx, a part of the upper respiratory tract.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Comprehensive Cancer Center">
      <data key="d0">Comprehensive Cancer Center</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A Comprehensive Cancer Center is an organization specializing in the evaluation and treatment of complex cancers like nasopharyngeal carcinoma, offering multidisciplinary care and expert resources.&lt;SEP&gt;A Comprehensive Cancer Center is an organization specializing in the evaluation and treatment of complex cancers like nasopharyngeal carcinoma, offering multidiscyplinary care and expert resources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing for NPC">
      <data key="d0">Testing for NPC</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing for NPC involves medical procedures such as imaging tests, biopsies, and biomarker testing to diagnose and stage the cancer, forming the basis for treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Epstein-Barr virus">
      <data key="d0">Epstein-Barr virus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) is a common virus that causes infectious mononucleosis and is associated with EBV-related cancers, particularly nasopharyngeal cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) is a viral infection linked to nasopharyngeal carcinoma (NPC), with DNA testing used to assess its role in cancer development and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eye and Endocrine Evaluation">
      <data key="d0">Eye and Endocrine Evaluation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Eye and endocrine evaluation assesses potential complications from head and neck cancers, including impacts on vision and hormonal balance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Head and neck cancer">
      <data key="d0">Head and neck cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Head and neck cancer refers to cancers affecting the head, neck, and surrounding areas, often impacting dental health, speech, swallowing, and hormonal functions.&lt;SEP&gt;Head and neck cancer refers to cancers affecting the head, neck, and throat, often linked to HPV infections and requiring specialized treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nasopharyngeal cancer">
      <data key="d0">Nasopharyngeal cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nasopharyngeal cancer is a type of head and neck cancer that can affect the eyes and requires specialized diagnostic tests such as eye exams.&lt;SEP&gt;Nasopharyngeal cancer is a type of head and neck cancer that is often EBV-related and is staged using TNM scoring systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dental exam">
      <data key="d0">Dental exam</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A dental exam is a diagnostic procedure to assess the health of the jaw, teeth, and gums, particularly important for patients with head and neck cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eye exam">
      <data key="d0">Eye exam</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An eye exam is recommended to detect potential effects of nasopharyngeal cancer on vision and to monitor treatment-related complications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endocrine tests">
      <data key="d0">Endocrine tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Endocrine tests evaluate hormone levels in the thyroid and pituitary glands, which may be affected by radiation therapy to the head and neck.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hearing test">
      <data key="d0">Hearing test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A hearing test identifies hearing loss caused by tumors or treatment of head and neck cancers, including conditions like acoustic neuroma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nutrition assessment">
      <data key="d0">Nutrition assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A nutrition assessment evaluates dietary needs to ensure adequate nutrition before cancer treatment, which can impact metabolism and overall health.&lt;SEP&gt;Nutrition assessment evaluates dietary habits and nutritional status to assess their impact on health and cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Speech and swallowing assessment">
      <data key="d0">Speech and swallowing assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">This assessment evaluates a patient's ability to swallow and speak, critical for planning treatment and managing post-treatment recovery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Performance status">
      <data key="d0">Performance status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Performance status is an assessment of a patient's physical ability to perform daily activities, often used to evaluate cancer progression and treatment effectiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance status measures a patient's general health and ability to perform daily tasks, influencing treatment plan decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging tests">
      <data key="d0">Imaging tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging tests (e.g., CT, MRI) are used to visualize tumors, assess cancer spread, and guide treatment decisions for head and neck cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging tests are a category of diagnostic procedures used to visualize internal structures, encompassing X-rays, bone scans, CT scans, MRI scans, and PET scans.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging tests are a category of diagnostic procedures used to visualize internal structures, including X-rays, bone scans, CT scans, MRI scans, and PET scans.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging tests are procedures used to visualize internal body structures, often employed in cancer diagnosis, staging, and monitoring treatment response.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging tests use techniques like X-rays, CT scans, or MRI to visualize internal structures and detect abnormalities such as tumors or ulcers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biomarker">
      <data key="d0">Biomarker</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A measurable indicator used to detect cancer, assess its progression, and guide treatment decisions through biopsy analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;A measurable indicator used to detect cancer, assess treatment response, or predict disease progression, often analyzed through biopsy samples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CDC.gov/tobacco">
      <data key="d0">CDC.gov/tobacco</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A U.S. government organization providing tobacco cessation resources and information on health risks associated with smoking and vaping.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SmokeFree.gov">
      <data key="d0">SmokeFree.gov</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A government website offering support and resources for individuals seeking to quit smoking or vaping, focusing on cancer prevention and health improvement.&lt;SEP&gt;SmokeFree.gov is a resource offering support for quitting tobacco use and understanding cancer-related health information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BeTobaccoFree.gov">
      <data key="d0">BeTobaccoFree.gov</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A public health initiative providing guidance and tools for quitting tobacco use, emphasizing its impact on cancer risk and treatment outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;BeTobaccoFree.gov is a website providing guidance and resources for individuals seeking to quit tobacco and manage cancer risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PD-L1 testing">
      <data key="d0">PD-L1 testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PD-L1 testing assesses the presence of a protein on cancer cells that can suppress immune responses, guiding treatment with immune checkpoint inhibitors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor HPV testing">
      <data key="d0">Tumor HPV testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor HPV testing identifies HPV infections in cancer tissues, particularly relevant for head and neck cancers, and informs treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Tumor biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor biopsy specifically refers to the extraction of tissue samples from a tumor to analyze genetic markers, mutations, or cancer types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunotherapy drugs">
      <data key="d0">Immunotherapy drugs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunotherapy drugs are treatments targeting cancer cells by enhancing the immune system's ability to recognize and attack tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Programmed cell death protein 1 (PD-1)">
      <data key="d0">Programmed cell death protein 1 (PD-1)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PD-1 is a protein on immune cells that can inhibit immune responses to cancer, making it a target for immunotherapy drugs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Programmed death ligand 1 (PD-L1)">
      <data key="d0">Programmed death ligand 1 (PD-L1)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PD-L1 is a protein on cancer cells that binds to PD-1, suppressing immune responses and influencing treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Staging">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Staging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Staging is a category that describes the process of determining the extent of cancer (tumor size, lymph node involvement, metastasis) to predict prognosis and guide treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer Staging is the process of determining the extent of bladder cancer, which helps in planning treatment and predicting prognosis based on the disease's progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging is a process used to describe the extent of cancer, including tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis, to guide treatment and predict prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging refers to the process of determining the extent of cancer spread, which influences treatment decisions and patient outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cervical Cancer is a common cancer of the female reproductive system, most commonly caused by long-term infection with human papillomavirus (HPV).&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical Cancer is a malignancy originating in the cervix, primarily caused by persistent HPV infection, and includes subtypes like squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical Cancer is a type of cancer affecting the cervix, diagnosed through procedures like biopsy and cone biopsy, and managed with various treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical Cancer is a type of cancer caused by HPV, with screening methods like HPV/Pap co-tests to detect early changes.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical Cancer is a type of cancer originating in the cervix, discussed in the context of treatment options, risks, and surgical procedures like hysterectomy and lymph node dissection.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical Cancer is a type of cancer originating in the cervix, requiring multidisciplinary diagnostic and treatment approaches including imaging, fertility preservation, and staging.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical Cancer is a type of cancer originating in the cervix, requiring regular follow-up procedures, imaging, and biomarker testing for monitoring and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical Cancer is a type of cancer that affects the cervix and is diagnosed through various procedures such as biopsy and cone biopsy.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical Cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the cervix and is discussed in the context of treatment options and risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical Cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the cervix, often monitored through follow-up procedures and imaging to detect recurrence or spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical cancer is a malignancy originating in the cervix, with treatment options varying based on staging and biological factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical cancer is a type of cancer originating in the cervix, often diagnosed through cervical biopsy and staged using the FIGO system.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the cervix, with staging systems like FIGO used to determine treatment options and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the cervix, with treatment options varying based on stages and risk factors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Dysplasia">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Dysplasia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cervical Dysplasia refers to areas of abnormal cells on the lining of the cervix, classified under CIN and progressing to cervical cancer if untreated.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical Dysplasia refers to areas of abnormal cells on the lining of the cervix, which can progress to cervical cancer if left untreated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CIN (Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia)">
      <data key="d0">CIN (Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CIN is a classification of cervical intraepithelial neoplasia, graded as 1, 2, or 3 based on the depth of abnormal cells on the cervical lining.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LSIL (Low-grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion)">
      <data key="d0">LSIL (Low-grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">LSIL refers to mild dysplasia (CIN 1), characterized by minor abnormalities in the cervical cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HSIL (High-grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion)">
      <data key="d0">HSIL (High-grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HSIL refers to moderate or severe dysplasia (CIN 2 and 3), considered a pre-cancerous condition that can progress to cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma (NPC)">
      <data key="d0">Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma (NPC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma (NPC) is a type of cancer that starts in the nasopharynx, a part of the upper throat.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human Papillomavirus (HPV)">
      <data key="d0">Human Papillomavirus (HPV)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Human Papillomavirus (HPV) is the most common sexually transmitted infection and the primary cause of cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Human papillomavirus (HPV) is a viral infection that can lead to abnormal cell changes, including anal cancer, and is a major risk factor for the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pap Smear">
      <data key="d0">Pap Smear</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pap Smear is a screening test that detects abnormal cells in the cervix, significantly reducing the incidence of cervical cancer in the United States.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endometrial Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Endometrial Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Endometrial Cancer is a type of uterine cancer that originates in the inner lining of the uterus (endometrium).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Uterine Sarcomas">
      <data key="d0">Uterine Sarcomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Uterine Sarcomas are rare cancers of the uterus that arise from the connective tissue cells, distinct from endometrial cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Stroma">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Stroma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cervical Stroma is the thick layer of muscular tissue beneath the cervical lining, which can be invaded by cervical cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parametrium">
      <data key="d0">Parametrium</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Parametrium is the fatty and connective tissue surrounding the uterus and cervix, which can be affected by cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ectocervix">
      <data key="d0">Ectocervix</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ectocervix is the outer part of the cervix, visible during a pelvic exam, lined with squamous cells and connected to the vagina.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endocervix">
      <data key="d0">Endocervix</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Endocervix is the inner part of the cervix, forming a canal between the vagina and uterus, lined with columnar (glandular) cells that produce mucus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamo-Columnar Junction (Transformation Zone)">
      <data key="d0">Squamo-Columnar Junction (Transformation Zone)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamo-Columnar Junction (Transformation Zone) is the area where the squamous cells of the ectocervix meet the columnar cells of the endocervix, where most cervical cancers originate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="External Os">
      <data key="d0">External Os</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">External Os is the opening of the ectocervix into the vagina, which opens slightly during menstruation to allow blood to pass.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Internal Os">
      <data key="d0">Internal Os</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Internal Os is the upper part of the endocervix that serves as the opening between the uterus and the cervix.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vagina">
      <data key="d0">Vagina</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vagina is the muscular passage through which babies are born and the site of the external os of the cervix.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Uterus">
      <data key="d0">Uterus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Uterus is the organ where a baby develops before birth, connected to the cervix, vagina, fallopian tubes, and ovaries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovaries">
      <data key="d0">Ovaries</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovaries are the female reproductive organs that produce eggs and hormones, located on either side of the uterus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fallopian Tubes">
      <data key="d0">Fallopian Tubes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fallopian Tubes are the narrow tubes that connect the ovaries to the uterus, facilitating the transport of eggs for fertilization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HSIL">
      <data key="d0">HSIL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HSIL (High-Grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion) refers to moderate or severe dysplasia (CIN 2 and 3), considered a pre-cancerous condition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LSIL">
      <data key="d0">LSIL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">LSIL (Low-Grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesion) refers to mild dysplasia (CIN 1), a less severe form of cervical dysplasia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CIN">
      <data key="d0">CIN</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CIN (Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia) is a classification system for abnormal cells on the cervix, ranging from mild (CIN 1) to severe (CIN 2/3).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Cervix (NECC)">
      <data key="d0">Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Cervix (NECC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NECC is a rare, aggressive subtype of cervical cancer characterized by neuroendocrine differentiation, distinct from squamous or glandular cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adenosquamous Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Adenosquamous Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adenosquamous Carcinoma is a mixed-type cervical cancer containing both squamous and glandular cell components, sometimes termed 'mixed' tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vaccine">
      <data key="d0">Vaccine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vaccine is a preventive medical intervention targeting HPV strains, reducing the risk of cervical cancer and related infections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cervical Biopsy is a diagnostic procedure involving tissue sampling from the cervix to detect cervical cancer and pre-cancerous conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical Biopsy is a procedure involving the removal of tissue samples from the cervix to diagnose cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical biopsy is a diagnostic procedure involving the removal and testing of cervical tissue to detect dysplasia or cancer, often followed by cone biopsy or treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cone Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Cone Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cone Biopsy is a procedure that removes a cone-shaped section of the cervix, including the transformation zone, and can serve as both a diagnostic test and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Cone Biopsy is a procedure that removes a cone-shaped section of the cervix, serving both as a diagnostic test and treatment for early-stage cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cone biopsy is a surgical procedure to remove a cone-shaped portion of the cervix, used for diagnosing or treating early-stage cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cold Knife Conization">
      <data key="d0">Cold Knife Conization</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cold Knife Conization is a surgical technique used in cone biopsy to remove cervical tissue with a scalpel.&lt;SEP&gt;Cold Knife Conization is a surgical technique using a scalpel to remove cervical tissue during a cone biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Loop Electrosurgical Excision Procedure">
      <data key="d0">Loop Electrosurgical Excision Procedure</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Loop Electrosurgical Excision Procedure (LEEP) is a method using an electrified wire loop to excise cervical tissue during a cone biopsy.&lt;SEP&gt;Loop Electrosurgical Excision Procedure (LEEP) is a technique used in cone biopsy to remove cervical tissue using an electrified wire loop.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endocervical Curettage">
      <data key="d0">Endocervical Curettage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Endocervical Curettage is a follow-up procedure using a spoon-shaped tool to scrape tissue from the cervical canal after a cone biopsy.&lt;SEP&gt;Endocervical Curettage is a procedure where a spoon-shaped tool is used to scrape tissue from the cervical canal after a cone biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Computed Tomography">
      <data key="d0">Computed Tomography</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Computed Tomography (CT) is an imaging technique that provides detailed cross-sectional images of the body to assess cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Computed Tomography (CT) provides detailed cross-sectional body images to evaluate cancer spread and guide treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET/CT">
      <data key="d0">PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PET/CT combines positron emission tomography (PET) and computed tomography (CT) to detect cancer and assess its spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health History and Physical Exam">
      <data key="d0">Health History and Physical Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Health History and Physical Exam involve assessing a patient's medical background, lifestyle, and physical condition to inform cervical cancer treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET">
      <data key="d0">PET</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">PET (Positron Emission Tomography) is an imaging test that uses radioactive materials to detect cancer by highlighting areas with high sugar metabolism, often combined with CT scans or used alongside MRI.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility and Pregnancy">
      <data key="d0">Fertility and Pregnancy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility and pregnancy considerations are critical for patients with cervical cancer, emphasizing fertility-sparing treatments and options like ovarian transposition, egg freezing, and surrogacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surrogacy">
      <data key="d0">Surrogacy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surrogacy is a reproductive option where a surrogate carries a pregnancy using frozen embryos or eggs, often considered for patients unable to conceive naturally after cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cystoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Cystoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cystoscopy is a medical procedure involving the examination of the bladder under anesthesia, used to detect signs of cancer in the urinary tract.&lt;SEP&gt;Cystoscopy is a medical procedure involving the use of a scope to visually examine the bladder and urinary tract, commonly used to detect bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cystoscopy is a medical procedure to examine the urethra and bladder for signs of cancer, involving biopsy and potential tumor removal during the procedure.&lt;SEP&gt;Cystoscopy is a medical procedure to visually inspect the urethra and bladder for cancer signs using a cystoscope, often followed by biopsy or tumor removal.&lt;SEP&gt;Cystoscopy is a procedure that uses a viewing device to examine the bladder for cancerous growths.&lt;SEP&gt;Cystoscopy is a procedure using a cystoscope to examine the bladder and urinary tract, often performed to detect cancerous abnormalities or biopsy suspicious areas.&lt;SEP&gt;Cystoscopy is an exam to look inside the urethra and bladder for signs of cancer, involving biopsy and potential tumor removal during the procedure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Proctoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Proctoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Proctos  is a procedure using a proctoscope to examine the anus and rectum, aiding in the detection of cancer spread or biopsy of suspicious tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Proctoscopy is a diagnostic procedure to examine the rectum and lower colon under anesthesia, used to identify cancerous signs in the bowel.&lt;SEP&gt;Proctoscopy is a procedure using a proctoscope to examine the anus and rectum, aiding in the detection of cancer spread or biopsy of suspicious tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tobacco Use">
      <data key="d0">Tobacco Use</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tobacco use is a significant risk factor for cancer, particularly cervical cancer, and is associated with nicotine addiction and withdrawal challenges.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Distress During Cancer Care">
      <data key="d0">Distress During Cancer Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Distress During Cancer Care encompasses emotional and psychological challenges faced by patients, including anxiety, depression, and financial concerns, requiring supportive care interventions.&lt;SEP&gt;Distress during cancer care refers to the emotional and psychological challenges faced by patients and families throughout the treatment process, requiring specialized support and resources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FIGO system">
      <data key="d0">FIGO system</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The FIGO system is a staging framework used to classify the extent of cervical cancer before treatment, guiding treatment decisions and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trachelectomy">
      <data key="d0">Trachelectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Trachelectomy is a fertility-sparing surgical procedure to remove the cervix, preserving the uterus and ovaries for potential future pregnancies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hysterectomy">
      <data key="d0">Hysterectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Hysterectomy is the surgical removal of the uterus (including the cervix), with variations in extent depending on cancer staging and treatment goals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Laparotomy">
      <data key="d0">Laparotomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Laparotomy is a traditional abdominal surgical approach used for extensive procedures like radical hysterectomy, involving a large incision.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Minimally Invasive Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Minimally Invasive Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Minimally invasive surgery, including laparoscopic techniques, involves small incisions and is associated with reduced recovery time and scarring compared to traditional methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Quitting Smoking">
      <data key="d0">Quitting Smoking</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Quitting Smoking is a preventive measure to reduce the risk of bladder cancer, as smoking is a major risk factor for the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Quitting smoking is a health behavior that can improve cancer treatment outcomes, with support available for individuals seeking to stop smoking.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cardiovascular Disease">
      <data key="d0">Cardiovascular Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cardiovascular Disease is a long-term risk associated with estrogen deficiency and is mentioned in the context of atrophy and its complications.&lt;SEP&gt;Cardiovascular Disease is a long-term risk associated with estrogen deficiency, mentioned in the context of atrophy and its complications.&lt;SEP&gt;Cardiovascular Disease is a long-term risk associated with low estrogen levels, often resulting from surgical menopause due to ovary removal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Osteoporosis">
      <data key="d0">Osteoporosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Osteoporosis is a bone density loss condition linked to estrogen deficiency, commonly seen in patients experiencing surgical menopause.&lt;SEP&gt;Osteoporosis is a condition characterized by bone loss, which is a long-term risk of estrogen deficiency as described in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;Osteoporosis is a condition characterized by bone loss, which is a long-term risk of estrogen deficiency as mentioned in the text.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Menopause">
      <data key="d0">Menopause</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Menopause is a physiological state associated with hormonal changes, often induced by radiation therapy or surgical removal of ovaries, leading to symptoms like early menopause.&lt;SEP&gt;Menopause is a physiological state linked to hormonal changes, often induced by radiation therapy or ovarian removal, leading to symptoms like early menopause.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node Dissection">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node Dissection</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph Node Dissection is a surgical procedure performed during cervical cancer treatment to remove and test lymph nodes for cancer spread, including sentinel lymph node biopsy.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph node dissection is a surgical procedure to remove cancerous lymph nodes, either as part of APR or as a standalone treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT)">
      <data key="d0">External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">EBRT is a non-invasive radiation therapy technique used to target cancerous tissues in cervical cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT) is a radiation treatment method used for anaplastic thyroid cancer and to relieve symptoms from cancer spread, using high-energy x-rays.&lt;SEP&gt;External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT) is a radiation treatment method using high-energy x-rays to target cancer cells, often used for anaplastic thyroid cancer or to relieve symptoms from metastatic spread.&lt;SEP&gt;External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT) is a radiation treatment method where radiation is delivered from outside the body, with IMRT being a specialized form used for anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT) is a specific radiation treatment method where a machine delivers high-energy waves to the cancer site in fractions over several weeks.&lt;SEP&gt;External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT) is a specific type of radiation therapy where a machine aims radiation at the cancer site, delivered in fractions over several weeks.&lt;SEP&gt;External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT) is a treatment option for aggressive or metastatic thyroid cancers that cannot be surgically removed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy (IMRT)">
      <data key="d0">Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy (IMRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy (IMRT) is a radiation treatment category that uses precise beams to target cancer cells, commonly recommended for anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy (IMRT) is an advanced form of EBRT that uses multiple beams to target cancer precisely, minimizing damage to surrounding healthy tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy (IMRT) is an advanced radiation technique using multiple beams to target cancer precisely, minimizing damage to healthy tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy (SBRT)">
      <data key="d0">Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy (SBRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">SBRT is a specialized radiation therapy technique used for limited metastases, delivering high-dose radiation precisely to tumors while minimizing damage to surrounding healthy tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;SBRT is a specialized radiation therapy used for limited metastases, delivering high-dose radiation precisely to tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy (SB, a highly focused form of EBRT that delivers precise radiation doses over a few sessions, targeting tumors in the liver, lungs, or bones.&lt;SEP&gt;Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy (SBRT) is a highly focused form of EBRT that delivers precise radiation doses over a few sessions, targeting tumors in the liver, lungs, or bones.&lt;SEP&gt;Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy (SBRT) is a specialized form of EBRT used for metastatic cervical cancer, delivering high doses in fewer sessions to tumors in the liver, lungs, or bones.&lt;SEP&gt;Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy (SBRT) is a specialized radiation approach for metastatic cervical cancer, delivering high doses in fewer sessions to tumors in the liver, lungs, or bones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Laparoscopic Surgical Staging">
      <data key="d0">Laparoscopic Surgical Staging</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Laparoscopic Surgical Staging is a minimally invasive procedure to access and remove lymph nodes for pre-treatment staging of cervical cancer, aiding in treatment decision-making.&lt;SEP&gt;Laparoscopic Surgical Staging is a minimally invasive procedure to access and remove lymph nodes for pre-treatment staging of cervical cancer, aiding in treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Lymph Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic Lymph Nodes are anatomical structures in the pelvic region commonly removed during cervical cancer treatment to assess cancer spread and guide therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Pelvic Lymph Nodes are anatomical structures in the pelvic region that are commonly removed during cervical cancer treatment to assess cancer spread and guide therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Para-Aortic Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Para-Aortic Lymph Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Para-Aortic Lymph Nodes are abdominal lymph nodes that may be removed during cervical cancer treatment if cancer has spread beyond the pelvic region.&lt;SEP&gt;Para-Aortic Lymph Nodes are lymph nodes in the abdomen that may be removed during cervical cancer treatment if cancer has spread beyond the pelvic region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Menopausal Hormone Therapy (MHT)">
      <data key="d0">Menopausal Hormone Therapy (MHT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Menopausal Hormone Therapy (MHT) is a hormone treatment to alleviate symptoms of early menopause caused by radiation therapy or ovarian removal.&lt;SEP&gt;Menopausal Hormone Therapy (MHT) is a type of hormone therapy used to alleviate symptoms of early menopause induced by radiation therapy or ovarian removal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormone Replacement Therapy (HRT)">
      <data key="d0">Hormone Replacement Therapy (HRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormone Replacement Therapy (HRT) is an older term for Menopausal Hormone Therapy (MHT), used to manage menopause symptoms following cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone Replacement Therapy (HRT) is an older term for Menopausal Hormone Therapy (MHT), used to manage menopause symptoms post-cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Survivorship">
      <data key="d0">Survivorship</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Survivorship encompasses long-term care and support for cancer patients, addressing treatment-related side effects and quality of life considerations.&lt;SEP&gt;Survivorship encompasses long-term care, monitoring for recurrence, and managing health after cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Survivorship encompasses ongoing care and monitoring for cancer survivors, including surveillance testing and symptom management.&lt;SEP&gt;Survivorship encompasses the ongoing care and support for cancer survivors, including regular surveillance testing and management of long-term effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Survivorship encompasses the physical, emotional, and social aspects of life after anal cancer treatment, including follow-up care and lifestyle adjustments.&lt;SEP&gt;Survivorship refers to the care and support provided to cancer survivors, encompassing physical, emotional, and financial aspects of life after treatment, including managing long-term side effects and maintaining health.&lt;SEP&gt;Survivorship refers to the care and support provided to cancer survivors, encompassing physical, emotional, and financial aspects of life after treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Survivorship refers to the long-term care and support for cancer patients, including managing treatment-related side effects and quality of life considerations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EBRT">
      <data key="d0">EBRT</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">EBRT (External Beam Radiation Therapy) is a non-invasive radiation treatment for cervical cancer recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;EBRT (External Beam Radiation Therapy) is a radiation treatment method that delivers high-energy beams to the tumor site, typically used as part of chemoradiation for locally advanced cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;EBRT (External Beam Radiation Therapy) is a radiation treatment modality used for cervical cancer recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT) is a non-invasive treatment that uses high-energy beams to target and destroy cancer cells in the pelvis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d0">Brachytherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Brachytherapy is a form of internal radiation therapy where radioactive material is placed directly into or near the tumor, allowing targeted high-dose radiation with minimal impact on surrounding tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is a form of radiation therapy that uses internal radiation sources to target cancerous tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is a form of radiation therapy used in treating localized cervical cancer recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is a form of radiation therapy used to treat localized cervical cancer recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is a localized radiation therapy technique using internal radiation sources to treat cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is a localized radiation therapy technique where radioactive sources are placed directly into or near the tumor to destroy cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HDR Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d0">HDR Brachytherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HDR (High Dose-Rate) Brachytherapy is a specialized brachytherapy technique where a high dose of radiation is delivered in short, intense sessions over a short period, commonly used for cervical cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LDR Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d0">LDR Brachytherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">LDR (Low Dose-Rate) Brachytherapy involves delivering a lower dose of radiation over a longer period, often used for more advanced cervical cancers requiring extended treatment sessions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intracavitary Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d0">Intracavitary Brachytherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Intracavitary Brachytherapy is a brachytherapy method where radioactive sources are placed inside body cavities (e.g., uterus, vagina) to target the tumor directly, often using an applicator like the tandem and ring system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Interstitial Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d0">Interstitial Brachytherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Interstitial Brachytherapy is a specialized brachytherapy technique where radioactive material is placed directly into the tumor or adjacent tissues, typically used for advanced cancers involving the parametrium.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tandem and Ring Applicator">
      <data key="d0">Tandem and Ring Applicator</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Tandem and Ring Applicator is a device used in HDR Brachytherapy to position radioactive sources within the cervix and uterus, enabling precise radiation delivery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tandem and Ovoid Applicator">
      <data key="d0">Tandem and Ovoid Applicator</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Tandem and Ovoid Applicator is an alternative device used in HDR Brachytherapy, featuring hollow, rounded capsules instead of a ring for radiation source placement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA)">
      <data key="d0">U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The FDA is the regulatory body responsible for approving cancer treatments, including drugs like Nivolumab and Pembrolizumab, and overseeing clinical trials for safety and efficacy.&lt;SEP&gt;The FDA is the regulatory body responsible for approving drugs and treatments for cancer, including clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is a federal agency responsible for approving drugs, devices, and treatments for medical use, including those developed through clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is a federal agency responsible for regulating and approving drugs, medical devices, and treatments, including those developed through clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Library of Medicine (NLM)">
      <data key="d0">National Library of Medicine (NLM)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The National Library of Medicine (NLM) is a U.S. federal agency that provides access to health information and resources, including clinical trial databases like ClinicalTrials.gov.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Library of Medicine (NLM) is a federal agency that provides access to health information and maintains the ClinicalTrials.gov database, which lists ongoing clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Options">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Options</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Options refer to the various therapeutic approaches available for bladder cancer, including surgery, chemotherapy, radiation, immunotherapy, and targeted therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment options encompass various therapeutic approaches, including surgery, radiation therapy, systemic therapy, and clinical trials, tailored to specific cancer types and stages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility-Sparing Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Fertility-Sparing Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility-sparing treatment refers to procedures that preserve reproductive function, such as trachelectomy, for patients with early-stage cervical cancer who wish to maintain fertility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Early-Stage Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Early-Stage Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Early-stage cancer refers to cancer that has not yet spread beyond the original site, often treated with surgery or localized therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Locally Advanced Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Locally Advanced Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Locally advanced cancer indicates cancer that has spread to nearby tissues or lymph nodes but not to distant organs, typically managed with chemoradiation or advanced surgical techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic Cancer is a category of cancer that has spread to other parts of the body, such as bone, and behaves differently from primary cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic Cancer refers to cancer that has spread to areas far from the neck, such as the lungs, liver, muscles, bones, brain, and spinal cord.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer is cancer that has spread from its original site to other organs, such as the liver, lungs, or bones, making it more challenging to treat.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer is cancer that has spread to distant organs, often requiring systemic therapies like chemotherapy, targeted therapy, or immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer refers to cancer that has spread beyond its original site, such as stage IV anal cancer, requiring aggressive treatment approaches like immunotherapy or chemoradiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer refers to cancer that has spread beyond its original site, such as stage IV anal cancer, requiring aggressive treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="early-stage cancer">
      <data key="d0">early-stage cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Early-stage cancer refers to cancer confined to the cervix, typically managed with surgery or fertility-sparing options.&lt;SEP&gt;Early-stage cancer refers to cancer that is only in the cervix, typically diagnosed and treated with surgery or fertility-sparing options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="locally advanced cancer">
      <data key="d0">locally advanced cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Locally advanced cancer has spread beyond the cervix but not to distant sites, and is treated with chemoradiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Locally advanced cancer has spread beyond the cervix but not to distant sites, requiring chemoradiation for treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="metastatic cancer">
      <data key="d0">metastatic cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic cancer has spread to distant organs, necessitating aggressive treatment strategies like chemoradiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer has spread to distant organs, requiring more aggressive treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1A1">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1A1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1A1 is a specific stage of early-stage cervical cancer, often treated with cone biopsy or radical trachelectomy.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 1A1 is an early-stage cervical cancer with small tumors, often treated with cone biopsy or radical trachelectomy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1A2">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1A2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1A2 involves slightly larger tumors without LVSI, with treatment options including cone biopsy or radical trachelectomy.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 1A2 is another stage of early-stage cervical cancer with specific criteria for treatment options like cone biopsy or radical trachelectomy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1B1">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1B1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1B1 involves cancer spreading to the upper vagina, treated with radical trachelectomy and pelvic lymph node evaluation.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 1B1 refers to cancer spreading to the upper vagina, treated with radical trachelectomy and pelvic lymph node evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1B2">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1B2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1B2 is a more advanced stage where fertility-sparing surgery is less common, often requiring abdominal procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 1B2 is a more advanced stage where fertility-sparing surgery is less common, often requiring abdominal surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="fertility-sparing treatment">
      <data key="d0">fertility-sparing treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility-sparing treatment options preserve the uterus and ovaries, allowing for future natural pregnancy.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility-sparing treatment preserves the uterus and ovaries, enabling future natural pregnancies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="chemoradiation">
      <data key="d0">chemoradiation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemoradiation combines chemotherapy and radiation therapy to treat locally advanced cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation is a combination of chemotherapy and radiation therapy used to treat locally advanced cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="radical trachelectomy">
      <data key="d0">radical trachelectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radical trachelectomy is a surgical procedure removing the cervix while preserving the uterus.&lt;SEP&gt;Radical trachelectomy is a surgical procedure to remove the cervix and surrounding tissues while preserving the uterus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LVSI">
      <data key="d0">LVSI</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">LVSI (lymphovascular space invasion) indicates cancer spread to lymph vessels, influencing lymph node removal decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;LVSI (lymphovascular space invasion) indicates cancer spread to lymph vessels, influencing treatment decisions like lymph node removal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="external beam radiation therapy (EBRT)">
      <data key="d0">external beam radiation therapy (EBRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">EBRT is a radiation therapy used post-surgery or as primary treatment for cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;EBRT is a type of radiation therapy used post-surgery or as a primary treatment for cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="vaginal brachytherapy">
      <data key="d0">vaginal brachytherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vaginal brachytherapy is internal radiation therapy used alongside EBRT for cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Vaginal brachytherapy is internal radiation therapy used in conjunction with EBRT for cervical cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="platinum chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">platinum chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Platinum chemotherapy involves platinum-based drugs, often used with radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="cone biopsy">
      <data key="d0">cone biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cone biopsy is a procedure to remove a cone-shaped portion of the cervix for diagnostic and therapeutic purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;Cone biopsy is a procedure to remove cervical tissue for diagnostic and therapeutic purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="extrafascial (simple) hysterectomy">
      <data key="d0">extrafascial (simple) hysterectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Extrafascial hysterectomy removes the uterus while preserving ovaries and surrounding structures.&lt;SEP&gt;Extrafascial hysterectomy removes the uterus while preserving the ovaries and surrounding structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="surveillance">
      <data key="d0">surveillance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surveillance involves monitoring for cancer recurrence without immediate treatment, often used in early-stage cases.&lt;SEP&gt;Surveillance involves monitoring for cancer recurrence without immediate treatment, used in early-stage cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="reproductive endocrinologists">
      <data key="d0">reproductive endocrinologists</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Reproductive endocrinologists are specialists in fertility who advise on fertility-sparing options.&lt;SEP&gt;Reproductive endocrinologists are specialists in fertility who may be consulted for fertility-sparing treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="lymph node removal">
      <data key="d0">lymph node removal</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph node removal is performed to address cancer spread, especially in cases with LVSI.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Extrafascial Hysterectomy">
      <data key="d0">Extrafascial Hysterectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Extrafascial hysterectomy is a surgical procedure to remove the uterus, often recommended for early-stage cervical cancer with specific criteria.&lt;SEP&gt;Extrafascial hysterectomy is a surgical procedure to remove the uterus, recommended for early-stage cervical cancer without lymphovascular space invasion (LVSI).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Modified Radical Hysterectomy">
      <data key="d0">Modified Radical Hysterectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Modified radical hysterectomy is a more extensive surgical procedure removing the uterus, cervix, and surrounding tissues for advanced cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Modified radical hysterectomy is a more extensive surgical procedure that removes the uterus, cervix, and surrounding tissues for advanced cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radical Hysterectomy">
      <data key="d0">Radical Hysterectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radical hysterectomy is a comprehensive surgical procedure for locally advanced cervical cancer, involving removal of the uterus, cervix, and pelvic tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Radical hysterectomy is a comprehensive surgical procedure that removes the uterus, cervix, and surrounding pelvic tissues for locally advanced cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Radical hysterectomy is a surgical procedure to remove the uterus, fallopian tubes, ovaries, and surrounding tissues, often used for advanced gynecological cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Lymph Node Dissection">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Lymph Node Dissection</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic lymph node dissection evaluates cancer spread in the pelvis, guiding treatment decisions for cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Pelvic lymph node dissection involves the removal of lymph nodes in the pelvis to assess cancer spread and guide treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Para-aortic Lymph Node Dissection">
      <data key="d0">Para-aortic Lymph Node Dissection</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Para-aortic lymph node dissection assesses cancer spread in the abdomen, often performed alongside pelvic lymph node dissection for advanced cases.&lt;SEP&gt;Para-aortic lymph node dissection is a procedure to remove lymph nodes in the abdomen, often performed alongside pelvic lymph node dissection for advanced cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vaginal Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d0">Vaginal Brachytherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vaginal brachytherapy delivers targeted radiation to the cervix and surrounding tissues, often used in conjunction with EBRT.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Platinum Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Platinum Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Platinum chemotherapy involves drugs like cisplatin, used in chemoradiation regimens for advanced cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Cell Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Cervix (NECC)">
      <data key="d0">Small Cell Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Cervix (NECC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NECC is a rare, aggressive subtype of cervical cancer requiring distinct treatment approaches compared to squamous cell carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Small Cell Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Cervix (NECC) is a rare, aggressive subtype of cervical cancer with neuroendocrine features.&lt;SEP&gt;Small Cell Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Cervix (NECC) is a specific subtype of cervical cancer characterized by neuroendocrine features.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphovascular Space Invasion (LVSI)">
      <data key="d0">Lymphovascular Space Invasion (LVSI)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">LVSI is a pathological feature indicating cancer spread into blood vessels or lymphatics, influencing treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PD-L1 Expression">
      <data key="d0">PD-L1 Expression</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PD-L1 Expression is a biomarker indicating potential responsiveness to immunotherapies in recurrent cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;PD-L1 Expression is a biomarker indicating potential responsiveness to immunotherapies in recurrent or metastatic cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follow-up Visits">
      <data key="d0">Follow-up Visits</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Follow-up Visits are scheduled medical check-ups after treatment to monitor for recurrence, involving physical exams and imaging.&lt;SEP&gt;Follow-up Visits are scheduled medical check-ups post-treatment to monitor for recurrence, involving physical exams, imaging, and blood tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exploratory Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Exploratory Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Exploratory Surgery is performed to determine the extent of cancer spread and guide treatment decisions for recurrent cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Molecular Testing">
      <data key="d0">Molecular Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Molecular Testing analyzes genetic mutations or proteins in tumors to inform targeted therapies and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular Testing analyzes genetic or protein markers in tumors to guide targeted therapies and treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genomic Testing">
      <data key="d0">Genomic Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genomic Testing examines the genetic makeup of tumors to identify mutations that may guide treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Comprehensive Genomic Profiling (CGP)">
      <data key="d0">Comprehensive Genomic Profiling (CGP)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Comprehensive Genomic Profiling (CGP) provides a detailed analysis of tumor genetics to identify actionable mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Gene Testing">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Gene Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor Gene Testing evaluates genetic changes in cancer cells to guide personalized treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NTRK Gene Fusion">
      <data key="d0">NTRK Gene Fusion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NTRK Gene Fusion is a genetic alteration in tumors that may indicate eligibility for targeted therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;NTRK Gene Fusion is a genetic mutation that can be targeted with specific therapies for thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HER2 RET Gene Fusion">
      <data key="d0">HER2 RET Gene Fusion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HER2 RET Gene Fusion refers to specific genetic fusions in tumors that may guide targeted treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Exenter">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Exenter</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic Exenter is a surgical procedure to remove pelvic organs affected by localized cervical cancer recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;Pelvic Exenter is a surgical procedure to remove pelvic organs affected by recurrent cervical cancer, often considered for localized recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physical Exams">
      <data key="d0">Physical Exams</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Physical Exams are conducted during follow-up visits to assess new or worsening symptoms indicative of cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pap Tests">
      <data key="d0">Pap Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Pap Tests screen for abnormal or precancerous cells in the cervix, though less effective at detecting recurrent cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Exenteration Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Exenteration Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic exenteration surgery is a radical procedure involving the removal of multiple pelvic organs to cure cancer by eliminating all affected tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ablative Therapies">
      <data key="d0">Ablative Therapies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ablative therapies are localized treatments that destroy cancerous lesions with minimal damage to surrounding healthy tissue, often using specialized needles or radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic cervical cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic cervical cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic cervical cancer is a type of cancer that is usually treated with platinum-based chemotherapy and immunotherapy, depending on biomarkers like PD-L1 and TMB-H.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mediastinal gray zone lymphoma (MGZL)">
      <data key="d0">Mediastinal gray zone lymphoma (MGZL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mediastinal gray zone lymphoma is a type of lymphoma with overlapping features of primary mediastinal B-cell lymphoma (PMBL) and Hodgkin lymphoma (HL), typically treated with chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hodgkin lymphoma (HL)">
      <data key="d0">Hodgkin lymphoma (HL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hodgkin lymphoma is a type of lymphoma characterized by Reed-Sternberg cells, which are large, abnormal lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Hodgkin lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Non-Hodgkin lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-Hodgkin lymphoma is a broad category of lymphomas that includes various subtypes, such as primary mediastinal B-cell lymphoma (PMBL) and gray zone lymphomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary mediastinal B-cell lymphoma (PMBL)">
      <data key="d0">Primary mediastinal B-cell lymphoma (PMBL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary mediastinal B-cell lymphoma is a type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma that differs from mediastinal gray zone lymphoma (MGZL) in treatment approaches and clinical features.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DLBCL-NOS">
      <data key="d0">DLBCL-NOS</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">DLBCL-NOS (Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma, not otherwise specified) is a lymphoma classification for cancers found outside the mediastinum, typically treated with chemotherapy and possibly rituximab.&lt;SEP&gt;DLBCL-NOS (Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma, not otherwise specified) is a type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma that includes disease found outside the mediastinum.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical trial">
      <data key="d0">Clinical trial</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical trials are research studies that may offer patients access to new treatments and are often recommended for patients with certain biomarkers or treatment-resistant cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PD-L1 biomarker">
      <data key="d0">PD-L1 biomarker</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PD-L1 biomarker is a protein expressed on cancer cells that indicates responsiveness to immunotherapy, particularly pembrolizumab (Keytruda).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TMB-H biomarker">
      <data key="d0">TMB-H biomarker</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">TMB-H (Tumor Mutational Burden-High) biomarker indicates a high number of genetic mutations in cancer cells, making them more responsive to immunotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MSI-H/dMMR biomarker">
      <data key="d0">MSI-H/dMMR biomarker</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MSI-H/dMMR (Microsatellite Instability-High/Deficient Mismatch Repair) biomarker indicates genetic instability in cancer cells, making them responsive to immunotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tisotumab vedotin-tftv (Tivdak)">
      <data key="d0">Tisotumab vedotin-tftv (Tivdak)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tisotumab vedotin-tftv (Tivdak) is a targeted therapy used for cancers without specific biomarkers, particularly in advanced stages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cemiplimab (Libtayo)">
      <data key="d0">Cemiplimab (Libtayo)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cemiplimab (Libtayo) is an immunotherapy option for cancers with specific biomarkers or as a second-line treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pembrolizumab (Keytruda)">
      <data key="d0">Pembrolizumab (Keytruda)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pembrolizumab (Keytruda) is an immune checkpoint inhibitor used for cancers with PD-L1–positive or TMB-H biomarkers.&lt;SEP&gt;Pembrolizumab (Keytruda) is an immunotherapy drug used to treat cancers with MSI-H/dMMR or TMB-H characteristics, leveraging the immune system to target tumor cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Topotecan">
      <data key="d0">Topotecan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Topotecan is a chemotherapy drug used in combination with other agents for treating metastatic cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MGZL">
      <data key="d0">MGZL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MGZL (Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphoma) is a rare type of lymphoma with features of both classical Hodgkin lymphoma (CHL) and non-Hodgkin PMBL, presenting treatment challenges due to lack of standard protocols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of cancer originating in the thyroid gland, characterized by uncontrolled cell growth. It includes various subtypes such as papillary, follicular, and medullary thyroid cancers. Treatment options include surgery, radioactive iodine therapy, targeted therapy, and immunotherapy. Recurrence and survivorship are critical aspects of its management.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid Cancer is a category of cancers originating in the thyroid gland, which can be metastatic and require specific treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid Cancer is a malignancy of the thyroid gland, categorized into differentiated (e.g., papillary, follicular), medullary, and anaplastic types, each with distinct treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid Cancer is a medical condition involving abnormal growth of thyroid cells, often treated with hormone therapy, radioactive iodine, or radiation, depending on the cancer type and risk level.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid Cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the thyroid gland and is discussed in the text regarding treatment and follow-up.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid cancer is a type of cancer affecting the thyroid gland, often curable with surgery, and has risk factors including radiation exposure and family history.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the thyroid gland, with subtypes like anaplastic thyroid cancer being particularly aggressive and requiring combination treatments like chemotherapy and radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cowden syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Cowden syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cowden syndrome is an inherited genetic disorder linked to follicular thyroid cancer and other cancers, often associated with mutations in the PTEN gene.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carney complex">
      <data key="d0">Carney complex</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Carney complex is a genetic syndrome associated with thyroid cancer, characterized by multiple endocrine neoplasias and other systemic manifestations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia (MEN)">
      <data key="d0">Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia (MEN)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia (MEN) is a genetic disorder linked to thyroid cancer, involving tumors in multiple endocrine glands and requiring family history assessment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid gland">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid gland</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The thyroid gland is a butterfly-shaped organ in the neck responsible for producing hormones that regulate metabolism, blood pressure, and other bodily functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid nodules">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid nodules</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid nodules are abnormal growths within the thyroid gland, most of which are benign, but some may indicate thyroid cancer or other conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation exposure">
      <data key="d0">Radiation exposure</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiation exposure is a significant risk factor for thyroid cancer, particularly from childhood treatments or environmental catastrophes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Family history">
      <data key="d0">Family history</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Family history of thyroid cancer or related syndromes increases an individual's risk of developing thyroid cancer, often indicating hereditary genetic mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hyperthyroidism">
      <data key="d0">Hyperthyroidism</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hyperthyroidism is a condition characterized by excessive thyroid hormone production, which can be associated with thyroid cancer or other endocrine disorders.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TSH test">
      <data key="d0">TSH test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">TSH test measures thyroid-stimulating hormone levels to assess thyroid function, aiding in diagnosing thyroid cancer and evaluating nodule activity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Nodule">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Nodule</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A thyroid nodule is a lump or abnormal growth within the thyroid gland, often evaluated for potential cancerous characteristics through diagnostic tests and imaging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TSH Test">
      <data key="d0">TSH Test</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The TSH (Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone) test measures thyroid hormone levels to assess nodule activity and detect hyperthyroidism or hypothyroidism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RAI Uptake Test">
      <data key="d0">RAI Uptake Test</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Radioactive Iodine (RAI) uptake test evaluates whether a thyroid nodule produces thyroid hormone, aiding in cancer risk assessment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy is a treatment approach to maintain TSH levels within a therapeutic range, preventing cancer recurrence and managing metabolic balance in thyroid cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy is a treatment approach to manage TSH levels in patients with thyroid cancer, preventing cancer recurrence and maintaining metabolic balance.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy is a treatment involving levothyroxine to manage hormone levels after thyroidectomy, essential for patients who have had their thyroid removed.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid hormone replacement therapy is a lifelong treatment for patients who have undergone total thyroidectomy, maintaining hormonal balance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Papillary Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Papillary Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Papillary carcinoma is the most common type of thyroid cancer, characterized by slow growth and spread, often managed with surgery and hormone therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follicular Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Follicular Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Follicular carcinoma is a type of differentiated thyroid cancer that originates from thyroid follicular cells, typically growing slowly and responding well to treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oncocytic Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Oncocytic Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Oncocytic Carcinoma is an uncommon thyroid cancer type that is more likely to spread to lymph nodes and requires thorough pathological examination.&lt;SEP&gt;Oncocytic Carcinoma is an uncommon thyroid cancer type that tends to spread to lymph nodes and requires detailed pathological examination for diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Oncocytic carcinoma is a rare type of thyroid cancer with distinctive cell features, often requiring surgical intervention and further testing for malignancy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Differentiated Thyroid Cancers">
      <data key="d0">Differentiated Thyroid Cancers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Differentiated thyroid cancers are a category of thyroid cancers that include papillary, follicular, and oncocytic carcinomas, typically growing slowly and responding well to treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Differentiated thyroid cancers include papillary, follicular, and oncocytic carcinomas, which grow and spread slowly, with treatment focusing on surgery and hormone therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medullary Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Medullary Thyroid Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Medullary thyroid cancer is a hereditary form of thyroid cancer that arises from parafollicular cells, often managed with surgery and genetic screening.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anaplastic Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Anaplastic Thyroid Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anaplastic thyroid cancer is an aggressive, rare form of thyroid cancer that primarily affects older adults, requiring intensive treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pathology Review">
      <data key="d0">Pathology Review</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Pathology review involves analyzing biopsy samples under a microscope to determine whether a nodule is cancerous and identify the cancer type.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Levothyroxine">
      <data key="d0">Levothyroxine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Levothyroxine is a synthetic thyroid hormone used to replace or supplement thyroid hormone in patients with hypothyroidism, particularly in managing thyroid cancer by suppressing TSH levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Levothyroxine is a thyroid hormone replacement therapy used to manage thyroid hormone levels in patients with thyroid cancer or hypothyroidism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Synthroid">
      <data key="d0">Synthroid</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Synthroid is a brand-name medication containing levothyroxine, used similarly to manage thyroid hormone levels and suppress TSH in thyroid cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Synthroid is a thyroid hormone replacement therapy similar to levothyroxine, used to regulate thyroid hormone levels in patients with thyroid disorders.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radioactive Iodine Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Radioactive Iodine Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radioactive Iodine Therapy (RAI) is a treatment method using iodine-131 to destroy thyroid cancer cells, particularly effective for differentiated thyroid cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Radioactive Iodine Therapy (RAI) is a treatment using iodine-131 to destroy thyroid cancer cells that absorb iodine, primarily for differentiated thyroid cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyrogen">
      <data key="d0">Thyrogen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyrogen is a medication used to temporarily increase TSH levels in patients undergoing radioactive iodine therapy, enabling continued thyroid hormone replacement.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyrogen is a synthetic hormone used to temporarily elevate TSH levels, enabling continued thyroid hormone replacement during radioactive iodine therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Whole-Body RAI Scan">
      <data key="d0">Whole-Body RAI Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Whole-Body RAI Scan is a diagnostic procedure performed after radioactive iodine therapy to detect remaining thyroid tissue or cancer cells in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Whole-Body RAI Scan is a diagnostic procedure performed after radioactive iodine therapy to detect residual thyroid tissue or cancer cells in the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Simulation">
      <data key="d0">Simulation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Simulation is a planning session conducted before radiation therapy to create a personalized radiation treatment plan using imaging techniques like CT scans.&lt;SEP&gt;Simulation is a planning session conducted before radiation therapy to create a personalized treatment plan using imaging techniques like CT scans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone (TSH)">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone (TSH)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone (TSH) is a hormone produced by the pituitary gland that regulates thyroid hormone production, monitored to adjust treatment for thyroid cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radioactive Iodine (RAI)">
      <data key="d0">Radioactive Iodine (RAI)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radioactive Iodine (RAI) is a radioactive form of iodine used in therapy to target and destroy thyroid cancer cells that absorb iodine, commonly used in differentiated thyroid cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroidectomy">
      <data key="d0">Thyroidectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroidectomy is a general term for the surgical removal of the thyroid gland, which can be performed as a total thyroidectomy or lobectomy depending on the cancer's severity.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroidectomy is a surgical procedure to remove all or part of the thyroid gland, often performed as a primary treatment for thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Calcium and Vitamin D Supplements">
      <data key="d0">Calcium and Vitamin D Supplements</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Calcium and Vitamin D Supplements are recommended to strengthen bones in patients undergoing thyroid hormone replacement therapy, mitigating the risk of osteoporosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endocrinologist">
      <data key="d0">Endocrinologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An endocrinologist is a medical specialist who diagnoses and treats disorders of the endocrine system, including thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Endocrinologist is a medical specialist who manages hormone-related conditions, including thyroid hormone replacement and TSH level monitoring in thyroid cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nuclear Medicine Specialist">
      <data key="d0">Nuclear Medicine Specialist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Nuclear Medicine Specialist is a physician who uses radioactive substances, such as iodine-131, for diagnosing and treating thyroid cancer through targeted therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Radiation Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Radiation Oncologist is a medical specialist who plans and administers radiation therapy to treat cancers, including anal cancer, and manages treatment-related side effects.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiation oncologist is a medical doctor who specializes in using radiation therapy to treat cancer, including thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiation oncologist plans and administers radiation therapy to target and treat cancer, often as part of a multidisciplinary approach.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiation Oncologist is a specialist who administers radiation therapy, such as external beam radiation, to treat thyroid cancer, particularly anaplastic variants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Iodine-131">
      <data key="d0">Iodine-131</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Iodine-131 is a radioactive isotope of iodine used in radioactive iodine therapy to target and destroy thyroid cancer cells that absorb iodine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyrotropin Alfa">
      <data key="d0">Thyrotropin Alfa</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyrotropin Alfa is a synthetic hormone used to temporarily increase TSH levels, enabling continued thyroid hormone replacement during radioactive iodine therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radioactive Iodine Scan">
      <data key="d0">Radioactive Iodine Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Radioactive Iodine Scan is a diagnostic procedure using iodine-131 to visualize thyroid tissue and detect cancerous cells, often performed before or after radioactive iodine therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation Planning Session">
      <data key="d0">Radiation Planning Session</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiation Planning Session is a pre-treatment event where imaging and patient-specific data are used to design an individualized radiation therapy plan for external beam radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pituitary Gland">
      <data key="d0">Pituitary Gland</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pituitary Gland is an endocrine gland in the brain that produces thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH), regulating thyroid hormone production and metabolic activity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Papillary Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Papillary Thyroid Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Papillary Thyroid Cancer is a type of differentiated thyroid cancer that is the most common form, often treated with surgery and radioactive iodine therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follicular and Oncocytic Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Follicular and Oncocytic Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Follicular and Oncocytic Carcinoma are subtypes of differentiated thyroid cancers, which may have varying growth rates and treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radioactive Iodine (RAI) Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Radioactive Iodine (RAI) Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radioactive Iodine (RAI) Therapy is a treatment used to destroy remaining cancer cells after thyroidectomy, particularly in cases of papillary thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Radioactive Iodine (RAI) Therapy is a treatment used to destroy remaining thyroid cancer cells after surgery, particularly in cases of differentiated thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Radioactive Iodine (RAI) Therapy is a treatment used to destroy residual thyroid cancer cells after surgery, particularly for differentiated thyroid cancers like follicular or oncocytic types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Active Surveillance">
      <data key="d0">Active Surveillance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Active Surveillance is a management approach for small, low-risk thyroid cancers where the tumor is monitored without immediate surgery, using ultrasound to track its size.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Total Thyroidectomy">
      <data key="d0">Total Thyroidectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Total Thyroidectomy is a surgical procedure to remove the entire thyroid gland, recommended for tumors that have spread beyond the thyroid or are larger than 4 cm.&lt;SEP&gt;Total Thyroidectomy is a surgical procedure to remove the entire thyroid gland, typically recommended for more aggressive or spread-out thyroid cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lobectomy">
      <data key="d0">Lobectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lobectomy is a surgical procedure to remove only part of the thyroid gland, considered an option for small, low-risk cancers where thyroid hormone replacement may not be necessary.&lt;SEP&gt;Lobectomy is a surgical procedure to remove part of the thyroid gland, often considered for less invasive cancers when the tumor is confined to the thyroid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nuclear Medicine Doctor">
      <data key="d0">Nuclear Medicine Doctor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A nuclear medicine doctor specializes in using radioactive substances to diagnose and treat diseases, including thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NIFTP">
      <data key="d0">NIFTP</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NIFTP (Noninvasive Follicular Thyroid Neoplasm with Papillary-Like Nuclear Features) is a low-risk, noninvasive thyroid tumor previously classified as an encapsulated follicular variant of papillary thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;NIFTP (Noninvasive Follicular Thyroid Neoplasm with Papillary-Like Nuclear Features) is a low-risk, noninvasive thyroid tumor previously known as the encapsulated follicular variant of papillary thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follicular Thyroid Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Follicular Thyroid Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Follicular Thyroid Carcinoma (FTC) is the second most common type of thyroid cancer, characterized by invasion of blood vessels and requiring surgical intervention.&lt;SEP&gt;Follicular Thyroid Carcinoma (FTC) is the second most common type of thyroid cancer, known for invading blood vessels and requiring surgical intervention.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pathologic Examination">
      <data key="d0">Pathologic Examination</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Pathologic examination is a medical procedure involving analysis of tissue samples to determine tumor malignancy, benignancy, or specific subtypes like NIFTP.&lt;SEP&gt;Pathologic examination is a medical procedure involving the analysis of tissue samples to determine the nature of a tumor, including whether it is benign or malignant.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monitoring">
      <data key="d0">Monitoring</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Monitoring refers to the ongoing follow-up care and testing to track the progression of thyroid cancer and assess the effectiveness of treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Monitoring refers to the ongoing follow-up care and testing to track thyroid cancer progression and assess treatment effectiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follow-Up Testing">
      <data key="d0">Follow-Up Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Follow-Up Testing involves regular medical evaluations, including blood tests and imaging, to detect any recurrence of thyroid cancer after treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Follow-Up Testing involves regular medical evaluations, including blood tests and imaging, to detect cancer recurrence after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RAI Therapy">
      <data key="d0">RAI Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">RAI Therapy (Radioactive Iodine Therapy) is a treatment option for thyroid cancers that take up iodine, particularly for metastatic papillary, follicular, or oncocytic thyroid cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follow-up Care">
      <data key="d0">Follow-up Care</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Follow-up Care involves monitoring and testing after thyroidectomy to detect cancer recurrence, including physical exams, blood tests, and neck ultrasounds.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neck Ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">Neck Ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Neck Ultrasound is a diagnostic tool used to monitor for the return of thyroid cancer after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TSH Blood Test">
      <data key="d0">TSH Blood Test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">TSH Blood Test measures thyroid-stimulating hormone levels to assess thyroid function and monitor for cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroglobulin (Tg)">
      <data key="d0">Thyroglobulin (Tg)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroglobulin is a protein produced by thyroid tissue; its levels in the blood can indicate the presence of cancer recurrence after thyroidectomy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anti-thyroglobulin Antibodies (Tg ab)">
      <data key="d0">Anti-thyroglobulin Antibodies (Tg ab)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anti-thyroglobulin Antibodies are proteins that can interfere with thyroglobulin testing, indicating a potential immune response to thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid-stimulating Hormone (TSH)">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid-stimulating Hormone (TSH)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid-stimulating Hormone is a hormone produced by the pituitary gland that regulates thyroid function; its levels are monitored to detect cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lenvatinib (Lenvima)">
      <data key="d0">Lenvatinib (Lenvima)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lenvatinib (Lenvima) is a targeted therapy recommended for advanced thyroid cancer that cannot be removed with surgery and is progressing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sorafenib (Nexavar)">
      <data key="d0">Sorafenib (Nexavar)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sorafenib (Nexavar) is another targeted therapy recommended for advanced thyroid cancer that cannot be removed with surgery and is progressing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cabozantinib (Cabometyx)">
      <data key="d0">Cabozantinib (Cabometyx)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cabozantinib (Cabometyy) is a targeted therapy recommended if cancer progresses after treatment with Lenvatinib or Sorafenib.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALK Gene Fusion">
      <data key="d0">ALK Gene Fusion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ALK Gene Fusion is a genetic mutation that can be targeted with specific therapies for thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRAF Gene Mutations">
      <data key="d0">BRAF Gene Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BRAF Gene Mutations are genetic changes that can be targeted with specific therapies for thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RET Gene Fusion">
      <data key="d0">RET Gene Fusion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">RET Gene Fusion is a genetic mutation found in some medullary thyroid cancers and can be targeted with specific therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mismatch Repair Deficiency">
      <data key="d0">Mismatch Repair Deficiency</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mismatch Repair Deficiency is a genetic feature that can be targeted with specific therapies for thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Mutational Burden-High (TMB-H)">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Mutational Burden-High (TMB-H)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor Mutational Burden-High (TMB-H) is a genetic feature that can be targeted with specific therapies for thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Next-generation Sequencing (NGS)">
      <data key="d0">Next-generation Sequencing (NGS)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Next-generation Sequencing (NGS) is a method used to test for multiple biomarkers simultaneously in thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients®">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients®</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An organization providing evidence-based guidelines for cancer care, including resources for rectal cancer and melanoma management.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Guidelines for Patients® is an organization that provides trusted, expert-guided information on cancer care and treatment recommendations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Bone Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone Cancer is a category of cancer affecting bones, discussed in the text regarding testing, staging, and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone Cancer is a category of cancer that originates in bone tissue or cartilage, including primary and secondary (metastatic) forms.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone Cancer is a type of cancer that affects bones, discussed in the text regarding testing, staging, and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone cancer refers to malignant tumors originating in the bones, which can be staged using the TNM system to assess tumor size, spread, and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chondrosarcoma">
      <data key="d0">Chondrosarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chondrosarcoma is a specific type of bone cancer discussed in the text, typically treated with surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;Chondrosarcoma is a type of bone cancer that originates in cartilage cells, often found in joints and other cartilage-rich areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Osteosarcoma">
      <data key="d0">Osteosarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Osteosarcoma is a malignant bone tumor that arises from cells forming bone tissue, commonly affecting areas of rapid bone growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Osteosarcoma is the most common type of bone cancer, typically treated with a combination of therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ewing Sarcoma">
      <data key="d0">Ewing Sarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ewing Sarcoma is a rare type of bone cancer that originates in the bone marrow, often found in long bones or flat bones like the pelvis.&lt;SEP&gt;Ewing Sarcoma is a type of bone cancer, often treated with systemic therapy as the initial approach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Giant Cell Tumor of Bone">
      <data key="d0">Giant Cell Tumor of Bone</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Giant Cell Tumor of Bone is a benign or malignant tumor that develops in the metaphysis of bones, near growth plates.&lt;SEP&gt;Giant Cell Tumor of Bone is a non-cancerous condition that requires aggressive treatment to prevent bone damage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chordoma">
      <data key="d0">Chordoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chordoma is a rare bone cancer that originates from notochordal cells, typically found in the spine or base of the skull.&lt;SEP&gt;Chordoma is a rare type of bone cancer, with treatment options including surgery or radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soft Tissue Sarcoma">
      <data key="d0">Soft Tissue Sarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Soft Tissue Sarcoma is a category of cancers arising from soft tissues like fat, muscles, and nerves, distinct from bone sarcomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multiple Myeloma">
      <data key="d0">Multiple Myeloma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Multiple Myeloma is a type of cancer that affects plasma cells in the bone marrow, often associated with bone destruction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Bone Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone Tumor is a general term for abnormal growths in bone tissue, which can be benign or malignant.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Malignant">
      <data key="d0">Malignant</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Malignant refers to cancerous tumors that grow uncontrollably and can spread to other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Benign">
      <data key="d0">Benign</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Benign refers to non-cancerous tumors that do not spread to other tissues and are typically less aggressive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Compact Bone">
      <data key="d0">Compact Bone</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Compact Bone is the dense, hard outer layer of bone tissue that provides structural support and protection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spongey Bone">
      <data key="d0">Spongey Bone</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Spongey Bone is the porous, inner layer of bone tissue containing red marrow and providing a lightweight yet strong structure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Red Marrow">
      <data key="d0">Red Marrow</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Red Marrow is the soft tissue within bones responsible for producing blood cells, including red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Yellow Marrow">
      <data key="d0">Yellow Marrow</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Yellow Marrow is the fatty tissue found in bones, primarily replacing red marrow as the body ages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormonal Birth Control">
      <data key="d0">Hormonal Birth Control</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormonal Birth Control is a method mentioned in the text to prevent pregnancy during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pregnancy test">
      <data key="d0">Pregnancy test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pregnancy tests are a category of diagnostic procedures to determine if a patient can become pregnant before starting cancer treatment, as cancer therapies can impact fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;Pregnancy tests are a category of diagnostic procedures used to determine if a patient can become pregnant before starting cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Complete blood count (CBC)">
      <data key="d0">Complete blood count (CBC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The complete blood count (CBC) is a category of blood test that measures red blood cells (RBCs), white blood cells (WBCs), and platelets (PLTs) to assess blood health.&lt;SEP&gt;The complete blood count (CBC) is a category of blood test that measures red blood cells (RBCs), white blood cells (WBCs), and platelets (PLTs) to evaluate blood health and detect abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X-ray">
      <data key="d0">X-ray</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">X-rays are a category of imaging tests that use ionizing radiation to create images of internal structures, such as bones and organs, often referred to as radiographs.&lt;SEP&gt;X-rays are a category of imaging tests that use radiation to create images of internal body structures, often referred to as radiographs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone scan">
      <data key="d0">Bone scan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone scans are a category of imaging tests that use a radiotracer to detect bone damage, which can be caused by cancer, treatment, or other conditions, highlighting areas of abnormal activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone scans are a category of imaging tests that use a radiotracer to detect bone damage, which can be caused by cancer, treatment, or other conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic cancer risk testing">
      <data key="d0">Genetic cancer risk testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic testing using blood or saliva identifies inherited gene mutations that increase cancer risk, aiding in family risk assessment and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ewing sarcoma">
      <data key="d0">Ewing sarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ewing sarcoma is a type of cancer characterized by specific genetic fusions, such as EWSR1-FLI1, resulting from somatic mutations during tissue development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer center">
      <data key="d0">Cancer center</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A cancer center is a specialized facility providing comprehensive cancer diagnosis, treatment, and research services, including biopsies, genetic testing, and advanced imaging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Core needle biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Core needle biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Core needle biopsy removes tissue samples with a hollow needle that can grab a piece of tissue, often performed under light sedation by an interventional radiologist.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Surgical biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical (open) biopsy removes a sample of the tumor through a small incision, requiring a trip to the operating room and performed by the treating surgeon.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone marrow aspirate">
      <data key="d0">Bone marrow aspirate</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone marrow aspirate takes liquid bone marrow from the sponge-like structure of the bone, typically from the hip, to evaluate cellular abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone marrow biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Bone marrow biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone marrow biopsy removes a core sample of the sponge-like bone marrow to assess cellular and structural abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Microsatellite instability (MSI)">
      <data key="d0">Microsatellite instability (MSI)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Microsatellite instability (MSI) refers to errors in DNA repair mechanisms that lead to genetic mutations, often associated with certain cancers and treatment responses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deficient mismatch repair (dMMR)">
      <data key="d0">Deficient mismatch repair (dMMR)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Deficient mismatch repair (dMMR) is a condition where DNA repair errors are not corrected, leading to microsatellite instability and specific cancer vulnerabilities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="bone cancer">
      <data key="d0">bone cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the bones, characterized by abnormal cell growth and can affect various parts of the skeletal system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="microsatellite instability (MSI)">
      <data key="d0">microsatellite instability (MSI)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Microsatellite instability (MSI) refers to a condition where errors in DNA repair mechanisms lead to an accumulation of mutations in microsatellite regions, often associated with certain cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="deficient mismatch repair (dMMR)">
      <data key="d0">deficient mismatch repair (dMMR)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Deficient mismatch repair (dMMR) is a genetic condition where the body's ability to correct DNA replication errors is impaired, leading to increased mutation rates and specific cancer vulnerabilities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="tumor mutational burden-high (TMB-H)">
      <data key="d0">tumor mutational burden-high (TMB-H)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor mutational burden-high (TMB-H) indicates a high number of mutations per million base pairs in tumor DNA, often linked to immunotherapy responsiveness and aggressive cancer behavior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="tumor mutation testing">
      <data key="d0">tumor mutation testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor mutation testing is a diagnostic process that analyzes DNA mutations in cancer cells to guide treatment decisions and identify potential therapeutic targets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="next-generation sequencing (NGS)">
      <data key="d0">next-generation sequencing (NGS)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Next-generation sequencing (NGS) is a high-throughput DNA sequencing technology used to detect genetic mutations and variations in tumor DNA with greater precision than traditional methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="polymerase chain reaction (PCR)">
      <data key="d0">polymerase chain reaction (PCR)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) is a molecular biology technique that amplifies specific DNA sequences, enabling the detection of genetic mutations and other biomarkers in cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IDH1 mutation">
      <data key="d0">IDH1 mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">IDH1 mutation refers to a specific genetic alteration in the isocitrate dehydrogenase 1 gene, commonly associated with certain types of cancers like chondrosarcoma and linked to targeted therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ivosidenib (Tibsovo)">
      <data key="d0">ivosidenib (Tibsovo)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ivosidenbed (Tibsovo) is a targeted therapy drug used to treat cancers with IDH1 mutations, particularly in chondrosarcoma patients, by inhibiting the mutated IDH1 enzyme.&lt;SEP&gt;Ivosidenib (Tibsovo) is a targeted therapy drug used to treat cancers with IDH1 mutations, particularly in chondrosarcoma patients, by inhibiting the mutated IDH1 enzyme.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="nivolumab (Opdivo)">
      <data key="d0">nivolumab (Opdivo)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nivolumab (Opdivo) is an immunotherapy drug that may be used for TMB-H tumors, working by blocking immune checkpoint proteins to enhance anti-tumor immune responses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ipilimumab (Yervoy)">
      <data key="d0">ipilimumab (Yervoy)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ipilimumab (Yervoy) is an immunotherapy drug that may be used for TMB-H tumors, targeting the CTLA-4 protein to boost T-cell activation against cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="chondrosarcoma">
      <data key="d0">chondrosarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chondrosarcoma is a type of bone cancer that originates in cartilage cells, often associated with IDH1 mutations and treated with targeted therapies like ivosidenib.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Appendicular Skeleton">
      <data key="d0">Appendicular Skeleton</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The appendicular skeleton consists of 126 bones in the upper and lower limbs, shoulder girdle, and pelvic girdle, and is a site for tumors that require specific staging criteria.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spine">
      <data key="d0">Spine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The spine, composed of 24 vertebrae, is a common location for bone tumors with distinct staging criteria based on vertebral involvement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvis">
      <data key="d0">Pelvis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Pelvis is the bony structure housing the anal canal and large bowel, with the anus located at its distal end.&lt;SEP&gt;The pelvis is a basin-shaped structure containing bones that connect the legs to the body, and tumors here are staged based on segmental involvement and spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CRUK">
      <data key="d0">CRUK</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">CRUK (Cancer Research UK) is an organization associated with the diagram illustrating the liver's anatomy and blood supply.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liver Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Liver Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Liver Cancer is a category of cancer that originates in the liver or spreads to the liver from other organs, classified into primary and secondary types.&lt;SEP&gt;Liver cancer refers to cancers that originate in the liver, including primary liver cancers like HCC and secondary liver cancers that metastasize from other organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grading">
      <data key="d0">Grading</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grading is a category that assesses how abnormal cancer cells appear under a microscope, indicating their growth and spread potential.&lt;SEP&gt;Grading is a classification system used to assess the aggressiveness and growth rate of cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Liver Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Primary Liver Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary Liver Cancer is a category of cancer that originates in the liver, including types like hepatocellular carcinoma and intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Secondary Liver Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Secondary Liver Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Secondary Liver Cancer is a category of cancer that spreads to the liver from other organs, such as colon cancer metastasizing to the liver.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC)">
      <data key="d0">Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC) is a category of primary liver cancer, with a subtype called fibrolamellar hepatocellular carcinoma (FLHC).&lt;SEP&gt;Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC) is the most common type of primary liver cancer, characterized by the uncontrolled growth of liver cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fibrolamellar Hepatocellular Carcinoma (FLHC)">
      <data key="d0">Fibrolamellar Hepatocellular Carcinoma (FLHC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fibrolamellar Hepatocellular Carcinoma (FLHC) is a rare subtype of hepatocellular carcinoma, typically occurring in younger individuals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intrahepatic Cholangiocarcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Intrahepatic Cholangiocarcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Intrahepatic Cholangiocarcinoma is a category of primary liver cancer that originates in the bile ducts within the liver.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mixed-Type Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Mixed-Type Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mixed-Type Tumor is a rare category of liver cancer composed of both hepatocellular carcinoma and cholangiocarcinoma components.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatobiliary System">
      <data key="d0">Hepatobiliary System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatobiliary System is a category encompassing the liver, gallbladder, and bile ducts, functioning in bile production and digestion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Digestive System">
      <data key="d0">Digestive System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Digestive System is a category of bodily systems that includes the liver, gallbladder, pancreas, and intestines, responsible for food breakdown and nutrient absorption.&lt;SEP&gt;The digestive system is responsible for taking in and breaking down food, absorbing nutrients, and removing waste from the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cirrhosis">
      <data key="d0">Cirrhosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cirrhosis is a chronic liver disease marked by scarring of the liver tissue, often caused by hepatitis, alcohol use, or non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatitis B virus">
      <data key="d0">Hepatitis B virus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatitis B virus (HBV) is a viral infection that causes liver inflammation, which can lead to chronic hepatitis, cirrhosis, and liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatitis C virus">
      <data key="d0">Hepatitis C virus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatitis C virus (HCV) is a viral infection that leads to chronic liver disease, increasing the risk of cirrhosis and liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD)">
      <data key="d0">Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) is a condition characterized by fat accumulation in the liver, which can progress to cirrhosis and liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic hemochromatosis">
      <data key="d0">Genetic hemochromatosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic hemochromatosis is an inherited condition causing excessive iron storage in the liver, which can lead to cirrhosis and liver cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic hemochromatosis is an inherited condition causing the liver to store too much iron from food, leading to liver damage and cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency">
      <data key="d0">Alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency is a genetic disorder that results in liver damage and an increased risk of liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary biliary cholangitis">
      <data key="d0">Primary biliary cholangitis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary biliary cholangitis is a chronic liver disease that causes damage to bile ducts, potentially leading to cirrhosis and liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatitis">
      <data key="d0">Hepatitis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatitis is an inflammation of the liver, often caused by viral infections like hepatitis B or C, which can progress to chronic liver disease and cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatobiliary system">
      <data key="d0">Hepatobiliary system</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The hepatobiliary system includes the liver, gallbladder, and bile ducts, and is involved in the production and transport of bile.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) test">
      <data key="d0">Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) test is a blood test used to screen for liver cancer by measuring the level of AFP, a protein associated with liver tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="liver cancer">
      <data key="d0">liver cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Liver cancer is a type of cancer that affects the liver and is the focus of the text. It is discussed in terms of risk factors, screening, diagnosis, and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="stomach cancer">
      <data key="d0">stomach cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stomach cancer is a type of cancer that affects the stomach and is discussed in the context of the digestive system and its relationship to other cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="digestive system">
      <data key="d0">digestive system</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The digestive system is a collection of organs responsible for breaking down food and absorbing nutrients, including the stomach, liver, and intestines. It is central to understanding the context of stomach and liver cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="risk factors">
      <data key="d0">risk factors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk factors are elements that increase the likelihood of developing liver cancer, such as cirrhosis, chronic hepatitis B, and other health conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="screening">
      <data key="d0">screening</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Screening involves regular tests to detect diseases early, particularly in individuals at risk for liver cancer, with the goal of improving treatment outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="diagnosis">
      <data key="d0">diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnosis refers to the process of confirming liver cancer through tests like blood tests, imaging studies, and biopsies, which inform treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="treatment">
      <data key="d0">treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment encompasses the medical interventions used to address liver cancer, based on diagnostic findings and patient-specific factors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="family history">
      <data key="d0">family history</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Family history involves the medical history of blood relatives, which can indicate a genetic predisposition to certain cancers, including liver and stomach cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="medical history">
      <data key="d0">medical history</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical history is a record of an individual's health issues, treatments, and conditions, which plays a role in diagnosing and managing liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="biopsy">
      <data key="d0">biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biopsy involves the removal and analysis of tissue samples to confirm the presence of cancer, often used in the diagnosis of liver and stomach cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="cirrhosis">
      <data key="d0">cirrhosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cirrhosis is a chronic liver disease that increases the risk of developing liver cancer, often resulting from long-term damage to the liver.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="chronic hepatitis B">
      <data key="d0">chronic hepatitis B</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic hepatitis B is a viral infection that can lead to liver damage and is a significant risk factor for liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stomach">
      <data key="d0">Stomach</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The stomach is a part of the digestive system where food is broken down into chyme using stomach contractions and enzymes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Esophagus">
      <data key="d0">Esophagus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The esophagus is a muscular tube that transports food and drink from the mouth to the stomach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Intestine">
      <data key="d0">Small Intestine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The small intestine is where nutrients from digested food are absorbed into the bloodstream.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colon">
      <data key="d0">Colon</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The colon is part of the digestive system that absorbs water and electrolytes from the remaining food material.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rectum">
      <data key="d0">Rectum</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The rectum is the final section of the large intestine where feces are stored before elimination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gallbladder">
      <data key="d0">Gallbladder</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The gallbladder stores and releases bile into the small intestine to aid in fat digestion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pancreas">
      <data key="d0">Pancreas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The pancreas produces digestive enzymes and hormones that help in the breakdown of food and regulation of blood sugar.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intestines">
      <data key="d0">Intestines</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The intestines are the organs responsible for the majority of nutrient absorption in the digestive system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stomach Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stomach Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stomach cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the cells lining the stomach, primarily adenocarcinomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="My Dad">
      <data key="d0">My Dad</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">My dad is a person who was diagnosed with stomach cancer and is now clinically cured, advocating for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Early-stage stomach cancer">
      <data key="d0">Early-stage stomach cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Early-stage stomach cancer has not grown beyond the first layer (mucosa) of the stomach wall. The tumor is often small (2 centimeters or less) and is not in any lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Locoregional or locally advanced stomach cancer">
      <data key="d0">Locoregional or locally advanced stomach cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Locoregional or locally advanced stomach cancer has invaded other layers of the stomach wall and/or spread to the lymph nodes or organs near the stomach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic stomach cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic stomach cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic stomach cancer has spread to other parts of the body, commonly affecting the liver, abdominal lining (peritoneum), distant lymph nodes, lungs, or bone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Glioma">
      <data key="d0">Glioma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Glioma is a category of brain tumors that originate from glial cells, classified by histology, grade, and biomarkers.&lt;SEP&gt;Glioma is a type of brain tumor originating from glial cells, discussed as a separate medical condition in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;Glioma is a type of brain tumor that originates from glial cells, which support nerve cells in the brain and spinal cord. It is characterized by the uncontrolled growth of glial cells and can disrupt normal brain function.&lt;SEP&gt;Glioma is a type of tumor characterized by the growth of abnormal cells in the brain or spinal cord, with varying grades (e.g., Grade 1, Grade 2, Grade 3, Grade 4) and distinct treatment approaches, including surgery, radiation, chemotherapy, and targeted therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Glioma is a type of tumor characterized by the growth of abnormal cells in the brain or spinal cord, with varying grades and treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Glioma is a type of tumor that grows in the brain, originating from glial cells and is the most common malignant brain tumor in adults.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Digestive system">
      <data key="d0">Digestive system</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The digestive system is a biological system responsible for taking in and breaking down food, absorbing nutrients, and removing waste from the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adenocarcinomas">
      <data key="d0">Adenocarcinomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of cancer originating in glandular cells, typically found in colorectal cancers, contrasting with squamous cell carcinomas in anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinomas are a type of stomach cancer that originates in cells lining the inside of the stomach wall and secrete mucus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing for stomach cancer">
      <data key="d0">Testing for stomach cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing for stomach cancer involves a series of diagnostic procedures to determine the presence and stage of cancer, including blood tests, imaging, biopsies, and genetic risk assessments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="H. pylori tests">
      <data key="d0">H. pylori tests</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">H. pylori tests detect the presence of Helicobacter pylori bacteria, a known risk factor for stomach cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="General health tests">
      <data key="d0">General health tests</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">General health tests evaluate overall health and medical history to identify risk factors or conditions that may contribute to cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Astrocytoma">
      <data key="d0">Astrocytoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Astrocytoma is a subtype of glioma that arises from astrocytes, a type of glial cell, and can vary in aggressiveness and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Astrocytoma is a type of glioma that develops from astrocytes, which support nerve cell function in the central nervous system.&lt;SEP&gt;Astrocytoma is a type of glioma that resembles astrocytes, often classified by histology, grade, and biomarkers like IDH mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oligodendroglioma">
      <data key="d0">Oligodendroglioma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Oligodendroglioma is a type of glioma that originates from oligodendrocytes, which protect electrical signaling in nerve cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Oligodendroglioma is a type of glioma that originates from oligodendrocytes, which support and insulate nerve cells in the central nervous system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Glioblastoma">
      <data key="d0">Glioblastoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Glioblastoma is an aggressive and malignant subtype of glioma, often characterized by rapid growth and resistance to treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Glioblastoma is the most destructive and dangerous type of glioma, arising from precursor cells that develop into astrocytes and oligodendrocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low-grade glioma">
      <data key="d0">Low-grade glioma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Low-grade glioma is a category of glioma characterized by slow growth and less aggressiveness, often associated with better prognosis compared to high-grade gliomas.&lt;SEP&gt;Low-grade glioma is a category of glioma that typically grows slowly and is less aggressive compared to high-grade gliomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-grade glioma">
      <data key="d0">High-grade glioma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-grade gli, which is a category of glioma that tends to grow rapidly and is more aggressive, often leading to severe symptoms and complications.&lt;SEP&gt;High-grade glioma is a category of glioma characterized by rapid growth and increased aggressiveness, often leading to severe symptoms and complications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brain Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Brain Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A brain tumor is an abnormal growth of cells in the brain, encompassing various types including gliomas, and can cause neurological symptoms and complications.&lt;SEP&gt;A brain tumor is an abnormal growth of cells in the brain, which can cause various symptoms such as nausea, weakness, and changes in personality.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low-Grade Glioma">
      <data key="d0">Low-Grade Glioma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Low-grade gliomas are slow-growing tumors that may not cause immediate symptoms and can develop for years before they’re diagnosed.&lt;SEP&gt;Low-grade gliomas are slow-growing tumors that may not cause immediate symptoms and can remain undetected for years.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Grade Glioma">
      <data key="d0">High-Grade Glioma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-grade gliomas are fast-growing, malignant tumors that often progress rapidly and require aggressive treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Headache">
      <data key="d0">Headache</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Headache is a common symptom of gliomas, often the first symptom in people with a glioma, and can be accompanied by nausea and vomiting.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seizures">
      <data key="d0">Seizures</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Seizures are frequent in people with brain tumors and are often an early sign of a glioma, especially in low-grade gliomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fatigue">
      <data key="d0">Fatigue</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fatigue is a common symptom of brain tumors, encompassing physical, cognitive, and emotional exhaustion.&lt;SEP&gt;Fatigue is a common symptom of gliomas, often debilitating and not related to sleep or activity levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Fatigue is a persistent tiredness or lack of energy often experienced by cancer survivors, which can impact daily activities and quality of life.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nausea and Vomiting">
      <data key="d0">Nausea and Vomiting</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nausea and vomiting can be caused by the tumor putting increased pressure on the brain, often associated with glioma progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lab Testing">
      <data key="d0">Lab Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lab testing involves analyzing biological samples to identify glioma subtypes and guide treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Survival Statistics">
      <data key="d0">Survival Statistics</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Survival statistics provide data on patient outcomes for gliomas, though they may not reflect modern treatment advancements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gliomas">
      <data key="d0">Gliomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Gliomas are a type of brain tumor that can cause symptoms like vision problems, sensory disturbances, and blood clots.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Resection">
      <data key="d0">Resection</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Resection is a surgical procedure to remove as much of a tumor as possible, serving both as a diagnostic tool and a treatment method.&lt;SEP&gt;Resection is a surgical procedure to remove tissue, often used to excise bladder tumors for diagnosis or treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Open Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Open Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Open biopsy is a surgical procedure to remove a tissue sample from a brain tumor, typically used when the tumor is in a difficult location.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nausea">
      <data key="d0">Nausea</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nausea is a symptom caused by increased pressure from a brain tumor on the brain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vomiting">
      <data key="d0">Vomiting</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vomiting is a symptom associated with brain tumors, often due to increased intracranial pressure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Confusion">
      <data key="d0">Confusion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Confusion is a cognitive symptom of brain tumors, indicating impaired thinking or memory function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Memory Loss">
      <data key="d0">Memory Loss</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Memory loss is a symptom of brain tumors, reflecting damage to brain regions responsible for memory.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Speech Difficulties">
      <data key="d0">Speech Difficulties</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Speech difficulties are symptoms of brain tumors affecting areas of the brain responsible for language and articulation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weakness">
      <data key="d0">Weakness</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Weakness is a physical symptom of brain tumors, often presenting as muscle weakness on one side of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Muscle Weakness">
      <data key="d0">Muscle Weakness</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Muscle weakness is a specific manifestation of brain tumors affecting motor functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Balance">
      <data key="d0">Balance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Balance issues are symptoms of brain tumors impacting coordination and spatial awareness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Personality Changes">
      <data key="d0">Personality Changes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Personality changes are behavioral symptoms of brain tumors affecting mood-regulating brain regions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mood Changes">
      <data key="d0">Mood Changes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mood changes are emotional symptoms of brain tumors, reflecting alterations in brain function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vision Problems">
      <data key="d0">Vision Problems</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vision problems are symptoms of brain tumors, including blurred vision, double vision, or loss of peripheral vision.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blurred Vision">
      <data key="d0">Blurred Vision</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blurred vision is a specific visual symptom of brain tumors affecting the visual pathways.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Double Vision">
      <data key="d0">Double Vision</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Double vision is a visual symptom caused by brain tumors disrupting eye coordination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Loss of Peripheral Vision">
      <data key="d0">Loss of Peripheral Vision</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Loss of peripheral vision is a visual symptom of brain tumors affecting the occipital lobe.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sensory Disturbances">
      <data key="d0">Sensory Disturbances</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sensory disturbances are symptoms of brain tumors, including numbness, tingling, or burning sensations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Numbness">
      <data key="d0">Numbness</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Numbness is a sensory symptom of brain tumors, often affecting one side of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tingling">
      <data key="d0">Tingling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tingling is a sensory symptom of brain tumors, indicating nerve damage or irritation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Burning Sensations">
      <data key="d0">Burning Sensations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Burning sensations are sensory symptoms of brain tumors, often associated with nerve involvement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Clots">
      <data key="d0">Blood Clots</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood clots are complications linked to high-grade gliomas, potentially causing leg swelling, chest pain, and shortness of breath.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Leg Swelling">
      <data key="d0">Leg Swelling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Leg swelling is a symptom of blood clots associated with high-grade gliomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chest Pain">
      <data key="d0">Chest Pain</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chest pain is a symptom of blood clots linked to high-grade gliomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shortness of Breath">
      <data key="d0">Shortness of Breath</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Shortness of breath is a symptom of blood clots associated with high-grade gliomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cognitive Fatigue">
      <data key="d0">Cognitive Fatigue</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cognitive fatigue is a type of fatigue characterized by mental exhaustion and reduced cognitive function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Emotional Fatigue">
      <data key="d0">Emotional Fatigue</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Emotional fatigue is a type of fatigue involving emotional exhaustion and mood changes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physical Fatigue">
      <data key="d0">Physical Fatigue</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Physical fatigue is a type of fatigue marked by physical exhaustion and reduced energy levels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="External Sources">
      <data key="d0">External Sources</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">External sources are factors like noise that contribute to fatigue in patients with brain tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Internal Sources">
      <data key="d0">Internal Sources</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Internal sources are factors like medication or poor nutrition that contribute to fatigue in patients with brain tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medication">
      <data key="d0">Medication</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Medication is an internal source of fatigue that can affect patients with brain tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Not Eating Properly">
      <data key="d0">Not Eating Properly</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Not eating properly is an internal source of fatigue contributing to the symptoms of brain tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Not Getting Enough Exercise">
      <data key="d0">Not Getting Enough Exercise</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Not getting enough exercise is an internal source of fatigue affecting patients with brain tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Provider">
      <data key="d0">Provider</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A provider is an organization or individual responsible for evaluating and managing the care of patients with brain tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parietal Lobe">
      <data key="d0">Parietal Lobe</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The parietal lobe is a region of the brain responsible for processing sensory information and spatial awareness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Occipital Lobe">
      <data key="d0">Occipital Lobe</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The occipital lobe is a region of the brain responsible for processing visual information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neuropathologist">
      <data key="d0">Neuropathologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A neuropathologist is a medical professional specializing in the diagnosis of neurological conditions, including gliomas, by analyzing tissue samples and identifying biomarkers to classify tumors and guide treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;A neuropathologist is a medical professional who tests tissue samples for specific biomarkers to aid in the diagnosis and classification of gliomas.&lt;SEP&gt;A neuropathologist is a specialist who examines tissue samples under a microscope to determine if a tumor is malignant or benign and to classify its type and grade.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biomarkers">
      <data key="d0">Biomarkers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biomarkers are molecular characteristics like genetic mutations (e.g., IDH gene) used to classify gliomas and predict their behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarkers are specific indicators found in the body that help in diagnosing and treating gliomas by providing information about the tumor's characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarkers are specific indicators found in the body that help in diagnosing and treating gliomas by providing information about the tumor's genetic, molecular, and pathological characteristics, enabling personalized treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IDH gene">
      <data key="d0">IDH gene</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The IDH gene is a biomarker associated with certain gliomas, such as astrocytoma IDH-mutant, indicating genetic mutations that influence tumor growth and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stereotactic biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Stereotactic biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A stereotactic biopsy is a surgical procedure where a thin needle is used to remove a tumor sample under precise imaging guidance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Open biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Open biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An open biopsy involves a craniotomy to remove a tumor sample, requiring surgical tools and a larger incision compared to stereotactic methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Molecular testing">
      <data key="d0">Molecular testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Molecular testing analyzes tumor samples for genetic markers and biomarkers to determine the tumor type, growth rate, and treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pathology report">
      <data key="d0">Pathology report</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A pathology report is a document generated after analyzing biopsy samples, detailing tumor classification, malignancy, and molecular findings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL)">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) is a type of blood cancer characterized by the slow growth of abnormal B cells in the bone marrow, leading to immune system dysfunction and requiring management strategies such as watchful waiting, chemotherapy, and targeted therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) is a type of blood cancer that affects B cells, characterized by slow growth and the potential need for treatment over time.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) is a type of cancer characterized by the overproduction of abnormal B lymphocytes in the blood, bone marrow, and lymphatic system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CLL">
      <data key="d0">CLL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) is a type of B-cell malignancy characterized by the accumulation of abnormal lymphocytes in the blood and bone marrow, often requiring specialized diagnostic and therapeutic approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) is a type of cancer affecting B-cells, characterized by the overproduction of abnormal white blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL) is a type of cancer affecting B cells in the blood and bone marrow, characterized by the accumulation of abnormal lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL) is a type of cancer affecting B cells in the blood and bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SLL">
      <data key="d0">SLL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Small lymphocytic lymphoma (SLL) is a type of cancer similar to CLL, primarily found in lymph nodes and the spleen, with overlapping clinical features.&lt;SEP&gt;Small lymphocytic lymphoma (SLL) is a type of cancer similar to CLL, primarily found in lymph nodes and the spleen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Richter transformation">
      <data key="d0">Richter transformation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Richter transformation is a condition category where CLL progresses to a more aggressive form of lymphoma, requiring intensified treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Richter transformation is a medical condition described in Chapter 5, associated with lymphomas and involving abnormal lymphocyte growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Richter transformation is a rare complication of CLL that can lead to more aggressive cancers like diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) or Hodgkin lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Richter transformation is a rare complication of CLL that can progress to more aggressive lymphomas like diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) or Hodgkin lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Watch and Wait">
      <data key="d0">Watch and Wait</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Watch and Wait is a clinical management event where CLL patients are monitored without immediate treatment, allowing for observation of disease progression and response to natural course.&lt;SEP&gt;Watch and Wait is a treatment approach event where patients with CLL are monitored without immediate intervention, allowing for observation of disease progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Watch and wait is a treatment approach where patients are monitored without immediate intervention for CLL, used when symptoms are minimal or blood cell counts are stable.&lt;SEP&gt;Watch and wait is a treatment approach where patients are monitored without immediate intervention for CLL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NIAID">
      <data key="d0">NIAID</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases (NIAID) is a U.S. federal agency that supports research on diseases like CLL, including the development of diagnostic tools and therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases (NIAID) is an organization that provides resources and research on diseases like CLL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Smear">
      <data key="d0">Blood Smear</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A blood smear is a diagnostic procedure where a drop of blood is examined under a microscope to identify abnormal cell morphology, aiding in the detection of leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunophenotype">
      <data key="d0">Immunophenotype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunophenotype refers to the unique pattern of proteins on cell surfaces, critical for diagnosing CLL and differentiating it from other blood cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monoclonal B Lymphocytes">
      <data key="d0">Monoclonal B Lymphocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Monoclonal B lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell that proliferates abnormally in CLL, with a threshold of 5,000 cells per microliter in blood for diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An oncologist is a healthcare professional (person) specializing in cancer treatment, including CLL, and managing treatment plans and prognostic assessments.&lt;SEP&gt;An oncologist is a physician specializing in cancer treatment, who collaborates with hematopathologists to interpret diagnostic results and plan CLL management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FISH (Fluorescence In Situ Hybridization)">
      <data key="d0">FISH (Fluorescence In Situ Hybridization)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FISH is a molecular test used to detect chromosomal abnormalities in CLL, aiding in risk stratification and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DNA Sequencing">
      <data key="d0">DNA Sequencing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">DNA sequencing analyzes genetic mutations in CLL cells, providing insights into disease progression and potential targeted therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CpG-Stimulated Karyotype">
      <data key="d0">CpG-Stimulated Karyotype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CpG-stimulated karyotype is a cytogenetic test that identifies chromosomal abnormalities in CLL, contributing to diagnostic and prognostic assessments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beta-2 Microglobulin">
      <data key="d0">Beta-2 Microglobulin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Beta-2 microglobulin is a protein marker measured in blood to assess CLL progression and response to treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LDH (Lactate Dehydrogenase)">
      <data key="d0">LDH (Lactate Dehydrogenase)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">LDH is an enzyme measured in blood to evaluate CLL activity, with elevated levels correlating with disease burden.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Quantitative Immunoglobulins">
      <data key="d0">Quantitative Immunoglobulins</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Quantitative immunoglobulins measure antibody levels in blood, providing insights into immune system dysfunction in CLL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reticulocyte Count">
      <data key="d0">Reticulocyte Count</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Reticulocyte count measures immature red blood cells in blood, reflecting bone marrow function and anemia risk in CLL patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Haptoglobin">
      <data key="d0">Haptoglobin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Haptoglobin is a protein that binds free hemoglobin in blood, with levels indicating hemolysis and anemia in CLL-related complications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Direct Antiglobulin (Coombs) Test">
      <data key="d0">Direct Antiglobulin (Coombs) Test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Coombs test detects antibodies attached to red blood cells, aiding in diagnosing hemolytic anemia in CLL patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Uric Acid">
      <data key="d0">Uric Acid</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Uric acid levels in blood are monitored to assess kidney function and the risk of tumor lysis syndrome in CLL patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatitis B and C Tests">
      <data key="d0">Hepatitis B and C Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatitis B and C tests screen for viral infections that may complicate CLL treatment, particularly in immunocompromised patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic CT Scans">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic CT Scans</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnostic CT scans of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis are used to detect lymph node enlargement and organ involvement in CLL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic Process">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic Process</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The diagnostic process for CLL involves a combination of blood tests, biopsies, and imaging to confirm the disease and assess its stage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic Tools">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic Tools</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnostic tools for CLL include blood tests, biopsies, imaging, and molecular assays to confirm the disease and assess its progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic Criteria">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic Criteria</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnostic criteria for CLL include monoclonal B lymphocyte counts, immunophenotype, and exclusion of other conditions to confirm the diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prognostic Factors">
      <data key="d0">Prognostic Factors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Prognostic factors for CLL include B symptoms, genetic markers (e.g., FISH, DNA sequencing), and performance status to predict disease progression and treatment outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Therapeutic Considerations">
      <data key="d0">Therapeutic Considerations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Therapeutic considerations for CLL include avoiding teratogenic agents in pregnant patients, managing comorbidities, and tailoring treatment based on genetic and clinical factors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Management">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Management</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical management of CLL involves monitoring disease progression, managing symptoms, and adjusting therapies based on diagnostic and prognostic data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B-cell lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">B-cell lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B-cell lymphoma is a category of cancers originating from B-cells, a type of white blood cell, and includes subtypes like cutaneous B-cell lymphoma (CBCL) and other lymphatic system disorders.&lt;SEP&gt;B-cell lymphoma is a category of lymphatic system cancers that originate from B-cells, a type of white blood cell involved in immune responses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic CT">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic CT</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnostic CT (Computed Tomography) is a medical imaging event used to visualize internal body structures, often with contrast agents, to assess cancer extent or detect abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagnostic CT (Computed Tomography) is a medical imaging event used to visualize internal structures, often with contrast agents, to assess cancer spread or detect abnormalities in CLL patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DNA sequencing">
      <data key="d0">DNA sequencing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">DNA sequencing is a genetic analysis category that identifies mutations in specific genes like TP53 and IGHV, which are critical for understanding CLL prognosis and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;DNA sequencing is a molecular analysis category that identifies mutations in genes like TP53 and IGHV, critical for understanding CLL prognosis and tailoring targeted therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beta-2 microglobulin">
      <data key="d0">Beta-2 microglobulin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Beta-2 microglobulin is a biomarker category that reflects disease progression in CLL, with elevated levels indicating a more aggressive disease course.&lt;SEP&gt;Beta-2 microglobulin is a biomarker category that reflects disease severity in CLL, with elevated levels indicating a more aggressive disease course and guiding treatment intensity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphatic system">
      <data key="d0">Lymphatic system</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The lymphatic system is a biological category responsible for immune function, housing lymphocytes and playing a role in the development of lymphomas like CBCL.&lt;SEP&gt;The lymphatic system is a major part of the immune system, consisting of tissues, organs, and vessels that transport lymph fluid and white blood cells, including lymph nodes, spleen, thymus, and bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cutaneous B-cell lymphoma (CBCL)">
      <data key="d0">Cutaneous B-cell lymphoma (CBCL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cutaneous B-cell lymphoma (CBCL) is a category of skin-related B-cell lymphomas, originating from B-cells and presenting as skin lesions, often linked to systemic conditions like CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;Cutaneous B-cell lymphoma (CBCL) is a category of skin-related B-cell lymphomas, originating from B-cells and presenting as skin lesions.&lt;SEP&gt;Cutaneous B-cell lymphoma (CBCL) is a type of cancer affecting the skin's B-cells, requiring accurate testing for diagnosis and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Cutaneous B-cell lymphoma (CBCL) is a type of primary cutaneous lymphoma that develops from abnormal B cells, presenting as skin nodules resembling papules.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CLL specialist">
      <data key="d0">CLL specialist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A CLL specialist is a healthcare professional (person) with expertise in diagnosing and managing chronic lymphocytic leukemia, providing specialized care and treatment recommendations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility specialist">
      <data key="d0">Fertility specialist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A fertility specialist is a healthcare professional (person) who assists patients with fertility preservation options, including sperm or egg freezing, during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility counselor">
      <data key="d0">Fertility counselor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A fertility counselor is a healthcare professional (person) who provides guidance on fertility preservation strategies for patients undergoing cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary cutaneous lymphomas">
      <data key="d0">Primary cutaneous lymphomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary cutaneous lymphomas (PCL) are a rare group of Non-Hodgkin lymphomas (NHL) that originate from abnormal B or T lymphocytes in the skin, not skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL)">
      <data key="d0">Non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL) is a cancer of lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell, characterized by uncontrolled growth of abnormal lymphocytes in the lymphatic system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Natural killer (NK) cells">
      <data key="d0">Natural killer (NK) cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Natural killer (NK) cells are lymphocytes that can kill tumor cells and virus-infected cells without prior activation, contributing to immune defense against cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cutaneous T-cell lymphoma (CTCL)">
      <data key="d0">Cutaneous T-cell lymphoma (CTCL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cutaneous T-cell lymphoma (CTCL) is a type of primary cutaneous lymphoma arising from abnormal T cells, with subtypes including mycosis fungoides and Sézary syndrome.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mycosis fungoides">
      <data key="d0">Mycosis fungoides</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mycosis fungoides is a subtype of Cutaneous T-cell lymphoma (CTCL), characterized by chronic skin inflammation and infiltration of abnormal T cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sézary syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Sézary syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sézary syndrome is an aggressive subtype of Cutaneous T-cell lymphoma (CTCL), involving widespread skin involvement and circulating malignant T cells in the blood.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary cutaneous T-cell lymphoproliferative disorders (PCLPDs)">
      <data key="d0">Primary cutaneous T-cell lymphoproliferative disorders (PCLPDs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary cutaneous T-cell lymphoproliferative disorders (PCLPDs) are rare subtypes of CTCL involving abnormal proliferation of T cells in the skin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph nodes">
      <data key="d0">Lymph nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph nodes are bean-shaped structures in the lymphatic system that filter lymph fluid, produce immune cells, and play a key role in immune responses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphatic vessels">
      <data key="d0">Lymphatic vessels</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphatic vessels are thin tubes that transport lymph fluid and white blood cells throughout the body, connecting to lymph nodes and organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph">
      <data key="d0">Lymph</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph is a fluid transported by the lymphatic system, containing white blood cells and nutrients, essential for immune function and tissue hydration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bladder cancer">
      <data key="d0">Bladder cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of cancer originating in the bladder, often treated with procedures like TURBT, radical cystectomy, and chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a malignant condition of the urinary tract, characterized by uncontrolled cell growth in the bladder's inner lining, requiring comprehensive diagnostic and treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical history">
      <data key="d0">Medical history</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical history is a record of a patient's health issues, treatments, and medications, crucial for informing treatment decisions and monitoring health status.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physical exam">
      <data key="d0">Physical exam</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Physical exam is a diagnostic procedure involving assessment of vital signs, organ function, and physical symptoms to evaluate a patient's health condition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Test results">
      <data key="d0">Test results</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Test results encompass findings from blood tests, biopsies, and imaging studies, essential for diagnosing conditions like CBCL and bladder cancer and guiding treatment plans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bladder Cancer is a malignant condition characterized by abnormal cell growth in the bladder, often treated with procedures like TURBT, radical cystectomy, and intravesical therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder Cancer is a type of cancer originating in the urothelial cells lining the bladder, classified by staging and grading.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder Cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the bladder, characterized by abnormal cell growth in the inner layer of the bladder. It is categorized into stages based on the extent of the disease and is a central focus of the text.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a malignant condition affecting the bladder, requiring a multidisciplinary approach for treatment and management.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a malignant condition affecting the bladder, treated with surgical, intravesical, and systemic therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer affecting the urinary bladder, often treated with chemotherapy, immunotherapy, or targeted therapies depending on its stage and genetic profile.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that affects the bladder and is treated with various therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the bladder, often linked to smoking and genetic factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the urinary bladder and is discussed in the context of treatment options, clinical trials, and associated side effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the urinary tract, specifically in the urothelium lining the bladder, kidneys, ureters, and urethra. It is characterized by uncontrolled cell growth and can present in various stages and types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urothelium">
      <data key="d0">Urothelium</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The urothelium is the inner lining of the urinary tract, including the bladder, kidneys, ureters, and urethra. It is the primary site where bladder cancer can develop.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urinary Tract">
      <data key="d0">Urinary Tract</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The urinary tract is a system of organs responsible for producing, storing, and excreting urine, including the kidneys, ureters, bladder, and urethra.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kidney">
      <data key="d0">Kidney</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The kidney is an organ in the urinary tract that filters blood to remove waste and excess fluids, forming urine. It is part of the system where bladder cancer can originate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ureter">
      <data key="d0">Ureter</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The ureter is a tube that transports urine from the kidney to the bladder. It is part of the urinary tract and a potential site for urothelial cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Small Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Small cell carcinoma is a rare and aggressive type of bladder cancer that originates in neuroendocrine cells, often associated with smoking and other risk factors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hematuria">
      <data key="d0">Hematuria</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hematuria refers to the presence of blood in urine, which is the most common symptom of bladder cancer and often indicates the presence of a tumor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urinalysis">
      <data key="d0">Urinalysis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urinalysis is a diagnostic test that analyzes urine for abnormalities, including blood, cells, or cancerous cells, to aid in the detection of bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cytology">
      <data key="d0">Cytology</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cytology is the study of cells, often used in bladder cancer diagnosis to examine urine samples for cancerous or precancerous cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemical Exposure">
      <data key="d0">Chemical Exposure</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemical exposure to certain industrial substances, such as dyes and solvents, increases the risk of bladder cancer by causing DNA damage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Age">
      <data key="d0">Age</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Age is a risk factor for bladder cancer, with the risk increasing significantly as individuals grow older.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hereditary Mutation">
      <data key="d0">Hereditary Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hereditary mutations are genetic changes passed down through families that increase susceptibility to bladder cancer and other cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic bladder cancer is cancer that has spread beyond the bladder to other parts of the body, typically managed with systemic chemotherapy alone or in combination with immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic bladder cancer refers to cancer that has spread beyond the bladder to other parts of the body, such as lymph nodes, bones, lungs, liver, or the abdominal cavity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Non-Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer is an early stage where the tumor is confined to the urothelial layer of the bladder without invading deeper muscle layers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Muscle-invasive bladder cancer is an advanced stage where the tumor has penetrated the bladder's muscle layer, increasing the risk of metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Abdomen">
      <data key="d0">Abdomen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The abdomen is a region where metastatic bladder cancer can spread, often involving the abdominal cavity and surrounding organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Aggressive Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Aggressive Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Aggressive cancer refers to cancer that grows or spreads more rapidly than average, often associated with advanced stages or high-grade tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnosis">
      <data key="d0">Diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnosis is the process of identifying bladder cancer through medical tests, symptoms, and clinical evaluation to determine the stage and type of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage">
      <data key="d0">Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage refers to the extent of bladder cancer, classified into non-muscle-invasive, muscle-invasive, or metastatic stages, which guide treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cystoscopy and Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Cystoscopy and Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cystoscopy and Biopsy are medical procedures used to diagnose bladder cancer, involving the examination of the bladder and removal of tissue samples for analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inherited Cancer Risk">
      <data key="d0">Inherited Cancer Risk</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Inherited Cancer Risk refers to the likelihood of developing bladder cancer due to genetic factors passed down through families, which can be assessed through genetic testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Inherited cancer risk refers to the increased likelihood of developing certain cancers due to genetic mutations passed down from parents.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gene Mutations">
      <data key="d0">Gene Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Gene Mutations are genetic changes that can lead to uncontrolled cell growth, contributing to the development of bladder cancer, particularly mutations in tumor suppressor genes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Abnormal Cells">
      <data key="d0">Abnormal Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Abnormal Cells are cells that have undergone changes in their structure or function, which can be indicative of cancerous or precancerous conditions in the bladder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Recurrence">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Recurrence</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Recurrence refers to the return of bladder cancer after treatment, which requires ongoing monitoring and management to prevent further progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Detection">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Detection</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Detection involves identifying bladder cancer at an early stage through screening, symptoms, and diagnostic tests to improve treatment outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Care">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Care refers to the comprehensive management of bladder cancer, including treatment, support, and follow-up care to enhance quality of life and survival rates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Prevention">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Prevention</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Prevention involves strategies to reduce the risk of developing bladder cancer, such as avoiding known risk factors like smoking and maintaining a healthy lifestyle.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Research">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Research</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Research is the scientific study of bladder cancer to develop new treatments, improve diagnostic methods, and understand the biological mechanisms underlying the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Diagnosis">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Diagnosis is the process of confirming the presence of bladder cancer through clinical evaluation, imaging, and laboratory tests to determine the stage and type of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Management">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Management</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Management involves the coordinated approach to treating and monitoring bladder cancer, including medical, surgical, and supportive care strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Therapy refers to the specific treatments used to combat bladder cancer, such as chemotherapy, radiation therapy, or targeted therapies, tailored to the patient's condition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Team">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Cancer Treatment Team is a multidisciplinary group of healthcare professionals, including oncologists, surgeons, and nurses, who collaborate to provide comprehensive care for bladder cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Multidisciplinary Team">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Multidisciplinary Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Cancer Multidisciplinary Team consists of specialists from various fields who work together to develop and implement personalized treatment plans for bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Care Team">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Care Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Cancer Care Team is responsible for the holistic management of bladder cancer patients, including treatment, symptom management, and emotional support.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Support">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Support</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Support includes resources and services provided to bladder cancer patients and their families to help manage the physical, emotional, and practical challenges of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Survivorship">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Survivorship</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Survivorship refers to the phase after cancer treatment, focusing on managing long-term effects, maintaining health, and addressing lifestyle and emotional challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer Survivorship refers to the phase of life after cancer treatment, focusing on long-term health, quality of life, and preventing recurrence in bladder cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Follow-Up">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Follow-Up</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Follow-Up involves regular medical check-ups and monitoring to detect any signs of cancer recurrence or complications after treatment for bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Monitoring">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Monitoring</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Monitoring is the ongoing assessment of bladder cancer patients to track disease progression, treatment response, and potential side effects, ensuring timely interventions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Recurrence Monitoring">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Recurrence Monitoring</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Recurrence Monitoring is a specialized form of cancer monitoring focused on detecting the return of bladder cancer after treatment, using advanced diagnostic tools and regular follow-up.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Modalities">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Modalities</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Modalities are the different methods or techniques used to treat bladder cancer, such as surgical resection, systemic chemotherapy, or localized radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Methods">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Methods encompass the specific strategies and protocols used to manage bladder cancer, including minimally invasive procedures, drug therapies, and supportive care measures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Protocols">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Protocols</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Protocols are standardized guidelines and procedures followed by healthcare professionals to ensure consistent, evidence-based care for bladder cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Guidelines">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Guidelines</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Guidelines are recommendations developed by medical organizations to direct the diagnosis, treatment, and management of bladder cancer based on the latest research and clinical evidence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Outcomes">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Outcomes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Outcomes refer to the results of cancer treatments, including remission, disease control, or progression, which are critical for evaluating the effectiveness of therapies for bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Success">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Success</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Success is the achievement of positive therapeutic outcomes, such as complete remission or prolonged survival, following treatment for bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Failure">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Failure</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Failure occurs when cancer does not respond to treatment or recurs despite initial success, necessitating alternative or more aggressive therapeutic strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Response">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Response</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Response indicates how effectively a treatment for bladder cancer is working, often assessed through imaging, lab tests, and clinical evaluation of symptoms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Resistance">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Resistance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Resistance is the phenomenon where bladder cancer cells become unresponsive to therapeutic interventions, requiring the development of new treatment approaches or combination therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Efficacy">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Efficacy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Efficacy measures the ability of a treatment to produce the desired therapeutic effect, such as tumor shrinkage or disease stabilization, in bladder cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Toxicity">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Toxicity</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Toxicity refers to the harmful side effects of therapies used to treat bladder cancer, which can impact the patient's quality of life and require management strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Side Effects">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Side Effects</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Side Effects are the adverse reactions experienced by bladder cancer patients undergoing treatment, such as fatigue, nausea, or immunosuppression, which need to be managed alongside therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Quality of Life">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Quality of Life</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Quality of Life focuses on the patient's overall well-being and ability to perform daily activities during and after bladder cancer treatment, emphasizing holistic care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Survival Rates">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Survival Rates</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Survival Rates are statistical measures indicating the proportion of bladder cancer patients who survive for a specified period after diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Life Expectancy">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Life Expectancy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Life Expectancy refers to the estimated duration of life a bladder cancer patient is expected to live following treatment, influenced by factors such as cancer stage and treatment response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Prognosis">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Prognosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Prognosis is the prediction of the likely course of bladder cancer, including the chances of recovery or recurrence, based on treatment outcomes and patient-specific factors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Survivorship">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Survivorship</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Survivorship is the phase of life after cancer treatment, focusing on long-term health, lifestyle adjustments, and preventing recurrence in bladder cancer survivors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Follow-Up">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Follow-Up</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Follow-Up involves regular medical assessments and monitoring to ensure that bladder cancer remains in remission and to address any late effects of treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Monitoring">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Monitoring</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Monitoring is the continuous evaluation of bladder cancer patients to track treatment effectiveness, detect recurrence, and manage any complications that may arise post-treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Research">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Research</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Research is the ongoing investigation into new and improved methods for treating bladder cancer, including novel drugs, therapies, and personalized treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Innovation">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Innovation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Innovation refers to the development of cutting-edge technologies and approaches, such as immunotherapy or targeted therapies, to enhance the effectiveness and reduce the side effects of bladder cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Education">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Education</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Education involves informing patients and healthcare providers about bladder cancer, its treatment options, and the importance of adherence to treatment plans to optimize outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Advocacy">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Advocacy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Advocacy is the promotion of awareness, research funding, and patient support initiatives to improve the care and outcomes for individuals with bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Policy">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Policy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Policy refers to the regulations, guidelines, and funding mechanisms established to ensure equitable access to high-quality care and treatment for bladder cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Equity">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Equity</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Equity focuses on ensuring that all bladder cancer patients, regardless of socioeconomic status or geographic location, have access to the same quality of care and treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Access">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Access</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Access refers to the availability and affordability of bladder cancer treatments, including medications, procedures, and supportive care services, to ensure that patients can receive necessary care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Costs">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Costs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Costs encompass the financial burden associated with bladder cancer treatment, including direct medical expenses and indirect costs related to lost productivity and caregiving.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Insurance">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Insurance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Insurance is a form of health insurance that covers the costs of bladder cancer treatments, including medications, procedures, and hospitalization, to reduce financial strain on patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Support Services">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Support Services</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Support Services include counseling, nutrition advice, and financial assistance programs provided to bladder cancer patients to help them manage the challenges of treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Research Funding">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Research Funding</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Research Funding is the allocation of financial resources to support scientific studies and clinical trials aimed at advancing the understanding and treatment of bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Clinical Trials">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Clinical Trials</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Clinical Trials are research studies that test new treatments, drugs, or procedures for bladder cancer, offering patients access to innovative therapies and contributing to medical advancements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Drug Development">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Drug Development</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Drug Development involves the research and creation of new pharmaceuticals specifically targeting bladder cancer, with the goal of improving treatment efficacy and reducing side effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Biomarkers">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Biomarkers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Biomarkers are biological indicators used to predict treatment response, monitor disease progression, and personalize therapy for bladder cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Personalized Medicine">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Personalized Medicine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Personalized Medicine is an approach that tailors cancer treatments to the individual characteristics of each bladder cancer patient, such as genetic profile and disease stage, to optimize therapeutic outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Precision Medicine">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Precision Medicine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Precision Medicine involves using advanced technologies and data analysis to develop targeted therapies for bladder cancer, based on the specific genetic and molecular features of the tumor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Immunotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Immunotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Immunotherapy is a type of therapy that harnesses the body's immune system to fight bladder cancer, including checkpoint inhibitors and monoclonal antibodies that target cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Targeted Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Targeted Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Targeted Therapy uses drugs or other substances to precisely attack cancer cells while minimizing damage to normal cells, often based on specific genetic mutations in bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Radiation Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Radiation Therapy employs high-energy radiation to kill bladder cancer cells or shrink tumors, often used in conjunction with other treatments such as surgery or chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Surgery involves the removal of bladder cancer tissue or the entire bladder, depending on the stage and location of the disease, as a primary or adjunctive treatment modality.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Chemotherapy uses drugs to kill bladder cancer cells, often administered intravenously or directly into the bladder, to treat both early-stage and advanced disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Palliative Care">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Palliative Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Palliative Care focuses on relieving symptoms and improving the quality of life for bladder cancer patients, particularly in advanced stages, rather than curative treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment End-of-Life Care">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment End-of-Life Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment End-of-Life Care provides support for bladder cancer patients nearing the end of life, emphasizing comfort, dignity, and the management of pain and other symptoms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Rehabilitation">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Rehabilitation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Rehabilitation involves restoring physical, emotional, and social functioning in bladder cancer patients following treatment, helping them regain independence and quality of life.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Nutrition Support">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Nutrition Support</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Nutrition Support is the provision of dietary advice and nutritional supplements to bladder cancer patients to maintain strength, manage treatment side effects, and support recovery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Psychological Support">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Psychological Support</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Psychological Support includes counseling, therapy, and support groups to address the emotional and mental health challenges faced by bladder cancer patients and their families.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Social Support">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Social Support</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Social Support involves community resources, family, and friends providing assistance and encouragement to bladder cancer patients throughout their treatment journey.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Spiritual Support">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Spiritual Support</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Spiritual Support refers to the inclusion of spiritual or religious practices and counseling to provide comfort, meaning, and coping strategies for bladder cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Legal Support">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Legal Support</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Legal Support involves assistance with legal matters such as advance directives, power of attorney, and understanding patient rights related to bladder cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Financial Counseling">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Financial Counseling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Financial Counseling provides guidance to bladder cancer patients on managing the financial aspects of treatment, including insurance, loans, and assistance programs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Education">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Education</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Education involves informing bladder cancer patients about their diagnosis, treatment options, potential side effects, and ways to manage their condition effectively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Advocacy">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Advocacy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Advocacy is the support and representation of bladder cancer patients to ensure their voices are heard, their needs are met, and they receive equitable and compassionate care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Empowerment">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Empowerment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Empowerment focuses on enabling bladder cancer patients to take an active role in their treatment decisions, fostering informed consent and shared decision-making with healthcare providers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Safety">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Safety</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Safety involves measures to ensure the well-being and safety of bladder cancer patients during treatment, including minimizing medical errors and adverse events.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction refers to the degree to which bladder cancer patients feel their treatment needs are met, their concerns are addressed, and their overall experience is positive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Experience">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Experience</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Experience encompasses the holistic experience of bladder cancer patients during treatment, including their interactions with healthcare providers, the environment, and the support systems in place.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Outcomes">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Outcomes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Outcomes are the measurable results of bladder cancer treatment, including survival rates, quality of life, and the ability to return to normal activities, which are critical for evaluating treatment effectiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Follow-Up">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Follow-Up</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Follow-Up involves ongoing communication and monitoring between bladder cancer patients and healthcare providers to ensure continued care, address any complications, and support long-term health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Monitoring">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Monitoring</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Monitoring is the systematic observation of bladder cancer patients to track treatment response, detect recurrence, and manage any late effects or complications that may arise after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Support Networks">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Support Networks</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Support Networks are groups or organizations that provide resources, information, and emotional support to bladder cancer patients and their families, fostering a sense of community and shared experience.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Community">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Community</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Community refers to the collective of bladder cancer patients, caregivers, and advocates who share experiences, knowledge, and support to enhance the treatment journey and improve outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Resources">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Resources</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Resources include educational materials, websites, hotlines, and support services designed to assist bladder cancer patients in understanding their condition, treatment options, and available support systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Information">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Information</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Information is the provision of accurate, up-to-date, and accessible information to bladder cancer patients about their diagnosis, treatment options, and the importance of adherence to treatment plans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Communication">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Communication</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Communication involves the effective exchange of information between bladder cancer patients and healthcare providers to ensure informed decision-making, treatment adherence, and optimal care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Engagement">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Engagement</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Engagement is the process of actively involving bladder cancer patients in their care decisions, treatment plans, and follow-up, promoting a sense of ownership and collaboration in the treatment process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Collaboration">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Collaboration</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Collaboration refers to the partnership between bladder cancer patients and healthcare providers to co-create treatment plans, monitor progress, and address any challenges that may arise during the treatment journey.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Partnership">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Partnership</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Partnership is a model of care that emphasizes shared decision-making, mutual respect, and collaboration between bladder cancer patients and healthcare providers to achieve the best possible outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction Surveys">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction Surveys</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction Surveys are tools used to assess the level of satisfaction among bladder cancer patients with their treatment experience, providing valuable feedback for improving care quality.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Experience Surveys">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Experience Surveys</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Experience Surveys are assessments designed to gather detailed insights into the experiences of bladder cancer patients during treatment, highlighting areas for improvement in care delivery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Feedback">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Feedback</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Feedback is the input provided by bladder cancer patients regarding their treatment experience, which is used to refine and enhance the quality of care and treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction Metrics">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction Metrics</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction Metrics are quantitative measures used to evaluate the level of satisfaction among bladder cancer patients with their treatment, helping to identify areas for improvement in healthcare services.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Experience Metrics">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Experience Metrics</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Experience Metrics are data-driven indicators that assess the quality of the treatment experience for bladder cancer patients, providing insights into the effectiveness of care delivery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction Research">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction Research</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction Research involves the study of patient satisfaction levels with bladder cancer treatments, aiming to identify factors that influence satisfaction and inform improvements in care.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction Research is the study of patient satisfaction levels with bladder cancer treatments, aimed at identifying factors that influence satisfaction and informing improvements in care delivery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Experience Research">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Experience Research</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Experience Research involves the investigation of the experiences of bladder cancer patients during treatment, with the goal of uncovering insights that can enhance the quality of care and patient outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer Treatment Patient Experience Research is the investigation of the experiences of bladder cancer patients during treatment, focusing on factors that contribute to positive or negative outcomes in care delivery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction Analysis">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction Analysis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction Analysis is the examination of data on patient satisfaction with bladder cancer treatments to identify trends, patterns, and areas for enhancement in healthcare services.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Experience Analysis">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Experience Analysis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Experience Analysis involves the evaluation of patient experiences during bladder cancer treatment to uncover insights that can inform improvements in care quality and patient outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction Improvement">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction Improvement</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction Improvement is the process of implementing changes and strategies to enhance the satisfaction levels of bladder cancer patients with their treatment experience.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Experience Improvement">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Experience Improvement</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Experience Improvement focuses on refining the treatment experience for bladder cancer patients to ensure it is more effective, compassionate, and aligned with their needs and expectations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction Enhancement">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction Enhancement</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction Enhancement involves proactive measures to increase the satisfaction levels of bladder cancer patients with their treatment, fostering a positive and supportive care environment.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction Enhancement involves proactive measures to increase the satisfaction levels of bladder, cancer patients with their treatment, fostering a positive and supportive care environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Experience Enhancement">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Experience Enhancement</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Experience Enhancement is the process of improving the overall experience of bladder cancer patients during treatment, ensuring that their needs are met and their care is both effective and compassionate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction Strategies">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction Strategies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction Strategies are methods and approaches used to improve the satisfaction levels of bladder cancer patients with their treatment, based on patient feedback and care quality assessments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Experience Strategies">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Experience Strategies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Experience Strategies involve the implementation of initiatives and practices aimed at enhancing the treatment experience for bladder cancer patients, ensuring their needs are prioritized and their care is optimized.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction Programs">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction Programs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Satisfaction Programs are structured initiatives designed to improve the satisfaction levels of bladder cancer patients with their treatment, incorporating patient feedback and quality improvement measures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Patient Experience Programs">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Patient Experience Programs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Patient Experience Programs are organized efforts to enhance the overall treatment experience for bladder cancer patients, focusing on patient-centered care and continuous quality improvement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liver Function Test">
      <data key="d0">Liver Function Test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A liver function test evaluates enzymes and substances produced by the liver, such as alkaline phosphatase and bilirubin, to assess liver health and detect potential issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kidney Function Test">
      <data key="d0">Kidney Function Test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A kidney function test measures waste products like creatinine and blood urea nitrogen (BUN) to evaluate kidney function and guide treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biomarker Tests">
      <data key="d0">Biomarker Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biomarker tests analyze specific substances in the body to provide clues about bladder cancer, though they are not yet reliable for diagnosis or surveillance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urine Cytology">
      <data key="d0">Urine Cytology</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urine cytology is a lab test that examines urine samples for cancer or precancerous cells, often used when blood is present in urine to detect bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urine Tests for Tumor Markers">
      <data key="d0">Urine Tests for Tumor Markers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urine tests for tumor markers detect cancer cells shed into urine, aiding in the diagnosis and monitoring of bladder cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multidisciplinary Care Team">
      <data key="d0">Multidisciplinary Care Team</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A multidisciplinary care team consists of various specialists collaborating to provide comprehensive care for bladder cancer patients, integrating medical, surgical, and supportive treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Care Doctor">
      <data key="d0">Primary Care Doctor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A primary care doctor is a healthcare provider who offers general medical care and coordinates a patient's overall health management, including follow-up care after cancer treatment and survivorship planning.&lt;SEP&gt;A primary care doctor is a healthcare provider who offers general medical care and coordinates a patient's overall health management, including follow-up care after cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A primary care doctor manages general medical care and coordinates cancer treatment, acting as a liaison between patients and the cancer care team.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urologic Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Urologic Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A urologic oncologist is a surgeon who diagnoses and treats cancers of the urinary tract and male reproductive organs using surgical and intravesical therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oncology Nurses">
      <data key="d0">Oncology Nurses</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Oncology nurses provide hands-on care, manage treatments, and support patients with cancer, including administering chemotherapy and managing side effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pharmacist">
      <data key="d0">Pharmacist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A pharmacist prepares chemotherapy and other medications, offering treatment advice and ensuring proper drug management for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic Radiologist">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic Radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A diagnostic radiologist interprets imaging tests like x-rays to assess for cancer and guide diagnostic and treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anesthesiologist">
      <data key="d0">Anesthesiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An anesthesiologist administers anesthesia to ensure patient comfort during surgical procedures, including cystoscopy and tumor removal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advanced Practice Providers">
      <data key="d0">Advanced Practice Providers</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Advanced practice providers, such as nurse practitioners and physician assistants, monitor patient health and deliver care as part of the multidisciplinary team.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Residents and Fellows">
      <data key="d0">Residents and Fellows</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Residents and fellows are medical trainees who contribute to patient care and research, often assisting in procedures like cystoscopy and biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nutritionists">
      <data key="d0">Nutritionists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Nutritionists provide dietary guidance tailored to the specific needs of cancer patients, supporting their overall health and treatment outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Psychologists and Psychiatrists">
      <data key="d0">Psychologists and Psychiatrists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Psychologists and psychiatrists address mental health concerns like depression and anxiety in cancer patients, offering emotional support and coping strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Counselors">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Counselors</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic counselors interpret family history and genetic risks to inform treatment decisions and provide guidance on hereditary cancer risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ureteroscopy">
      <data key="d0">Ureteroscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ureteroscopy is a procedure similar to cystoscopy that examines the upper urinary tract (ureters and kidneys) for abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;Ureteroscopy is a similar exam to cystoscopy but examines the upper urinary tract (ureters and kidneys) for abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blue Light Cystoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Blue Light Cystoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blue light cystoscopy uses a special dye and blue light to enhance tumor detection during bladder exams, improving visibility of abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Narrow-Band Imaging">
      <data key="d0">Narrow-Band Imaging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Narrow-band imaging is a technology that improves tumor detection during cystoscopy without requiring contrast dye, offering clearer visualization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urogram">
      <data key="d0">Urogram</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A urogram uses contrast and CT or MRI to visualize the urinary tract and assess the extent of bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Urogram uses a contrast solution with CT or MRI to evaluate the function and structure of the entire urinary tract, including kidneys and bladder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">Renal Ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A renal ultrasound is a non-invasive imaging technique that provides real-time images of the kidneys, ureters, and bladder.&lt;SEP&gt;Renal ultrasound provides real-time imaging of the kidneys, ureters, and bladder using sound waves, often used for non-invasive diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pyelogram">
      <data key="d0">Pyelogram</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A pyelogram uses x-rays and contrast solution to detect blockages in the ureters or kidneys, such as tumors or kidney stones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Counseling">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Counseling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic counseling is a service that helps individuals understand their risk for inherited cancers and the implications of genetic testing results.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CDKN2A">
      <data key="d0">CDKN2A</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CDKN2A is a gene that, when mutated, can contribute to inherited cancer risk, particularly in conditions like bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FGFR3">
      <data key="d0">FGFR3</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FGFR3 is a gene associated with inherited cancer risk, including mutations linked to bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PIK3CA">
      <data key="d0">PIK3CA</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PIK3CA is a gene that, when mutated, can increase the risk of certain cancers, including bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ERBB">
      <data key="d0">ERBB</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ERBB is a gene associated with inherited cancer risk, including mutations linked to bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CDC.gov">
      <data key="d0">CDC.gov</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">CDC.gov is a government agency providing information on tobacco use and cancer staging and grading.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 0a">
      <data key="d0">Stage 0a</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 0a describes cancer cells that grow from the inner lining toward the hollow center of the bladder, rather than into the bladder wall.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 0is">
      <data key="d0">Stage 0is</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 0is stands for carcinoma in situ (CIS), a flat patch of cancer cells on the bladder lining that is fast-growing and aggressive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 3A">
      <data key="d0">Stage 3A</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 3A is bladder cancer that has spread to nearby lymph nodes or reproductive organs but not distant sites.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low Grade (LG)">
      <data key="d0">Low Grade (LG)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Low Grade (LG) refers to cancer cells that closely resemble normal cells and grow slowly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High Grade (HG)">
      <data key="d0">High Grade (HG)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High Grade (HG) refers to cancer cells that differ significantly from normal cells and grow rapidly, indicating aggressiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urothelial Cells">
      <data key="d0">Urothelial Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urothelial Cells are the primary cell type in the inner lining of the bladder where most bladder cancers originate.&lt;SEP&gt;Urothelial cells are the specialized cells lining the inner surface of the bladder, serving as the origin of urothelial cancer and other bladder-related conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urothelial Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Urothelial Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urothelial cancer is a type of bladder cancer that originates in the urothelial cells lining the bladder, and it has various subtypes based on cellular characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Glandular Subtype">
      <data key="d0">Glandular Subtype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Glandular subtype is a rare variant of urothelial cancer where cancer cells resemble glandular structures, indicating a distinct cellular morphology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Subtype">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Subtype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous cell subtype is a rare form of urothelial cancer characterized by squamous cell differentiation, which may indicate aggressive behavior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Micropapillary Subtype">
      <data key="d0">Micropapillary Subtype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Micropapillary subtype is a rare and aggressive variant of urothelial cancer, often associated with poor prognosis and rapid progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Plasmacytoid Subtype">
      <data key="d0">Plasmacytoid Subtype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Plasmacytoid subtype is a rare type of urothelial cancer with cells resembling plasma cells, which may influence treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sarcomatoid Subtype">
      <data key="d0">Sarcomatoid Subtype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sarcomatoid subtype is a rare and highly aggressive form of urothelial cancer, often requiring more intensive treatment due to its rapid growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nested Subtype">
      <data key="d0">Nested Subtype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nested subtype is a rare variant of urothelial cancer characterized by a distinct growth pattern, which may affect prognosis and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphoepithelioma-like Subtype">
      <data key="d0">Lymphoepithelioma-like Subtype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphoepithelioma-like subtype is a rare and aggressive form of urothelial cancer with features resembling lymphoid and epithelial cell characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Microcystic and Tubular Subtype">
      <data key="d0">Microcystic and Tubular Subtype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Microcystic and tubular subtype is a rare variant of urothelial cancer with unique structural features, potentially influencing treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TURBT">
      <data key="d0">TURBT</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">TURBT (Transurethral Resection of Bladder Tumor) is a surgical procedure to examine and remove tumors on the bladder wall, often used for bladder cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;TURBT (Transurethral Resection of Bladder Tumor) is a surgical procedure to remove bladder tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;TURBT (Transurethral Resection of Bladder Tumor) is a surgical procedure to remove visible bladder tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;TURBT (Transurethral Resection of Bladder Tumor) is a surgical procedure used to remove bladder tumors, often combined with intravesical therapy for early-stage bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;TURBT (transurethral resection of bladder tumor) is a surgical procedure to remove visible bladder cancer, often part of trimodal therapy for bladder preservation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intravesical Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Intravesical Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Intravesical Therapy is a treatment involving the administration of medication directly into the bladder, often used in combination with TURBT for bladder cancer management.&lt;SEP&gt;Intravesical therapy involves administering medication directly into the bladder to destroy residual cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Intravesical therapy involves instilling chemotherapy or immunotherapy directly into the bladder to treat superficial bladder cancer, often used post-TURBT.&lt;SEP&gt;Intravesical therapy is a treatment method where medicine is placed directly into the bladder to destroy cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low Grade">
      <data key="d0">Low Grade</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Low grade bladder cancer is characterized by slow-growing cells that are less likely to invade deeper tissues, often associated with better prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High Grade">
      <data key="d0">High Grade</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High grade bladder cancer consists of rapidly growing cells with a higher likelihood of invasion and metastasis, requiring more aggressive treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radical Cystectomy">
      <data key="d0">Radical Cystectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Radical Cystectomy is a major surgical procedure to remove the entire bladder, nearby lymph nodes, and sometimes other organs (e.g., prostate, seminal vesicles) for muscle-invasive bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urinary Diversion">
      <data key="d0">Urinary Diversion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urinary Diversion refers to surgical procedures to redirect urine flow after bladder removal, including types like ileal conduit, neobladder, and continent cutaneous pouch.&lt;SEP&gt;Urinary diversion is a surgical procedure rerouting urine flow, including the Indiana pouch and other methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Urinary diversion is a surgical procedure that reroutes urine flow, such as the Indiana pouch, to manage bladder function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neobladder">
      <data key="d0">Neobladder</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Neobladder is a type of urinary diversion where a segment of small intestine is used to create a functional bladder substitute, allowing urine to exit through the urethra.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ileal Conduit">
      <data key="d0">Ileal Conduit</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ileal Conduit is a urinary diversion method where a section of the small intestine is used to create a tube (conduit) for urine drainage through a stoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Ileal Conduit is a urinary diversion method where a section of the small intestine is used to create a tube (conduit) for urine to exit the body through a stoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Continent Cutaneous Pouch (Indiana Pouch)">
      <data key="d0">Continent Cutaneous Pouch (Indiana Pouch)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Continent Cutaneous Pouch (Indiana Pouch) is a urinary diversion technique using a portion of the large intestine to create a pouch for urine storage, connected to a stoma in the abdominal wall.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Partial Cystectomy">
      <data key="d0">Partial Cystectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Partial Cystectomy is a surgical procedure to remove part of the bladder, rarely used for bladder cancer due to limited applicability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Indiana University">
      <data key="d0">Indiana University</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Indiana University is a medical institution where surgeons developed the Indiana pouch as a urinary diversion procedure.&lt;SEP&gt;Indiana University is a medical institution where surgeons developed the Indiana pouch.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Indiana pouch">
      <data key="d0">Indiana pouch</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Indiana pouch is a type of urinary diversion procedure used to manage urine flow.&lt;SEP&gt;The Indiana pouch is a type of urinary diversion surgery that reroutes urine flow from the bladder to a stoma in the abdomen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bacillus Calmette-Guérin (BCG)">
      <data key="d0">Bacillus Calmette-Guérin (BCG)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BCG is a live bacterial vaccine used in intravesical therapy to stimulate the immune system against bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Body Image">
      <data key="d0">Body Image</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Body image concerns are a factor in treatment decisions, particularly for patients preferring less noticeable urinary diversion options.&lt;SEP&gt;Body image concerns influence patient preferences for less noticeable urinary diversion options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trimodal Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Trimodal Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Trimodal therapy is a treatment approach combining TURBT (transurethral resection of bladder tumor), radiation, and chemotherapy to preserve the bladder in certain bladder cancer cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phases">
      <data key="d0">Phases</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Phases refer to the stages of clinical trials, from Phase 1 (safety testing) to Phase 4 (long-term safety studies), which evaluate the effectiveness and risks of new treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FGFR3 Gene Mutations">
      <data key="d0">FGFR3 Gene Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FGFR3 gene mutations are genetic alterations in bladder cancer cells that targeted therapies like Balversa (erdafitinib) specifically address.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transurethral resection of bladder tumor (TURBT)">
      <data key="d0">Transurethral resection of bladder tumor (TURBT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical procedure used to examine and remove tumors from the bladder wall, commonly employed in the treatment of bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radical cystectomy">
      <data key="d0">Radical cystectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A major surgical procedure for muscle-invasive bladder cancer that involves removing the bladder, nearby lymph nodes, and pelvic organs, often followed by a urinary diversion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urinary diversion">
      <data key="d0">Urinary diversion</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical procedure performed alongside radical cystectomy to create a new pathway for urine to exit the body, bypassing the removed bladder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intravesical therapy">
      <data key="d0">Intravesical therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A treatment method involving the direct administration of chemotherapy drugs into the bladder via a catheter, used to target bladder cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anal cancer">
      <data key="d0">Anal cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A rare cancer affecting the anal canal or perianal region, linked to human papillomavirus (HPV) infection and managed through specialized treatment protocols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human papillomavirus (HPV)">
      <data key="d0">Human papillomavirus (HPV)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A sexually transmitted virus strongly linked to anal cancer, with high-risk strains like HPV16 contributing to cellular changes that may lead to malignancy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HPV16">
      <data key="d0">HPV16</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A high-risk strain of human papillomavirus (HPV) associated with an increased risk of anal and other cancers, including cervical and penile cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rectal cancer">
      <data key="d0">Rectal cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of cancer affecting the rectum, often discussed alongside anal cancer in treatment guidelines and management strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnosis and treatment planning">
      <data key="d0">Diagnosis and treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A critical phase in cancer management involving the assessment of a patient's condition and the development of a personalized treatment strategy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anal Cancer is a malignant condition originating in the anal canal or perianal region, classified into stages based on tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal Cancer is a specific type of cancer affecting the anal region, often discussed in the context of treatment options and patient support.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal Cancer is a specific type of cancer affecting the anal region, often discussed in the context of treatment options, clinical trials, and patient support.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal Cancer is a type of cancer that affects the anal region and is associated with HPV infection, with specific stages and diagnostic methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal Cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the anal canal or perianal region, classified into stages based on tumor size and spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal Cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the anal region, specifically in the anal canal or perianal area, and is associated with various risk factors such as HPV infection and immunosuppression.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer is a malignancy affecting the anal canal, primarily treated with chemoradiation regimens combining chemotherapy and radiation therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer is a type of cancer affecting the anal canal, often managed with chemoradiation regimens involving chemotherapy and radiation therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer is a type of cancer affecting the anal canal, often treated with chemotherapy, radiation, or immunotherapy depending on its stage and progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer is a type of cancer affecting the anal canal, often treated with chemotherapy, radiation, or immunotherapy depending on its stage.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer is a type of cancer affecting the anal canal, with treatment considerations including pelvic floor therapy, sexual health management, and lifestyle adjustments.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer is a type of cancer that develops in the anal canal, often caused by human papillomavirus (HPV) infection, and requires specific treatment approaches such as chemoradiation or surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer is a type of cancer that develops in the anal canal, often linked to human papillomavirus (HPV) infection and other risk factors, and is treated with chemoradiation, surgery, or immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer is a type of cancer that develops in the anal canal, often linked to human papillomavirus (HPV) infection and other risk factors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Resolution Anoscopy">
      <data key="d0">High-Resolution Anoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">High-Resolution Anoscopy is a detailed examination technique used to detect and assess anal dysplasia and cancer, involving magnification and specialized tools.&lt;SEP&gt;High-Resolution Anoss copy is a detailed examination technique used to detect and assess anal dysplasia and cancer, involving magnification and specialized tools.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HPV Vaccine">
      <data key="d0">HPV Vaccine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The HPV Vaccine is a preventive measure against certain high-risk strains of HPV, reducing the risk of HPV-related cancers such as anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anal Dysplasia">
      <data key="d0">Anal Dysplasia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anal Dysplasia refers to pre-cancerous lesions in the anal region, classified as low-grade or high-grade, which can progress to anal cancer if untreated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SISCCA">
      <data key="d0">SISCCA</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">SISCCA (Superficially Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma) is the earliest form of anal cancer, characterized by small, non-invasive lesions often detected incidentally during biopsies or removal of benign growths.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anal Squamous Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Anal Squamous Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anal Squamous Cell Carcinoma is a type of cancer affecting the anal canal and perianal region, the focus of this guide for treatment and diagnostic procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal Squamous Cell Carcinoma is a type of cancer that affects the anal canal and is the focus of this guide for treatment and diagnostic testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Computed Tomography (CT) Scan">
      <data key="d0">Computed Tomography (CT) Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Computed Tomography (CT) Scan is an imaging test that provides cross-sectional body images, used to evaluate cancer extent and detect metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;Computed Tomography (CT) Scan is an imaging test that provides detailed cross-sectional images of the body, used to assess the extent of anal cancer and detect metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI)">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) is an imaging test that uses magnetic fields and radio waves to create detailed images of soft tissues, aiding in staging anal cancer within the pelvis.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) is an imaging test using magnetic fields and radio waves to visualize soft tissues, critical for staging anal cancer in the pelvis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Positron Emission Tomography (PET) Scan">
      <data key="d0">Positron Emission Tomography (PET) Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Positron Emission Tomography (PET) Scan is an imaging test detecting metabolic activity, often combined with CT or MRI to identify cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Positron Emission Tomography (PET) Scan is an imaging test that detects metabolic activity in tissues, often combined with CT or MRI to identify cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIV Testing">
      <data key="d0">HIV Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">HIV Testing is a diagnostic procedure to determine HIV status, as HIV-positive individuals are at higher risk for anal cancer and require specialized treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;HIV Testing is a diagnostic procedure to determine an individual's HIV status, as people with HIV are at higher risk for anal cancer and require specialized treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;HIV Testing is a recommended diagnostic procedure to identify HIV infection, which is a significant risk factor for anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gynecologic Exam">
      <data key="d0">Gynecologic Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Gynecologic Exam includes cervical cancer screening, recommended for individuals at risk for anal cancer due to shared etiological factors like HPV.&lt;SEP&gt;Gynecologic Exam is a medical procedure that includes cervical cancer screening, which is recommended for individuals at risk for anal cancer due to shared etiological factors like HPV.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HPV/Pap Co-Test">
      <data key="d0">HPV/Pap Co-Test</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">HPV/Pap Co-Test is a combined screening method detecting HPV infection and cervical cell changes, serving as a preventive measure for anal and cervical cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;HPV/Pap Co-Test is a combined screening method that detects HPV infection and abnormal cervical cells, serving as a preventive measure for cervical and anal cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node Exam">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph Node Exam assesses cancer spread to groin (inguinal) and pelvic lymph nodes, a critical component of staging and treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph Node Exam is a diagnostic process to assess the presence of cancer in lymph nodes, particularly in the groin (inguinal lymph nodes) and pelvis, to determine cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Precancer">
      <data key="d0">Precancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Precancer refers to abnormal cells on the anal canal or perianal surface, representing early-stage lesions that may progress to invasive cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Precancer refers to abnormal cells on the surface of the anal canal or perianal region, representing early-stage lesions that may progress to invasive anal cancer if untreated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Michael">
      <data key="d0">Michael</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Michael is an anal cancer survivor who advocates for mentorship and community support for patients facing similar challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;Michael is an anal cancer survivor who emphasizes the importance of mentorship and community support for patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oocyte Cryopreservation">
      <data key="d0">Oocyte Cryopreservation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Oocyte Cryopreservation is a fertility preservation method where unfertilized eggs are frozen and stored for potential future use.&lt;SEP&gt;Oocyte Cryopreservation is a fertility preservation technique where unfertilized eggs are frozen and stored for potential future use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oophoropexy">
      <data key="d0">Oophoropexy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Oophoropexy is a surgical procedure relocating ovaries and fallopian tubes out of the radiation field to protect fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;Oophoropexy is a surgical procedure that moves ovaries and fallopian tubes out of the radiation field to protect fertility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anal Canal">
      <data key="d0">Anal Canal</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Anal Canal is the anatomical passage connecting the rectum to the anus, a primary site for anal cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Perianal Region">
      <data key="d0">Perianal Region</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Perianal Region includes the anal opening and surrounding skin, a secondary site for anal cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Large Bowel">
      <data key="d0">Large Bowel</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Large Bowel (colorectum) is the anatomical pathway through which stool exits the body, with the anal canal at its terminus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Part 2: Treatment Guide">
      <data key="d0">Part 2: Treatment Guide</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Part 2: Treatment Guide is a section of the NCCN resource detailing specific treatment protocols for anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Perianal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Perianal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Perianal Cancer refers to cancer occurring in the perianal region, which includes the anal opening and surrounding skin, and is often associated with similar risk factors as anal canal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Perianal cancer is a malignancy originating in the perianal region, classified by differentiation grades (G1-G4) and managed through excision, chemoradiation, or surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;Perianal cancer refers to cancer located around the anus, typically treated with chemoradiation for stages I-III, though local excision may be an option for low-risk cases.&lt;SEP&gt;Perianal cancer refers to cancers located around the anus, typically managed with chemoradiation for stages I–III, though local excision may be an option for low-risk cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunosuppressant">
      <data key="d0">Immunosuppressant</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunosuppressants are medications that suppress the immune system, increasing the risk of anal cancer by reducing the body's ability to combat HPV infections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Autoimmune Disorders">
      <data key="d0">Autoimmune Disorders</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Autoimmune disorders are conditions where the immune system attacks the body's own tissues, potentially increasing the risk of anal cancer through chronic inflammation and immune dysregulation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anal Sex">
      <data key="d0">Anal Sex</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anal sex is a risk factor for anal cancer, as it increases the likelihood of HPV transmission and exposure to sexually transmitted infections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sexually Transmitted Infection">
      <data key="d0">Sexually Transmitted Infection</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sexually Transmitted Infections (STIs) are risk factors for anal cancer, particularly when they involve HPV or other pathogens that contribute to chronic inflammation and malignancy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DRE">
      <data key="d0">DRE</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">DRE (Digital Rectal Exam) is a diagnostic procedure used to assess the anal region for abnormalities, often part of routine screening for anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Anoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anoscopy is a diagnostic procedure to examine the anal canal and rectum, used for monitoring recurrence in patients without APR.&lt;SEP&gt;Anoscopy is a medical procedure involving the visualization of the anal canal and rectum to detect abnormalities or cancerous changes.&lt;SEP&gt;Anoscopy is a medical procedure involving the visualization of the anal canal and rectum to detect pre-cancerous lesions or early-stage anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Ansopy is a diagnostic procedure to examine the anal canal and rectum, used for monitoring recurrence in patients without APR.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Cancer Screening">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Cancer Screening</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cervical Cancer Screening involves tests like Pap smears to detect cervical cancer, which is often linked to HPV and shares similar risk factors with anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IMRT">
      <data key="d0">IMRT</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">IMRT (Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy) is an advanced radiation technique specifically used to treat anal cancer, allowing precise targeting of cancerous tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;IMRT (Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy) is an advanced radiation technique that delivers precise doses of radiation to cancer cells while minimizing damage to surrounding healthy tissue, often used in chemoradiation.&lt;SEP&gt;IMRT (Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy) is an advanced radiation technique that delivers precise doses of radiation to cancer cells while minimizing damage to surrounding healthy tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;IMRT (Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy) is an advanced radiation technique used to deliver precise, high-dose radiation to anal cancer tumors while minimizing damage to surrounding healthy tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;IMRT (Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy) is an advanced radiation technique used to precisely target cancerous tissues in anal cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Linear Accelerator">
      <data key="d0">Linear Accelerator</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Linear Accelerator is a medical device used in EBRT to generate high-energy x-rays for targeting cancerous tissues in the anal region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Guide">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Guide</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Treatment Guide provides evidence-based recommendations for managing anal cancer, including chemoradiation, surgical options, and follow-up care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follow-up and Surveillance">
      <data key="d0">Follow-up and Surveillance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Follow-up and Surveillance involve monitoring patients post-treatment for recurrence, managing long-term side effects, and ensuring early detection of any cancerous changes.&lt;SEP&gt;Follow-up and surveillance is a structured process involving regular medical exams, imaging, and procedures to monitor cancer recurrence and treatment outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic Anal Cancer is cancer that has spread beyond the anal region to other parts of the body, typically treated with systemic therapies like chemotherapy or immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic anal cancer refers to cancer that has spread beyond the pelvis, commonly to the liver or lungs, and requires specialized treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sexual Health">
      <data key="d0">Sexual Health</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sexual Health is impacted by anal cancer treatments, including radiation and chemotherapy, which may lead to changes in sexual function and intimacy.&lt;SEP&gt;Sexual health encompasses the physical, emotional, and social aspects of sexual well-being, often impacted by cancer treatments and managed through specialized therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bowel and Bladder Function">
      <data key="d0">Bowel and Bladder Function</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bowel and Bladder Function are potential long-term side effects of anal cancer treatments, requiring management strategies to improve quality of life.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stages I–III">
      <data key="d0">Stages I–III</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stages I–III classify perianal cancer as nonmetastatic, guiding treatment decisions such as chemoradiation or local excision based on tumor grade and location.&lt;SEP&gt;Stages I–III describe the progression of perianal cancer, indicating nonmetastatic disease confined to the pelvis, and guide treatment decisions like chemoradiation or local excision.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Excision">
      <data key="d0">Local Excision</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Local excision is a surgical category involving the removal of tumors and surrounding tissue, recommended for low-grade perianal cancers where the sphincter muscle is not invaded.&lt;SEP&gt;Local excision is a surgical procedure for removing low-grade (G1) and intermediate-grade (G2) perianal cancers, with follow-up based on surgical margins.&lt;SEP&gt;Local excision is a surgical procedure involving removal of the tumor and surrounding tissue, recommended for low-grade perianal cancers where the cancer has not invaded the sphincter muscle.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hand-Foot Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Hand-Foot Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hand-foot syndrome is a chemotherapy-related side effect caused by capecitabine, characterized by skin irritation and pain in the palms and soles.&lt;SEP&gt;Hand-foot syndrome is a side effect of capecitabine, characterized by redness, swelling, and pain in the palms and soles, requiring dose adjustments to manage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mitomycin">
      <data key="d0">Mitomycin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mitomycin is a chemotherapy drug used in regimens for anal cancer, often combined with capecitabine or 5-FU for chemoradiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5-FU">
      <data key="d0">5-FU</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">5-FU (fluorouracil) is a chemotherapy drug administered via continuous infusion, used in regimens combining with cisplatin or mitomycin for anal cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Port">
      <data key="d0">Port</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A port is a central venous access device used to administer chemotherapy drugs like 5-FU, allowing home-based treatment during radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PICC Line">
      <data key="d0">PICC Line</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A PICC line is a peripherally inserted central catheter used for long-term chemotherapy administration, such as for 5-FU, during radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Complete remission">
      <data key="d0">Complete remission</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Complete remission is a state where there are no signs of cancer in the body after treatment, indicating successful eradication of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Persistent disease">
      <data key="d0">Persistent disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Persistent disease refers to cancer that remains in the body but has not grown or spread, requiring continued monitoring and possible treatment adjustments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Progressive disease">
      <data key="d0">Progressive disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Progressive disease indicates that the cancer has grown or spread after initial treatment, necessitating further interventions such as surgery or chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Digital Rectal Examination (DRE)">
      <data key="d0">Digital Rectal Examination (DRE)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Digital Rectal Examination (DRE) is a procedure used to assess the rectum and surrounding areas for signs of cancer recurrence or complications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local excision">
      <data key="d0">Local excision</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Local excision is a surgical procedure for removing low-grade (G1) and intermediate-grade (G2) perianal cancers, with follow-up based on surgical margins.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Abdominoperineal resection (APR)">
      <data key="d0">Abdominoperineal resection (APR)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Abdominoperineal resection (APR) is a radical surgical procedure to remove the anus and surrounding tissues in cases of locally progressive or recurrent anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Janet Anal cancer survivor">
      <data key="d0">Janet Anal cancer survivor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Janet is an individual who has survived anal cancer, highlighting the importance of early detection and treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follow-up and surveillance">
      <data key="d0">Follow-up and surveillance</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Follow-up and surveillance is a structured process involving regular medical exams, imaging, and procedures to monitor cancer recurrence and treatment outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Perianal cancer">
      <data key="d0">Perianal cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Perianal cancer is a type of anal cancer originating in the perianal region, classified by differentiation grades (G1-G4) and treated with excision, chemoradiation, or surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anal canal cancer">
      <data key="d0">Anal canal cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anal canal cancer is a malignancy occurring in the anal canal, often treated with chemoradiation and requiring similar follow-up protocols as perianal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="G1">
      <data key="d0">G1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">G1 represents low-grade (well-differentiated) perianal cancer, characterized by minimal cellular atypia and slower growth rates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="G2">
      <data key="d0">G2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">G2 denotes intermediate-grade (moderately differentiated) perianal cancer, showing moderate cellular atypia and growth potential.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="G3">
      <data key="d0">G3</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">G3 signifies high-grade (poorly differentiated) perianal cancer, marked by significant cellular atypia and aggressive growth behavior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="G4">
      <data key="d0">G4</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">G4 indicates undifferentiated (high-grade) perianal cancer, characterized by complete loss of cellular differentiation and extreme aggressiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anal Canal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Anal Canal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anal canal cancer is a malignancy occurring in the anal canal, often treated with chemoradiation and requiring similar follow-up protocols as perianal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Complete Remission">
      <data key="d0">Complete Remission</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Complete remission is a state where there are no signs of cancer in the body after treatment, indicating successful eradication of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Persistent Disease">
      <data key="d0">Persistent Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Persistent disease refers to cancer that remains in the body but has not grown or spread, requiring continued monitoring and possible treatment adjustments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Progressive Disease">
      <data key="d0">Progressive Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Progressive disease indicates that the cancer has grown or spread after initial treatment, necessitating further interventions such as surgery or chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Janet Anal Cancer Survivor">
      <data key="d0">Janet Anal Cancer Survivor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Janet is an individual who has survived anal cancer, highlighting the importance of early detection and treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Disease">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic disease refers to cancer that has spread to distant organs, necessitating systemic treatments like chemoradiation or immunotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunotherapy with Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor">
      <data key="d0">Immunotherapy with Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunotherapy with immune checkpoint inhibitors is a treatment option for advanced anal cancer, potentially delaying surgery in some patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemotherapy Regimen">
      <data key="d0">Chemotherapy Regimen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemotherapy regimens, such as Carboplatin and paclitaxel or 5-FU and cisplatin, are standard treatment options for metastatic anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Digital Rectal Exam (DRE)">
      <data key="d0">Digital Rectal Exam (DRE)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Digital rectal exams are medical procedures to assess rectal health, recommended for surveillance in patients with lymph node-only cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thom">
      <data key="d0">Thom</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Thom is an anal cancer survivor who shares his experience of navigating post-treatment challenges, including physical and emotional recovery.&lt;SEP&gt;Thom is an anal cancer survivor who shares personal experiences about managing treatment and advocating for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Thom is an anal cancer survivor who shares personal experiences about managing treatment, advocating for patients, and emphasizing the importance of informed decision-making in clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Palliative Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Palliative Radiation Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Palliative radiation therapy aims to reduce tumor size and alleviate symptoms in advanced cancer cases, avoiding repeated radiation to the same area.&lt;SEP&gt;Palliative radiation therapy is used to alleviate symptoms and reduce tumor size in advanced cancer cases, avoiding repeated radiation to previously treated areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Melissa">
      <data key="d0">Melissa</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Melissa is an anal cancer survivor who shares her experience and provides guidance on clinical trials and managing cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Melissa is an anal cancer survivor who shares her experience and provides guidance on clinical trials, treatment options, and managing cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="APR Surgery">
      <data key="d0">APR Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">APR surgery (Abdominoperineal Resection) is a surgical procedure to remove the anus and surrounding tissues in advanced anal cancer cases, often required if cancer does not respond to initial treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;APR surgery (Abdominoperineal Resection) is a surgical procedure to remove the anus and surrounding tissues in advanced anal cancer cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Late and Long-Term Side Effects">
      <data key="d0">Late and Long-Term Side Effects</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Late and long-term side effects are health issues that may persist or develop after cancer treatment, affecting bodily functions and quality of life.&lt;SEP&gt;Late and long-term side effects are health issues that may persist or develop after cancer treatment, affecting bodily functions such as bowel, urinary, and sexual health, and requiring ongoing management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Healthy Habits">
      <data key="d0">Healthy Habits</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Healthy habits are lifestyle practices that promote well-being and help cancer survivors manage treatment-related effects and maintain quality of life.&lt;SEP&gt;Healthy habits are lifestyle practices that promote well-being and help cancer survivors manage treatment-related effects, maintain quality of life, and prevent complications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oncology Team">
      <data key="d0">Oncology Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An oncology team is a multidisciplinary group of healthcare professionals specializing in cancer treatment and care, including oncologists, nurses, and other specialists, who collaborate with primary care doctors for comprehensive care.&lt;SEP&gt;An oncology team is a multidisciplinary group of healthcare professionals specializing in cancer treatment and care, including oncologists, nurses, and other specialists.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fecal Incontinence">
      <data key="d0">Fecal Incontinence</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fecal incontinence is a medical condition involving the involuntary leakage of stool, often managed through dietary changes, fiber supplements, and medical interventions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Floor Physical Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Floor Physical Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic floor physical therapy involves strengthening pelvic muscles to improve bowel and bladder control, as well as sexual function, through exercises and hands-on techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vaginal Stenosis">
      <data key="d0">Vaginal Stenosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vaginal stenosis is a condition caused by radiation therapy, leading to vaginal narrowing and shortening, often managed with vaginal dilator therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anal Cancer Foundation">
      <data key="d0">Anal Cancer Foundation</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Anal Cancer Foundation is an organization dedicated to raising awareness, providing educational resources, and supporting individuals affected by anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The Anal Cancer Foundation is an organization dedicated to raising awareness, providing resources, and supporting individuals affected by anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The Anal Cancer Foundation is an organization dedicated to supporting individuals with anal cancer, offering resources and advocacy for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The Anal Cancer Foundation is an organization dedicated to supporting individuals with anal cancer, offering resources, advocacy, and educational programs for patients and caregivers.&lt;SEP&gt;The Anal Cancer Foundation is an organization providing peer-to-peer support, resources, and programs for anal cancer patients and caregivers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Peer-To-Peer Support Program">
      <data key="d0">Peer-To-Peer Support Program</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Peer-To-Peer Support Program is an event organized by the Anal Cancer Foundation to connect patients and caregivers for emotional and practical support.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mental Health">
      <data key="d0">Mental Health</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mental health refers to psychological well-being, which can be affected by cancer treatment and its side effects, requiring support from mental health professionals and resources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ostomy Care">
      <data key="d0">Ostomy Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ostomy care involves managing an ostomy, including lifestyle adjustments, physical activity considerations, and consultations with healthcare professionals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Screening">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Screening</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer screening includes regular medical tests to detect other cancers, such as breast, cervical, colorectal, and prostate cancer, as part of post-treatment monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Healthy Lifestyle">
      <data key="d0">Healthy Lifestyle</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A healthy lifestyle includes maintaining physical activity, a balanced diet, preventive health measures, and regular medical check-ups to improve overall quality of life.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vaginal Dilator Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Vaginal Dilator Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vaginal dilator therapy is a treatment approach to manage vaginal stenosis by gradually stretching the vagina using specialized devices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ostomy">
      <data key="d0">Ostomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ostomy is a medical condition involving the creation of an opening in the abdomen for waste elimination, requiring lifestyle adjustments and specialized care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Complementary and Alternative Therapies">
      <data key="d0">Complementary and Alternative Therapies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Complementary and Alternative Therapies are practices and products used to support conventional cancer treatment, aiming to improve comfort and well-being during and after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Integrative Oncology">
      <data key="d0">Integrative Oncology</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Integrative Oncology combines standard cancer treatments with complementary therapies to provide holistic care for patients, addressing both physical and emotional needs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Radiation Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiation Treatment is a medical procedure used to target and destroy cancer cells, but it may have side effects such as weakening bones in the pelvis, increasing fracture risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Healthy Diet">
      <data key="d0">Healthy Diet</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Healthy Diet emphasizes plant-based foods, including vegetables, fruits, and whole grains, to support overall health and reduce cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Plant-based Foods">
      <data key="d0">Plant-based Foods</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Plant-based Foods are nutrient-rich foods derived from plants, recommended as part of a healthy diet to promote cancer prevention and survivorship.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer-Related Late and Long-Term Effects">
      <data key="d0">Cancer-Related Late and Long-Term Effects</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer-Related Late and Long-Term Effects encompass physical, emotional, and psychological challenges that survivors may face years after treatment, such as fatigue, sexual dysfunction, and sleep disorders.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anxiety, Depression, and Distress">
      <data key="d0">Anxiety, Depression, and Distress</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anxiety, Depression, and Distress are emotional and psychological challenges commonly experienced by cancer survivors, requiring support and management strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cognitive Dysfunction">
      <data key="d0">Cognitive Dysfunction</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cognitive Dysfunction refers to difficulties with memory, concentration, and problem-solving that some cancer survivors may experience as a long-term effect of treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pain">
      <data key="d0">Pain</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pain is a common symptom experienced by cancer survivors, which may result from the disease, treatment, or its long-term effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sexual Dysfunction">
      <data key="d0">Sexual Dysfunction</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sexual Dysfunction refers to physical or emotional difficulties affecting sexual health and intimacy, which can occur as a side effect of cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sleep Disorders">
      <data key="d0">Sleep Disorders</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sleep Disorders are disturbances in sleep patterns that may affect cancer survivors, contributing to fatigue, mood changes, and reduced quality of life.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunizations">
      <data key="d0">Immunizations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunizations are preventive measures to protect against infections, which are particularly important for cancer survivors with compromised immune systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Employment, Insurance, and Disability Concerns">
      <data key="d0">Employment, Insurance, and Disability Concerns</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Employment, Insurance, and Disability Concerns address practical challenges faced by cancer survivors, including financial stability, work-related adjustments, and access to healthcare.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Density Monitoring">
      <data key="d0">Bone Density Monitoring</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone Density Monitoring is a medical process to assess bone health and fracture risk, particularly after radiation treatment to the pelvis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Decisions">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Decisions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment Decisions involve evaluating and selecting the most appropriate cancer treatment options based on patient preferences, medical advice, and individual circumstances.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shared Decision-making">
      <data key="d0">Shared Decision-making</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Shared Decision-making is a collaborative process between patients and healthcare providers to discuss treatment options, risks, and benefits, ensuring alignment with patient values and goals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Hope Network">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Hope Network</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Cancer Hope Network is an organization that offers emotional and practical support to cancer patients and their families, including those with anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The Cancer Hope Network is an organization that offers support and resources to cancer patients and their families, including information on anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The Cancer Hope Network is an organization that provides emotional support, peer connections, and practical resources for cancer patients, including those with anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The Cancer Hope Network is an organization that provides support and information for cancer patients, including those with anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer.Net">
      <data key="d0">Cancer.Net</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer.Net is a website providing comprehensive, peer-reviewed information on cancer, including details about anal cancer, treatment options, and patient resources.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer.Net is a website providing peer-reviewed, comprehensive information on cancer, including detailed resources on anal cancer, treatment options, and patient support.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer.Net is an organization that offers comprehensive information about cancer types, including anal cancer, and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer.Net is an organization that offers comprehensive, evidence-based information about cancer types, treatment options, and clinical trials, specifically for patients with anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CancerCare">
      <data key="d0">CancerCare</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">CancerCare is an organization that provides counseling, financial assistance, and support services for cancer patients, including those with anal cancer, addressing emotional and practical challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;CancerCare is an organization that provides free counseling, educational resources, and support services to individuals facing cancer, including anal cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;CancerCare is an organization that provides free support services, including counseling and educational resources, to individuals facing cancer, including anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;CancerCare is an organization that provides support services, including counseling and financial assistance, for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Support Community">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Support Community</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Cancer Support Community is an organization offering peer support groups, educational programs, and resources for cancer patients and survivors, including those with anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The Cancer Support Community is an organization offering peer support, educational programs, and resources for cancer patients and survivors.&lt;SEP&gt;The Cancer Support Community is an organization offering programs and resources to help cancer patients and survivors, including those with anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The Cancer Support Community is an organization offering programs, resources, and peer support to cancer patients and survivors, including those with anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colorectal Cancer Alliance">
      <data key="d0">Colorectal Cancer Alliance</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
