    <node id="Imaging Tests">
      <data key="d0">Imaging Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging Tests are a category of diagnostic procedures that utilize radiological techniques, such as computed tomography (CT), magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), positron emission tomography (PET), ultrasound, and bone scans, to visualize internal body structures and detect cancerous abnormalities. These tests are critical for assessing the spread of cancers such as acute myeloid leukemia (AML), prostate cancer, breast cancer, ovarian cancer, colon cancer, and leukemia beyond bone marrow, as well as monitoring treatment response and recurrence. They aid in staging cancers by evaluating organ involvement, including the brain, heart, liver, spleen, skin, and gastrointestinal tract, while minimizing radiation exposure where possible. Imaging Tests encompass specific applications, such as mammography and ultrasounds for breast tissue evaluation, CT and MRI scans for visualizing tumor extent and metastasis, and PET/CT scans for assessing disease progression. They are also used to detect complications like infections, bleeding, or organ dysfunction in conditions such as leukemia and regional squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC). By providing detailed anatomical and functional insights, Imaging Tests guide treatment planning and improve patient outcomes across diverse oncological contexts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CT Scan">
      <data key="d0">CT Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">**CT Scan (Computed Tomography Scan)** is a diagnostic imaging technique that utilizes X-rays and computer technology to generate detailed cross-sectional images of the body. This method combines multiple X-ray images taken from different angles to produce comprehensive views of internal structures, often enhanced with contrast material to improve visibility of tissues, organs, and abnormalities. CT scans are widely used to detect tumors, assess cancer spread, and evaluate conditions such as prostate cancer, ovarian cancer, colon cancer, and lymphoma. They are particularly valuable for identifying metastasis to abdominal or chest regions, enlarged lymph nodes, and the extent of disease in cancers like colon or ovarian cancer. CT scans are frequently employed as an alternative to MRI in specific scenarios, such as diagnosing CNS lymphoma when MRI is unavailable or not recommended. Additionally, they are often combined with PET scans for comprehensive cancer staging, offering insights into tumor size, location, and treatment response. The procedure is noted for its ability to provide anatomical details comparable to MRI, though it may differ in procedure duration and application specifics. Overall, CT scans remain a critical tool in medical diagnostics for visualizing internal structures, monitoring disease progression, and guiding treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The **NCCN Guidelines for Patients** are a comprehensive set of **educational resources** provided by the **National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN)** through its website, **NCCN.org**. These guidelines serve as patient-focused tools designed to empower individuals and their caregivers with clear, evidence-based information about cancer care, treatment options, and management strategies. They are written in plain language to ensure accessibility and are grounded in the latest research and clinical practices from leading cancer centers.  

The guidelines cover a wide range of cancers and related conditions, including **breast cancer**, **chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL)**, **cervical cancer**, **primary CNS lymphoma**, **gliomas**, and **throat cancer**, among others. They provide detailed explanations of treatment planning, testing procedures, and management protocols, such as **chemotherapy side effect management** (e.g., nausea and vomiting), **distress management during treatment**, and **fertility preservation strategies** (e.g., sperm banking and egg freezing) for adolescent and young adult patients.  

Additionally, the **NCCN Guidelines for Patients** include **patient advocacy guidance**, **care recommendations**, and **expert-recommended protocols** for conditions like **chronic myeloid leukemia (CML)**, **acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL)**, and **basal cell skin cancer**. They also address specific therapeutic areas, such as **immunotherapy side effects**, **diagnosis and management of CLL**, and **management of chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL)**.  

These resources are intended to guide **cancer care decisions** for both patients and providers, offering trusted, actionable insights based on **evidence-based practices**. By integrating clinical expertise with patient-centered information, the **NCCN Guidelines for Patients** aim to improve treatment outcomes and support informed decision-making across diverse cancer care scenarios.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN">
      <data key="d0">NCCN</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The **National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN)** is a global nonprofit organization dedicated to advancing cancer care by providing evidence-based clinical guidelines, patient resources, and educational materials. Established as a leading authority in oncology, NCCN develops comprehensive standards for cancer prevention, diagnosis, treatment, and management across a wide range of cancers, including but not limited to kidney cancer, squamous cell skin cancer, breast cancer, lung cancer (Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer [NSCLC] and Small Cell Lung Cancer [SCLC]), head and neck cancers, colorectal cancer, cervical cancer, anal cancer, and Hodgkin lymphoma.  

NCCN’s guidelines address critical aspects of patient care, such as fertility preservation protocols, distress screening, test result access, and support services, while also incorporating recommendations for imaging standards, treatment planning, and genetic testing. The organization emphasizes patient education through tools like the **NCCN Guidelines for Patients**, a mobile app (NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer), and resources tailored to specific cancers, including protocols for immunotherapy side effects, prostate cancer management, and CLL (chronic lymphocytic leukemia) care.  

Additionally, NCCN provides guidance on specialized areas such as bone cancer treatment, adrenal tumor management, and chronic conditions like chronic myeloid leukemia (CML). It also supports clinical research and trial information, ensuring patients and healthcare providers have access to up-to-date, actionable recommendations. By integrating clinical expertise with patient-centered resources, NCCN plays a pivotal role in standardizing cancer care and improving outcomes globally.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI">
      <data key="d0">MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MRI (Magnetic Resonance Imaging) is a non-invasive imaging technique that utilizes magnetic fields and radio waves to generate detailed images of internal body structures, particularly soft tissues, organs, and complex anatomical regions. It is a non-radiation-based method that employs advanced computer technology to produce high-resolution images, making it invaluable for diagnosing and assessing a wide range of conditions. Key applications include evaluating soft tissues such as the brain, liver, rectum, bladder, urinary tract, ovaries, and pelvic structures. It is especially critical for identifying and characterizing tumors, including gliomas, CNS lymphoma, and head and neck cancers, as well as assessing cervical and ovarian cancer spread. MRI is also widely used in cancer diagnosis and staging, such as evaluating rectal cancer depth, lymph node involvement, and local tumor extent, often enhanced with contrast agents or rectal gel for improved imaging clarity. Additionally, it aids in clarifying unclear CT scan results and provides critical insights into the extent of disease in organs like the liver and rectum. Its versatility and ability to visualize soft tissues without radiation exposure make it a cornerstone in diagnostic imaging for both neurological and oncological applications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Radiation Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiation Therapy is a non-invasive treatment modality that employs high-energy radiation (such as x-rays, photons, electrons, or protons) to target and destroy cancer cells, shrink tumors, and manage cancer-related symptoms. It is widely used across various cancer types, including prostate, cervical, anal, thyroid, non-small cell lung (NSCLC), brain (e.g., gliomas, CNS lymphoma), bladder, skin, and central nervous system cancers. The treatment is often administered over several weeks by a radiation oncologist and may involve advanced techniques like intensity-modulated radiation therapy (IMRT) to minimize damage to healthy tissue.  

Radiation Therapy is frequently combined with chemotherapy in chemoradiation protocols to enhance efficacy, particularly for localized or metastatic cancers such as anal, bladder, and cervical cancer. It may also be used alongside surgery or systemic therapies for advanced cases, such as squamous cell skin cancer, to eliminate residual cancer cells. However, it is associated with potential side effects, including fertility complications (e.g., ovarian damage), menopause, vision problems, cataracts, nervous system damage, vaginal stenosis, and localized tissue effects. For certain cancers, such as prostate or cervical cancer, it serves as a primary treatment to delay or avoid more aggressive interventions.  

The treatment’s application varies by cancer type: for example, external beam radiation therapy (EBRT) is used for prostate and cervical cancer, while whole-brain radiation therapy (WBRT) targets CNS lymphoma. In cases where surgery is not feasible (e.g., advanced thyroid cancer), Radiation Therapy provides a critical alternative. Its effectiveness is often balanced with strategies to manage side effects, ensuring optimal patient outcomes while minimizing harm to healthy tissues. Overall, Radiation Therapy remains a cornerstone of oncology, adapting to diverse clinical scenarios through specialized techniques and multimodal approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">**Chemotherapy** is a systemic treatment category that employs drugs to kill fast-growing cancer cells, often administered intravenously, orally, or intravesically, and is used across a wide range of cancers. It is a standard of care for conditions such as acute myeloid leukemia (AML), where it targets rapidly dividing cells, including both cancerous and normal cells. Chemotherapy is frequently combined with other therapies, such as radiation, immunotherapy, or surgery, to enhance efficacy. For example, it is used in conjunction with radiation for cervical, anal, and perianal cancers, and with immunotherapy for cancers with high tumor mutational burden or specific genetic markers.  

The treatment is commonly employed for cancers like bladder cancer, squamous cell skin cancer, inflammatory breast cancer, gliomas, ovarian cancer, and CNS lymphoma. In ovarian cancer, platinum-based drugs (e.g., cisplatin, carboplatin) and taxanes (e.g., paclitaxel) are often used, either before or after surgery to shrink tumors or manage disease progression. For bladder cancer, chemotherapy may be administered systemically or intravesically, depending on the stage and type. In colon cancer, it is recommended for specific stages to reduce recurrence risk, while in CNS lymphoma, it is used alongside other therapies to target malignant cells.  

Common side effects include nausea, hair loss, and kidney damage, particularly with platinum-based regimens. Chemotherapy is also utilized for thyroid cancers, though its effectiveness is limited against most types, with radiation therapy often combined for aggressive subtypes like anaplastic thyroid cancer. The treatment typically involves cyclical administration to allow the body to recover between sessions, ensuring manageable toxicity while maximizing therapeutic outcomes. Overall, chemotherapy remains a cornerstone of oncology, tailored to individual patient needs and cancer characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Systemic Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Systemic Therapy refers to a category of cancer treatment involving medications that travel through the bloodstream to target cancer cells throughout the body, often administered intravenously or orally. It encompasses a range of treatments, including chemotherapy, targeted therapy, and immunotherapy, which act systemically to address cancers that have spread beyond localized areas or recur after initial treatment. Systemic Therapy is commonly used for metastatic or recurrent cervical cancer, advanced or muscle-invasive bladder cancer, high-risk cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC), and other advanced or metastatic cancers. These treatments are designed to combat cancer cells systemically, either as a primary approach or following surgery, radiation, or other localized therapies. The approach is particularly effective for cancers that have progressed to advanced stages, such as those affecting distant organs or tissues, and may involve combinations of therapies to maximize efficacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Targeted Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Targeted Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">**Targeted Therapy** is a treatment approach that specifically attacks cancer cells while minimizing damage to healthy cells, often used in conjunction with chemotherapy. It is a category of systemic therapy that focuses on specific molecular targets on cancer cells, such as genetic mutations, proteins, or biomarkers like the epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR), to inhibit cancer growth and survival. This treatment method leverages drugs or other substances to identify and attack specific cancer cells, often with fewer side effects compared to traditional therapies.  

Targeted Therapy is particularly effective for certain cancers, including thyroid cancers with specific genetic mutations or biomarkers, especially in recurrent or advanced cases resistant to radioactive iodine (RAI) therapy or other treatments. It is also an advanced option for thyroid cancers that have metastasized or do not respond to conventional therapies. Additionally, it is used in specific cases of acute myeloid leukemia (AML) and other malignancies where molecular changes, such as FGFR3 mutations, are present.  

Examples of targeted therapy drugs include Balversa (erdafitinib) and Padcev (enfortumab vedotin-ejfv), which are designed to attack cancer cells with specific genetic alterations. This treatment is often recommended for patients ineligible for immunotherapy or clinical trials and is frequently combined with other therapies to enhance outcomes. By focusing on molecular features unique to cancer cells, targeted therapy aims to improve efficacy while reducing harm to normal tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Immunotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">**Immunotherapy** is a systemic treatment approach that enhances the body's immune system to recognize, attack, and destroy cancer cells. It leverages immune checkpoint inhibitors, such as **Keytruda (pembrolizumab)**, **Opdivo (nivolumab)**, **Bavencio (avelumab)**, **Tecentriq (atezolizumab)**, and **cemiplimab-rwlc (Libtayo)**, to reactivate immune responses by blocking checkpoints like PD-1 and CTLA-4. This modality is particularly effective for cancers with specific genetic or molecular characteristics, such as **dMMR/MSI-H tumors**, **PD-L1–positive cancers**, and **TMB-H (high tumor mutational burden)** cancers.  

Immunotherapy is also used in combination with other therapies, including targeted treatments, for recurrent or advanced cases of **thyroid cancer**, **AML (acute myeloid leukemia)**, and **squamous cell skin cancer**. Key drugs like **rituximab** are employed to target specific proteins on cancer cells, making it effective for systemic treatment of **Primary CNS Lymphoma** and **HIV-related CNS lymphoma**. Additionally, it plays a role in **bladder cancer** management and is a critical component of systemic therapy for **bladder cancer**.  

The treatment is guided by biomarker profiles for specific cancer types, ensuring personalized approaches. For example, **checkpoint inhibitors** like **Keytruda** and **Opdivo** are utilized in advanced or inoperable **squamous cell skin cancer**, while **rituximab** remains a key drug for **primary CNS lymphoma**. Overall, immunotherapy represents a versatile and evolving category of cancer treatment, enhancing immune system efficacy to combat diverse malignancies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Trials">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Trials</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical Trials are structured medical research studies designed to evaluate new treatments, therapies, or interventions for various cancers, including breast cancer, anal cancer, bladder cancer, primary CNS lymphoma, acute myeloid leukemia (AML), non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), squamous cell skin cancer, and pancreatic cancer. These studies involve human participants to assess the safety, efficacy, and long-term benefits of experimental therapies, such as chemotherapy regimens, immunotherapy, targeted therapies, and combinations like chemoradiation. Biomarker testing is often used to determine patient eligibility, ensuring treatments are tailored to individual biological profiles. Clinical Trials span different phases (Phase 1 to Phase 4) and may offer patients access to innovative therapies, particularly for advanced or high-risk conditions. They play a critical role in advancing medical knowledge, contributing to FDA approval processes, and improving outcomes for patients with weakened immune systems or limited treatment options. By testing new drugs, interventions, and therapeutic approaches, Clinical Trials drive advancements in cancer care and experimental treatments for a wide range of malignancies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemoradiation">
      <data key="d0">Chemoradiation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemoradiation is a combined treatment modality that integrates chemotherapy and radiation therapy to address various cancers, particularly those in advanced or locally advanced stages. It is widely utilized for nonmetastatic and metastatic anal cancers, where it serves as a primary treatment for non-metastatic cases or is employed after specific chemotherapy regimens for metastatic instances. The approach is also critical for locally advanced cervical cancer, where concurrent chemotherapy and external beam radiation therapy (EBRT) are used to target cancer cells effectively, often as an initial treatment or to enhance outcomes in advanced stages. Similarly, chemoradiation is a standard strategy for bladder cancer, aiming to improve treatment efficacy, shrink tumors, and destroy cancer cells. For rectal cancer, it is a treatment category typically reserved for locally advanced cases. Additionally, it is recommended for nonmetastatic perianal and anal canal cancers that are not suitable for local excision. While some descriptions emphasize its use in advanced stages like metastatic anal cancer or locally advanced cervical cancer, others highlight its role in nonmetastatic settings, underscoring its adaptability across different cancer types and disease stages. The concurrent administration of chemotherapy and radiation therapy remains central to its mechanism, aiming to maximize cancer cell destruction and improve patient outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">**Biopsy** is a diagnostic medical procedure involving the removal of tissue or fluid samples from suspected cancerous areas for laboratory analysis. This procedure is critical for confirming cancer diagnoses, determining tumor histology, and characterizing malignancies to guide treatment strategies. Biopsy methods include core needle biopsies, surgical biopsies, and other techniques tailored to specific organs or tissues, such as thyroid nodules, prostate tissue, breast tissue, ovarian tissue, brain tumors, bladder cancer, stomach lining, and metastatic sites. The extracted tissue or fluid is examined microscopically to identify cancerous cells, assess molecular features (e.g., DNA mutations, protein levels), and evaluate aggressiveness metrics like Gleason scores and Grade Groups. Biopsy also plays a role in diagnosing precancerous changes and providing data for personalized treatment planning. While generally effective, the procedure may carry risks such as infection, bleeding, or complications associated with invasive sampling. It remains a cornerstone in oncology for accurate diagnosis and therapeutic decision-making across diverse cancer types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SSTR-PET/None">
      <data key="d0">SSTR-PET/None</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
      <data key="d2">FDG-PET/CT and SSTR-PET/CT scans are both types of PET scans used in oncology for different tumor detection purposes.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCC, NCCN">
      <data key="d0">NCC, NCCN</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN emphasizes regular skin cancer screening for patients with lymphoma due to compromised immune function.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunosuppressants">
      <data key="d0">Immunosuppressants</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
      <data key="d2">Organ transplantation requires immunosuppressants, which weaken the immune system and increase cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Small Cll Lung Cancer (NSCLC)">
      <data key="d0">Non-Small Cll Lung Cancer (NSCLC)</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical trials are part of the treatment landscape for NSCLC, offering experimental therapies for patients at various stages.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follic'ular Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Follic'ular Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
      <data key="d2">Follicular lymphoma (FL) can transform into diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL), particularly in cases of FL grade 3B (FL3B) or transformed FL.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
    </node>
    <node id="|&gt;&quot;Social Support">
      <data key="d0">|&gt;"Social Support</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
      <data key="d2">Support Groups are a form of Social Support that provides emotional and practical assistance to cancer patients and their families.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
    </node>
    <node id="cancer treatment">
      <data key="d0">cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility is affected by cancer treatment, necessitating preservation strategies to maintain reproductive options for patients across genders.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility is affected by cancer treatment, necessitating preservation strategies to maintain reproductive options for patients.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
    </node>
    <node id="support and counseling">
      <data key="d0">support and counseling</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
      <data key="d2">Distress screening is linked to support and counseling services, which provide emotional and psychological assistance to cancer patients experiencing distress.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
    </node>
    <node id="head and neck tumors">
      <data key="d0">head and neck tumors</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
      <data key="d2">Hearing tests are conducted to evaluate hearing loss caused by head and neck tumors, providing insights into the extent of auditory impairment from tumor growth or treatment.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
    </node>
    <node id="treatment planning">
      <data key="d0">treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
      <data key="d2">Performance status is a vital factor in treatment planning for cancer patients, guiding decisions on the intensity and type of therapeutic interventions.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testicular Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Testicular Cancer</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e</data>
      <data key="d2">Smoking increases the risk of developing testicular cancer and may reduce the effectiveness of cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
    </node>
    <node id="APL">
      <data key="d0">APL</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
      <data key="d2">APL is a specific subtype of AML characterized by distinct genetic markers and targeted therapeutic approaches.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BPDCN">
      <data key="d0">BPDCN</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
      <data key="d2">BPDCN is another rare subtype of AML involving plasmacytoid dendritic cells, highlighting the diversity of AML subtypes.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SCC">
      <data key="d0">SCC</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
      <data key="d2">SCC utilizes the TNM staging system to classify tumors based on size, depth of invasion, and metastasis potential.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC) Panel">
      <data key="d0">Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC) Panel</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
      <data key="d2">The coagulation panel and DIC panel are synonymous terms for assessing clotting disorders, often used interchangeably in medical contexts.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cervix">
      <data key="d0">Cervix</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
      <data key="d2">Cervix is a component of the Female Reproductive System.&lt;SEP&gt;The cervix is part of the female reproductive system, connecting the vagina to the uterus and playing a role in menstrual and reproductive cycles.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarand Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Ovarand Cancer</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
