      <data key="d5">Clinical Trials are research studies focused on developing new therapies for Lymphoma, including primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical innovation, treatment development</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stereotactic Biopsy" target="Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">20.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stereotactic biopsy is a key diagnostic event used to confirm the presence of lymphoma in the central nervous system.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedure, cancer detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lumbar Puncture" target="Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lumbar puncture is performed to detect cancer cells in the cerebrospinal fluid, aiding in the diagnosis and staging of lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">fluid analysis, cancer spread detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT Scan" target="Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CT scan is employed to visualize potential cancer spread in areas like the chest, abdomen, and pelvis, aiding in lymphoma staging.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging for metastasis, diagnostic imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CBC" target="Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CBC is a specific blood test used to evaluate the extent of lymphoma and monitor treatment response.</data>
      <data key="d6">hematological evaluation, treatment monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN guidelines recommend proactive skin cancer screening for individuals with chronic lymphocytic leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d6">immunocompromise, preventive care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="AIDS">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN underscores the importance of lifelong skin cancer monitoring for AIDS patients with weakened immune systems.</data>
      <data key="d6">immunocompromise, surveillance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Biopsy" target="Skin Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Skin Biopsy is a type of biopsy used to analyze superficial skin abnormalities for potential cancerous changes.</data>
      <data key="d6">skin analysis, superficial biopsy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Breast Cancer" target="Skin Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Skin biopsy is used to diagnose inflammatory breast cancer by analyzing inflamed skin samples.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic method, cancer subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Immunosuppressants" target="Organ Transplantation">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Organ transplantation requires immunosuppressants, which weaken the immune system and increase cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d6">immune suppression, drug impact</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CSCC" target="Skin Punch Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Skin punch biopsy is a diagnostic procedure used to remove and analyze skin tissue for the detection of cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedure, cancer detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Leukemia Predisposition Syndromes" target="Skin Punch Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">34.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Skin punch biopsy investigates suspected hereditary leukemia predisposition syndromes, providing diagnostic evidence for genetic risk factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Skin punch biopsy is used to investigate suspected hereditary leukemia predisposition syndromes, providing diagnostic evidence for genetic risk factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Skin punch biopsy is used to test for inherited leukemia predisposition syndromes by analyzing DNA from non-affected tissue, distinguishing them from acquired mutations in AML cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">hereditary risk, diagnostic procedure&lt;SEP&gt;hereditary risk, tissue analysis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CSCC" target="Excision Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Excision biopsy is a treatment method for removing lesions, including those diagnosed as cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma, to prevent further spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment, lesion removal</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Cells" target="Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cancer cells can travel through the lymphatic system and accumulate in lymph nodes, which may indicate metastasis and require further testing.</data>
      <data key="d6">metastasis, immune system interaction</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lymphatic System" target="Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lymph Nodes are components of the Lymphatic System that filter lymph and house immune cells to combat infections."|&gt;"system components, immune defense</data>
      <data key="d6">8</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Head and Neck" target="Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lymph nodes in the head and neck are critical for cancer spread, with over 300 nodes often involved in throat cancer progression.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical spread, regional involvement</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-cell ALL" target="Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">B-cell ALL is associated with lymph node involvement, where B-cell lymphoblasts accumulate, forming B-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">lymphatic system, tumor localization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lymph Nodes are critical in assessing the stage of Prostate Cancer, as metastasis to these nodes indicates advanced disease progression.</data>
      <data key="d6">metastasis detection, staging assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Digital Rectal Exam" target="Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Digital Rectal Exam can help assess the involvement of Lymph Nodes in Prostate Cancer, indicating disease progression and treatment needs.</data>
      <data key="d6">metastasis assessment, staging evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastasis" target="Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lymph nodes are common sites for metastasis, requiring biopsy to confirm cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer spread, anatomical location</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM System" target="Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM system evaluates regional lymph node involvement (N classification) to assess cancer spread and guide staging decisions.</data>
      <data key="d6">lymphatic spread, node involvement</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder Cancer" target="Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lymph nodes are common sites for metastatic bladder cancer, as cancer cells can spread through the lymphatic system.</data>
      <data key="d6">metastasis, spread pathway</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Contrast Dye" target="Cancer Cells">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Contrast dye helps visualize cancer cells in imaging scans by highlighting abnormal metabolic activity or tissue density.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging detection, cancer visualization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT Scan" target="Contrast Dye">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Contrast dye is used in CT scans to enhance the visibility of tissues and organs, improving diagnostic clarity.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging enhancement, diagnostic tool</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="Contrast Dye">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Contrast dye is used in MRI scans to enhance the visibility of tissues and organs, improving diagnostic clarity.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging enhancement, diagnostic tool</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="actinic keratoses" target="Cryotherapy">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cryotherapy is used to treat actinic keratoses by freezing and destroying abnormal skin cells."|&gt;"treatment method, skin lesion removal</data>
      <data key="d6">8</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inflammatory breast cancer" target="Radiation therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy is a local therapy used to treat the chest wall and lymph node area after surgery.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation treatment, post-surgical care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inflammatory breast, breast cancer" target="Radiation therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy is used post-surgery to treat the chest wall and lymph node area in inflammatory breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation treatment, post-surgical care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary CNS lymphoma" target="Radiation therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy is a treatment method used for Primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer treatment, therapeutic use</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Whole-brain radiation therapy (WBRT)" target="Radiation therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Whole-brain radiation therapy (WBRT) is a specific type of radiation therapy used for CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment specialization, therapeutic method</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inflammatory breast cancer" target="Systemic therapy">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Inflammatory breast cancer is treated with systemic therapy to shrink the tumor before surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;Inflammatory breast cancer is treated with systemic therapy to shrink tumors and prepare for surgical intervention.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer treatment, therapeutic approach</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chemotherapy" target="Systemic therapy">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is a core component of systemic therapy for eliminating cancer cells throughout the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy is a type of systemic therapy used to kill cancer cells and shrink tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">therapeutic subcategory, treatment method</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HER2-targeted therapy" target="Systemic therapy">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">HER2-targeted therapy is a specialized form of systemic therapy targeting the HER2 protein in cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;HER2-targeted therapy is a specific type of systemic therapy that targets the HER2 protein in cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">specific therapy, systemic treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Immunotherapy" target="Systemic therapy">
      <data key="d4">27.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Immunotherapy is a type of systemic therapy that enhances the body's immune system to fight cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is a type of systemic therapy that targets cancer cells throughout the body by reactivating the immune system."|&gt;"systemic treatment, immune activation&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is an integrative part of systemic therapy that enhances immune response against cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">9&lt;SEP&gt;immunological treatment, systemic approach</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Endocrine therapy" target="Systemic therapy">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Endocrine therapy is a targeted systemic therapy for hormone receptor-positive cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Endocrine therapy is a type of systemic therapy that targets hormones contributing to cancer growth.</data>
      <data key="d6">hormonal treatment, systemic therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Clinical trials" target="Systemic therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical trials often test new systemic therapies to improve outcomes for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">research development, therapeutic innovation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AML" target="Clinical trials">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical trials are events where new treatment options for AML are tested, including experimental chemotherapies and targeted therapies.</data>
      <data key="d6">research, treatment development</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inflammatory breast cancer" target="Clinical trials">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical trials provide access to experimental therapies and advance treatment options for inflammatory breast cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">research innovation, treatment access</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Imaging tests" target="Cancer staging">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Imaging tests are critical in determining cancer staging by visualizing tumor size, spread, and involvement of lymph nodes or distant organs.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Biopsy" target="Cancer staging">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biopsy results contribute to cancer staging by confirming the presence of cancer and identifying specific genetic or molecular markers.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic confirmation, staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mohs surgery" target="actinic keratoses">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mohs surgery is a treatment for actinic keratoses, allowing precise removal of cancerous tissue with immediate margin assessment."|&gt;"skin cancer treatment, surgical precision</data>
      <data key="d6">9</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Topical tazarotene" target="actinic keratoses">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Topical tazarotene is applied to treat actinic keratoses by promoting skin cell turnover and reducing abnormal growths."|&gt;"topical treatment, skin renewal</data>
      <data key="d6">7</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Topical keratolytics" target="actinic keratoses">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Topical keratolytics are used to soften thickened skin layers before treating actinic keratoses."|&gt;"pre-treatment, skin preparation</data>
      <data key="d6">8</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Topical diclofenac" target="actinic keratoses">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Topical diclofenac is used to treat actinic keratoses by reducing inflammation and abnormal cell growth."|&gt;"anti-inflammatory treatment, skin condition management</data>
      <data key="d6">7</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chemical peels" target="actinic keratoses">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemical peels are used to resurface the skin and treat actinic keratoses by removing damaged layers."|&gt;"skin resurfacing, lesion treatment</data>
      <data key="d6">8</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Laser or dermabrasion" target="actinic keratoses">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Laser or dermabrasion techniques are employed to treat actinic keratoses by removing damaged skin layers."|&gt;"skin resurfacing, lesion removal</data>
      <data key="d6">8</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="5-fluorouracil" target="actinic keratoses">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">5-fluorouracil is a topical chemotherapy drug used to treat actinic keratoses by targeting abnormal skin cells."|&gt;"chemotherapy, skin lesion treatment</data>
      <data key="d6">9</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Calcipotriene" target="actinic keratoses">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Calcipotriene is a vitamin D derivative used in combination with 5-FU to treat actinic keratoses by modulating the immune system."|&gt;"immunomodulatory treatment, skin condition management</data>
      <data key="d6">8</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Imiquimod" target="actinic keratoses">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Imiquimod is an immune response modifier used to treat actinic keratoses by stimulating the immune system to attack abnormal cells."|&gt;"immune therapy, skin cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d6">9</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tirbanibulin" target="actinic keratoses">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tirbanibulin is a microtubule inhibitor used topically to treat actinic keratoses by killing fast-growing abnormal cells."|&gt;"targeted therapy, skin lesion removal</data>
      <data key="d6">8</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hyperkeratosis" target="actinic keratoses">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hyperkeratosis is a condition that requires pre-treatment with keratolytics or chemical peels before applying therapies for actinic keratoses."|&gt;"pre-treatment condition, skin preparation</data>
      <data key="d6">7</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mohs surgery" target="squamous cell skin cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mohs surgery is a recommended treatment for squamous cell skin cancer that has not spread to lymph nodes."|&gt;"skin cancer treatment, surgical excision</data>
      <data key="d6">9</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Standard surgical excision" target="squamous cell skin cancer">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Standard surgical excision is used to remove squamous cell skin cancer, along with surrounding tissue for testing."|&gt;"surgical removal, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d6">8</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lymph node dissection" target="squamous cell skin cancer">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lymph node dissection is performed when squamous cell skin cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes."|&gt;"cancer spread, surgical intervention</data>
      <data key="d6">7</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Sentinel lymph node biopsy" target="squamous cell skin cancer">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Sentinel lymph node biopsy is used to determine if squamous cell skin cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes."|&gt;"cancer staging, lymph node assessment</data>
      <data key="d6">8</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PDEMA" target="Mohs surgery">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PDEMA is a type of surgical technique used in Mohs surgery to assess and remove cancer while preserving normal tissue."|&gt;"surgical technique, margin assessment</data>
      <data key="d6">9</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Curettage and electrodesiccation (C&amp;E)" target="Mohs surgery">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Curettage and electrodesiccation (C&amp;E) is a procedure that can be used in conjunction with Mohs surgery for certain skin cancers."|&gt;"surgical procedures, combined treatment</data>
      <data key="d6">7</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hyperkeratosis" target="Mohs surgery">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hyperkeratosis is a condition that may require pre-treatment steps before Mohs surgery to ensure optimal surgical outcomes."|&gt;"pre-treatment condition, surgical preparation</data>
      <data key="d6">6</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="low-risk" target="Mohs surgery">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mohs surgery is not typically the first-line treatment for low-risk CSCC, but may be used if deeper invasion is unexpectedly found during procedures.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment adaptation, surgical escalation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="high-risk" target="Mohs surgery">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mohs surgery is the preferred treatment for high-risk CSCC due to its precision in removing cancerous tissue and minimizing healthy tissue loss.</data>
      <data key="d6">precision oncology, surgical efficacy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="very high-risk" target="Mohs surgery">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mohs surgery is recommended for very high-risk CSCC, especially when lymphovascular invasion or deep invasion is present, to ensure complete tumor removal.</data>
      <data key="d6">aggressive treatment, tumor eradication</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="High-risk CSCC" target="PDEMA">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PDEMA is an alternative surgical approach for high-risk squamous cell skin cancer when Mohs surgery is not feasible."|&gt;"surgical alternative, cancer excision</data>
      <data key="d6">8</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="low-risk" target="Curettage and electrodesiccation (C&amp;E)">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Curettage and electrodesiccation is the primary treatment for low-risk CSCC, targeting superficial lesions with minimal tissue disruption.</data>
      <data key="d6">minimal intervention, lesion removal</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation Therapy" target="Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Sentinel lymph node biopsy and radiation therapy are both diagnostic and therapeutic events used in cancer staging and treatment planning, often performed in conjunction to assess cancer spread and guide therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, treatment integration</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Axillary Lymph Node" target="Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy focuses on axillary lymph nodes to determine if cancer has spread from the primary tumor.</data>
      <data key="d6">lymphatic spread, metastasis detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Breast Cancer" target="Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Sentinel lymph node biopsy is a diagnostic procedure used to determine if breast cancer has spread to lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedure, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mastectomy" target="Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Sentinel lymph node biopsy is performed during mastectomy to assess cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical context, cancer detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lumpectomy" target="Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Sentinel lymph node biopsy is performed during lumpectomy to assess cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical context, cancer detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Staging System" target="Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy is a procedure within the Staging System that assesses cancer spread to lymph nodes, critical for determining disease progression.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer spread, staging evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Test" target="Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Test includes Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy as a category of procedures used to detect cancer spread to lymph nodes, aiding in staging and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer spread, staging evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Standard Surgical Excision" target="Mohs Procedure">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mohs procedure and standard surgical excision are both surgical events for removing skin cancer, with Mohs being more precise and involving microscopic examination of tissue layers.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical techniques, cancer removal</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="High-risk CSCC" target="Standard Surgical Excision">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Standard surgical excision is a recommended treatment for high-risk or very high-risk squamous cell skin cancer."|&gt;"treatment method, cancer management</data>
      <data key="d6">9</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation Therapy" target="Adjuvant Therapy">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy is a common form of adjuvant therapy used post-surgery to eliminate residual cancer cells and prevent recurrence, particularly for high-risk tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-operative care, recurrence prevention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation Therapy" target="Basal Cell Nevus Syndrome">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy is contraindicated in patients with basal cell nevus syndrome due to their heightened risk of radiation-induced skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic risk, treatment contraindications</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation Therapy" target="Xeroderma Pigmentosum">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy is contraindicated in patients with xeroderma pigmentosum due to their genetic predisposition to skin cancer from DNA repair defects.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic predisposition, treatment restrictions</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation Therapy" target="Lupus">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy is contraindicated in patients with lupus due to the disease's impact on tissue healing and increased risk of radiation-induced complications.</data>
      <data key="d6">contraindications, tissue vulnerability</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary CNS Lymphoma" target="Lupus">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lupus, an autoimmune disease, is associated with increased risk of primary CNS lymphoma due to immune system compromise.</data>
      <data key="d6">autoimmune link, disease interaction</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation Therapy" target="Scleroderma">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy is limited in patients with scleroderma due to the disease's effect on tissue integrity and healing, increasing the risk of non-healing ulcers and scarring.</data>
      <data key="d6">tissue integrity, treatment limitations</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT Scan" target="CSCC">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CT scans are employed to visualize deeper cancerous tissues and assess the extent of cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma beyond the surface layer.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="CSCC">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MRI provides detailed images of soft tissues, aiding in the assessment of cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma's spread to surrounding structures and lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">tissue imaging, cancer spread analysis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="low-risk" target="CSCC">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Low-risk CSCC is a classification of squamous cell skin cancer with minimal metastatic potential, often managed with less invasive treatments.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, treatment hierarchy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="high-risk" target="CSCC">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">High-risk CSCC is a more aggressive form of squamous cell skin cancer with a higher likelihood of recurrence or spread, requiring advanced therapeutic interventions.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer progression, treatment escalation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="very high-risk" target="CSCC">
      <data key="d4">20.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Very high-risk CSCC represents the most severe form of squamous cell skin cancer, necessitating intensive treatment strategies due to its potential for rapid metastasis and resistance to standard therapies.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer severity, treatment complexity</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="smoking" target="CSCC">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Smoking is a major risk factor for CSCC, contributing to its development and complicating treatment outcomes due to its impact on healing and immune function.</data>
      <data key="d6">risk factor, treatment complications</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Clinical Trial" target="FDA">
      <data key="d4">54.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical trials are evaluated and approved by the FDA to ensure the safety and efficacy of new treatments before they are made available to the public.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials often involve investigational treatments that may eventually be approved by the FDA for broader use.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials often involve investigational treatments that may eventually be approved by the FDA.&lt;SEP&gt;The FDA oversees the approval and regulation of investigational treatments used in clinical trials, ensuring their safety and efficacy.&lt;SEP&gt;The FDA oversees the approval and regulation of investigational treatments used in clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The FDA reviews and approves drugs developed through clinical trials, ensuring their safety and efficacy before market release.</data>
      <data key="d6">regulatory approval, drug development&lt;SEP&gt;regulatory oversight, drug approval&lt;SEP&gt;regulatory process, drug development</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Clinical Trials" target="FDA">
      <data key="d4">126.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical Trials may lead to FDA approval of new drugs or treatments, as the FDA evaluates trial results for regulatory clearance."|&gt;"regulatory approval, drug development&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials are essential for developing new treatments that may eventually be approved by the FDA for widespread use.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials are evaluated by the FDA to determine the safety and effectiveness of new cancer treatments before approval.&lt;SEP&gt;The FDA ensures clinical trials meet regulatory standards, approving new treatments based on trial data to benefit patients and healthcare providers.&lt;SEP&gt;The FDA evaluates data from Clinical Trials to determine whether new treatments meet regulatory standards for approval."|&gt;"regulatory oversight, treatment validation&lt;SEP&gt;The FDA oversees and approves treatments tested in clinical trials, ensuring their safety and efficacy for public use.&lt;SEP&gt;The FDA regulates and approves clinical trials, ensuring the safety and efficacy of new cancer treatments before they become available to patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The FDA regulates and approves clinical trials, ensuring the safety and efficacy of new cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;The FDA reviews and approves new cancer treatments based on data from clinical trials, ensuring their safety and efficacy for public use.</data>
      <data key="d6">8&lt;SEP&gt;9&lt;SEP&gt;regulatory approval, drug development&lt;SEP&gt;regulatory approval, medical validation&lt;SEP&gt;regulatory compliance, therapeutic development&lt;SEP&gt;regulatory evaluation, treatment validation&lt;SEP&gt;regulatory oversight, drug approval&lt;SEP&gt;regulatory oversight, treatment approval</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN Guidelines" target="FDA">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The FDA and NCCN Guidelines both play roles in regulating and informing cancer treatment standards, though the FDA focuses on approval while NCCN provides clinical guidance.</data>
      <data key="d6">regulatory guidance, treatment standards</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Anal Cancer" target="FDA">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The FDA regulates the approval of treatments for anal cancer, including chemoradiation and immunotherapy, ensuring their safety and effectiveness.</data>
      <data key="d6">regulatory oversight, treatment approval</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">216.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical trials are informed by the guidelines provided by the NCCN for cancer treatment and management.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials often align with the guidelines provided by the NCCN for cancer treatment and management.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN provides guidelines and resources for managing side effects and treatment options, including information on clinical trial participation.</data>
      <data key="d6">guideline support, patient education&lt;SEP&gt;research participation, guideline alignment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Phase 1" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Phase 1 is the initial stage of a clinical trial that evaluates the safety and dosage of a new treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">trial phases, safety testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Phase 2" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Phase 2 is a stage of clinical trials that assesses the effectiveness of a treatment against a specific cancer type while monitoring side effects.</data>
      <data key="d6">trial phases, efficacy testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Phase 3" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Phase 3 compares an investigational treatment to standard therapies to confirm its effectiveness and long-term safety.</data>
      <data key="d6">trial phases, comparative analysis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Phase 4" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Phase 4 studies the long-term safety and benefits of an FDA-approved treatment after market release.</data>
      <data key="d6">trial phases, post-market monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chemotherapy" target="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d4">25.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is a common treatment evaluated in clinical trials to assess its efficacy and safety in cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials may include chemotherapy as a treatment option for patients with advanced-stage squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
