    <edge source="Philadelphia Chromosome" target="Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML)">
      <data key="d4">40.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The Philadelphia Chromosome is a genetic abnormality directly responsible for the development of Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) through the BCR::ABL1 gene fusion.&lt;SEP&gt;The Philadelphia chromosome is a genetic hallmark of CML, arising from the translocation that creates the BCR::ABL1 gene.</data>
      <data key="d6">causal relationship, genetic basis&lt;SEP&gt;genetic causation, chromosomal abnormalities</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BCR::ABL1 Gene" target="Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML)">
      <data key="d4">20.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The BCR::ABL1 gene is the molecular driver of CML, resulting from the chromosomal translocation that forms the Philadelphia chromosome.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic mutation, disease mechanism</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Blood Cells" target="Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML)">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CML affects the production and function of blood cells, particularly white blood cells and platelets, due to the BCR::ABL1 gene mutation.</data>
      <data key="d6">hematological impact, cellular dysfunction</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Imaging Tests" target="Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML)">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Imaging tests are used to monitor for signs of cancer spread in patients with chronic myeloid leukemia, particularly for regional CSCC.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic monitoring, cancer progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BCR::ABL1" target="Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML)">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The BCR::ABL1 gene fusion is the molecular mechanism underlying Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML), driving the uncontrolled growth of leukemic cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic cause, disease mechanism</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Core Binding Factor (CBF)" target="Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML)">
      <data key="d4">21.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Core Binding Factor (CBF) is a genetic abnormality that can contribute to the development of Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) by disrupting normal blood cell production.&lt;SEP&gt;Core Binding Factor (CBF) is a genetic abnormality that may co-occur with Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) and contributes to the production of immature blood cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic association, disease mechanism</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN Guidelines for Patients" target="Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML)">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients provide evidence-based recommendations for the diagnosis, treatment, and management of Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML).</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical guidelines, disease management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BCR::ABL1 Gene" target="Philadelphia Chromosome">
      <data key="d4">36.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The BCR::3ABL1 gene fusion is a defining feature of the Philadelphia chromosome and drives CML progression.&lt;SEP&gt;The BCR::ABL1 gene fusion is a defining feature of the Philadelphia chromosome and drives CML progression.&lt;SEP&gt;The Philadelphia chromosome and BCR::ABL1 gene are directly linked, as the translocation creates the BCR::ABL1 fusion gene.</data>
      <data key="d6">chromosomal translocation, genetic fusion&lt;SEP&gt;genetic fusion, oncogenesis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </edge>
