      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN provides guidelines and resources for managing CML, including treatment protocols and patient education.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical guidelines, cancer care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Blast Phase CML" target="CML">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Blast phase CML is an advanced stage of CML characterized by the proliferation of immature blasts, requiring more aggressive treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease progression, treatment escalation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Myeloid Progenitor Cells" target="CML">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CML arises from the overproduction of myeloid progenitor cells, which differentiate into immature blasts.</data>
      <data key="d6">cell proliferation, disease origin</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BCR::ABL1 gene" target="CML">
      <data key="d4">20.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The BCR::ABL1 gene leads to uncontrolled blood cell growth, which is the underlying cause of CML and its progression.</data>
      <data key="d6">causal relationship, genetic mutation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Philadelphia chromosome" target="CML">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The Philadelphia chromosome is a diagnostic marker of CML, indicating the presence of the BCR::ABL1 gene and its pathological effects.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic marker, genetic abnormality</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
