    <edge source="NCCN Guidelines for Patients" target="CML">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients provide comprehensive information on CML care, including testing, treatment, and patient advocacy strategies.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical guidelines, patient care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Testing" target="CML">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Testing is essential for diagnosing CML, confirming the presence of the Philadelphia chromosome, and guiding treatment decisions based on genetic and blood test results.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic process, medical evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Blast phase CML" target="CML">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Blast phase CML is an advanced stage of CML characterized by increased progenitor cell proliferation, which alters treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease progression, treatment variation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lung Cancer" target="NSCLC">
      <data key="d4">34.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NSCLC is a subtype of lung cancer, classified under the broader category of lung cancer due to its association with similar risk factors and diagnostic methods.&lt;SEP&gt;NSCLC is a subtype of lung cancer, representing the most common form of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, disease subtypes&lt;SEP&gt;medical classification, cancer subtypes</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="NSCLC">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN provides patient guidelines and resources for NSCLC, including information on causes, risk factors, and treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d6">information source, medical guidance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Carcinogens" target="NSCLC">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Carcinogens, such as radon and asbestos, are linked to the development of NSCLC through exposure and genetic factors.</data>
      <data key="d6">causal relationship, environmental risk</data>
