      <data key="d6">cancer subtypes, medical classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Adenosquamous carcinoma" target="Non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC)">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Adenosquamous carcinoma is a rare subtype of NSCLC combining features of adenocarcinoma and squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer subtypes, medical classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Sarcomatoid carcinoma" target="Non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC)">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Sarcomatoid carcinoma is a rare and aggressive subtype of NSCLC with spindle-shaped cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer subtypes, medical classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="Adenocarcinoma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The NCCN provides guidelines for the treatment and management of adenocarcinoma, the most common type of colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">guideline provision, cancer management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colon Cancer" target="Adenocarcinoma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Adenocarcinoma is a malignant form of colon cancer that originates in the adenoma, characterized by the invasion of surrounding tissues and potential metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer progression, malignant transformation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervical Cancer" target="Adenocarcinoma">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Adenocarcinoma is a less common but distinct histological type of cervical cancer affecting the endocervix.</data>
      <data key="d6">histological variation, tumor origin</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder Cancer" target="Adenocarcinoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Adenocarcinoma is a rare type of bladder cancer that originates in glandular cells, which are not typically found in the urinary tract.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer type, classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="SCLC">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN provides patient guidelines and resources for SCLC, including information on causes, risk factors, and treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d6">information source, medical guidance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Carcinogens" target="SCLC">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Carcinogens, such as radon and asbestos, are linked to the development of SCLC through exposure and genetic factors.</data>
      <data key="d6">causal relationship, environmental risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Secondhand Smoke" target="SCLC">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Secondhand smoke increases the risk of SCLC by exposing individuals to harmful chemicals without direct smoking.</data>
      <data key="d6">risk factor, environmental exposure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Nicotine Addiction" target="SCLC">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Nicotine addiction is a risk factor for SCLC, as it contributes to smoking habits and lung damage.</data>
      <data key="d6">addiction, health risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer App">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app is an extension of NCCN's organization, providing digital access to patient-specific cancer information and guidelines.</data>
      <data key="d6">digital resource, organizational extension</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stomach Cancer" target="NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer App">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app offers mobile resources for Stomach Cancer patients."|&gt;"medical resource, cancer information</data>
      <data key="d6">8</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Esophageal Squamous Cell Carcinoma" target="Esophageal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma is a specific subtype of esophageal cancer originating in squamous cells of the upper and middle esophagus.&lt;SEP&gt;Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma is a specific type of esophageal cancer originating in the squamous cells of the upper and middle esophagus.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer subtypes, cellular origin</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Esophageal Adenocarcinoma" target="Esophageal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Esophageal adenocarcinoma is a specific subtype of esophageal cancer arising from mucus-producing cells in the lower esophagus, often linked to Barrett esophagus.&lt;SEP&gt;Esophageal adenocarcinoma is a specific type of esophageal cancer arising from mucus-producing cells in the lower esophagus, often linked to Barrett esophagus.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer subtypes, etiology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Siewert Types" target="Esophageal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Siewert types are used to classify the location of esophageal tumors relative to the esophagogastric junction, aiding in staging and treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, anatomical classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stages of Esophageal Cancer" target="Esophageal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">20.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The stages of esophageal cancer describe the progression of the disease, from localized tumors to distant metastasis, guiding treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease progression, treatment framework</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Esophagogastric Junction" target="Esophageal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The esophagogastric junction is a critical anatomical site where tumors can occur, influencing cancer staging and treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical relevance, staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stomach Cancer" target="Esophageal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Esophageal Cancer can occur in the cardia of the stomach and is treated similarly to esophageal cancers."|&gt;"cancer type, related conditions</data>
      <data key="d6">7</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Barrett Esophagus" target="Esophageal Adenocarcinoma">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Barrett esophagus is a risk factor for developing esophageal adenocarcinoma due to chronic acid reflux-induced cellular changes.</data>
      <data key="d6">pathogenesis, risk factor</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </edge>
