      <data key="d5">T4b is a stage in the TNM system for very advanced local disease with critical structure invasion.</data>
      <data key="d6">advanced disease, staging categories</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tumor" target="Resectable">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Resectable describes tumors that can be surgically removed, influencing treatment options and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical resection, treatment criteria</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Numbered Stages" target="Resectable">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Resectable tumors are typically classified in early numbered stages (e.g., stage 0 or I), while unresectable tumors align with advanced stages (e.g., stage IV).</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical feasibility, staging correlation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tumor" target="Unresectable">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Unresectable tumors are those that cannot be completely removed surgically, often requiring alternative therapeutic strategies.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment limitations, tumor biology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Locoregional" target="Metastatic">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Locoregional disease represents intermediate cancer spread within the head and neck, while metastatic disease indicates distant spread to other body systems.</data>
      <data key="d6">spread severity, progression levels</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AML" target="Blood cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">AML is a type of blood cancer originating in the bone marrow, affecting the production of mature blood cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, hematologic malignancies</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AML" target="Bone marrow">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">AML originates in the bone marrow, where abnormal myeloid blasts accumulate and disrupt normal blood cell production.</data>
      <data key="d6">tumor origin, hematopoiesis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lymphatic system" target="Bone marrow">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bone marrow is a key organ of the lymphatic system where lymphocytes are produced and mature.</data>
      <data key="d6">system origin, cell production</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Blood" target="Plasma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Plasma is the liquid component of blood that carries nutrients, hormones, and waste products, and serves as a medium for blood cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">tissue composition, blood function</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AML" target="Red Blood Cells">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">AML disrupts the normal development of red blood cells, leading to reduced oxygen-carrying capacity in the blood.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer impact, blood cell dysfunction</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Blood Stem Cells" target="Red Blood Cells">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Blood stem cells differentiate into red blood cells, which are essential for oxygen transport in the body.</data>
      <data key="d6">cell differentiation, blood cell development</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AML" target="White Blood Cells">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">AML impairs the production of white blood cells, weakening the immune system's ability to fight infections.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer impact, immune system compromise</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Blood Stem Cells" target="White Blood Cells">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Blood stem cells differentiate into white blood cells, which are critical for immune defense against pathogens and infections.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood stem cells differentiate into white blood cells, which are critical for immune defense against pathogens.</data>
      <data key="d6">cell differentiation, immune system&lt;SEP&gt;immune system, cell differentiation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AML" target="Platelets">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">AML affects platelet production, increasing the risk of uncontrolled bleeding due to impaired clotting mechanisms.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer impact, coagulation disorder</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Blood Stem Cells" target="Platelets">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Blood stem cells differentiate into platelets, which are vital for blood clotting and preventing excessive bleeding.</data>
      <data key="d6">cell differentiation, coagulation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN Guidelines for Patients" target="AML">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients provide care recommendations and treatment strategies for AML, guiding patient management and decision-making.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical guidelines, cancer care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="APL" target="AML">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">APL is a specific subtype of AML characterized by distinct genetic markers and targeted therapeutic approaches.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer subtypes, genetic classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BPDCN" target="AML">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">BPDCN is another rare subtype of AML involving plasmacytoid dendritic cells, highlighting the diversity of AML subtypes.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer subtypes, rare diseases</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Blood Stem Cells" target="AML">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">AML arises from mutations in blood stem cells, which disrupt their normal differentiation into functional blood cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer origin, stem cell mutation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AML Specialist" target="AML">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">AML Specialists are healthcare professionals who diagnose and treat AML, utilizing genetic and biomarker testing to guide personalized treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical expertise, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AML Medical History and Physical Exam" target="AML">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">AML Medical History and Physical Exam are critical components of the diagnostic and management process for AML patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease management, diagnostic process</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Immunophenotyping" target="AML">
      <data key="d4">34.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Immunophenotyping helps identify surface antigens on white blood cells, which is essential for diagnosing and subclassifying Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML).&lt;SEP&gt;Immunophenotyping identifies surface antigens on white blood cells, essential for diagnosing Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML).&lt;SEP&gt;Immunophenotyping is critical for diagnosing AML by detecting specific antigens on white blood cells that distinguish it from other hematologic malignancies.</data>
      <data key="d6">antigen detection, classification&lt;SEP&gt;diagnosis, classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Leukemia Predisposition Syndromes" target="AML">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">AML can be influenced by inherited leukemia predisposition syndromes, which may affect treatment response and prognosis, necessitating genetic testing of unaffected tissues.</data>
      <data key="d6">hereditary factors, treatment impact</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chemotherapy" target="AML">
      <data key="d4">29.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is the standard of care for AML, targeting fast-dividing cells in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy is the standard of care for AML, targeting fast-dividing leukemic cells while managing side effects and treatment cycles.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy is the standard of care for AML, targeting fast-dividing leukemic cells while managing side effects and treatment cycles.")</data>
      <data key="d6">standard treatment, cancer therapy&lt;SEP&gt;treatment standard, cancer therapy</data>
