    </edge>
    <edge source="Hypomethylating agents" target="AML">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hypomethylating agents are used to treat AML by reversing DNA methylation and reactivating genes.</data>
      <data key="d6">epigenetic therapy, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Targeted therapy" target="AML">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Targeted therapy is an alternative approach for AML, focusing on specific molecular targets like CD33.</data>
      <data key="d6">precision medicine, genetic targeting</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Core binding factor (CBF)" target="AML">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CBF-related AML is treated with combinations of chemotherapy and targeted therapies to address genetic abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic mutation, treatment strategy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FLT3" target="AML">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">FLT3 mutations in AML are targeted with specific drugs like Gilteritinib and Quizartinib to inhibit cancer cell growth.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic targeting, mutation therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="AML">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The NCCN provides guidelines for the diagnosis and treatment of AML, helping patients understand their care options.</data>
      <data key="d6">guidelines, medical care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bone Marrow Tests" target="AML">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bone marrow tests are critical for diagnosing AML by identifying the presence of myeloid blasts in the bone marrow.</data>
