    <edge source="Epithelium" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Most ovarian cancers originate in the epithelium, which is the layer of tissue surrounding the ovaries, making it a primary site for malignancy.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer origin, anatomical location</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="High-grade Serous Carcinoma" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">High-grade serous carcinoma is the most common subtype of ovarian cancer, characterized by its aggressive nature and association with epithelial origin.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, subtype relationship</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="High-grade Endometrioid Carcinoma" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">High-grade endometrioid carcinoma is a less common but significant subtype of ovarian cancer, often linked to hormonal influences and endometrial-like features.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, subtype relationship</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Low-grade Serous Carcinoma" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Low-grade serous carcinoma is a rare subtype of ovarian cancer, typically slower-growing compared to high-grade variants and often associated with genetic factors.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, subtype relationship</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Low-grade Endometrioid Carcinoma" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Low-grade endometrioid carcinoma is a distinct subtype of ovarian cancer, characterized by endometrial-like histology and a generally better prognosis compared to high-grade counterparts.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, subtype relationship</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Carcinosarcoma" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Carcinosarcoma is a rare and aggressive form of ovarian cancer, combining features of both carcinoma and sarcoma, often requiring multidisciplinary treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, rare subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Clear Cell Carcinoma" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clear cell carcinoma is a rare subtype of ovarian cancer, often associated with endometriosis and characterized by distinct histological features under the microscope.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, rare subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mucinous Carcinoma" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mucinous carcinoma is a rare ovarian cancer type characterized by the production of mucus and often associated with multiple cystic structures in the ovary.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, rare subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Borderline Epithelial Tumor" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Borderline epithelial tumors are non-invasive or minimally invasive ovarian tumors with a lower risk of metastasis compared to malignant carcinomas, often managed differently in treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, low-risk variant</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Malignant Sex-cord Stromal Tumors" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Malignant sex-cord stromal tumors are rare ovarian cancers arising from supportive tissues, often linked to hormone-related symptoms and genetic factors.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, rare subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Malignant Germ Cell Tumors" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Malignant germ cell tumors are rare ovarian cancers originating from reproductive cells, often affecting younger women and associated with specific genetic markers.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, rare subtype</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary Peritoneal Cancer" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Primary peritoneal cancer shares similar treatment approaches with ovarian cancer and often arises in the peritoneum, the tissue lining the abdominal cavity.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, related malignancy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fallopian Tube Cancer" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Fallopian tube cancer is a rare type of gynecological cancer that develops in the fallopian tubes, often with overlapping features with ovarian cancer in terms of origin and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, related malignancy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Gynecologic Oncologists" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Gynecologic oncologists specialize in diagnosing and treating ovarian cancer, often leading surgical interventions and coordinating multidisciplinary care teams.</data>
      <data key="d6">specialization, treatment role</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Medical Oncologists" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Medical oncologists manage ovarian cancer treatment using systemic therapies like chemotherapy, collaborating with gynecologic oncologists for comprehensive care.</data>
      <data key="d6">specialization, treatment role</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Abdominal Exam" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The abdominal exam detects physical signs of ovarian cancer, such as fluid buildup or organ enlargement, during initial assessment.&lt;SEP&gt;The abdominal exam is used to detect physical signs of ovarian cancer, such as fluid buildup or organ enlargement, during initial assessment.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedure, symptom detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvic Exam" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The pelvic exam checks for abnormalities in reproductive organs, including ovarian masses or cervical changes, to assess for ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The pelvic exam checks for abnormalities in reproductive organs, including ovarian masses or changes in the cervix, to assess for ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedure, physical examination</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Abdominal and Pelvic Exam" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The combined abdominal and pelvic exam is a comprehensive method to evaluate for ovarian cancer symptoms and structural changes.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic process, integrated assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectovaginal Exam" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The rectovaginal exam helps detect tumors in the area between the rectum and vagina, which may be affected by ovarian cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer spread, localized examination</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT Scan" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">34.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CT Scans are critical for detecting cancer metastasis and monitoring treatment response in ovarian cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;CT Scans are used to detect cancer spread and monitor treatment response in ovarian cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;CT scans are used to determine if cancer has spread beyond the ovaries to other abdominal or chest regions.&lt;SEP&gt;CT scans determine if cancer has spread beyond the ovaries to other abdominal or chest regions, aiding in staging.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, metastasis detection&lt;SEP&gt;imaging diagnostics, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">34.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MRI provides detailed anatomical images to assess ovarian cancer spread and guide treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI provides detailed images of internal structures to assess ovarian cancer extent and guide treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI provides detailed images of soft tissues, helping to assess ovarian cancer spread and pelvic structures with high precision.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging diagnostics, cancer staging&lt;SEP&gt;soft tissue imaging, spread evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Imaging Tests" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">29.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Imaging tests are essential for visualizing ovarian tumors, determining cancer staging, and assessing treatment effectiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging tests like ultrasound and endoscopy are essential for visualizing ovarian cancer and guiding diagnostic and therapeutic interventions.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, staging evaluation&lt;SEP&gt;diagnostic imaging, therapeutic guidance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pap Test" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The Pap test is used to screen for cervical cancer, though it is not directly related to ovarian cancer diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;The Pap test screens for cervical cancer, though it is not directly related to ovarian cancer diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">screening procedure, cervical cancer detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Endometrial Biopsy" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">12.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An endometrial biopsy helps rule out uterine cancer, which may present with similar symptoms as ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">differential diagnosis, uterine cancer exclusion</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Transvaginal Ultrasound" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Transvaginal ultrasound provides detailed imaging of the ovaries, aiding in detecting abnormalities specific to ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">specialized imaging, tumor detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BRCA1" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">BRCA1 mutations are a known cause of hereditary ovarian cancer, increasing the risk of developing the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA1 mutations are a primary genetic cause of hereditary ovarian cancer, increasing susceptibility to the disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic association, hereditary risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BRCA2" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">BRCA2 mutations are linked to hereditary ovarian cancer, contributing to the risk of developing the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA2 mutations contribute to hereditary ovarian cancer, heightening the risk of developing the disease through genetic pathways.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic association, hereditary risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Diagnostic Laparoscopy" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Diagnostic Laparoscopy is used to assess the extent of ovarian cancer in the abdomen by examining tissue samples and visualizing internal structures.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagnostic Laparoscopy is used to evaluate the extent of ovarian cancer in the abdomen through tissue sampling and visualization.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, diagnostic procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Gynecology Care Team" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The Gynecology Care Team specializes in diagnosing and managing ovarian cancer, including pre-surgical evaluations and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d6">specialized care, cancer management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Oncology Care Team" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The Oncology Care Team oversees the treatment of ovarian cancer, including chemotherapy, radiation, and targeted therapies.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer treatment, multidisciplinary care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chest X-ray" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chest X-rays are used to check for lung metastasis in ovarian cancer patients, evaluating cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Chest X-rays are used to evaluate lung metastasis in ovarian cancer patients, assessing cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer metastasis, diagnostic imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fine-Needle Aspiration (FNA)" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Fine-Needle Aspiration is a biopsy technique to obtain tissue samples for diagnosing ovarian cancer and characterizing its features.&lt;SEP&gt;Fine-Needle Aspiration is a procedure to obtain tissue samples for diagnosing ovarian cancer and assessing its characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d6">biopsy technique, cancer diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Core Biopsy" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Core Biopsy extracts tissue samples to confirm ovarian cancer presence and evaluate its biological properties.&lt;SEP&gt;Core Biopsy extracts tissue samples to confirm the presence of ovarian cancer and evaluate its biological features.</data>
      <data key="d6">biopsy technique, cancer diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Paracentesis" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Paracentesis removes abdominal fluid to assess cancer spread and obtain samples for diagnostic analysis.</data>
      <data key="d6">fluid analysis, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BRCA Genes" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">BRCA mutations are critical biomarkers for planning ovarian cancer treatment and are tested in tumors to determine homologous recombination deficiency (HRD) status.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic risk, treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tumor Markers" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tumor markers like CA-125 are used alongside other data to monitor ovarian cancer progression and response to treatment, though they are not definitive for diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic tools, monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Biopsy" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biopsy is a critical event in diagnosing ovarian cancer, often performed during initial surgery or before chemotherapy to confirm the presence of cancerous tissue.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic confirmation, surgical procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Liver Function Tests" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Liver function tests are used to monitor the impact of ovarian cancer on liver health and to detect potential spread of the disease to the liver.</data>
      <data key="d6">organ function, cancer spread</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tumor Mutational Burden (TMB)" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">TMB analysis of ovarian cancer tumors helps determine potential responses to immunotherapy and other targeted treatments based on genetic mutations.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic profiling, treatment response</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Microsatellite Instability (MSI)" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MSI in ovarian cancer tumors indicates defects in DNA repair mechanisms, influencing treatment options such as immunotherapy and prognosis assessment.</data>
      <data key="d6">DNA repair, therapeutic response</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mismatch Repair (MMR)" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Defective MMR in ovarian cancer tumors is associated with genetic instability and may influence treatment strategies, including targeted therapies and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic instability, treatment adaptation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HER2 Expression" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">HER2 expression in ovarian cancer tumors may impact treatment options and prognosis, though its role is less defined compared to other biomarkers in this context.</data>
      <data key="d6">protein expression, therapeutic relevance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Blood Chemistry Profile" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Blood chemistry profiles are used to evaluate the systemic effects of ovarian cancer on organ function and metabolic processes.</data>
      <data key="d6">systemic impact, metabolic assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AJCC" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The AJCC staging system is used to classify the progression of ovarian cancer, providing critical information for treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FIGO" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The FIGO staging system is widely used to describe the extent of ovarian cancer spread, guiding clinical decisions and treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, clinical guidelines</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Peritoneal Washing" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Peritoneal Washing is a diagnostic procedure used to detect microscopic metastases of ovarian cancer in the abdominal cavity.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer detection, diagnostic procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surgical Menopause" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surgical removal of both ovaries during ovarian cancer treatment leads to Surgical Menopause, causing a sudden drop in estrogen levels.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical impact, hormonal changes</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Maintenance Therapy" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Maintenance therapy is a treatment approach specifically applied to ovarian cancer to reduce recurrence risk.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer treatment, disease management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chemotherapy" target="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is a primary treatment method for ovarian cancer, administered intravenously or intraperitoneally.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer treatment, drug administration</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="Transvaginal Ultrasound">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Transvaginal ultrasound and MRI are both used to evaluate cervical anatomy, with MRI offering more detailed soft tissue imaging when patients cannot undergo MRI scans.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging alternatives, anatomical assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="BRCA1">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mutations in BRCA1 are associated with an increased risk of hereditary prostate cancer and other cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic predisposition, hereditary cancer</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hereditary Gene Testing" target="BRCA1">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hereditary gene testing specifically examines BRCA1 mutations, which are linked to increased cancer risks in multiple organ systems.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic mutation, cancer risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tumor Testing" target="BRCA1">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tumor testing identifies BRCA1 mutations in cancer cells, which can guide treatment with targeted therapies like PARP inhibitors.</data>
      <data key="d6">molecular analysis, targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PARP inhibitors" target="BRCA1">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PARP inhibitors are specifically effective in patients with BRCA1/BRCA2 mutations, as these mutations impair DNA repair mechanisms."|&gt;"genetic mutation, targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d6">9</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="BRCA2">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mutations in BRCA2 are linked to a higher risk of prostate cancer and other malignancies due to impaired DNA repair mechanisms.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic risk, DNA repair</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hereditary Gene Testing" target="BRCA2">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hereditary gene testing also analyzes BRCA2 mutations, which are associated with similar cancer risks as BRCA1 mutations.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic mutation, cancer risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tumor Testing" target="BRCA2">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tumor testing detects BRCA2 mutations in cancer cells, which influence treatment strategies for patients with specific genetic markers.</data>
      <data key="d6">molecular analysis, targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Germline Testing" target="Genetic Counselor">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Genetic Counselors provide guidance and support for patients undergoing germline testing and interpreting its results.</data>
