    </edge>
    <edge source="Anal Cancer" target="Staging">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Anal Cancer is classified into stages (I-IV) based on tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical staging, disease progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN Guidelines for Patients" target="Survey">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surveys are used to gather patient feedback on the NCCN Guidelines, improving their relevance and usability for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient engagement, guideline refinement</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pathologic Stage" target="Clinical Stage">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical and pathologic stages are distinct phases of cancer staging, with the latter providing more accurate post-surgical details about cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">staging phases, diagnostic progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM System" target="Clinical Stage">
      <data key="d4">20.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM system is used to assign numerical scores to clinical stages based on tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d6">staging framework, diagnostic criteria</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM System" target="Pathologic Stage">
      <data key="d4">20.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM system provides the basis for pathologic staging, which refines cancer details after surgical tissue analysis.</data>
      <data key="d6">staging framework, post-surgical analysis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="p16-Positive" target="TNM System">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">p16-positive cancer uses the TNM system for staging, with distinct criteria for HPV-related tumors compared to p16-negative cases.</data>
      <data key="d6">HPV-related staging, tumor classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Numbered Stages" target="TNM System">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Numbered stages (0-4) are derived from TNM scores, reflecting the progression of cancer from localized to metastatic spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">progression stages, TNM integration</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate Cancer" target="TNM System">
      <data key="d4">27.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM system is a critical tool for staging prostate cancer, describing tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis to determine disease extent.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM system is used to stage prostate cancer based on tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, diagnostic classification&lt;SEP&gt;cancer staging, diagnostic framework</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Gleason Score" target="TNM System">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM system and Gleason score both contribute to staging and grading prostate cancer, providing a comprehensive view of disease extent and aggressiveness.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic integration, staging and grading</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Breast Cancer" target="TNM System">
      <data key="d4">20.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM System is used to stage breast cancer, determining the extent of tumor growth, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d6">staging framework, clinical classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Breast Cancer" target="TNM System">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Metastatic Breast Cancer is classified using the TNM System, indicating cancer has spread to distant sites (M stage).</data>
      <data key="d6">metastasis classification, staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="American Joint Committee on Cancer" target="TNM System">
      <data key="d4">36.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) developed the TNM System as a standardized method for classifying cancer stages based on tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) developed the TNM system as a standardized method for cancer staging.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) develops and maintains the TNM System, a standardized framework for staging cancer based on tumor, node, and metastasis factors.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, medical guidelines&lt;SEP&gt;staging system development, cancer classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Staging" target="TNM System">
      <data key="d4">20.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM System is a core component of cancer staging, using T, N, and M scores to classify stages and guide prognosis and treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM system is a core component of cancer staging, using T, N, and M scores to classify stages.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical staging, tumor classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bone Cancer" target="TNM System">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM system is the primary method used to classify and stage bone cancer based on tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, classification system</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Appendicular Skeleton" target="TNM System">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM system includes specific staging criteria for tumors located in the appendicular skeleton, such as limbs and pelvic girdle.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical staging, tumor location</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Spine" target="TNM System">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM system provides distinct staging categories for tumors located in the spine, based on vertebral involvement and spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">spinal tumor staging, anatomical classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvis" target="TNM System">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM system includes detailed staging guidelines for pelvic tumors, accounting for segmental involvement and extrabone spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">pelvic tumor staging, regional spread</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastasis" target="TNM System">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM system's M classification identifies distant metastasis, which significantly impacts cancer staging and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">distant spread, metastatic classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Grade" target="TNM System">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TNM system incorporates cancer grade as a factor to assess tumor aggressiveness and growth potential.</data>
      <data key="d6">tumor aggressiveness, histological classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL)">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The NCCN provides clinical guidelines and resources for managing ALL, including treatment recommendations and care standards.</data>
      <data key="d6">guideline provision, medical standards</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="B-cell ALL" target="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL)">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">B-cell ALL is a specific subtype of ALL, characterized by the involvement of B-cell lymphocytes and distinct clinical features.&lt;SEP&gt;B-cell ALL is a subtype of ALL that specifically affects B lymphocytes, leading to the overproduction of immature B-cell lymphoblasts.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer subtypes, cell proliferation&lt;SEP&gt;subclassification, medical classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T-cell ALL" target="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL)">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">T-cell ALL is a subtype of ALL that originates in T lymphocytes, characterized by the proliferation of immature T-cell lymphoblasts.&lt;SEP&gt;T-cell ALL is another subtype of ALL, originating from T-cell lymphocytes and presenting unique pathological and therapeutic challenges.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer subtypes, cell differentiation&lt;SEP&gt;subclassification, medical classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="National Comprehensive Cancer Network® (NCCN®)" target="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL)">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The NCCN provides guidelines and educational resources about ALL, including its diagnosis, treatment, and management strategies.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical guidance, cancer education</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Blood" target="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL)">
      <data key="d4">20.0</data>
      <data key="d5">ALL is a type of blood cancer that disrupts normal blood cell production by causing excessive lymphoblasts to crowd out healthy blood cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, blood dysfunction</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Burkitt Leukemia" target="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL)">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Burkitt leukemia is a rare subtype of ALL, distinguished by its aggressive growth and association with specific genetic abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer subtypes, genetic factors</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ALL" target="B-cell ALL">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">B-cell ALL is a subtype of ALL, originating from B-cell lymphocytes and more common than T-cell ALL.</data>
      <data key="d6">subclassification, medical classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="B-cell ALL">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The NCCN provides specialized guidelines for managing B-cell ALL, including treatment protocols and patient care recommendations.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical guidance, medical standards</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="T-cell ALL" target="B-cell ALL">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">B-cell ALL and T-cell ALL are distinct subtypes of ALL, differing in their cellular origin, prevalence, and treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical differentiation, subtype comparison</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Burkitt Leukemia" target="B-cell ALL">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Burkitt leukemia is a rare, aggressive subtype of B-cell ALL associated with genetic abnormalities and rapid tumor growth.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer subtypes, genetic factors</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia" target="B-cell ALL">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">B-cell ALL is a specific subtype of ALL characterized by the proliferation of B-cell lymphoblasts in lymph nodes rather than bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer subtypes, biological differentiation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ALL" target="T-cell ALL">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">T-cell ALL is a subtype of ALL, originating from T-cell lymphocytes and can cause an enlarged thymus with potential breathing complications.</data>
      <data key="d6">subclassification, medical classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="T-cell ALL">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The NCCN offers guidelines tailored for T-cell ALL, addressing its unique clinical manifestations and therapeutic approaches.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical guidance, medical standards</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia" target="T-cell ALL">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">T-cell ALL is another subtype of ALL involving T-cell lymphoblasts, often associated with thymus enlargement and distinct treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer subtypes, treatment differentiation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AYA" target="Pediatric">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">AYA is a subset of the Pediatric category, encompassing individuals aged 15 to 39 years who may receive treatment in pediatric or adult centers depending on their condition.&lt;SEP&gt;Pediatric and AYA are categories that classify patients based on age, with AYAs being 15 to 39 years old and possibly treated in pediatric or adult centers.</data>
