    <edge source="Chemotherapy" target="Rectal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is a treatment option for rectal cancer, often used in combination with radiation or after surgery to eliminate lingering cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer treatment, drug therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chemoradiation" target="Rectal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemoradiation is a treatment approach used for locally advanced rectal cancer, combining chemotherapy and radiation to target cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">combined therapy, cancer management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage I" target="Rectal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage I is a classification of rectal cancer indicating the tumor has grown into the muscle layer but not beyond, requiring no further treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, treatment decision</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage II" target="Rectal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage II is a classification of rectal cancer indicating the tumor has grown through the rectal wall into nearby tissues, requiring additional treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, treatment necessity</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage III" target="Rectal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage III is a classification of rectal cancer indicating cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes, necessitating more treatment like chemoradiation or chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, advanced treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Clinical trial" target="T-ALL">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical trials are a treatment option for T-ALL, reflecting its less common and complex nature.</data>
      <data key="d6">research-based care, experimental treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN Guidelines for Patients" target="Testing for ALL">
      <data key="d4">26.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients provide detailed information on diagnostic testing procedures for ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Guidelines for Patients provides information on diagnostic tests and treatment planning for ALL.</data>
      <data key="d6">resource provision, patient education&lt;SEP&gt;resource, treatment guidance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mismatch Repair Testing" target="Mismatch Repair (MMR) Genes">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Testing for mutations in MMR genes is critical for diagnosing Lynch syndrome and guiding treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic testing, cancer diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Monitoring" target="Carcinoembryonic Antigen (CEA)">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CEA levels are monitored to assess treatment effectiveness and detect potential cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment monitoring, recurrence detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Patient" target="Carcinoembryonic Antigen (CEA)">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The patient's cancer may be monitored using CEA levels to assess treatment response and progression.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker monitoring, disease tracking</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Liquid Biopsy" target="Circulating Tumor DNA (ctDNA)">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">ctDNA is analyzed through liquid biopsy to detect cancer cells in the bloodstream, supporting non-invasive monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d6">non-invasive testing, cancer detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Clinical Trials" target="Circulating Tumor DNA (ctDNA)">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">ctDNA testing is under study in clinical trials to evaluate its role in predicting cancer recurrence and treatment outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d6">research innovation, clinical evaluation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Patient" target="Circulating Tumor DNA (ctDNA)">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The patient's cancer may be analyzed using ctDNA to detect and monitor tumor dynamics in the bloodstream.</data>
      <data key="d6">liquid biopsy, tumor detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastasis" target="Liquid Biopsy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Liquid Biopsy is used to detect metastasis by analyzing blood samples for circulating cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">non-invasive detection, metastasis monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="rectal cancer" target="CT">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CT scans detect cancer metastasis to distant organs like the liver and lungs, aiding in comprehensive staging and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d6">metastasis detection, imaging diagnostics</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="rectal cancer" target="Endorectal Ultrasound">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Endorectal ultrasound evaluates local cancer extent in the pelvis and guides biopsies of lymph nodes or surrounding tissues.</data>
      <data key="d6">local staging, biopsy guidance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MSI-H" target="dMMR">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">dMMR and MSI-H are interrelated biomarkers, with MSI-H representing the phenotypic expression of dMMR defects in cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker correlation, genetic instability</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ovarian Transposition" target="Radiation-Induced Premature Menopause">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Ovarian transposition is performed to mitigate radiation-induced premature menopause by relocating ovaries outside the radiation field.</data>
      <data key="d6">fertility protection, radiation mitigation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="fertility preservation" target="Sperm Banking">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Sperm banking is a key fertility preservation strategy for male patients undergoing cancer treatments that may affect reproductive function.</data>
      <data key="d6">reproductive preservation, cryopreservation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fertility and Family Planning" target="Sperm Banking">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Sperm banking is a specific method within fertility preservation planning for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">fertility methods, reproductive care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="fertility preservation" target="Egg Freezing">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Egg freezing is a critical method for female patients to preserve oocytes before treatments that may cause infertility, such as radiation or chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">ovarian preservation, reproductive technology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fertility and Family Planning" target="Egg Freezing">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Egg freezing is a key strategy in fertility preservation planning for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">fertility methods, reproductive care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fertility and Pregnancy" target="Egg Freezing">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Fertility and pregnancy goals lead to egg freezing as a method to preserve oocytes for future use after cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">fertility preservation, reproductive options</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fertility and Pregnancy" target="Ovarian Transposition">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Fertility and pregnancy considerations drive the use of ovarian transposition to preserve fertility before radiation therapy for cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">fertility preservation, treatment integration</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation Therapy" target="Ovarian Transposition">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy damages the ovaries, making ovarian transposition a critical fertility preservation strategy for premenopausal patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">fertility preservation, treatment impact</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Menopause" target="Ovarian Transposition">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Ovarian Transposition is a procedure to prevent Menopause by relocating ovaries outside the radiation field, preserving hormonal function.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian Transposition prevents Menopause by relocating ovaries outside the radiation field, preserving hormonal function.</data>
      <data key="d6">preventive measure, hormonal preservation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Endoscopic submucosal dissection (ESD)" target="Non-metastatic cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">ESD is a recommended treatment for non-metastatic cancer confined to the mucosal layer of the rectum.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment protocol, localized therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Transanal local excision" target="Early rectal cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Transanal local excision is a treatment option for early-stage rectal cancer localized to the rectal wall.</data>
      <data key="d6">localized treatment, early intervention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Polypectomy" target="Polyps with cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Polypectomy is a procedure to remove polyps with cancer, preventing progression to invasive disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">preventive treatment, early intervention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Imaging with endoscopic ultrasound (EUS)" target="Sessile polyps">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Imaging with EUS is recommended for sessile polyps to assess their potential for recurrence and guide surgical decisions.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, recurrence risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surveillance after ESD" target="Endoscopic submucosal dissection (ESD)">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surveillance is recommended for at least 5 years after ESD to monitor for cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-treatment monitoring, recurrence detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Surveillance after transanal local excision" target="Transanal local excision">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Surveillance includes regular imaging and colonoscopies to monitor for recurrence after transanal local excision.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-operative follow-up, cancer monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RCC (Renal Cell Carcinoma)" target="Kidney Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">RCC (Renal Cell Carcinoma) is a subtype of kidney cancer, characterized by its origin in the kidneys and specific spread patterns.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer subtypes, renal malignancy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RCC" target="Kidney Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">RCC is a primary subtype of kidney cancer, representing the most common form of this disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, medical taxonomy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urothelial Carcinoma" target="Kidney Cancer">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Urothelial Carcinoma is a distinct subtype of kidney cancer, though it shares characteristics with bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer subtypes, cross-organ pathology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Wilms Tumor" target="Kidney Cancer">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Wilms Tumor is a rare but recognized subtype of kidney cancer, primarily affecting children.</data>
      <data key="d6">pediatric oncology, rare cancer types</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Sarcoma" target="Kidney Cancer">
      <data key="d4">12.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Renal Sarcoma is a rare category of kidney cancer originating from connective tissue or blood vessels.</data>
      <data key="d6">tissue-specific cancer, rare malignancies</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="Kidney Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN provides comprehensive guidelines and resources for the diagnosis, treatment, and management of kidney cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical guidelines, cancer care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)" target="Kidney Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC) is the most common type of Kidney Cancer, characterized by tumors in the renal tubules.&lt;SEP&gt;Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC) is the most common type of Kidney Cancer, originating in renal tubules.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, subtype hierarchy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastasis" target="Kidney Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Metastasis is a process Forces Kidney Cancer to spread to other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer progression, metastatic spread</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colostomy" target="Transabdominal Surgery">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Colostomy is a procedure often required after transabdominal surgery to manage stool diversion in cases where colorectal anastomosis is not feasible.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical outcomes, post-operative care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Abdominoperineal Resection (APR)" target="Transabdominal Surgery">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Abdominoperineal Resection (APR) is a specific type of transabdominal surgery used for lower rectal tumors, requiring a permanent colostomy.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical specialization, treatment variation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colonoanal Anastomosis" target="Transabdominal Surgery">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Colonoanal Anastomosis is an alternative surgical procedure performed during transabdominal surgery when colorectal anastomosis is not feasible, directly connecting the colon to the anus.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical alternative, anatomical connection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colectomy" target="Colostomy">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Colostomy is often performed alongside colectomy to manage stool diversion when colon reconnection is not feasible.&lt;SEP&gt;Colostomy is often performed alongside colectomy to manage stool diversion when colon reconnection is not possible.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical adjunct, post-operative care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colon Cancer" target="Colostomy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Colostomy is often performed alongside colectomy to manage stool diversion when colon reconnection is not possible.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical adjunct, post-operative care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Abdominoperineal Resection (APR)" target="Colostomy">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Colostomy is a necessary consequence of APR, creating a permanent stoma for stool elimination after the anus is removed.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical outcome, post-operative care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Anal Cancer" target="Colostomy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Colostomy is a surgical procedure often required after APR for anal cancer patients to divert stool.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical procedure, post-treatment care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Transab than abdominal Surgery" target="Colorectal Anastomosis">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Colorectal Anastomosis is a procedure performed during transabdominal surgery to reconnect the colon to the remaining rectum, enabling near-normal bowel function.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical technique, functional restoration</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Progressive Disease" target="Abdominoperineal Resection (APR)">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Abdominoperineal resection (APR) is the standard surgical intervention for progressive disease, where cancer has recurred or spread beyond localized areas.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical management, advanced disease</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Anal Cancer" target="Abdominoperineal Resection (APR)">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">APR is a primary treatment for metastatic anal cancer, involving the removal of the anus and rectum to address recurrent or advanced disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment, surgical intervention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lymph Node Dissection" target="Abdominoperineal Resection (APR)">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lymph node dissection is performed during APR to remove cancerous lymph nodes in the groin, reducing recurrence risk.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical procedure, cancer removal</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Anal Cancer" target="Abdominoperineal Resection (APR)">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">APR is a surgical category used for advanced anal cancer cases that do not respond to initial treatment, involving removal of the anus and rectum.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical intervention, cancer management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chemotherapy" target="Oxaliplatin">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Oxaliplatin is a chemotherapy drug used in rectal cancer regimens, contributing to the treatment's effectiveness but causing potential side effects.</data>
      <data key="d6">drug therapy, chemotherapy regimen</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chemotherapy" target="Irinotecan">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Irinotecan is a chemotherapy drug used in rectal cancer regimens, associated with specific side effects like gastrointestinal issues and hair loss.</data>
      <data key="d6">drug therapy, chemotherapy regimen</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
