    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Tubules" target="Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC) originates in Renal Tubules, which are the primary site of tumor development.</data>
      <data key="d6">tumor origin, cellular structure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Papillary RCC" target="Non-Clear Cell RCC">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Papillary RCC is the most common subtype within Non-Clear Cell RCC, characterized by finger-like growths under a microscope.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype relationship, cancer subtypes</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chromophobe RCC" target="Non-Clear Cell RCC">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chromophobe RCC is a distinct subtype within Non-Clear Cell RCC, characterized by larger, pale cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype relationship, cancer subtypes</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Translocation RCC" target="Non-Clear Cell RCC">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Translocation RCC is a rare subtype of Non-Clear Cell RCC, associated with genetic fusions that drive tumor growth.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype relationship, genetic factors</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Unclassified RCC" target="Non-Clear Cell RCC">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Unclassified RCC is a category within Non-Clear Cell RCC for tumors that do not fit into specific subtypes.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype classification, cancer subtypes</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Collecting Duct RCC" target="Non-Clear Cell RCC">
      <data key="d4">12.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Collecting Duct RCC is a rare subtype of Non-Clear Cell RCC, forming in the collecting ducts of the kidney.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype relationship, cancer subtypes</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Medullary Carcinoma (RMC)" target="Non-Clear Cell RCC">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Renal Medullary Carcinoma (RMC) is a rare subtype of Non-Clear Cell RCC, often linked to genetic and sickle cell traits.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype relationship, genetic factors</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Clear Cell Papillary Renal Cell Tumor" target="Non-Clear Cell RCC">
      <data key="d4">12.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clear Cell Papillary Renal Cell Tumor is a rare subtype of Non-Clear Cell RCC combining features of Clear Cell and Papillary RCC.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype relationship, cancer subtypes</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fumarate Hydratase-Deficient RCC" target="Non-Clear Cell RCC">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Fumarate Hydrat,ase-Deficient RCC is a rare subtype of Non-Clear Cell RCC linked to mutations in the Fumarate Hydratase gene.&lt;SEP&gt;Fumarate Hydratase-Deficient RCC is a rare subtype of Non-Clear Cell RCC linked to mutations in the Fumarate Hydratase gene.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype relationship, genetic mutations</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ELOC (formerly TCEB1)-Mutated RCC" target="Non-Clear Cell RCC">
      <data key="d4">12.0</data>
      <data key="d5">ELOC (formerly TCEB1)-Mutated RCC is a rare subtype of Non-Clear Cell RCC associated with mutations in the TCEB1 gene.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype relationship, genetic factors</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Glioma" target="Histology">
      <data key="d4">20.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Gliomas are classified by histology, which examines the microscopic structure of tumor cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">classification, tumor analysis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lymph Node" target="Metastasis">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lymph Node is a common site for metastasis in prostate cancer, often detected through imaging techniques.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer spread, metastatic site</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Biopsy" target="Metastasis">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biopsies of metastatic sites, such as the liver, are performed to analyze cancer spread and guide treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Biopsy is used to confirm the presence of metastasis and guide targeted treatment strategies for cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer spread, diagnostic procedure&lt;SEP&gt;diagnostic confirmation, treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bones" target="Metastasis">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bones are anatomical structures where cancer may metastasize, necessitating biopsy for diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer spread, anatomical location</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Internal Organs" target="Metastasis">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Internal organs are anatomical structures where cancer may metastasize, requiring biopsy for analysis.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer spread, anatomical location</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage 4" target="Metastasis">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Metastasis is the spread of cancer cells from the primary tumor to distant organs, a critical factor in determining cancer stages and treatment plans.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer progression, staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer Staging" target="Metastasis">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Metastasis is a key factor in cancer staging, as it determines the spread of cancer to distant sites and influences prognosis and treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease progression, treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Liver Cancer" target="Metastasis">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Liver Cancer spreads through metastasis, where cancer cells migrate to distant organs like the lung or bone.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer spread, metastatic process</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Secondary Liver Cancer" target="Metastasis">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Secondary Liver Cancer is a result of metastasis, where cancer spreads to the liver from other organs like the colon.</data>
      <data key="d6">metastatic spread, cancer progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acute Myeloid Leukemia" target="Blood">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">AML affects blood by disrupting normal blood cell production, leading to an overabundance of immature blasts and a deficiency of mature blood cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">blood disorders, cellular dysfunction</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Blasts" target="Blood">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Blasts are immature blood cells that, in AML, fail to mature properly, leading to the accumulation of abnormal cells in the blood and bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d6">cellular development, cancer progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hematopoietic Stem Cells" target="Red Blood Cells (RBCs)">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hematopoietic stem cells differentiate into red blood cells, which are responsible for oxygen transport in the blood.</data>
      <data key="d6">cell differentiation, blood cell formation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hematopoietic Stem Cells" target="Platelets (PLTs)">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hematopoietic stem cells differentiate into platelets, which are essential for blood clotting and preventing excessive bleeding.</data>
      <data key="d6">cell differentiation, hemostasis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hematopoethetic Stem Cells" target="Platelets (PLTs)">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hematopoietic stem cells differentiate into platelets, which are essential for blood clotting and preventing excessive bleeding.</data>
      <data key="d6">cell differentiation, hemostasis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Burkitt Lymphoma" target="Burkitt Leukemia">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Burkitt leukemia shares similarities with Burkitt lymphoma, including rapid cell proliferation and B-cell origin, but differs in treatment approaches due to distinct biological characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, biological differences</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Blasts" target="Acute Myeloid Leukemia">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">AML is characterized by the proliferation of abnormal blasts, which crowd out healthy blood cells and disrupt normal marrow function.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer pathology, cellular disruption</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Blood tests" target="Comprehensive metabolic panel (CMP)">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The comprehensive metabolic panel (CMP) is a specific type of blood test that evaluates organ function and electrolyte levels, such as calcium and alkaline phosphatase.&lt;SEP&gt;The comprehensive metabolic panel (CMP) is a specific type of blood test that evaluates organ function and metabolic indicators, including electrolyte levels and calcium concentrations.</data>
      <data key="d6">organ function, metabolic analysis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="coagulopathy" target="Blood clotting tests">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Blood clotting tests are critical for diagnosing coagulopathy, an impaired clotting process commonly associated with leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic testing, blood disorders</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="allogeneic hematopoietic cell transplant (HCT)" target="HLA typing">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">HLA typing is performed before an allogeneic hematopoietic cell transplant (HCT) to ensure donor-recipient compatibility.</data>
      <data key="d6">transplant preparation, compatibility</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="treatment plan" target="Performance status (PS)">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Performance status (PS) is a key factor considered when determining a patient's treatment plan and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment decision, health assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cancer center" target="Bone marrow tests">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cancer centers conduct bone marrow aspirate and biopsy procedures to assess bone marrow health and detect abnormalities in cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">hematological evaluation, diagnostic procedures</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="Bone marrow tests">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MRI and bone marrow tests are both used in cancer diagnosis, with MRI providing structural imaging and bone marrow tests evaluating cellular abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic overlap, complementary testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL)" target="Differential">
