      <data key="d7">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Imaging Tests" target="Diagnostic Tests">
      <data key="d4">45.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Imaging Tests are a critical subset of Diagnostic Tests, providing visual confirmation of cancer presence and spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging Tests are a subset of Diagnostic Tests used to visualize internal structures and detect cancerous abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging Tests are a subset of Diagnostic Tests used to visualize prostate structures and detect cancerous lesions.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, tumor visualization&lt;SEP&gt;imaging confirmation, diagnostic subset&lt;SEP&gt;imaging, detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Biopsy" target="Diagnostic Tests">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biopsy is a key diagnostic test within Diagnostic Tests to confirm cancer presence and guide treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d6">tissue analysis, definitive diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Biopsies" target="Diagnostic Tests">
      <data key="d4">27.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biopsies are a direct method within Diagnostic Tests to confirm cancerous tissue through histological analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Biopsies are a key component of Diagnostic Tests, providing histological confirmation of cancerous tissue.</data>
      <data key="d6">tissue analysis, confirmation&lt;SEP&gt;tissue confirmation, diagnostic method</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Imaging Tests" target="MRI, CT, PET, ultrasound, bone scan">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MRI, CT, PET, ultrasound, and bone scan are specific imaging modalities within Imaging Tests for prostate cancer detection.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging modalities, diagnostic tools</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PSMA" target="PSMA/PET">
      <data key="d4">20.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PSMA is the target protein for PSMA/PET imaging, enabling the visualization of prostate cancer cells and their spread through the body.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted imaging, molecular detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Imaging Tests" target="Bone Scan">
      <data key="d4">36.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A bone scan is a specific type of imaging test used to detect cancer metastasis to the bones.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone Scan is a specific type of Imaging Test to detect bone metastases in advanced Prostate Cancer cases.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging types, cancer detection&lt;SEP&gt;metastasis detection, bone involvement</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiotracer" target="Bone Scan">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A radiotracer is used in a bone scan to detect cancerous bone metastases through radiation imaging.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging agent, cancer detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Sodium Fluoride PET-CT" target="Bone Scan">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Sodium Fluoride PET-CT is an alternative to bone scans for evaluating bone metastases.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic alternatives, imaging comparison</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT Scan" target="PET/CT Scan">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CT Scan is a component of the PET/CT Scan, which combines anatomical and functional imaging for cancer staging.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging integration, diagnostic combination</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI Scans" target="Biopsy Procedure">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MRI Scans provide detailed imaging of the prostate, guiding the precision of the biopsy procedure and assessing cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI scans provide detailed images of the prostate, guiding the precision of the biopsy procedure and helping assess cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging guidance, diagnostic support</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Invasive Tests" target="Biopsy Procedure">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The Biopsy Procedure is a specific example of an invasive test, involving tissue sampling for cancer diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">invasive procedure, diagnostic method</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urologist" target="Biopsy Procedure">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The Urologist performs the biopsy procedure, using imaging techniques to guide the needle insertion and obtain tissue samples.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural execution, medical expertise</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Germline Mutations" target="Mutations">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Germline mutations are a subset of mutations that are inherited and present in all cells, contributing to hereditary cancer risks.</data>
      <data key="d6">inherited risk, genetic inheritance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Acquired Mutations" target="Mutations">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Acquired mutations occur spontaneously in specific cells and are a major cause of somatic cancer development, distinct from inherited mutations.</data>
      <data key="d6">spontaneous mutation, somatic cancer</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inherited Mutations" target="Mutations">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Inherited mutations are passed down through families and are a key factor in germline cancer predisposition, such as in hereditary prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">hereditary risk, genetic inheritance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Treatment Team" target="PSA Test">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The Treatment Team relies on PSA Test results to monitor Prostate Cancer progression and response to treatment over time.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment monitoring, biomarker tracking</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Best Practices" target="PSA Test">
      <data key="d4">15.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Best Practices emphasize the importance of PSA Test results in conjunction with other diagnostic tools for accurate Prostate Cancer detection and monitoring.&lt;SEP&gt;PSA Test results are a key component of Best Practices for Prostate Cancer detection, requiring standardized interpretation and follow-up.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic integration, biomarker analysis&lt;SEP&gt;diagnostic standardization, biomarker tracking</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Prostate-Specific Antigen (PSA)" target="PSA Test">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The PSA Test measures the level of Prostate-Specific Antigen in the blood, which is a key biomarker for Prostate Cancer detection and monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker measurement, diagnostic screening</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urinary Tract Infection" target="PSA Test">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PSA Test results may be temporarily elevated by Urinary Tract Infections, requiring careful interpretation to avoid misdiagnosis of Prostate Cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic interference, medical complication</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Multidisciplinary Care Team" target="Urologist">
      <data key="d4">108.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The Multidisciplinary Care Team includes the Urologist, who performs Cystoscopy and other procedures to diagnose and treat bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The Urologist is a critical member of the Multidisciplinary Care Team, diagnosing and treating bladder cancer with surgical and intravesical therapies.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural execution, cancer treatment&lt;SEP&gt;specialized care, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cystoscopy" target="Urologist">
      <data key="d4">20.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The Urologist performs Cystoscopy as a key diagnostic and therapeutic procedure to inspect the bladder and remove tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Urologist performs cystoscopy to examine the urinary tract and diagnose bladder cancer, directly associated with the procedure.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical procedure, diagnostic role&lt;SEP&gt;procedural execution, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Biopsy" target="Urologist">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Urologist conducts biopsies during cystoscopy to obtain tissue samples for cancer testing.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural execution, diagnostic testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TURBT" target="Urologist">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The urologist performs the TURBT procedure to examine and remove bladder tumors, making it a key role in bladder cancer diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical expertise, surgical procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radical Cystectomy" target="Urologist">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The urologist leads the radical cystectomy surgery, which involves removing the bladder and nearby organs to treat muscle-invasive bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical leadership, oncological treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urinary Diversion" target="Urologist">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The urologist designs and performs urinary diversion procedures to manage urine flow after bladder removal, tailoring the approach to the patient’s needs.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-operative management, surgical specialization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Partial Cystectomy" target="Urologist">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The urologist performs partial cystectomy to remove part of the bladder, though it is rarely used for bladder cancer due to limited applicability.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical procedure, rare application</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tumor Profiling" target="Molecular Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tumor Profiling is a component of molecular biomarker testing that evaluates a tumor's genetic and molecular characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d6">molecular analysis, tumor characteristics</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pro,states Cancer" target="MSH6">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MSH6 mutations are associated with hereditary cancer predisposition and may play a role in prostate cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d6">hereditary cancer, DNA repair</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN" target="Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCC, provides educational materials and guidelines on breast cancer, covering its progression, metastasis, and treatment strategies."|&gt;"medical education, cancer awareness&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN offers educational materials and guidelines on breast cancer, covering its progression, metastasis, and treatment strategies."|&gt;"medical education, cancer awareness</data>
      <data key="d6">8</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bone" target="Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Breast cancer can metastasize to the bone, which is a common site for distant spread due to its vascular connections and metabolic activity.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer metastasis, anatomical spread</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lungs" target="Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Breast cancer can spread to the lungs through the bloodstream or lymphatic system, making the lungs a common site for metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d6">metastatic spread, systemic disease</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Liver" target="Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Breast cancer can metastasize to the liver, often due to its role in filtering blood and its vascular connections to the breast tissue.</data>
      <data key="d6">organ metastasis, systemic spread</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Brain" target="Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Breast cancer can spread to the brain via the bloodstream, often affecting the central nervous system and leading to neurological complications.</data>
      <data key="d6">neurological metastasis, systemic disease</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Axillary Lymph Nodes" target="Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
