    </edge>
    <edge source="HIV" target="Primary CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">HIV weakens the immune system, increasing susceptibility to primary CNS lymphoma, which is frequently linked to HIV infection.</data>
      <data key="d6">immunodeficiency, viral association</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Epstein-Barr Virus" target="Primary CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) is a known risk factor for primary CNS lymphoma, with latent viral activity contributing to lymphocyte transformation.</data>
      <data key="d6">viral association, oncogenic potential</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Autoimmune Disease" target="Primary CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Autoimmune diseases like lupus may elevate the risk of primary CNS lymphoma due to immune system dysregulation.</data>
      <data key="d6">immune dysregulation, disease comorbidity</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Organ Transplant" target="Primary CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Organ transplant recipients face higher risks of primary CNS lymphoma due to immunosuppressive therapy weakening immune defenses.</data>
      <data key="d6">immunosuppression, transplant risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Older Adult" target="Primary CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Primary CNS lymphoma is more commonly diagnosed in older adults, though it can occur at any age, with age being a significant risk factor.</data>
      <data key="d6">age-related risk, demographic association</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Central Nervous System" target="Primary CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Primary CNS lymphoma originates and proliferates within the central nervous system, affecting the brain and spinal cord.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical origin, disease localization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Meninges" target="Primary CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Primary CNS lymphoma can spread to the meninges, causing inflammation and neurological symptoms.</data>
      <data key="d6">tumor spread, meningeal involvement</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cerebrospinal Fluid" target="Primary CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lymphoma cells may disseminate into the cerebrospinal fluid, contributing to symptom progression and diagnostic challenges.</data>
      <data key="d6">fluid dissemination, diagnostic complexity</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Immune System" target="Primary CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Primary CNS lymphoma arises from dysregulated lymphocytes in the immune system, highlighting its connection to immune dysfunction.</data>
      <data key="d6">immune origin, cellular pathology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Immunodeficiency Disorder" target="Primary CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Immunodeficiency disorders impair immune surveillance, allowing lymphoma development in the CNS.</data>
      <data key="d6">immune surveillance failure, disease causation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Symptoms" target="Primary CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Primary CNS lymphoma causes neurological symptoms like headaches and seizures due to CNS tumor pressure and infiltration.</data>
      <data key="d6">neurological impact, symptomatology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Amenorrhea" target="Primary CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">5.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Amenorrhea may occur during endocrine therapy for premenopause, unrelated to primary CNS lymphoma but noted in the text.</data>
      <data key="d6">hormonal therapy, treatment side effects</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Clinical Trials" target="Primary CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical Trials are treatment options for primary CNS lymphoma, offering potential therapies to slow growth, reduce symptoms, and improve outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment research, medical innovation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="Primary CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MRI is the most critical imaging modality for diagnosing primary CNS lymphoma, providing detailed CNS structure visualization for tumor detection and localization.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI is the most useful imaging technique for identifying and diagnosing primary CNS lymphoma, as it provides detailed images of the brain and spinal cord.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, CNS imaging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT Scan" target="Primary CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CT scans are used as an alternative diagnostic tool for CNS lymphoma when MRI is unavailable, offering cross-sectional imaging for tumor assessment.&lt;SEP&gt;CT scans are used as an alternative to MRI for diagnosing CNS lymphoma, particularly when MRI is unavailable or not recommended.</data>
      <data key="d6">alternative imaging, diagnostic methods&lt;SEP&gt;diagnostic imaging, alternative methods</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Neurological Exam" target="Primary CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A neurological exam is performed to assess symptoms and determine the need for imaging tests like MRI or CT to confirm CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A neurological exam is performed to evaluate symptoms and guide the selection of imaging tests (e.g., MRI, CT) for CNS lymphoma diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic evaluation, symptom assessment&lt;SEP&gt;symptom assessment, diagnostic workflow</data>
