      <data key="d7">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hyperplastic Polyp" target="Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hyperplastic polyps are non-cancerous but can be associated with other conditions, though they rarely become cancerous.</data>
      <data key="d6">benign growth, cancer risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inflammatory Polyp" target="Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Inflammatory polyps are non-cancerous and rarely develop into colon cancer, but they may indicate underlying inflammation.</data>
      <data key="d6">benign growth, inflammatory conditions</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Polyp" target="Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Colon cancer often develops from polyps, particularly adenomas, which can progress to cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer progression, polyp association</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Treatment Phases" target="Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Colon cancer is managed through various treatment phases, including screening, prevention, and therapeutic interventions.&lt;SEP&gt;Colon cancer management involves treatment phases such as screening, prevention, and therapeutic interventions.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer management, treatment stages</data>
