    <edge source="Radiation Therapy" target="Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy (IMRT)">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy (IMRT) is an advanced Radiation Therapy technique enhancing precision and reducing side effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy (IMRT) is an advanced form of Radiation Therapy that enhances precision and reduces side effects.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation technique, medical innovation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Anal Cancer" target="Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy (IMRT)">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">IMRT is a recommended radiation therapy category for anal cancer, offering precise targeting of cancerous tissues.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation therapy, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation Therapy" target="Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy (SBRT)">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy (SBRT) is a specialized Radiation Therapy approach for metastatic cervical cancer, delivering high doses in fewer sessions.</data>
      <data key="d6">targeted treatment, metastatic care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ablative Therapies" target="Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy (SBRT)">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">SBRT is a specialized form of ablative therapy that delivers highly precise radiation to tumors in specific locations.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation precision, localized treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Cancer" target="Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy (SBRT)">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">SBRT is used for limited metastases in metastatic cancer, delivering targeted radiation to control tumor growth while minimizing damage to surrounding tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;SBRT is used for limited metastases in metastatic cancer, delivering targeted radiation to control tumor growth.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment approach, cancer management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation Therapy" target="Survivorship">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Survivorship involves long-term care and management of side effects from Radiation Therapy, including fertility and sexual health concerns.&lt;SEP&gt;Survivorship involves long-term care for side effects from Radiation Therapy, including fertility and sexual health concerns.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment aftermath, quality of life</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Follow-up Visits" target="Survivorship">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Survivorship includes ongoing Follow-up Visits to manage long-term health and monitor for cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d6">long-term care, cancer surveillance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Survivorship">
      <data key="d4">1.0</data>
      <data key="d5">phase-of</data>
      <data key="d6">Survivorship is a phase in the care of Thyroid Cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Follow-up Care" target="Survivorship">
      <data key="d4">3.0</data>
      <data key="d5">phase-of</data>
      <data key="d6">Survivorship is a phase of ongoing follow-up care for Thyroid Cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Follow-up and Surveillance" target="Survivorship">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Survivorship involves ongoing monitoring and management of long-term effects, including fertility, sexual health, and bowel function, after anal cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-treatment care, quality of life</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Late and Long-Term Side Effects" target="Survivorship">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Survivorship involves managing late and long-term side effects of cancer treatment, such as bowel, urinary, and sexual function changes.&lt;SEP&gt;Survivorship involves managing late and long-term side effects of cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-treatment care, health management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Primary Care Doctor" target="Survivorship">
      <data key="d4">24.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Primary care doctors are integral to survivorship care, managing long-term health and preventing complications for cancer survivors.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary care doctors play a key role in long-term follow-up and survivorship care for cancer patients, coordinating with oncology teams.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary care doctors play a key role in long-term follow-up and survivorship care for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">chronic management, post-treatment care&lt;SEP&gt;healthcare coordination, chronic management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chemotherapy" target="EBRT">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy and EBRT are combined in chemoradiation, a treatment strategy for locally advanced cervical cancer, to maximize tumor shrinkage and improve treatment outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d6">chemoradiation, combined therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Brachytherapy" target="EBRT">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">EBRT is often followed by brachytherapy to allow precise applicator placement, as shrinking the tumor with EBRT improves the effectiveness of targeted radiation in brachytherapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment sequence, radiation synergy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Systemic Therapy" target="EBRT">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">EBRT is often followed by systemic therapy to target any remaining cancer cells that may have spread beyond the pelvis.</data>
      <data key="d6">sequential treatment, multi-modal therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ablative Therapies" target="EBRT">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">EBRT and ablative therapies are both localized radiation techniques used to target cancerous tissues with precision.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation modalities, localized treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMRT" target="EBRT">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">IMRT is a specialized form of EBRT that enhances precision in radiation delivery for anal cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation technique, specialization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="HDR Brachytherapy" target="Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d4">20.0</data>
      <data key="d5">HDR Brachytherapy is a specific type of brachytherapy that delivers high-dose radiation in short, intense sessions over a short period, commonly used for cervical cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;HDR Brachytherapy is a specific type of brachytherapy that delivers high-dose radiation in short, intense sessions, commonly used for cervical cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation delivery, treatment modality</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LDR Brachytherapy" target="Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">LDR Brachytherapy is another type of brachytherapy that involves lower-dose radiation delivered over a longer period, suitable for certain advanced cervical cancer cases.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation duration, dose management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tandem and Ring Applicator" target="Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The Tandem and Ring Applicator is a critical tool in HDR Brachytherapy, enabling precise placement of radioactive sources for targeted tumor treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation delivery, device integration</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tandem and Ovoid Applicator" target="Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The Tandem and Ovoid Applicator is an alternative device used in HDR Brachytherapy, providing flexibility in radiation source placement for complex tumor geometries.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation delivery, device variation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvic Exenteration Surgery" target="Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic exenteration may be combined with brachytherapy to address residual cancer cells after surgery.</data>
      <data key="d6">complementary treatments, combined therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Interstitial Brachytherapy" target="Intracavitary Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Intracavitary and interstitial brachytherapy are both subtypes of brachytherapy, differing in the placement of radioactive material (cavities vs. tissues) and their application for specific cancer stages.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation placement, treatment specialization</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Clinical Trials" target="National Library of Medicine (NLM)">
      <data key="d4">32.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The NLM maintains ClinicalTrials.gov, a database listing ongoing clinical trials, which helps patients find treatment opportunities."|&gt;"information access, trial availability&lt;SEP&gt;The NLM provides access to clinical trial databases, enabling patients and researchers to find and participate in trials.</data>
      <data key="d6">9&lt;SEP&gt;resource accessibility, trial discovery</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Systemic Therapy" target="Cancer Treatment Options">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Systemic therapy is a subset of cancer treatment options that targets cancer cells throughout the body, including chemotherapy, targeted therapy, and immunotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">therapeutic scope, treatment diversity</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation Therapy" target="Cancer Treatment Options">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy is a treatment option that uses high-energy radiation to kill cancer cells, often combined with chemotherapy in chemoradiation.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation application, combined therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chemoradiation" target="Locally Advanced Cancer">
      <data key="d4">36.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemoradiation is a primary treatment option for locally advanced cancer, combining chemotherapy and radiation to target cancer cells effectively.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation is the standard treatment for locally advanced cancer, using chemotherapy to enhance the effectiveness of radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">combined modality, localized control&lt;SEP&gt;combined therapy, advanced disease management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Systemic Therapy" target="Metastatic Cancer">
      <data key="d4">38.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Systemic therapy is the primary treatment for metastatic cancer, aiming to control growth and prevent further spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic therapy is the primary treatment for metastatic cancer, targeting cancer cells that have spread to distant organs through drugs or immunological approaches.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer progression, systemic treatment&lt;SEP&gt;systemic attack, advanced disease</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="Metastatic Cancer">
      <data key="d4">1.0</data>
      <data key="d5">category-subcategory</data>
      <data key="d6">Metastatic Cancer is a type of Thyroid Cancer.</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RAI Therapy" target="Metastatic Cancer">
      <data key="d4">1.0</data>
      <data key="d5">treatment-for</data>
      <data key="d6">RAI Therapy is a treatment option for Metastatic Cancer.</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN Guidelines for Patients®" target="Metastatic Cancer">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients® address metastatic cancer, explaining its behavior and treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, treatment protocols</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Immunotherapy" target="Metastatic Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Immunotherapy is recommended for metastatic cancer to boost the immune system against cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy is recommended for metastatic cancer to enhance the immune system's ability to detect and destroy cancer cells, improving survival outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment for advanced disease, immune enhancement</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="fertility-sparing treatment" target="early-stage cancer">
      <data key="d4">15.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Early-stage cancer may involve fertility-sparing treatment to preserve reproductive options.</data>
      <data key="d6">fertility preservation, treatment options</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="extrafascial (simple) hysterectomy" target="early-stage cancer">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Extrafascial hysterectomy is a surgical treatment option for early-stage cancers that do not require fertility preservation.&lt;SEP&gt;Extrafascial hysterectomy is a surgical treatment option for early-stage cancers without fertility preservation.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical intervention, cancer management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="chemoradiation" target="locally advanced cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Locally advanced cancers are primarily treated with chemoradiation to target cancer cells and prevent spread.</data>
      <data key="d6">therapeutic approach, cancer management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="cone biopsy" target="Stage 1A1">
      <data key="d4">36.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cone biopsy is a diagnostic and therapeutic procedure for Stage 1A1 cancers to assess and treat cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 1A1 cancers are diagnosed and treated with cone biopsy to remove cervical tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 1A1 cancers are often diagnosed and treated with cone biopsy to remove cervical tissue.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedure, treatment&lt;SEP&gt;diagnostic tool, treatment method</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="radical trachelectomy" target="Stage 1A1">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 1A1 cancers may require radical trachelectomy to remove the cervix and surrounding tissues.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical intervention, treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="LVSI" target="Stage 1A1">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">LVSI in Stage 1A1 cancers leads to additional treatments like pelvic lymph node evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d6">risk factor, staging impact</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="radical trachelectomy" target="Stage 1B1">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage 1B1 cancers are treated with radical tracheectomy and pelvic lymph node evaluation.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 1B1 cancers are treated with radical trachelectomy and pelvic lymph node evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, lymphatic assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="reproductive endocrinologists" target="fertility-sparing treatment">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Fertility-sparing treatment may involve consultation with reproductive endocrinologists for fertility preservation.</data>
      <data key="d6">specialist consultation, reproductive health</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="platinum chemotherapy" target="chemoradiation">
      <data key="d4">25.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Platinum chemotherapy is a core component of chemoradiation therapy for advanced cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Platinum chemotherapy is a key component of chemoradiation for locally advanced cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">chemotherapy, radiation synergy&lt;SEP&gt;therapeutic synergy, chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="lymph node removal" target="LVSI">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">LVSI indicates cancer spread to lymph vessels, leading to removal of nearby lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;LVSI indicates cancer spread to lymph vessels, leading to the removal of nearby lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d6">risk factor, lymphatic spread</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="cancer recurrence" target="external beam radiation therapy (EBRT)">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">EBRT is used to treat or monitor for cancer recurrence after surgery or as a primary treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation therapy, post-operative care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="vaginal brachytherapy" target="external beam radiation therapy (EBRT)">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Vaginal brachytherapy is often combined with EBRT for comprehensive cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">complementary therapy, radiation synergy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pelvic Exenteration Surgery" target="Radical Hysterectomy">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Both procedures are radical surgical options for removing pelvic organs, though pelvic exenteration involves more extensive removal of surrounding structures.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical procedures, radical interventions</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cervyical Cancer" target="Pelvic Lymph Node Dissection">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic lymph node dissection is performed to assess cancer spread and guide treatment decisions, particularly for advanced stages.</data>
      <data key="d6">lymph node assessment, staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Physical Exams" target="Follow-up Visits">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Physical Exams are part of Follow-up Visits to monitor for new or worsening symptoms indicative of cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical surveillance, symptom monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ablative Therapies" target="Pelvic Exenteration Surgery">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic exenteration may be paired with ablative therapies to eliminate residual cancer cells post-surgery.</data>
      <data key="d6">combined oncology approaches</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Immunotherapy" target="Metastatic cervical cancer">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Immunotherapy is used for metastatic cervical cancer with specific biomarkers like PD-L1–positive or TMB-H.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment, biomarker targeting</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Clinical trial" target="Metastatic cervical cancer">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical trials may offer new treatment options for metastatic cervical cancer patients with specific biomarkers or treatment-resistant disease.</data>
      <data key="d6">research, treatment innovation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tisotumab vedotin-tftv (Tivdak)" target="Metastatic cervical cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Tisotumab vedotin-tftv (Tivdak) is used for cancers without specific biomarkers or in advanced stages.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment, targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cemiplimab (Libtayo)" target="Metastatic cervical cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cemiplimab (Libtayo) is an immunotherapy option for cancers with specific biomarkers or as a second-line treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment, immunotherapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Non-Hodgkin lymphoma" target="Mediastinal gray zone lymphoma (MGZL)">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Mediastinal gray zone lymphoma is a subtype of non-Hodgkin lymphoma with overlapping features of Hodgkin lymphoma and PMBL.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtypes, lymphoma classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hodgkin lymphoma (HL)" target="Mediastinal gray zone lymphoma (MGZL)">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MGZL shares overlapping features with Hodgkin lymphoma, including Reed-Sternberg cells and large abnormal lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d6">overlapping features, lymphoma types</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MGZL" target="DLBCL-NOS">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MGZL and DLBCL-NOS are distinct lymphoma subtypes, both classified under non-Hodgkin lymphomas, with overlapping treatment considerations.</data>
      <data key="d6">lymphoma subtypes, cancer classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="DLBCL-NOS">
      <data key="d4">1.0</data>
      <data key="d5">DLBCL-NOS is a lymphoma classification with potential associations to thyroid cancer, necessitating comprehensive evaluation for accurate diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">related malignancies, classification overlap"{tuple_delimiter}8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Immunotherapy" target="PD-L1 biomarker">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">PD-L1 biomarker indicates responsiveness to immunotherapy, particularly pembrolizumab (Keytruda).</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker, treatment targeting</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pembrolizumab (Keytruda)" target="PD-L1 biomarker">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pembrolizumab (Keytruda) is used for cancers with PD-L1–positive biomarkers.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker, immunotherapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Immunotherapy" target="TMB-H biomarker">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">TMB-H biomarker indicates responsiveness to immunotherapy due to high genetic mutations.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker, treatment targeting</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Immunotherapy" target="MSI-H/dMMR biomarker">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MSI-H/dMMR biomarker indicates genetic instability, making cancer cells responsive to immunotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">biomarker, treatment targeting</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MSI-H/dMMR" target="Pembrolizumab (Keytruda)">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pembrolizumab (Keytruda) is a treatment for MSI-H/dMMR tumors, which are cancers with microsatellite instability and deficient mismatch repair mechanisms.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment, genetic markers</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TMB-H" target="Pembrolizumab (Keytruda)">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pembrolizumab (Keytruda) is used for TMB-H tumors, which have a high tumor mutational burden and may respond to immunotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">immunotherapy, mutation load</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Immunotherapy" target="Pembrolizumab (Keytruda)">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Immunotherapy also involves Pembrolizumab, another immune checkpoint inhibitor used in advanced cancer treatment to boost immune responses against tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy also involves Pembrolizumab, another immune checkpoint inhibitor used in advanced cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment modality, drug association</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chemotherapy" target="MGZL">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MGZL is typically treated with chemotherapy, reflecting its classification as a lymphoma with aggressive features.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment, lymphoma therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Cancer" target="MGZL">
      <data key="d4">1.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MGZL is a distinct lymphoma type with overlapping features to thyroid cancer, requiring specialized diagnostic and therapeutic approaches.</data>
      <data key="d6">related malignancies, diagnostic overlap"{tuple_delimiter}8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN Guidelines for Patients" target="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">NCCN Guidelines for Patients provide evidence-based recommendations for diagnosing and managing thyroid cancer, ensuring standardized care.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Guidelines for Patients provides information and treatment recommendations for thyroid cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical guidelines, treatment protocols"{tuple_delimiter}9.5&lt;SEP&gt;medical guidelines, patient resources</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid gland" target="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">1.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Thyroid cancer originates in the thyroid gland, a structure critical for hormone production and regulation of metabolic processes.</data>
      <data key="d6">organ pathology, disease origin"{tuple_delimiter}9.5</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid nodules" target="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">1.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Thyroid nodules are abnormal growths that may develop into thyroid cancer, requiring evaluation for malignancy.</data>
      <data key="d6">tumor progression, diagnostic indicators"{tuple_delimiter}9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation exposure" target="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">1.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation exposure is a major risk factor for thyroid cancer, particularly from medical treatments or environmental sources.</data>
      <data key="d6">etiologic factor, carcinogenic risk"{tuple_delimiter}9.2</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cowden syndrome" target="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">1.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cowden syndrome is a genetic disorder that elevates the risk of thyroid cancer, often requiring genetic counseling and surveillance.</data>
      <data key="d6">hereditary condition, syndrome association"{tuple_delimiter}9.1</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Carney complex" target="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">1.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Carney complex is a genetic syndrome associated with thyroid cancer, requiring multidisciplinary care and monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic syndromes, endocrine disorders"{tuple_delimiter}9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia (MEN)" target="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">1.0</data>
      <data key="d5">MEN is a genetic disorder that increases the risk of thyroid cancer alongside tumors in other endocrine glands, requiring family history assessment.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic syndromes, endocrine tumors"{tuple_delimiter}9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TSH test" target="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">1.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TSH test helps assess thyroid function and detect abnormalities that may indicate thyroid cancer or other endocrine disorders.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic tool, hormone assessment"{tuple_delimiter}9.2</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Biopsy" target="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">1.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biopsy of thyroid nodules is essential for confirming malignancy and guiding treatment decisions in thyroid cancer management.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural diagnosis, histopathology"{tuple_delimiter}9.4</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hyperthyroidism" target="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">1.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hyperthyroidism may co-occur with thyroid cancer, requiring careful differentiation to avoid misdiagnosis and inappropriate treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">comorbid conditions, differential diagnosis"{tuple_delimiter}8.5</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radioactive Iodine Therapy" target="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radioactive Iodine Therapy is a targeted treatment for differentiated thyroid cancers, using iodine-131 to destroy cancer cells that absorb iodine.&lt;SEP&gt;Radioactive Iodine Therapy is a treatment method specifically used to target and destroy thyroid cancer cells, particularly for differentiated thyroid cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer treatment, targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy" target="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy is used to maintain TSH levels within a therapeutic range, preventing cancer recurrence and managing metabolic function in thyroid cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy is used to suppress TSH levels, preventing cancer recurrence and managing metabolic function in thyroid cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">hormone regulation, cancer prevention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Targeted Therapy" target="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">35.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Targeted Therapy is an advanced treatment option for thyroid cancers that do not respond to other treatments or have metastasized, using drugs that target specific cancer cell mechanisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapies are specifically used for thyroid cancers that have metastasized or are resistant to other treatments, offering tailored cancer cell attack strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;treatment-for</data>
      <data key="d6">Targeted Therapy is used for Thyroid Cancer with specific biomarkers.&lt;SEP&gt;cancer specialization, precision medicine&lt;SEP&gt;cancer treatment, drug targeting</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Endocrinologist" target="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An Endocrinologist is a specialist who manages hormone-related conditions, including thyroid cancer, and oversees hormone replacement therapies.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical specialist, hormone management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Nuclear Medicine Doctor" target="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A Nuclear Medicine Doctor utilizes radioactive substances to diagnose and treat Thyroid Cancer, including targeted therapies and functional imaging.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Immunotherapy" target="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">1.0</data>
      <data key="d5">treatment-for</data>
      <data key="d6">Immunotherapy is used for Thyroid Cancer with specific biomarkers.</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
