      <data key="d5">indicator-for</data>
      <data key="d6">TSH levels are monitored to detect Thyroid Cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation Therapy" target="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">1.0</data>
      <data key="d5">treatment-for</data>
      <data key="d6">Radiation Therapy is used for Thyroid Cancer that cannot be removed with surgery.</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lenvatinib (Lenvima)" target="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">1.0</data>
      <data key="d5">treatment-for</data>
      <data key="d6">Lenvatinib is a targeted therapy for advanced Thyroid Cancer.</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Sorafenib (Nexavar)" target="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">1.0</data>
      <data key="d5">treatment-for</data>
      <data key="d6">Sorafenib is another targeted therapy for advanced Thyroid Cancer.</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cabozantinib (Cabometyx)" target="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">1.0</data>
      <data key="d5">treatment-for</data>
      <data key="d6">Cabozantinib is used if Thyroid Cancer progresses after treatment with Lenvatinib or Sorafenib.</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NCCN Guidelines for Patients®" target="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients® address thyroid cancer, including treatment options for metastatic cases.</data>
      <data key="d6">endocrine cancer, treatment protocols</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thy nad" target="Family history">
      <data key="d4">1.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Family history of thyroid cancer or genetic syndromes increases susceptibility to the disease, often indicating hereditary mutations.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic predisposition, familial risk"{tuple_delimiter}9.3</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TSH Test" target="Thyroid Nodule">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The TSH test is used to assess thyroid hormone levels in relation to a thyroid nodule, helping determine if the nodule is producing hormones or causing hyperthyroidism.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic testing, hormone regulation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RAI Uptake Test" target="Thyroid Nodule">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The RAI uptake test evaluates whether a thyroid nodule is hormone-producing, which is a key factor in assessing its cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d6">hormone production, cancer risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Biopsy" target="Thyroid Nodule">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A biopsy is performed to obtain tissue samples from a thyroid nodule to determine if it is cancerous, guided by ultrasound findings.</data>
      <data key="d6">tissue analysis, malignancy detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Medullary Thyroid Cancer" target="Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Thyroid hormone replacement therapy may be used in conjunction with surgery for medullary thyroid cancer, though genetic screening is often prioritized.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment integration, genetic factors</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Anaplastic Thyroid Cancer" target="Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Thyroid hormone replacement therapy is less effective for anaplastic thyroid cancer, which requires aggressive treatment strategies due to its rapid progression.</data>
      <data key="d6">aggressive treatment, hormone therapy limitations</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Differentiated Thyroid Cancers" target="Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Thyroid hormone replacement therapy is a standard treatment for differentiated thyroid cancers, maintaining hormonal balance after thyroidectomy.</data>
      <data key="d6">hormone regulation, cancer management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Calcium and Vitamin D Supplements" target="Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Calcium and Vitamin D Supplements are recommended to counteract bone loss caused by thyroid hormone replacement therapy, ensuring patient safety.</data>
      <data key="d6">nutritional support, treatment side effects</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Total Thyroidectomy" target="Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy is required after Total Thyroidectomy to maintain normal hormone levels and prevent recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-surgical care, hormone management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Differentiated Thyroid Cancers" target="Papillary Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Papillary carcinoma is a subtype of differentiated thyroid cancer, characterized by its slow growth and responsiveness to treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, treatment response</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Differentiated Thyroid Cancers" target="Follicular Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Follicular carcinoma is another subtype of differentiated thyroid cancer, originating from thyroid follicular cells and managed similarly to papillary carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, treatment approach</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Differentiated Thyroid Cancers" target="Oncocytic Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Oncocytic carcinoma is a rare subtype of differentiated thyroid cancer, often requiring surgical intervention and further testing for malignancy.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, diagnostic testing</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lobectomy" target="Oncocytic Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lobectomy may be an option for oncocytic carcinoma if the cancer is less invasive and confined to the thyroid.&lt;SEP&gt;Lobectomy may be an option for oncocytic carcinoma if the cancer is less invasive and has not spread beyond the thyroid.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pathologic Examination" target="Oncocytic Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pathologic examination is critical for confirming oncocytic carcinoma diagnosis, as FNA alone cannot definitively identify it.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic confirmation, pathological analysis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Active Surveillance" target="Differentiated Thyroid Cancers">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Active Surveillance is a management approach for certain low-risk cases of Differentiated Thyroid Cancers, where the tumor is monitored without immediate surgery.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer management, non-invasive monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Papillary Thyroid Cancer" target="Differentiated Thyroid Cancers">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Papillary Thyroid Cancer is a subtype of Differentiated Thyroid Cancers, characterized by its growth patterns and treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer subtypes, treatment differentiation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chemotherapy" target="Anaplastic Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is often combined with radiation therapy for anaplastic thyroid cancer, a highly aggressive subtype that requires aggressive treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d6">aggressive treatment, combination therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Biopsy" target="Pathology Review">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pathology review is conducted on biopsy samples to determine if a nodule is cancerous and identify the specific cancer type.</data>
      <data key="d6">tissue analysis, malignancy detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyrogen" target="Radioactive Iodine Therapy">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Thyrogen is used to elevate TSH levels, ensuring optimal iodine uptake by thyroid cancer cells during radioactive iodine therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyrogen is used to temporarily elevate TSH levels, enabling continued thyroid hormone replacement during radioactive iodine therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">hormone management, treatment coordination</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Whole-Body RAI Scan" target="Radioactive Iodine Therapy">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The Whole-Body RAI Scan is a diagnostic event conducted post-therapy to locate residual thyroid tissue or cancer cells, ensuring complete treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The Whole-Body RAI Scan is a diagnostic procedure conducted after radioactive iodine therapy to locate any remaining thyroid tissue or cancer cells in the body.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, treatment monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone (TSH)" target="Radioactive Iodine Therapy">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">TSH levels are critical for the effectiveness of radioactive iodine therapy, as they determine iodine uptake by thyroid cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">hormonal regulation, treatment efficacy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radioactive Iodne (RAI)" target="Radioactive Iodine Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radioactive Iodine (RAI) is the radioactive form of iodine used in Radioactive Iodine Therapy to target and destroy thyroid cancer cells that absorb iodine.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation therapy, iodine targeting</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radioactive Iodine (RAI)" target="Radioactive Iodine Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radioactive Iodine (RAI) is the radioactive form of iodine used in Radioactive Iodine Therapy to target and destroy thyroid cancer cells that absorb iodine.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation therapy, iodine targeting</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyroidectomy" target="Radioactive Iodine Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Thyroidectomy is a surgical procedure often followed by radioactive iodine therapy to eliminate residual cancer cells and prevent recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, post-operative care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Iodine-131" target="Radioactive Iodine Therapy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Iodine-131 is the radioactive isotope used in Radioactive Iodine Therapy to target and destroy thyroid cancer cells that absorb iodine.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation source, targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Thyrotropin Alfa" target="Radioactive Iodine Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Thyrotropin Alfa is used to elevate TSH levels, ensuring optimal iodine uptake by thyroid cancer cells during radioactive iodine therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">hormone management, treatment coordination</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radioactive Iodine Scan" target="Radioactive Iodine Therapy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The Radioactive Iodine Scan is a diagnostic event used to visualize thyroid tissue and detect cancerous cells, often performed before or after radioactive iodine therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic imaging, treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pituitary Gland" target="Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone (TSH)">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The Pituitary Gland produces Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone (TSH), which regulates thyroid hormone production and metabolic activity in the body.</data>
      <data key="d6">hormonal regulation, endocrine function</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radioactive Iodine (RAI) Therapy" target="Radiation Oncologist">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A Radiation Oncologist administers Radioactive Iodine (RAI) Therapy to target and destroy cancer cells in Thyroid Cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation treatment, cancer management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Multid'isciplinary Care Team" target="Radiation Oncologist">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The Radiation Oncologist is part of the Multidisciplinary Care Team, planning and administering radiation therapy to treat cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">radiation therapy, team coordination</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Multidisciplinary Care Team" target="Radiation Oncologist">
      <data key="d4">99.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The Multidisciplinary Care Team includes the Radiation Onc, "Oncologist, who may use findings from Cystoscopy to plan and adjust radiation therapy for bladder cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The Multidisciplinary Care Team includes the Radiation Onc, "Radiation Oncologist, who may use findings from Cystoscopy to plan and adjust radiation therapy for bladder cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The Multidisciplinary Care Team includes the Radiation Oncologist, who may use findings from Cystoscopy to plan and adjust radiation therapy for bladder cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">procedural integration, treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMRT" target="Radiation Oncologist">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation Oncologists design and oversee IMRT treatment plans to ensure optimal targeting of anal cancer while minimizing side effects.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical expertise, treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation Therapy" target="Radiation Oncologist">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
