      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Low-grade glioma is a category within gliomas characterized by slow growth and less aggressiveness.</data>
      <data key="d6">subclassification, tumor grading</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="High-grade glioma" target="Glioma">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">High-grade glioma is a category within gliomas characterized by rapid growth and increased aggressiveness.</data>
      <data key="d6">subclassification, tumor grading</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radiation Therapy" target="Glioma">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radiation therapy is frequently used alongside surgery to target residual glioma cells and prevent recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d6">complementary treatment, cancer management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chemotherapy" target="Glioma">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is employed to kill glioma cells that may remain after surgery or to manage tumors that are resistant to other treatments.</data>
      <data key="d6">systemic treatment, chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Brain Tumor" target="Glioma">
      <data key="d4">20.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Glioma is a specific type of brain tumor, categorized by its origin from glial cells and its impact on neurological function.</data>
      <data key="d6">tumor classification, medical terminology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Low-Grade Glioma" target="Glioma">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Low-grade gliomas are less aggressive forms of glioma that may grow slowly and have a better prognosis compared to high-grade variants.</data>
      <data key="d6">tumor progression, grading system</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="High-Grade Glioma" target="Glioma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">High-grade gliomas are malignant and rapidly growing tumors that often require aggressive treatment strategies due to their invasive nature.</data>
      <data key="d6">tumor progression, aggressive malignancy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Headache" target="Glioma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Headache is a common symptom of gliomas, often the first symptom in people with a glioma, indicating tumor-related pressure or irritation.</data>
      <data key="d6">symptom association, neurological impact</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Seizures" target="Glioma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Seizures are frequent in people with brain tumors and are often an early sign of a glioma, especially in low-grade gliomas.</data>
      <data key="d6">symptom association, early detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fatigue" target="Glioma">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Fatigue is a common symptom of gliomas, often resulting from tumor-related metabolic changes or neurological disruption.</data>
      <data key="d6">symptom association, quality of life</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Nausea and Vomiting" target="Glioma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Nausea and vomiting can be caused by the tumor putting increased pressure on the brain, often associated with glioma progression.</data>
      <data key="d6">symptom association, tumor growth</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Biopsy" target="Glioma">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biopsy is a procedure to obtain tissue samples from a suspected glioma to confirm the diagnosis and determine the tumor type.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedure, histopathology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lab Testing" target="Glioma">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Lab testing involves analyzing biological samples to identify glioma subtypes and guide treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic tool, molecular analysis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Survival Statistics" target="Glioma">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
