    <edge source="Test results" target="Medical history">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Medical history provides context for interpreting test results, helping clinicians understand a patient's overall health and potential risk factors.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical context, diagnostic interpretation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Test results" target="Physical exam">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Physical exam findings complement test results to form a comprehensive assessment of a patient's condition, guiding accurate diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">clinical evaluation, diagnostic integration</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urothelium" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bladder cancer originates in the urothelium, the inner lining of the urinary tract, making it a primary site for tumor development.</data>
      <data key="d6">origin, cancer site</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urinary Tract" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bladder cancer affects the urinary tract, including the kidneys, ureters, and urethra, where the urothelium is present.</data>
      <data key="d6">system involvement, cancer spread</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Kidney" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bladder cancer can originate in the kidney, though it is less common compared to the bladder itself.</data>
      <data key="d6">organ involvement, cancer origin</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ureter" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bladder cancer can develop in the ureter, which transports urine from the kidney to the bladder.</data>
      <data key="d6">organ involvement, cancer origin</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Small Cell Carcinoma" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Small cell carcinoma is a rare and aggressive type of bladder cancer associated with neuroendocrine cells and smoking.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer type, classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hematuria" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hematuria is the most common symptom of bladder cancer, often indicating the presence of a tumor in the urinary tract.</data>
      <data key="d6">symptom, diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urinalysis" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Urinalysis is a test that analyzes urine for abnormalities, including blood and cancerous cells, to aid in bladder cancer diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic method, detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cytology" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Cytology involves examining urine samples for cancerous or precancerous cells, a key method in bladder cancer diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic method, detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Biopsy" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biopsy involves removing a tissue sample to confirm a bladder cancer diagnosis through laboratory analysis.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic method, confirmation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chemical Exposure" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemical exposure to industrial substances increases the risk of bladder cancer by causing DNA damage in the urinary tract.</data>
      <data key="d6">risk factor, environmental exposure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Age" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Age is a significant risk factor for bladder cancer, with the risk increasing as individuals grow older.</data>
      <data key="d6">risk factor, demographic factor</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hereditary Mutation" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hereditary mutations are genetic changes passed down through families that increase susceptibility to bladder cancer and other cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic risk factor, hereditary condition</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Metastatic Bladder Cancer" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Metastatic bladder cancer is an advanced stage where cancer has spread beyond the bladder to other parts of the body, such as lymph nodes or organs.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer progression, metastasis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Non-Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer is an early stage where the tumor is confined to the urothelial layer of the bladder without invading deeper muscle layers.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, early stage</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Muscle-invasive bladder cancer is an advanced stage where the tumor has penetrated the bladder's muscle layer, increasing the risk of metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, progression</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Abdomen" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The abdomen is a region where metastatic bladder cancer can spread, often involving the abdominal cavity and surrounding organs.</data>
      <data key="d6">metastasis, spread location</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Aggressive Cancer" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Aggressive cancer refers to bladder cancer that grows or spreads more rapidly than average, often associated with advanced stages or high-grade tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer progression, aggressiveness</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Diagnosis" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Diagnosis of bladder cancer involves identifying the tumor's stage, type, and spread through medical tests and clinical evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic process, identification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Stage" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage refers to the extent of bladder cancer, classified into non-muscle-invasive, muscle-invasive, or metastatic stages, which guide treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer staging, classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Biomarker Tests" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Biomarker tests provide clues about bladder cancer by analyzing specific substances in the body, though they are not yet reliable for diagnosis or surveillance.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic tools, cancer monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urine Cytology" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Urine Cytology is a test used to detect cancer or precancerous cells in urine, aiding in the diagnosis of bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic testing, cancer detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urine Tests for Tumor Markers" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Urine tests for tumor markers detect cancer cells shed into urine, helping to monitor bladder cancer recurrence and treatment response.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer surveillance, diagnostic tools</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Liver Function Test" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Liver Function Test results help assess the impact of bladder cancer on liver health and guide treatment decisions for patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">organ function, treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Kidney Function Test" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Kidney Function Test results are crucial for evaluating the effects of bladder cancer on kidney health and adjusting treatment strategies accordingly.</data>
      <data key="d6">organ function, treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urogram" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A urogram is used to detect and assess the extent of bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical diagnosis, cancer detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urothelial Cells" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bladder Cancer originates in urothelial cells, which are the primary cell type in the bladder's inner lining.</data>
      <data key="d6">origin, cellular basis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Low Grade" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Low grade bladder cancer is characterized by slow-growing cells that are less likely to invade deeper tissues, associated with a better prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d6">growth rate, prognosis</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="High Grade" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">High grade bladder cancer consists of rapidly growing cells with a higher likelihood of invasion and metastasis, requiring more aggressive treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">aggressive growth, treatment intensity</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TURBT" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">38.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bladder Cancer is the primary condition treated by TURBT, which removes tumors from the bladder wall to prevent progression to more advanced stages.&lt;SEP&gt;TURBT is a surgical procedure used to remove bladder tumors associated with bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">disease treatment, tumor excision&lt;SEP&gt;treatment method, disease management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Radical Cystectomy" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">20.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Radical Cystectomy is a definitive treatment for muscle-invasive bladder cancer, involving the removal of the bladder and surrounding tissues to eliminate cancerous cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">oncological surgery, curative intervention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Intravesical Therapy" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">38.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Intravesical Therapy is used to reduce the risk of bladder cancer recurrence after initial treatments like TURBT or cystectomy, delivering medication directly into the bladder.&lt;SEP&gt;Intravesical therapy is used to destroy cancer cells in the bladder for bladder cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer treatment, localized therapy&lt;SEP&gt;preventive treatment, chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Partial Cystectomy" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Partial Cystectomy is a less common treatment for Bladder Cancer, involving the removal of part of the bladder in select cases.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical intervention, limited use</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urinary Diversion" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Urinary Diversion is a critical component of treatment for Bladder Cancer, managing urine flow after bladder removal.</data>
      <data key="d6">post-operative care, disease management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Clinical Trials" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">45.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clinical Trials are a key method for testing new treatments for bladder cancer, including chemotherapy, immunotherapy, and surgical interventions."|&gt;"treatment evaluation, cancer research&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials are part of the treatment options for bladder cancer, testing new therapies like chemotherapy, immunotherapy, and targeted therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials are research studies testing new treatments for bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">9&lt;SEP&gt;medical research, treatment development&lt;SEP&gt;research-based treatment, clinical innovation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chemotherapy" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is a primary treatment for bladder cancer, targeting cancer cells throughout the body via systemic drug administration.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment modality, cancer therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Immunotherapy" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Immunotherapy, including checkpoint inhibitors, is used to treat bladder cancer by enhancing the immune system's response to cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d6">immune-based treatment, cancer immunotherapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chemotherapy" target="Metastatic Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is often the primary treatment for metastatic bladder cancer, targeting cancer cells that have spread beyond the bladder.</data>
      <data key="d6">advanced cancer treatment, systemic therapy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </edge>
